(window.KG_PARTS = window.KG_PARTS || []).push({"group":"ettuthogai_devotional","chapters":{"pr:1":{"book":"paripadal","num":1,"title":"திருமால் (1, 2)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 1 and 2, to Thirumal: the god in the fused forms of Balarama and Krishna, his ornaments and his war on the Danavas, his unknowability and identity with all things, the ages of the world in the order of the elements, the boar, the rank of the two brothers left open, the sacrifice as his food, and prayers to dwell at his feet with kin and to keep a mind without crookedness."},"pr:2":{"book":"paripadal","num":2,"title":"திருமால் (3)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 3, to Tirumal: all classes of being spread out from the god, the Veda's praises of the bird, the boar and the swan, the uncounted hands and forms that only he can know, the lord of both gods and asuras for whom there is no enemy or friend, the god as the essence of each thing ruling the worlds with grace as umbrella and aram as sceptre, and a closing litany of names."},"pr:3":{"book":"paripadal","num":3,"title":"திருமால் (4)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 4, to Tirumal: the praise that cannot add to his fame, his body like gem, sea and cloud, the Prahlada story of the embrace and the claws, his qualities parcelled out among the sun, moon, rain, earth, flower, water, sky and wind, all banners his and the snake-eating eagle decked with snakes, his harshness and grace answering what things hold, and his many places and names, ending with the god as the servant and refuge of each devotee."},"pr:4":{"book":"paripadal","num":4,"title":"செவ்வேள் (5)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 5, to Sevvel: the vel's deeds, the velan's rite placed within the god's bound, the birth from Siva's seed through the sacrificial fire and the six sages' wives, the gifts of the gods, and a prayer for grace, love and aram instead of wealth."},"pr:5":{"book":"paripadal","num":5,"title":"வையை (6)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 6, the Vaiyai in flood: rain fills the river, the town goes down to water-play, and a husband's quarrel with his wife over the women of the water is settled at the festival."},"pr:6":{"book":"paripadal","num":6,"title":"வையை (7)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 7, to the Vaiyai: the rain-fed flood coming down from the hills to the Pandya fields, the town's cries and the river's spoils likened to the king's conquest, the lovers' quarrels of the water-play, and a closing prayer never to leave the river."},"pr:7":{"book":"paripadal","num":7,"title":"செவ்வேள் (8)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 8, to Sevvel at Parankundram: the thirty-three gods take refuge in him and the hill equals the Himalaya of his birth; the rains, creatures and flowers make his procession to Koodal; the hill's fame is unending love; a wife forbids her guilty husband to swear by Murugan and turns her grief to worship; the festival crowd's offerings and petitions; bathing lovers at the pool, and a prayer that the hill's waters outlast the rain."},"pr:8":{"book":"paripadal","num":8,"title":"செவ்வேள் (9)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 9, to Sevvel at Parankunram: his birth from Siva and his wedding to Indra's daughter on the hill, a lesson to Vedic scholars in the Tamil order of love and the sulk, the quarrel of his two wives and their women's mock battle, contests of the performers, and closing praise of the lord of both karpu and love."},"pr:9":{"book":"paripadal","num":9,"title":"வையை (10)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 10, the Vaigai: the flood announced by drums, the whole of Koodal going out to bathe, lovers' embraces and sulks, a tusker calming his frightened mate, love and toddy hidden from gossip, offerings and water-play, evening in the town, and the flood praised as giving like the Pandya."},"pr:10":{"book":"paripadal","num":10,"title":"வையை (11)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 11, to the Vaigai: the flood dated by the stars, the flowers it carries to the ford, a plea that the river go gently, the town's water-sport drawn as war, the maidens' Thai bath under the brahmins' rite, a youth swept off by the current as a girl takes his heart, the bathers' prayers for lasting love, and a close that names the song a kaikkilai."},"pr:11":{"book":"paripadal","num":11,"title":"வையை (12)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 12, to the Vaiyai: the flood coming down from the hills with their trees, the town of Madurai dressing and riding out to meet it, the music at the ford, the onlookers' gossip of a husband's straying look, his wife's garland-blows and coloured water and the flood that ends her sulk, the river muddy, clear and blood-red as the war-king's river, and the festival whose fame the world cannot hold."},"pr:12":{"book":"paripadal","num":12,"title":"திருமால் / செவ்வேள் (13, 14)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 13 and 14: Nallezhuthiyar's hymn to Thirumal, the god as mountain, as the five senses and the elements, as the reclining one, the plough-bearer and the boar, counted down through five, four, three, two and one; and Kesavanar's hymn to Sevvel, his hill in the rains and a litany of his delights; both close by calling the god mother and asking only for more worship."},"pr:13":{"book":"paripadal","num":13,"title":"திருமால் / வையை (15, 16)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 15 and 16: Ilamperuvazhuthiyar's hymn to Thirumal at Maalirunkunram, best of hills, where heaven is easily won and the two brothers of different form and one deed are worshipped with kin; and Nalvazhisiyar's Vaiyai song, the flood's coming praised place by place, a feigned bar of menstruation overrun by a lover, the flowered river as the starry sky, and a closing prayer that rain and river never fail."},"pr:14":{"book":"paripadal","num":14,"title":"செவ்வேள் (17, 18)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 17 and 18, to Murugan of Parankunram: worship on the hill where every festival sound meets a rival sound of nature, the crowded road from Koodal, the hill rivalling the god's other hill, lovers' sulks mended and a bard's lies refuted on it, and prayers to dwell at the god's feet with kin."},"pr:15":{"book":"paripadal","num":15,"title":"செவ்வேள் (19)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 19, to Sevvel at Parankunram: the hill as heaven's gift to earth and its rival weddings, the pilgrims' road from Koodal, the Pandya king circling the hill, the painted hall with its explainers, the lost girl and the echo, the flowering slopes, the maidens' rite of the elephant's leftover, and the closing praise of the red god."},"pr:16":{"book":"paripadal","num":16,"title":"வையை (20)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 20, the Vaiyai: the rain-borne flood with its scents, the town's comic rush to the river, a wife who finds her bangle on her husband's courtesan, the abuse and the courtesan's reply, the onlookers' counsel that love keeps no bounds, and the river's nature of joining lovers."},"pr:17":{"book":"paripadal","num":17,"title":"செவ்வேள் / வையை (21, 22)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal 21, to Sevvel at Parankunram: the god named by what he rides, wears, holds and dwells on, the hill's women in their sulks and adornments seen as a painting, the hill's creatures as an orchestra, a husband's plunge after his wife in a pool, and a wife's dance to her husband's drum before the prayer to dwell at his feet; and Paripadal 22, to the Vaiyai, broken by gaps: the rain told as the king's war and gifts, the procession to the bank, lovers who may be the river's ornament or it theirs, and the sounds of the Thirumarutham ford."},"pr:18":{"book":"paripadal","num":18,"title":"திருமால் (thirattu:1)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Thirumal at Irunthaiyur by Koodal: the ford, the ring of hill, pond and field, the peoples and shrines of his town, and the serpent lord whose deeds are its glory (Paripadal Thirattu 1)."},"pr:19":{"book":"paripadal","num":19,"title":"வையை (thirattu:2)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Paripadal thirattu 2, the Vaiyai fragment: the rain-fed flood reaches Koodal, the whole city rides out to the ford, lovers quarrel over garlands the water carries, the priests refuse the stained river, the bathing at the royal ford of Tirumarutham, and Maran in the flood likened to Indra in the river of the sky."},"pr:20":{"book":"paripadal","num":20,"title":"வையை / திரட்டு / மதுரை (thirattu:3, thirattu:4, thirattu:5, thirattu:6, thirattu:7, thirattu:8, thirattu:9, thirattu:10, thirattu:11, thirattu:12, thirattu:13)","section":"பரிபாடல்","theme":"Eleven fragments of lost Paripadal songs, kept by later writers: a prayer to the Vaigai, the flood likened to the Pandya's cavalry, a dancer or bride and her ornaments, and a run of city praises in which Koodal outweighs the world, is laid out as Mayon's lotus, outlasts the king's hill, river and word, outranks heaven by honouring giver and receiver, and bewilders its own people with pleasures."}},"verses":{"pr:1:1-13":{"book":"paripadal","num":1,"t":["ஆயிரம் விரித்த அணங்குடை அருந் தலை","தீ உமிழ் திறலொடு முடிமிசை அணவர,","மாயுடை மலர் மார்பின், மை இல் வால் வளை மேனிச்","சேய் உயர் பணைமிசை எழில் வேழம் ஏந்திய,","வாய் வாங்கும் வளை நாஞ்சில், ஒரு குழை ஒருவனை;","எரிமலர் சினைஇய கண்ணை; பூவை","விரிமலர் புரையும் மேனியை; மேனித்","திரு ஞெமிர்ந்து அமர்ந்த மார்பினை; மார்பில்","தெரிமணி பிறங்கும் பூணினை; மால் வரை","எரி திரிந்தன்ன பொன் புனை உடுக்கையை","சேவல் அம் கொடியோய்! நின் வல வயின் நிறுத்தும்","ஏவல் உழந்தமை கூறும்,","நா வல் அந்தணர் அரு மறைப் பொருளே."],"p":"","r":"The song opens on two forms of Thirumal: first the one with the thousand awful hooded heads spitting fire rising over his crown, a body white as a flawless conch, the plough with its curved mouth and a single earring; then the one with eyes like clustered fire-flowers, a body like the opened kaaya bloom, Tiru seated on his chest among bright jewels and a golden garment like fire running over a great hill, lord of the male-bird banner, whom the eloquent brahmins' rare scripture tells of.","n":"The first five lines carry Balarama's emblems (the serpent hood, the conch-white body, the plough, the one earring) and the next five Krishna's (dark kaaya colour, Tiru on the chest, the golden garment), yet all are addressed to one god in the second person singular (ஒருவனை, கண்ணை, மேனியை): the poem fuses the two brothers into a single object of praise. The thing raised on the tall staff in line 4 (வேழம்) is not secure: elephant or reed; the known banner of the plough-bearer is the palmyra.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","vedham","andhanar"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 1, lines 1-13","lb":"PR1:1-13","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",4,"maa","scene","elephant or reed (emblem; unsure)"],["வளை","வளை",3,"pira","scene","conch"],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",5,"seythi","ornament","plough"],["எரிமலர்","எரி மலர்",6,"maram","scene","fire-coloured flower"],["பூவை","பூவை",6,"maram","simile","kaaya flower"],["மணி","மணி",9,"pira","ornament","gem"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",11,"pul","scene","male bird (Garuda) of the banner"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:1:14-28":{"book":"paripadal","num":2,"t":["இணைபிரி அணி துணி பணி எரி புரை","விடர் இடு சுடர் படர் பொலம் புனை வினைமலர்","நெரி திரடெரி புரை தன மிகு தன முரண் மிகு","கடறரு மணியொடும் முத்து யாத்த நேரணி","நெறி செறி வெறி உறு முறல் விறல் வணங்கு அணங்கு வில்","தார் அணி துணி மணி வெயில் உறழ் எழில் புகழ் அலர் மார்பின்","எரி வயிர நுதி எறி படை எருத்து மலை இவர் நவையினிற்","றுணி படல் இன மணி வெயில் உறழ் எழில் நக்கு","இமை இருள் அகல முறு கிறுகு புரி ஒரு புரி நாள்மலர்","மலர் இலகின வளர் பரிதியின் ஒளி மணி மார்பு அணி","மணம் மிக நாறு உருவின விரை வளி மிகு கடு விசை","உடுவுறு தலை நிரை இதழ் அணி வயிறிரிய அமரரைப்","போரெழுந்து உடன்று இரைத்து உரைஇய தானவர்","சிரம் உமிழ் புனல் பொழிபு இழிந்து உரம்","உதிர்பு அதிர்பு அலம் தொடா அமர் வென்ற கணை."],"p":"","r":"In a dense run of compounds the poet piles up the god's ornaments: worked golden flowers, gems from the wilds strung with pearls, a garland on a famed chest bright as sunlight, a fire-pointed weapon, and the arrow that won the war against the Danavas who rose roaring to fight the gods until their heads poured blood like a stream.","n":"Lines 14 to 28 are written for sound more than syntax, in short chiming words (இணைபிரி அணி துணி பணி எரி புரை; நெறி செறி வெறி உறு முறல் விறல்), a sung, metrical display that suits a song set to music; the grammar of the run can be guessed but not fixed, and the reading above gives only its gist. The sentence closes on the single word கணை, the arrow, at line 28.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["maram","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 1, lines 14-28","lb":"PR1:14-28","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["மணியொடும்","மணி",17,"pira","ornament","gem"],["முத்து","முத்து",17,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["வெயில்","வெயில்",19,"pira","simile","sunlight"],["வயிர","வயிரம்",20,"pira","ornament","diamond"],["நாள்மலர்","நாள் மலர்",22,"maram","ornament","fresh flower"],["பரிதியின்","பரிதி",23,"pira","scene","sun"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:1:29-39":{"book":"paripadal","num":3,"t":["‘பொருவேம்‘ என்றவர் மதம் தபக் கடந்து,","செரு மேம்பட்ட செயிர் தீர் அண்ணல்!","இருவர் தாதை! இலங்கு பூண் மாஅல்!","தெருள நின் வரவு அறிதல்","மருள் அறு தேர்ச்சி முனைவர்க்கும் அரிதே:","அன்ன மரபின் அனையோய்! நின்னை","இன்னன் என்று உரைத்தல் எமக்கு எவன் எளிது?","அருமை நற்கு அறியினும், ஆர்வம் நின்வயின்","பெருமையின் வல்லா யாம் இவண் மொழிபவை","மெல்லிய எனாஅ வெறாஅது, அல்லி அம்","திரு மறு மார்ப! நீ அருளல் வேண்டும்."],"p":"","r":"Hailing the faultless lord who broke the pride of those who said 'we will fight', the father of two, Maal with shining jewels, the poet says that even sages of clear inquiry can hardly know his coming, so how could it be easy for us to say he is such and such; knowing how hard it is, yet driven by longing, he asks the god with Tiru's mark on his chest not to scorn as weak the words we speak here, but to grant grace.","n":"The ineffability is argued a fortiori: if sages free of confusion (மருள் அறு தேர்ச்சி முனைவர்) cannot know him, singers cannot; the hymn then asks pardon for itself, so that the praise in the following lines is offered as acknowledged inadequacy. Who the two are whose father he is (இருவர் தாதை) is not said in the poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","thiruvarul","panivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 1, lines 29-39","lb":"PR1:29-39","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["அல்லி","அல்லி",38,"maram","scene","lotus petal"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:1:40-51":{"book":"paripadal","num":4,"t":["விறல் மிகு விழுச் சீர் அந்தணர் காக்கும்","அறனும், ஆர்வலர்க்கு அளியும், நீ;","திறன் இலோர்த் திருத்திய தீது தீர் சிறப்பின்","மறனும், மாற்றலர்க்கு அணங்கும், நீ;","அம் கண் ஏர் வானத்து அணி நிலாத் திகழ்தரும்","திங்களும், தெறு கதிர்க் கனலியும், நீ;","ஐந் தலை உயிரிய அணங்குடை அருந் திறல்","மைந்துடை ஒருவனும், மடங்கலும், நீ;","நலம் முழுது அளைஇய புகர் அறு காட்சிப்","புலமும், பூவனும், நாற்றமும், நீ;","வலன் உயர் எழிலியும், மாக விசும்பும்,","நிலனும், நீடிய இமயமும், நீ."],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the god what he is: the aram that the great brahmins guard and the tenderness shown to those who love him, the valour that set right the faithless and the terror of his foes, the moon shining in the lovely sky and the burning sun, the mighty one of the five upraised heads and the lion, the sense-field of flawless vision with the flower and its scent, the rising cloud, the vast sky, the earth and the tall Himalaya.","n":"Every line ends on நீ (you): this is identity, not likeness, and no comparison marker appears. The pairs are built as a king's praise is built (grace to friends and terror to foes, moon and sun), but cosmic items follow the moral ones, so that aram and the Himalaya are predicated of the god in the same breath. ஐந் தலை and மடங்கல் are not secure: the five-headed one may be the serpent or a god, and மடங்கல் may be the lion or the fire of the world's end.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["viyaabagam","iraivan","aram","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 1, lines 40-51","lb":"PR1:40-51","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["நிலா","நிலா",44,"pira","scene","moonlight"],["திங்களும்","திங்கள்",45,"pira","scene","moon"],["கனலியும்","கனலி",45,"pira","scene","sun"],["மடங்கலும்","மடங்கல்",47,"maa","scene","lion (or doom-fire)"],["பூவனும்","பூ",49,"maram","scene","flower"],["எழிலியும்","எழிலி",50,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[["hill","இமயமும்",51]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:1:52-68":{"book":"paripadal","num":5,"t":["அதனால்,","‘இன்னோர் அனையை; இனையையால்‘ என,","அன்னோர் யாம் இவண் காணாமையின்,","பொன் அணி நேமி வலம் கொண்டு ஏந்திய","மன்னுயிர் முதல்வனை ஆதலின்,","நின்னோர் அனையை, நின் புகழோடும் பொலிந்தே!","நின் ஒக்கும் புகழ் நிழலவை;","பொன் ஒக்கும் உடையவை;","புள்ளின் கொடியவை; புரி வளையினவை;","எள்ளுநர்க் கடந்து அட்ட இகல் நேமியவை;","மண்ணுறு மணி பாய் உருவினவை;","எண் இறந்த புகழவை; எழில் மார்பினவை,","ஆங்கு,","காமரு சுற்றமொடு ஒருங்கு நின் அடியுறை","யாம் இயைந்து ஒன்றுபு வைகலும் பொலிக! என,","ஏமுறு நெஞ்சத்தேம் பரவுதும்-","வாய்மொழிப் புலவ! நின் தாள்-நிழல் தொழுதே;"],"p":"","r":"Therefore, since we see no one here of whom to say 'you are like these, you are such', and since he is the first of all lasting lives who holds the gold-adorned discus, the god is like only himself; his forms have fame like his, garments like gold, the bird banner, the twisted conch, the discus that killed the scornful, bodies like a washed gem and fame past counting; and so, the poet says, we, of joyful and secure heart, with our dear kin, pray to dwell together at your feet and prosper every day, worshipping the shade of your feet, poet of the true word.","n":"The hymn ends its search for a likeness by comparing the god to himself (நின்னோர் அனையை, நின் ஒக்கும் புகழ்): a poetics built on comparison turns the device on its own subject and finds no vehicle. The attributes of lines 58 to 63 are in the plural (நிழலவை, உடையவை, கொடியவை), as if of his many forms. The god is finally named a poet (வாய்மொழிப் புலவ), the author of the true word, the singers' own calling raised to him. The prayer asks for life together with kin (சுற்றமொடு), not for release.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","sutram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 1, lines 52-68","lb":"PR1:52-68","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பொன்","பொன்",59,"pira","simile","gold"],["புள்ளின்","புள்",60,"pul","scene","bird (Garuda) of the banner"],["வளையினவை","வளை",60,"pira","ornament","conch"],["மணி","மணி",62,"pira","scene","gem"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:2:1-19":{"book":"paripadal","num":6,"t":["தொல் முறை இயற்கையின் மதிய",".. ..... ... மரபிற்று ஆக,","பசும் பொன்னுலகமும் மண்ணும் பாழ்பட,","விசும்பில் ஊழி ஊழ்ஊழ் செல்லக்,","கரு வளர் வானத்து இசையின் தோன்றி,","உரு அறிவாரா ஒன்றன் ஊழியும்;","உந்து வளி கிளர்ந்த ஊழ்ஊழ் ஊழியும்;","செந் தீச்சுடரிய ஊழியும்; பனியொடு","தண் பெயல் தலைஇய ஊழியும்; அவையிற்று","உள் முறை வெள்ளம் மூழ்கி ஆர்தருபு,","மீண்டும் பீடு உயர்பு ஈண்டி, அவற்றிற்கும்","உள்ளீடு ஆகிய இரு நிலத்து ஊழியும்;","நெய்தலும், குவளையும், ஆம்பலும், சங்கமும்,","மை இல் கமலமும், வெள்ளமும், நுதலிய","செய் குறி ஈட்டம் கழிப்பிய வழிமுறை","கேழல் திகழ்வரக் கோலமொடு பெயரிய","ஊழி ஒருவினை உணர்த்தலின், முதுமைக்கு","ஊழி யாவரும் உணரா;","ஆழி முதல்வ! நிற் பேணுதும், தொழுதும்"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the ages of the world in order: when the golden world and the earth went to waste and age upon age passed in the sky, came the age of the formless one that appeared as sound in the sky where the embryo grew, the age of the driving wind, the age of blazing red fire, the age of dew and cold rain, and within them, after flooding and filling and gathering again, the age of the great earth; past the counts named by numbers, the god made known one age, named for the boar's form, so that no one knows ages enough for his age, and the poet worships him, lord of the discus.","n":"The ages follow the order of the elements: sky (as sound), wind, fire, water, earth, each born of the one before, a cosmogony told as history. நெய்தல், குவளை, ஆம்பல், சங்கம், கமலம், வெள்ளம் in lines 13 and 14 are not flowers here but the names of vast numbers, the counts by which ages are reckoned; they are therefore left out of karu. Line 2 is broken in the text, so the opening clause is incomplete.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oozhi","iraivan","anaadhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 2, lines 1-19","lb":"PR2:1-19","poet":"கீரந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["வளி","வளி",7,"pira","scene","wind"],["பனியொடு","பனி",8,"pira","scene","dew"],["பெயல்","பெயல்",9,"pira","scene","rain"],["கேழல்","கேழல்",16,"maa","scene","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:2:20-35":{"book":"paripadal","num":7,"t":["நீயே, ‘வளையடி புரையும் வாலியோற்கு அவன்","இளையன்‘ என்போர்க்கு இளையை ஆதாம்,","‘புதை இருள் உடுக்கைப் பொலம் பனைக்கொடியோற்கு","முதியை என்போர்க்கு முதுமை தோன்றலும்,","வடு இல் கொள்கையின் உயர்ந்தோர் ஆய்ந்த","கெடு இல் கேள்வியுள் நடு ஆகுதலும்,","இந் நிலைத் தெரி பொருள் தேரின், இந் நிலை","நின் நிலைத் தோன்றும் நின் தொல் நிலைச்சிறப்பே.","ஓங்கு உயர் வானின் வாங்கு வில் புரையும்","பூண் அணி கவைஇய ஆர் அணி நித்தில","நித்தில மதாணி அத்தகு மதி மறுச்","செய்யோள் சேர்ந்த நின் மாசு இல் அகலம்-","வளர் திரை மண்ணிய கிளர் பொறி நாப்பண்","வை வால் மருப்பின் களிறு மணன் அயர்பு","‘புள்ளி நிலனும் புரைபடல் அரிது‘ என","உள்ளுநர் உரைப்போர் உரையொடு சிறந்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"To those who say he is younger than the pure white one like a conch, the god is younger, and to those who call him elder to the lord of the palmyra banner with the dark garment, he appears elder; he is the centre of the flawless scripture studied by men of spotless rule, and whoever inquires into this finds his ancient greatness in his present state; his stainless chest, where the red goddess dwells like the spot on the moon amid ornaments curved like the rainbow and pearl garlands, is praised, and so is the deed of the white-tusked one who wedded the earth amid the waves, so that no word of those who think on it can mark it.","n":"Both halves of lines 20 to 23 name the same brother, Balarama (white like conch; the palmyra banner), so the poet does not decide between the traditions that make Thirumal his younger or his elder: the god takes the rank the worshipper gives him. In line 30 the chest is the moon and the goddess on it its dark mark, a figure that turns the Srivatsa mark into a moon spot. Lines 32 to 35, on the boar and the earth, are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 2, lines 20-35","lb":"PR2:20-35","poet":"கீரந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["வளையடி","வளை",20,"pira","simile","conch"],["பனைக்கொடியோற்கு","பனை",22,"maram","ornament","palmyra (banner)"],["வில்","வில்",28,"pira","simile","rainbow"],["நித்தில","நித்திலம்",29,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["மதி","மதி",30,"pira","scene","moon"],["திரை","திரை",32,"pira","scene","wave"],["களிறு","களிறு",33,"maa","scene","boar (the tusked one)"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:2:36-51":{"book":"paripadal","num":8,"t":["ஒடியா உள்ளமொடு உருத்து, ஒருங்கு உடன் இயைந்து,","இடி எதிர் கழறும்-கால் உறழ்பு எழுந்தவர்","கொடி அறுபு இறுபு, செவி செவிடு படுபு,","முடிகள் அதிர, படிநிலை தளர,","நனி முரல் வளை முடி அழிபு, இழிபு,","தலை இறுபு தாரொடு புரள-","நிலை தொலைபு, வேர், தூர், மடல்,","குருகு, பறியா நீள் இரும் பனைமிசைப்","பல பதினாயிரம் குலை தரை உதிர்வபோல்-","நில்லாது, ஒருமுறை கொய்பு கூடி,","ஒருங்கு உருண்டு, பிளந்து, நெரிந்து, உருள்பு சிதறுபு,","அளறு சொரிபு, நிலம் சோர,","சேரார் இன் உயிர் செகுக்கும்-","போர் அடு குரிசில்! நீ ஏந்திய படையே:","ஒன்னார் உடங்கு உண்ணும் கூற்றம் உடலே;","பொன் ஏர்பு அவிர் அழல் நுடக்கு அதன் நிறனே."],"p":"","r":"Those who rose together in fury, unbending, roaring against the thunder and swift as the wind, had their banners broken, their ears deafened, their crowns shaken and their footing lost, their loud conches and crowns destroyed, and their heads, garlands and all, rolled down, falling at once like countless clusters from tall dark palmyras torn up root and trunk, rolling, split, crushed and scattered, pouring mire till the earth sank: such is the weapon the god bears, which takes the lives of foes; his body is the Death that devours his enemies, and its colour is a flickering flame shining like gold.","n":"The long simile of the uprooted palmyras is built in the same breathless chain of verbal participles (ஒருங்கு உருண்டு, பிளந்து, நெரிந்து, உருள்பு சிதறுபு) as the fall of the enemies, so sound and sense fall together. The god's body is not likened to Death but called it (கூற்றம் உடலே), the strongest form of puram praise turned on a god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","kootru","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 2, lines 36-51","lb":"PR2:36-51","poet":"கீரந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["இடி","இடி",37,"pira","scene","thunder"],["கால்","கால்",37,"pira","simile","wind"],["வளை","வளை",40,"pira","scene","conch"],["குருகு","குருகு",43,"maram","simile","tender shoot"],["பனைமிசைப்","பனை",43,"maram","simile","palmyra"],["குலை","குலை",44,"maram","simile","fruit cluster"],["அளறு","அளறு",47,"pira","scene","mire"],["கூற்றம்","கூற்றம்",50,"deyvam","scene","Death"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:2:52-60":{"book":"paripadal","num":9,"t":["நின்னது திகழ் ஒளி சிறப்பு இருள் திருமணி;","கண்ணே, புகழ்சால் தாமரை அலர் இணைப் பிணையல்;","வாய்மை, வயங்கிய வைகல்; சிறந்த","நோன்மை நாடின், இரு நிலம்; யாவர்க்கும்,","சாயல் நினது, வான் நிறை-என்னும்","நா வல் அந்தணர் அரு மறைப் பொருளே:","அவ்வும் பிறவும் ஒத்தனை; உவ்வும்","எவ் வயினோயும் நீயே.","செவ்வாய் உவணத்து உயர் கொடியோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet gives what the eloquent brahmins' rare scripture says of the god: his shining light is the dark sapphire, his eyes a pair of blooming lotuses, his truth the bright day, his patience the great earth, his grace the full sky; he is like that and the others, and he is also this and the one in every place, lord of the high banner of the red-beaked eagle.","n":"Lines 58 and 59 work with the Tamil pointing letters: அவ்வும் (that, the far), உவ்வும் (this, the near) and எவ்வயினோயும் (whichever place): the god is whatever any demonstrative can point to. Line 57 repeats Paripadal 1 line 13 word for word, a fixed refrain of the Thirumal songs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","iraivan","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 2, lines 52-60","lb":"PR2:52-60","poet":"கீரந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["திருமணி","மணி",52,"pira","scene","sapphire"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",53,"maram","scene","lotus"],["நிலம்","நிலம்",55,"pira","scene","earth"],["உவணத்து","உவணம்",60,"pul","scene","eagle (Garuda)"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:2:61-76":{"book":"paripadal","num":10,"t":["கேள்வியுள் கிளந்த ஆசான் உரையும்,","படி நிலை வேள்வியுள் பற்றி ஆடு கொளலும்,","புகழ் இயைந்து இசை மறை உறு கனல் முறை மூட்டித்","திகழ் ஒளி ஒண் சுடர் வளப்பாடு கொளலும்,","நின் உருபுடன் உண்டி;","பிறர் உடம்படுவாரா","நின்னொடு புரைய","அந்தணர் காணும் வரவு.","வாயடை அமிர்தம் நின் மனத்தகத்து அடைத்தர,","மூவா மரபும் ஓவா நோன்மையும்","சாவா மரபின் அமரர்க்காச் சென்ற நின்",".. ... ... மரபினோய் நின் அடி","தலை உற வணங்கினேம், பல் மாண் யாமும்;","கலி இல் நெஞ்சினேம் ஏத்தினேம் வாழ்த்தினேம்,","கடும்பொடும் கடும்பொடும் பரவுதும்-","‘கொடும்பாடு அறியற்க, எம் அறிவு!‘ எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The teacher's words told in the scripture, the seizing of the goat in the sacrifice of the proper order, and the kindling of the fire by the chanted rite to take the abundance of its bright flame are food that comes with the god's own form, the coming that the brahmins see as equal to him, which others will not accept; to him who sealed the nectar in and went for the immortals, giving them unaging tradition and unceasing strength, the poet and his many kin bow their heads at his feet, praise and bless him with quiet hearts, and pray: may our understanding never know crookedness.","n":"The animal of the sacrifice (ஆடு) is counted among the god's food without hesitation; the hymn sides with the brahmins' rite against those who do not accept it (பிறர் உடம்படுவாரா), a hint that the rite already had dissenters. The closing prayer asks not for wealth or release but for a mind without crookedness (கொடும்பாடு). Line 72 is broken, so the deed of lines 69 to 72 cannot be fully construed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["aaguthi","vedham","andhanar","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 2, lines 61-76","lb":"PR2:61-76","poet":"கீரந்தையார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["ஆடு","ஆடு",62,"maa","gift_food","goat"],["கனல்","கனல்",63,"pira","scene","fire"],["அமிர்தம்","அமிர்தம்",69,"unavu","gift_food","nectar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:1"},"pr:3:1-14":{"book":"paripadal","num":11,"t":["மா அயோயே! மாஅயோயே!","மறு பிறப்பு அறுக்கும் மாசு இல் சேவடி","மணி திகழ் உருபின் மா அயோயே!","தீ வளி விசும்பு நிலன் நீர் ஐந்தும்,","ஞாயிறும், திங்களும், அறனும், ஐவரும்,","திதியின் சிறாரும், விதியின் மக்களும்,","மாசு இல் எண்மரும், பதினொரு கபிலரும்,","தா மா இருவரும், தருமனும், மடங்கலும்,","மூ-ஏழ் உலகமும், உலகினுள் மன்பதும்,","மாயோய்! நின்வயின் பரந்தவை உரைத்தேம்","மாயா வாய்மொழி உரைதர வலந்து:","‘வாய்மொழி ஓடை மலர்ந்த","தாமரைப் பூவினுள் பிறந்தோனும், தாதையும்,","நீ‘ என பொழியுமால், அந்தணர் அரு மறை."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the dark one whose flawless red feet cut off rebirth and says that the five elements, the sun and moon, aram, the sons of Diti and of Vidhi, the eight, the eleven, the two, Dharma, the lion and the three-times-seven worlds with all that lives in them have been told as things spread out from him, as the unfailing true word declares; the rare Veda of the brahmins pours forth that he is both the one born in the lotus and that one's father.","n":"The catalogue of lines 4 to 9 lists cosmic classes by number (the eight, the eleven, the two) without naming them, as a hearer who knew the list would supply them; the poet does not claim to see this himself but reports it as what the Veda says (lines 11 and 14), so the hymn's authority is borrowed from the brahmins' scripture. Line 5 differs strongly from sangamtamil.com's text.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","viyaabagam","vedham","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 3, lines 1-14","lb":"PR3:1-14","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மணி","மணி",3,"pira","scene","sapphire"],["ஓடை","ஓடை",12,"pira","scene","pool"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",13,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:2"},"pr:3:15-30":{"book":"paripadal","num":12,"t":["‘ஏஎர், வயங்கு பூண் அமரரை வௌவிய அமிழ்தின்,","பயந்தோள் இடுக்கண் களைந்த புள்ளினை;","பயந்தோள் இடுக்கண் களைந்த புள்ளின்","நிவந்து ஓங்கு உயர் கொடிச் சேவலோய்! நின்","சேவடி தொழாரும் உளரோ? அவற்றுள்","கீழ் ஏழ் உலகமும் உற்ற அடியினை;","தீ செங் கனலியும், கூற்றமும், ஞமனும்,","மாசு இல் ஆயிரம் கதிர் ஞாயிறும், தொகூஉம்","ஊழி ஆழிக்கண், இரு நிலம், உரு கெழு","கேழலாய் மருப்பின் உழுதோய்‘ எனவும்,","‘மா விசும்பு ஒழுகு புனல் வறள அன்னச்","சேவலாய்ச் சிறகர்ப் புலர்த்தியோய்‘ எனவும்,","ஞாலத்து உறையுள் தேவரும் வானத்து","நால் எண் தேவரும் நயந்து நிற் பாடுவோர்","பாடும் வகையே: எம் பாடல்தாம் அப்","பாடுவார் பாடும் வகை"],"p":"","r":"The poet gives the praises the gods sing: you are the bird that seized the nectar from the jewelled immortals and ended its mother's distress, lord of the tall banner of that bird; who does not worship your red feet, the foot that reached the seven lower worlds; when fire, death, Yama and the thousand-rayed sun gathered in the sea at the age's end you ploughed up the great earth with a boar's tusk, and when the waters of the sky dried you dried your wings as a swan; our song follows the way those singers sing.","n":"Lines 16 and 17 repeat the same half-line (பயந்தோள் இடுக்கண் களைந்த புள்ளின்), a chaining device that turns the bird first into the god himself and then into his banner. The boar and swan deeds are put in quotation as the gods' own hymn (எனவும் ... எனவும், lines 24, 26), and the poet then claims only to sing in their manner, so the human song is modelled on a heavenly one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 3, lines 15-30","lb":"PR3:15-30","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["அமிழ்தின்","அமிழ்து",15,"unavu","scene","nectar"],["புள்ளினை","புள்",16,"pul","scene","the bird Garuda"],["சேவலோய்","சேவல்",18,"pul","scene","male bird of the banner"],["கேழலாய்","கேழல்",24,"maa","scene","boar"],["புனல்","புனல்",25,"pira","scene","waters"],["அன்னச்","அன்னம்",25,"pul","scene","swan"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:2"},"pr:3:31-50":{"book":"paripadal","num":13,"t":["வடிவு வேற்றுமையும் பெயர் வேற்றுமையும்","எரி சினம் கொன்றோய்! நின் புகழ் உருவின, கை;","நகை அச்சாக நல் அமிர்து கலந்த","நடுவுநிலை திறம்பிய நயம் இல் ஒரூ கை,","இரூ கை மாஅல் !","முக் கை முனிவ! நாற் கை அண்ணல்!","ஐங் கைம் மைந்த! அறு கை நெடு வேள்!","எழு கையாள! எண் கை ஏந்தல்!","ஒன்பதிற்றுத் தடக் கை மன் பேராள!","பதிற்றுக் கை மதவலி! நூற்றுக் கை ஆற்றல்!","ஆயிரம் விரித்த கைம் மாய மள்ள!","பதினாயிரம் கை முதுமொழி முதல்வ!","நூறாயிரம் கை ஆறு அறி கடவுள்!","அனைத்தும் அல்ல பல அடுக்கல் ஆம்பல்","இனைத்து என எண் வரம்பு அறியா யாக்கையை!","நின்னைப் புரை நினைப்பின் நீ அலது உணர்தியோ,","முன்னை மரபின் முதுமொழி முதல்வ!","நினக்கு-விரிந்து அகன்ற கேள்வி அனைத்தினும்,","வலியினும், மனத்தினும், உணர்வினும், எல்லாம்-","வனப்பு வரம்பு அறியா மரபினோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Your forms and names differ, slayer in burning anger, and your hands take the shape of your fame: the one hand without fairness that mixed the nectar and swerved from even-handedness, then two hands, three, four and on through ten, a hundred, a thousand, ten thousand and a hundred thousand, and more than these, a body whose count no number bounds; if one thinks of something like you, who but you could know it, first of the ancient word, whose beauty in learning, strength, mind and perception knows no limit?","n":"The first hand in the count is a reproach, not a praise: the hand that dealt out the nectar unfairly (lines 33 and 34) remembers the god's partiality to the gods against the asuras, which the next unit seems to answer by making him first of both. ஆம்பல் in line 44 is most likely the name of a very large number, not the water lily. The object of எரி சினம் கொன்றோய் (line 32) is missing; see source_issues.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","viyaabagam","arivu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 3, lines 31-50","lb":"PR3:31-50","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["அமிர்து","அமிர்து",33,"unavu","gift_food","nectar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:2"},"pr:3:51-62":{"book":"paripadal","num":14,"t":["அணி நிழல் வயங்கு ஒளி, ஈர்-எண் தீம் கதிர்,","பிறை வளர், நிறை மதி உண்டி,","அணி மணிப் பைம் பூண், அமரர்க்கு முதல்வன் நீ;","திணி நிலம் கடந்தக்கால், திரிந்து அயர்ந்து, அகன்று ஓடி,","நின் அஞ்சிக் கடற் பாய்ந்த பிணி நெகிழ்பு அவிழ் தண் தார்","அன்னவர் பட, அல்லா அவுணர்க்கும் முதல்வன் நீ;","அதனால், ‘பகைவர் இவர்; இவர் நட்டோர்‘ என்னும்","வகையும் உண்டோ, நின் மரபு அறிவோர்க்கே?","ஆயிர அணர் தலை அரவு வாய்க் கொண்ட","சேவல் ஊர்தியும், ‘செங் கண் மாஅல்!","ஓ!‘ எனக் கிளக்கும் கால முதல்வனை;","ஏஎ இன கிளத்தலின் இனைமை நற்கு அறிந்தனம்;"],"p":"","r":"You are first of the immortals, who wear gem-set ornaments and feed on the moon of sixteen sweet rays as it waxes from crescent to full, and you are first also of the asuras who, when you strode across the earth, turned, fainted, fled and leapt into the sea in fear of you, their cool garlands loosening; so for those who know your nature, is there any sorting of these as enemies and those as friends? Rider of the bird that took the thousand-headed serpent in its beak, first of time whom they call red-eyed Maal, we know your nature from the way they cry out to you.","n":"The claim of line 58 is placed on the knower, not only on the god: it is those who know his nature (நின் மரபு அறிவோர்க்கே) who can no longer divide the world into his enemies and friends, though the same lines tell how the asuras fled from him in terror. Line 62 is hard to construe. Line 54 differs strongly from sangamtamil.com's text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","samanilai","pagai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 3, lines 51-62","lb":"PR3:51-62","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மதி","மதி",52,"pira","gift_food","moon"],["கடற்","கடல்",55,"pira","scene","sea"],["தார்","தார்",55,"maram","ornament","garland"],["அரவு","அரவு",59,"pira","scene","serpent"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",60,"pul","scene","the bird Garuda"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:2"},"pr:3:63-80":{"book":"paripadal","num":15,"t":["தீயினுள் தெறல் நீ; பூவினுள் நாற்றம் நீ;","கல்லினுள் மணியும் நீ; சொல்லினுள் வாய்மை நீ;","அறத்தினுள் அன்பு நீ; மறத்தினுள் மைந்து நீ;","வேதத்து மறை நீ; பூதத்து முதலும் நீ;","வெஞ் சுடர் ஒளியும் நீ; திங்களுள் அளியும் நீ;","அனைத்தும் நீ; அனைத்தின் உட்பொருளும் நீ; ஆதலின்,","உறையும் உறைவதும் இலையே; உண்மையும்","மறவியில் சிறப்பின் மாயமார் அனையை;","முதல்முறை, இடைமுறை, கடைமுறை, தொழிலில்","பிறவாப் பிறப்பு இலை; பிறப்பித்தோர் இலையே;","பறவாப் பூவைப் பூவினோயே!","அருள் குடையாக, அறம் கோலாக,","இரு நிழல் படாமை மூ-ஏழ் உலகமும்","ஒரு நிழல் ஆக்கிய ஏமத்தை மாதோ;","பாழ் என, கால் என, பாகு என, ஒன்று என,","இரண்டு என, மூன்று என, நான்கு என, ஐந்து என,","ஆறு என, ஏழு என, எட்டு என, தொண்டு என,","நால்வகை ஊழி எண் நவிற்றும் சிறப்பினை;"],"p":"","r":"You are the burning in fire and the fragrance in the flower, the gem among stones and the truth in speech, the love in aram and the strength in valour, the secret of the Veda and the first of the elements, the light of the hot sun and the coolness of the moon; you are all things and the inner substance of all, so you have neither a dwelling nor a place to dwell; in the work of the first, middle and last orders there is no birth you have not taken and no one who gave you birth; with grace as your umbrella and aram as your sceptre you made the three-times-seven worlds a single shade with no second shade, and you are the one the count of the ages, void, quarter, half, one, two and on to nine, proclaims.","n":"The 'X in Y' series (lines 63 to 68) names in each pair the quality that makes a thing what it is, so the god is the essence, not the bulk, of each; this is why the poet can conclude that he has no dwelling place. Lines 71 and 72 hold both that he is born in every order and that no one bore him. இரு நிழல் படாமை (line 75) borrows the king's boast of a single umbrella with no rival over the land. The numbers of line 77 (பாழ், கால், பாகு) may be zero, a quarter and a half, or void and categories of a cosmology; the reading is not fixed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","iraivan","anaadhi","arul"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 3, lines 63-80","lb":"PR3:63-80","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பூவினுள்","பூ",63,"maram","scene","flower"],["மணியும்","மணி",64,"pira","scene","gem"],["பூவைப்","பூவை",73,"maram","scene","kaayaa flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:2"},"pr:3:81-94":{"book":"paripadal","num":16,"t":["செங் கட் காரி! கருங் கண் வெள்ளை!","பொன் கட் பச்சை! பைங் கண் மாஅல்!","இடவல! குட அல! கோவல! காவல!","காணா மரப! நீயா நினைவ!","மாயா மன்ன! உலகு ஆள் மன்னவ!","தொல் இயல் புலவ! நல் யாழ்ப் பாண!","மாலைச் செல்வ! தோலாக் கோட்ட!","பொலம் புரி ஆடை! வலம்புரி வண்ண","பருதி வலவ! பொரு திறல் மல்ல!","திருவின் கணவ! பொரு விறல் மள்ள!","மா நிலம் இயலா முதல்முறை அமையத்து,","நாம வெள்ளத்து நடுவண் தோன்றிய","வாய்மொழி மகனொடு மலர்ந்த","தாமரைப் பொகுட்டு நின் நேமி நிழலே!"],"p":"","r":"The poem closes in a litany of names: red-eyed dark one, black-eyed white one, gold-eyed green one, green-eyed Maal, cowherd and guardian, unseen ancient, unforgetting mind, unfailing king who rules the world, poet of the old way, bard of the fine lute, lord of the garland, of the undefeated horn, of the gold-twisted garment and the colour of the right-whorled conch, wielder of the wheel, wrestler of fighting strength, husband of Tiru; and it ends on the lotus cup that, in the first time before the great earth took shape, bloomed in the midst of the fearful flood together with the son of the true word, the shade of your wheel.","n":"The names of lines 81 and 82 pair each eye with a body of another colour (red-eyed dark, black-eyed white, gold-eyed green, green-eyed Maal), giving the god several coloured forms at once, which echoes the difference of forms named in line 31. The last line ties the creation lotus that bore Brahma (line 93, cf. line 13) to the god's own wheel, but the syntax of line 94 is loose, and இடவல and குடஅல (line 83) are not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thirunaamam","iraivan","anaadhi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 3, lines 81-94","lb":"PR3:81-94","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",86,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["மாலைச்","மாலை",87,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",88,"pira","scene","right-whorled conch"],["வெள்ளத்து","வெள்ளம்",92,"pira","scene","flood"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",94,"maram","scene","lotus"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:2"},"pr:4:1-10":{"book":"paripadal","num":17,"t":["ஐந்து இருள் அற நீக்கி, நான்கினுள் துடைத்துத் தம்","ஒன்று ஆற்றுப்படுத்த நின் ஆர்வலர் தொழுது ஏத்தி,","நின் புகழ் விரித்தனர்; கிளக்குங்கால், அவை நினக்கு","இறும்பூது அன்மை நற்கு அறிந்தேம், ஆயினும்,","நகுதலும் தகுதி, ஈங்கு ஊங்கு நிற் கிளப்ப;","திருமணி, திரைபாடு அவிந்த முந்நீர்,","வரு மழை இருஞ் சூல்-மூன்றும் புரையும் மா மெய்;","மாஅ மெய்யொடு முரணிய உடுக்கையை;","நோனார் உயிரொடு முரணிய நேமியை;","செயிர் தீர் செங் கட் செல்வ! நிற் புகழ"],"p":"","r":"Your devotees, having driven out the five darknesses and wiped clean within the four so that their one thing is guided to you, bow and spread your fame; we know well that our telling is no wonder to you, and that you may fairly laugh at our naming you here and there, yet we speak: your great body is like the sacred gem, the sea whose waves have stilled and the dark womb of the coming rain cloud, your garment is at odds with that dark body, your discus at odds with the lives of those who will not bear you, red-eyed lord without fault.","n":"The counted words of lines 1 and 2 (ஐந்து இருள், நான்கினுள், தம் ஒன்று) are left unexplained by the poem; they read as a discipline of the worshipper (five things cleared away, four cleansed, one left to be guided to the god), not as part of the god's portrait. The double use of முரணிய (lines 8 and 9) sets the gold garment against the dark body and the discus against the god's foes in one figure of contrast.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","iraivan","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 4, lines 1-10","lb":"PR4:1-10","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["திருமணி","மணி",6,"pira","simile","gem"],["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",6,"pira","simile","sea"],["மழை","மழை",7,"pira","simile","rain cloud"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:3"},"pr:4:11-24":{"book":"paripadal","num":18,"t":["புகைந்த நெஞ்சின், புலர்ந்த சாந்தின்","பிருங்கலாதன் பலபல பிணி பட","வலந்துழி, மலர்ந்த நோய் கூர் கூம்பிய நடுக்கத்து","அலர்ந்த புகழோன், தாதை ஆகலின்","இகழ்வோன், இகழா நெஞ்சினனாக, நீ இகழா","நன்றா நட்ட அவன் நன் மார்பு முயங்கி,","ஒன்றா நட்டவன் உறு வரை மார்பின்-","படிமதம் சாம்ப ஒதுங்கி,","இன்னல் இன்னரொடு இடி முரசு இயம்ப,","வெடி படா ஒடி தூண் தடியொடு,","தடி தடி பல பட-வகிர் வாய்த்த உகிரினை;","புருவத்துக் கரு வல் கந்தத்தால்","தாங்கி, இவ் உலகம் தந்து அடிப்படுத்ததை நடுவண்","ஓங்கிய பலர் புகழ் குன்றினோடு ஒக்கும்;"],"p":"","r":"To praise you, the poet tells how Prahlada, heart smouldering and sandal paste dried, was bound and afflicted with many torments by his father, who scorned you while the son could not scorn you; you embraced the good chest of the one who loved you well and, with the drum roaring like thunder, burst from the broken pillar and with your claws tore again and again the mountain-like chest of the one who would not join you; and your bearing this world and bringing it under your feet is like the much-praised mountain that rises in the middle.","n":"The poem sets the god's two acts on two chests side by side: நன்றா நட்ட அவன் நன் மார்பு முயங்கி (line 16) and ஒன்றா நட்டவன் உறு வரை மார்பின் (line 17); the same hands that embrace the son tear the father. The son is named பிருங்கலாதன் (line 12), a Tamil rendering of Prahlada, and the pillar appears only as ஒடி தூண் (line 20). Lines 22 to 24, on bearing the world, are hard to construe and may refer to another deed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","adaikkalam","paththi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 4, lines 11-24","lb":"PR4:11-24","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["vekuli","கொலை"],["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",11,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["முரசு","முரசு",19,"parai","scene","war drum"],["குன்றினோடு","குன்று",24,"pira","simile","mountain"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:3"},"pr:4:25-36":{"book":"paripadal","num":19,"t":["நின் வெம்மையும் விளக்கமும் ஞாயிற்று உள;","நின் தண்மையும் சாயலும் திங்கள் உள;","நின் சுரத்தலும் வண்மையும் மாரி உள;","நின் புரத்தலும் நோன்மையும் ஞாலத்து உள;","நின் நாற்றமும் ஒண்மையும் பூவை உள;","நின் தோற்றமும் அகலமும் நீரின் உள;","நின் உருவமும் ஒலியும் ஆகாயத்து உள;","நின் வருதலும் ஒடுக்கமும் மருத்தின் உள;","அதனால், இவ்வும், உவ்வும், அவ்வும், பிறவும்,","ஏமம் ஆர்த்த நிற் பிரிந்து,","மேவல் சான்றன, எல்லாம்","சேவல் ஓங்கு உயர் கொடியோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Your heat and brightness are in the sun, your coolness and gentleness in the moon, your pouring and bounty in the rain, your sheltering and endurance in the earth, your fragrance and lustre in the flower, your appearance and breadth in water, your form and sound in the sky, your coming and withdrawing in the wind; so these things here, there and yonder, and all the rest, parted from you who are bound with protection, have come to their fitting places, lord of the high banner of the cock-bird.","n":"The run of eight lines does not say the god is the sun or the moon; it says two of his qualities are found in each (நின் வெம்மையும் விளக்கமும் ஞாயிற்று உள), and line 34 says the things came about by parting from him (நிற் பிரிந்து). The world holds his qualities in pieces, each element two of them. The pairs are chosen to fit their element (sound to the sky, coming and withdrawing to the wind), which matches the order of qualities to elements later counted in the tattva lists.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","iraivan","aakaasam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 4, lines 25-36","lb":"PR4:25-36","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மாரி","மாரி",27,"pira","scene","rain"],["பூவை","பூ",29,"maram","scene","flower"],["நீரின்","நீர்",30,"pira","scene","water"],["சேவல்","சேவல்",36,"pul","ornament","male bird of the banner"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:3"},"pr:4:37-48":{"book":"paripadal","num":20,"t":["சேவல் ஓங்கு உயர் கொடி","நின் ஒன்று உயர் கொடி பனை;","நின் ஒன்று உயர் கொடி நாஞ்சில்;","நின் ஒன்று உயர் கொடி யானை;","நின் ஒன்றா உயர் கொடி ஒன்று இன்று;","விடமுடை அரவின் உடல் உயிர் உருங்கு உவணம்;","அவன் மடிமேல் வலந்தது பாம்பு;","பாம்பு தொடி; பாம்பு முடி மேலன;","பாம்பு பூண்; பாம்பு தலைமேலது;","பாம்பு சிறை தலையன;","பாம்பு படி மதம் சாய்த்தோய்! பசும் பூணவை","கொடிமேல் இருந்தவன் தாக்கு இரையது பாம்பு;"],"p":"","r":"Your high banner is the cock-bird; one high banner of yours is the palm, one the plough, one the elephant, and there is no high banner that is not yours; the eagle that crushes the body and life of the venomous snake wears snakes: a snake coiled on its lap, snakes for bangles, on its crown, as its ornament, on its head and on its wings; you who broke the pride of the snake, the one who sits on your banner with green ornaments has the snake for the prey he strikes.","n":"The palm and the plough are the white elder god's marks (Paripadal 1 gives him the plough; Purananuru 56 gives him the palm banner), so the hymn takes the brother's banners as the dark god's own and then says no banner is not his (line 41). The snake passage turns on a paradox: the bird that devours snakes is adorned all over with them, the enemy worn as jewellery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","pugazh","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 4, lines 37-48","lb":"PR4:37-48","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பனை","பனை",38,"maram","ornament","palmyra (banner)"],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",39,"seythi","ornament","plough (banner)"],["யானை","யானை",40,"maa","ornament","elephant (banner)"],["அரவின்","அரவு",42,"pira","gift_food","snake"],["உவணம்","உவணம்",42,"pul","scene","eagle (Garuda)"],["பாம்பு","பாம்பு",44,"pira","ornament","snake"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:3"},"pr:4:49-56":{"book":"paripadal","num":21,"t":["கடு நவை அணங்கும் கடுப்பும், நல்கலும்,","கொடுமையும் செம்மையும், வெம்மையும் தண்மையும்","உள்வழி உடையை; இல்வழி இலையே;","போற்றார் உயிரினும், போற்றுநர் உயிரினும்,","மாற்று ஏமாற்றல் இலையே; ‘நினக்கு","மாற்றோரும் இலர்; கேளிரும் இலர்‘ எனும்","வேற்றுமை இன்று, அது போற்றுநர்ப் பெறினே:","மனக்கோள் நினக்கு என வடிவு வேறு இலையே;"],"p":"","r":"Harm that afflicts and fierceness, and favour, cruelty and rightness, heat and coolness: where these are, you have them, and where they are not, you have none; you make no exchange between the lives of those who do not praise you and those who do; the saying that you have neither foes nor kin admits no exception, even when you gain those who praise you; you have no form other than the one the mind takes you to be.","n":"Line 51 (உள்வழி உடையை; இல்வழி இலையே) makes the god's harshness and grace depend on where they are found, not on his preference, and line 56 makes his form the one the worshipper's mind holds (மனக்கோள் நினக்கு என வடிவு). The construal of lines 53 to 55 (மாற்று ஏமாற்றல்; அது போற்றுநர்ப் பெறினே) is doubtful: they may say instead that the god's impartiality yields to those who praise him.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","samanilai","viyaabagam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 4, lines 49-56","lb":"PR4:49-56","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:3"},"pr:4:57-73":{"book":"paripadal","num":22,"t":["கோள் இருள் இருக்கை ஆய் மணி மேனி,","நக்கு அலர் துழாஅய் நாறு இணர்க் கண்ணியை;","பொன்னின் தோன்றிய புனை மறு மார்ப!","நின்னில் தோன்றிய நிரை இதழ்த் தாமரை","அன்ன நாட்டத்து அளப்பரியவை;","நின்னின் சிறந்த நின் தாள் இணையவை;","நின்னில் சிறந்த நிறை கடவுளவை;","அன்னோர் அல்லா வேறும் உள; அவை","நின் ஓர் அன்ஓர் அந்தணர் அருமறை","அழல் புரை குழை கொழு நிழல் தரும் பல சினை","ஆலமும், கடம்பும் நல் யாற்று நடுவும்,","கால் வழக்கு அறு நிலைக் குன்றமும், பிறவும்,","அவ்வவை மேவிய வேறு வேறு பெயரோய்!","எவ் வயினோயும் நீயே; நின் ஆர்வலர்","தொழுத கை அமைதியின் அமர்ந்தோயும் நீயே;","அவரவர் ஏவலாளனும் நீயே;","அவரவர் செய்பொருட்கு அரணமும் நீயே"],"p":"","r":"Your body is a choice gem that holds the darkness, you wear a fragrant cluster-garland of opened tulasi, your chest bears the adorned mark that rose from gold; the lotus of ranked petals born from you, the measureless things of lotus-like eyes, your pair of feet that surpass you, the full gods that surpass you, and others besides: in the rare Veda of the brahmins, in the banyan of many branches with leaves like flame and rich shade, in the kadamba, in the middle of the good river, in the hill that stands where no foot goes and elsewhere, you dwell under a different name in each; you are the one in every place, the one who sits at peace in the folded hands of your devotees, the servant of each of them and the refuge of what each does.","n":"Lines 62 and 63 say the god's feet and the full gods surpass the god himself (நின்னின் சிறந்த நின் தாள் இணையவை), so what is worshipped may be ranked above the one it belongs to. The closing lines go further: the god is the servant (ஏவலாளன்) of each worshipper. The sacred places of line 67 and 68 are the banyan, the kadamba, the river islet and the hill, each with its own name for the god.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["viyaabagam","vazhipaadu","adaikkalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 4, lines 57-73","lb":"PR4:57-73","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மணி","மணி",57,"pira","simile","gem"],["துழாஅய்","துழாய்",58,"maram","ornament","tulasi"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",60,"maram","simile","lotus"],["ஆலமும்","ஆலம்",67,"maram","scene","banyan"],["கடம்பும்","கடம்பு",67,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["யாற்று","யாறு",67,"pira","scene","river"],["குன்றமும்","குன்றம்",68,"pira","scene","hill"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:3"},"pr:5:1-21":{"book":"paripadal","num":23,"t":["பாய் இரும் பனிக் கடல் பார் துகள் படப் புக்கு,","சேய் உயர் பிணிமுகம் ஊர்ந்து, அமர் உழக்கி,","தீ அழல் துவைப்பத் திரிய விட்டெறிந்து,","நோயுடை நுடங்கு சூர் மா முதல் தடிந்து,","வென்றியின் மக்களுள் ஒருமையொடு பெயரிய","கொன்று உணல் அஞ்சாக் கொடு வினைக் கொல் தகை","மாய அவுணர் மருங்கு அறத் தபுத்த வேல்,","நாவல்அம் தண் பொழில் வட பொழில் ஆயிடை,","குருகொடு பெயர் பெற்ற மால் வரை உடைத்து,","மலை ஆற்றுப் படுத்த மூ-இரு கயந்தலை!","மூ-இரு கயந்தலை, முந் நான்கு முழவுத் தோள்,","ஞாயிற்று ஏர் நிறத் தகை! நளினத்துப் பிறவியை!","காஅய் கடவுட் சேஎய்! செவ்வேள்!","சால்வ! தலைவ! எனப் பேஎ விழவினுள்,","வேலன் ஏத்தும் வெறியும் உளவே;","அவை வாயும் அல்ல, பொய்யும் அல்ல,","நீயே வரம்பிற்று இவ் உலகம் ஆதலின்;","சிறப்போய் சிறப்பு இன்றிப் பெயர்குவை;","சிறப்பினுள் உயர்பு ஆகலும்,","பிறப்பினுள் இழிபு ஆகலும்,","ஏனோர் நின் வலத்தினதே;"],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the spear that went into the wide cold sea until its floor was churned to dust, that rode the tall mount, cut down Sur at the root of his mango form and wiped out the deceiving asuras, and that split the great hill named after the crane; he names the god six-headed, twelve-shouldered, sun-coloured, born in the lotus, red Vel, son of the god of the kaaya tree, and then says that the praise and the possession rite the velan performs at festivals are neither true nor false, since this world has its limit in him and the high and low among all others are in his power.","n":"Lines 16 and 17 are a small theology of the veri rite: the velan's cry is not dismissed as error (as the akam friend dismisses it) nor taken as plain truth; it is valid only because everything stands within the god's bound. Line 10 ends on மூ-இரு கயந்தலை and line 11 opens on the same words, a deliberate linking repetition. சிறப்போய் சிறப்பு இன்றிப் பெயர்குவை (line 18) can be read as 'you who are excellence go about without needing honour'; the sense is not secure. The hill split for its crane name is the one later poets call Krauncha.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","maram","veri"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 5, lines 1-21","lb":"PR5:1-21","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["கடல்","கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["பிணிமுகம்","பிணிமுகம்",2,"maa","scene","the god's mount"],["மா","மா",4,"maram","scene","mango (Sur's form)"],["நாவல்அம்","நாவல்",8,"maram","scene","jambu tree"],["குருகொடு","குருகு",9,"pul","scene","crane (in the hill's name)"],["நளினத்துப்","நளினம்",12,"maram","scene","lotus"],["காஅய்","காயா",13,"maram","scene","kaaya tree"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",15,"seythi","scene","diviner"]],"en":[["hill","குருகொடு பெயர் பெற்ற மால் வரை",9]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:4"},"pr:5:22-47":{"book":"paripadal","num":24,"t":["ஆதி அந்தணன் அறிந்து பரி கொளுவ,","வேத மா பூண் வையத் தேர் ஊர்ந்து,","நாகம் நாணா, மலை வில்லாக,","மூவகை ஆர் எயில் ஓர் அழல்-அம்பின் முளிய,","மாதிரம் அழல, எய்து அமரர் வேள்விப்","பாகம் உண்ட பைங் கட் பார்ப்பான்","உமையொடு புணர்ந்து, காம வதுவையுள்,","அமையாப் புணர்ச்சி அமைய, நெற்றி","இமையா நாட்டத்து ஓரு வரம் கொண்டு,","‘விலங்கு‘ என, விண்னோர் வேள்வி முதல்வன்","விரி கதிர் மணிப் பூணவற்குத் தான் ஈத்தது","அரிது என மாற்றான், வாய்மையன் ஆதலின்,","எரி கனன்று ஆனாக் குடாரி கொண்டு அவன் உருவு","திரித்திட்டோன், இவ் உலகு ஏழும் மருள;","கருப் பெற்றுக் கொண்டோர், கழிந்த சேய் யாக்கை","நொசிப்பின், ஏழ் உறு முனிவர், நனி உணர்ந்து,","வசித்ததைக் கண்டம் ஆக மாதவர்,","‘மனைவியர், நிறைவயின், வசி தடி சமைப்பின்,","சாலார்; தானே தரிக்க என, அவர் அவி","உடன் பெய்தோரே, அழல் வேட்டு; அவ் அவித்","தடவு நிமிர் முத் தீப் பேணிய மன் எச்சில்,","வடவயின், விளங்கு ஆல், உறை எழு மகளிருள்","கடவுள் ஒரு மீன் சாலினி ஒழிய,","அறுவர் மற்றையோரும் அந் நிலை அயின்றனர்;","மறு அறு கற்பின் மாதவர் மனைவியர்","நிறைவயின் வழா அது நிற் சூலினரே;"],"p":"","r":"The poet tells the birth: the green-eyed brahmin who ate the gods' share of the sacrifice, who with the first brahmin as charioteer, the Veda yoked to the earth for chariot, the serpent for bowstring and the mountain for bow, burned the three forts with one fiery arrow, joined Uma in a marriage whose union would not end; Indra won from the unwinking forehead eye a boon to stop it, and the truthful god, unwilling to take back his gift, split the embryo with a blazing axe; the seven sages, knowing, would not let their wives carry it and poured it as oblation into the fire, and of the seven women who live in the north six ate what remained of the three fires, all but Saalini, the one star, so that the great sages' wives of spotless chastity conceived you without failing in their chastity.","n":"The story is built to keep chastity whole: the mothers conceive through the fire's leftover, not through a man, and the one wife who does not eat (Saalini, the star by the Seven) is singled out. Lines 36 to 41 are compressed and the exact order of the sages' decision (cut, refuse, pour into fire) is a reading, not a certainty.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","sivam","aaguthi","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 5, lines 22-47","lb":"PR5:22-47","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பரி","பரி",22,"maa","scene","horse"],["நாகம்","நாகம்",24,"pira","scene","serpent"],["ஆல்","ஆல்",43,"maram","scene","banyan"],["மீன்","மீன்",44,"pira","scene","star"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:4"},"pr:5:48-70":{"book":"paripadal","num":25,"t":["நிவந்து ஓங்கு இமயத்து நீலப் பைஞ் சுனைப்","பயந்தோர் என்ப, பதுமத்துப் பாயல்;","பெரும் பெயர் முருக! நிற் பயந்த ஞான்றே,","அரிது அமர் சிறப்பின் அமரர்செல்வன்,","எரி உமிழ் வச்சிரம் கொண்டு, இகந்து வந்து, எறிந்தென,","அறு வேறு துணியும் அறுவர் ஆகி,","ஒருவனை; வாழி, ஓங்கு விறல் சேஎய்!","ஆரா உடம்பின் நீ அமர்ந்து விளையாடிய","போரால் வறுங் கைக்குப் புரந்தரன் உடைய,","அல்லல் இல் அனலன் தன் மெய்யின் பிரித்து,","செல்வ வாரணம் கொடுத்தோன்; வானத்து","வளம் கெழு செல்வன் தன் மெய்யின் பிரித்துத்","திகழ் பொறிப் பீலி அணி மயில் கொடுத்தோன்;","திருந்து கோல் ஞமன் தன் மெய்யின் பிரிவித்து,","இருங் கண் வெள் யாட்டு எழில் மறி கொடுத்தோன்;","ஆஅங்கு அவரும் பிறரும் அமர்ந்து படை அளித்த","மறியும், மஞ்ஞையும், வாரணச் சேவலும்,","பொறி வரிச் சாபமும், மரனும், வாளும்,","செறி இலை ஈட்டியும், குடாரியும், கணிச்சியும்","தெறு கதிர்க் கனலியும் மாலையும் மணியும்,","வேறு வேறு உருவின் இவ் ஆறு இரு கைக் கொண்டு,","மறு இல் துறக்கத்து அமரர் செல்வன்தன்","பொறி வரிக் கொட்டையொடு புகழ் வரம்பு இகந்தோய்"],"p":"","r":"They say the six bore you on a bed of lotus in the green pool of the towering Himalaya; on the day of your birth Indra came with his fire-spitting thunderbolt and struck, and you, cut into six, became six and were one; when in your play-war Indra was left empty-handed, Agni drew from his own body the cock, the rich lord of the sky the peacock with its spotted plumes, and Yama the ram, and with these and the weapons the others gave, bow, sword, spear, axe, pick, the sun, garland and bell, held in twelve hands, you passed beyond the fame of Indra himself.","n":"அறு வேறு துணியும் அறுவர் ஆகி, ஒருவனை (lines 53, 54): Indra's blow, meant to destroy, is what makes him six and one; the gods' gifts come out of their own bodies (தன் மெய்யின் பிரித்து, three times), so the vahanas are presented as parts of the older gods given up to the younger. Lines 65 to 67 (மரனும், கனலியும்) and 70 (கொட்டை) are uncertain in what they name.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 5, lines 48-70","lb":"PR5:48-70","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["சுனைப்","சுனை",48,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["பதுமத்துப்","பதுமம்",49,"maram","scene","lotus"],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",58,"pul","gift_food","cock"],["மயில்","மயில்",60,"pul","gift_food","peacock"],["மறி","மறி",62,"maa","gift_food","ram"],["மாலையும்","மாலை",67,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["hill","இமயத்து",48]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:4"},"pr:5:71-82":{"book":"paripadal","num":26,"t":["நின் குணம் எதிர்கொண்டோர் அறம் கொண்டோர் அல்லதை,","மன் குணம் உடையோர் மாதவர் வணங்கியோர் அல்லதை-","செறு தீ நெஞ்சத்துச் சினம் நீடினோரும்,","சேரா அறத்துச் சீர் இலோரும்,","அழி தவப் படிவத்து அயரியோரும்,","மறு பிறப்பு இல் எனும் மடவோரும், சேரார்","நின் நிழல்;","அன்னோர் அல்லது இன்னோர்","சேர்வார் ஆதலின், யாஅம் இரப்பவை","பொருளும் பொன்னும் போகமும் அல்ல; நின்பால்","அருளும், அன்பும், அறனும், மூன்றும்-","உருள் இணர்க் கடம்பின் ஒலி தாரோயே!"],"p":"","r":"Only those who take up your virtues and hold to aram, the good who bow to great ascetics, reach your shade; those whose hearts burn with lasting anger, those without aram, those whose penance has broken down and the fools who say there is no rebirth do not; so what we ask is not wealth, gold or pleasure but from you grace, love and aram, these three, you who wear the garland of round-clustered kadamba.","n":"The prayer names what it refuses (பொருளும் பொன்னும் போகமும்) before what it asks, and the three it asks are ethical states, not gifts. Among those shut out are மறு பிறப்பு இல் எனும் மடவோர், the deniers of rebirth: a Sangam devotional poem already treats rebirth as a belief whose denial excludes one from the god.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thiruvarul","anbu","aram","piravi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 5, lines 71-82","lb":"PR5:71-82","poet":"கடுவன் இளவெயினனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",82,"maram","ornament","kadamba"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:4"},"pr:6:1-13":{"book":"paripadal","num":27,"t":["நிறை கடல் முகந்து உராய், நிறைந்து, நீர் துளும்பும் தம்","பொறை தவிர்பு அசைவிடப் பொழிந்தன்று, வானம்;","நிலம் மறைவது போல் மலிர் புனல் தலைத் தலைஇ,","மலைய இனம் கலங்க, மலைய மயில் அகவ,","மலை மாசு கழியக் கதழும் அருவி இழியும்","மலி நீர் அதர் பல கெழுவு தாழ் வரை,","மாசு இல் பனுவற் புலவர் புகழ் புல","நாவின் புனைந்த நன் கவிதை மாறாமை,","மேவிப் பரந்து விரைந்து, வினை நந்தத்","தாயிற்றே தண் அம் புனல்","புகை, பூ, அவி ஆராதனை, அழல், பல ஏந்தி,","நகை அமர் காதலரை நாளணிக் கூட்டும்","வகைசாலும், வையை வரவு."],"p":"","r":"The sky, having drawn water from the full sea, pours down to set its burden at rest; the flood rises as if to hide the land, the hill herds are troubled and the peacocks call, falls wash the mountain clean, and the cool water runs down the slopes and spreads fast and without pause, like the praise faultless poets keep weaving, so that the work of the fields thrives; and the Vaiyai's coming, met with incense, flowers, oblations and fire, is fit to bring smiling lovers together in their festival finery.","n":"The song opens on the whole water cycle, sea to cloud to hill to river, and ends the sentence not on the harvest but on worship and on lovers dressed for the day (நாளணி): the river's use, for this poet, is the festival. The poets' unceasing verse is the river's measure, so the song names its own craft inside its first image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","vazhipaadu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 1-13","lb":"PR6:1-13","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கடல்","கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["புனல்","புனல்",3,"pira","scene","flood water"],["இனம்","இனம்",4,"maa","scene","hill herds"],["மயில்","மயில்",4,"pul","scene","peacock"],["அருவி","அருவி",5,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["புலவர்","புலவர்",7,"seythi","scene","poets"],["பூ","பூ",11,"maram","gift_food","flowers offered"],["அவி","அவி",11,"unavu","gift_food","oblation"]],"en":[["river","வையை",13]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:6:14-25":{"book":"paripadal","num":28,"t":["வையையின் கரை உடைதலும், ஊரார்கிளர்ந்து எழுதலும்","தொடி தோள் செறிப்பத் தோள்வளை இயங்கக்","கொடி சேரா, திருக் கோவை காழ் கொளத்","தொகு கதிர் முத்துத் தொடை கலிழ்பு மழுக,","உகிரும் கொடிறும் உண்ட செம் பஞ்சியும்,","நகில் அணி அளறு நனி வண்டல் மண்ட,","இலையும் மயிரும் ஈர்ஞ் சாந்து நிழத்த,","முலையும் மார்பும் முயங்கு அணி மயங்க,","விருப்பு ஒன்றுபட்டவர் உளம் நிறை உடைத்தென,","வரைச் சிறை உடைத்ததை வையை: ‘ வையைத்","திரைச் சிறை உடைத்தன்று கரைச்சிறை; அறைக எனும்","உரைச் சிறைப் பறை எழ, ஊர் ஒலித்தன்று"],"p":"","r":"As lovers of one desire, in an embrace that tightens bangles, shifts armlets, knots the girdle, muddies the pearl strings, smears the lac of nails and cheeks, turns breast paste to silt and mingles every ornament of breast and chest, break through the restraint that fills the heart, so the Vaiyai broke the dam of its hills; the drum of proclamation sounded, crying that the river's waves had broken the bank, and the whole town roared.","n":"The simile runs the usual way backwards: seven lines of disordered ornaments come before we learn that the subject is a river; the reader first takes it for a love scene. நிறை (the heart's self-restraint) and சிறை (the dam) are set side by side, so the bank that breaks and the reserve that breaks are one word-play. Line 14 is not verse but a modern prose heading left in the text; it is marked here only because the unit must cover it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 14-25","lb":"PR6:14-25","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["தொடி","தொடி",15,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["முத்துத்","முத்து",17,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["பஞ்சியும்","செம் பஞ்சி",18,"pira","ornament","red lac dye"],["சாந்து","சாந்து",20,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["பறை","பறை",25,"parai","scene","proclamation drum"]],"en":[["river","வையை",23]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:6:26-43":{"book":"paripadal","num":29,"t":["அன்று, போர் அணியின் புகர்முகம் சிறந்தென,","நீர் அணி அணியின் நிரைநிரை பிடி செல:","ஏர் அணி அணியின் இளையரும் இனியரும்--","ஈரணி அணியின், இகல் மிக நவின்று,","தணி புனல் ஆடும் தகை மிகு போர்க்கண்","துணி புனல் ஆக, துறை வேண்டும் மைந்தின்","அணி அணி ஆகிய தாரர், கருவியர்,","அடு புனலது செல அவற்றை இழிவர்;","கைம்மான் எருத்தர், கலி மட மாவினர்,","நெய்ம் மாண் சிவிறியர், நீர் மணக் கோட்டினர்,","வெண் கிடை மிதவையர், நன் கிடைத் தேரினர்,","சாரிகை மறுத்துத் தண்டா உண்டிகை","ஓர் இயவு உறுத்தர ஊர்ஊர்பு இடம் திரீஇச்","சேரி இளையர் செல அரு நிலையர்,","வலியர் அல்லோர் துறைதுறை அயர,","மெலியர் அல்லோர் விருந்து புனல் அயர,","சாறும் சேறும் நெய்யும் மலரும்","நாறுபு நிகழும், யாறு வரலாறு."],"p":"","r":"On that day the cow elephants go down in rows, arrayed for water as the spotted-faced tusker is arrayed for war, and the young and the lovers, in fine dress and in wet dress, practise their rivalry in the play of the settling flood as if in battle: with garlands and gear they go down after the rushing current, on elephants' necks and spirited horses, with syringes and horns of scented water, on white pith floats and raft-chariots, the strong playing ford after ford and the well-off at the new water, until the river comes on smelling of juice, mud, ghee and flowers.","n":"The festival is staged as a war (போர் அணி, இகல், அணி அணி ஆகிய தாரர்), with mounts, weapons and ranks, so the puram muster is borrowed for a love game. Lines 37 to 39 (சாரிகை மறுத்து ... சேரி இளையர்) are hard to construe and the reading of them is loose.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","ilamai","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 26-43","lb":"PR6:26-43","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["புகர்முகம்","புகர்முகம்",26,"maa","simile","war elephant"],["பிடி","பிடி",27,"maa","scene","cow elephant"],["கைம்மான்","கைம்மான்",34,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மாவினர்","மா",34,"maa","scene","horse"],["சாறும்","சாறு",42,"unavu","scene","juice"],["நெய்யும்","நெய்",42,"unavu","scene","ghee"],["மலரும்","மலர்",42,"maram","scene","flowers"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:6:44-60":{"book":"paripadal","num":30,"t":["நாறுபு நிகழும் யாறு கண்டு, அழிந்து","வேறுபடு புனல் என, விரை மண்ணுக் கலிழை,","புலம் புரி அந்தணர் கலங்கினர், மருண்டு","மாறு மென் மலரும், தாரும் கோதையும்,","வேரும் தூரும், காயும் கிழங்கும்,","பூரிய மாக்கள் உண்பது மண்டி","நார் அரி நறவம் உகுப்ப ‘நலன் அழிந்து,","வேறாகின்று இவ் விரி புனல் வரவு‘ என,","வரை அழி வால் அருவி வாதாலாட்ட,","கரை அழி வால் அருவிக் கால் பாராட்ட,","‘இரவில் புணர்ந்தோர் இடைமுலை அல்கல்","புரைவது பூந் தாரான் குன்று‘ எனக் கூடார்க்கு","உரையோடு இழிந்து உராய், ஊரிடை ஓடிச்,","சலப் படையான் இரவில் தாக்கியது எல்லாம்","புலப்படப் புன்அம் புலரியின் நிலப்படத்","தான் மலர்ந்தன்றே","தமிழ் வையைத் தண்ணம் புனல்"],"p":"","r":"Seeing the river come on reeking, the brahmins bent on their learning are dismayed, take it for a changed flood thick with fragrant clay, and cry that its goodness is gone, for flowers, garlands and wreaths, roots and stalks, fruits and tubers, the food of common people and spilled filtered toddy crowd its water; while the falls that break the hills are praised and the bank-breaking channels cheered, the flood goes down telling the unmated that the god's hill is like the rest between the breasts of lovers met by night, runs through the town, and at faint dawn the cool water of Tamil Vaiyai opens out on the land and shows plainly all that its water-army did in the dark.","n":"The poet sets two judgments of one flood side by side without settling them: for the brahmins the festival water is defiled, for the town it is the season's joy, and the unit closes by calling it தமிழ் வையை, a river owned by the language. The night raid made visible at dawn is the lovers' night told as a river's: the flood's 'army' is the town's lovemaking. Lines 52 and 53 (வாதாலாட்ட, கால் பாராட்ட) are hard to fix, and the speakers of the two quoted cries are not named.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["andhanar","thoomai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 44-60","lb":"PR6:44-60","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",46,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["மலரும்","மலர்",47,"maram","scene","flowers"],["கோதையும்","கோதை",47,"maram","ornament","wreath"],["கிழங்கும்","கிழங்கு",48,"unavu","scene","tubers"],["மாக்கள்","மாக்கள்",49,"seythi","scene","common folk"],["நறவம்","நறவம்",50,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["அருவி","அருவி",52,"pira","scene","falls"]],"en":[["hill","பூந் தாரான் குன்று",55],["river","வையை",60]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:6:61-79":{"book":"paripadal","num":31,"t":["‘விளியா விருந்து விழுவார்க்குக் கொய்தோய்,","‘தளிர் அறிந்தாய், தாம் இவை.‘","‘பணிபு ஒழி பண்ப!--பண்டெல்லாம் நனி உருவத்து;","என்னோ துவள் கண்டீ?--","எய்தும் களவு இனி: நின் மார்பின் தார் வாடக்","கொய்ததும் வாயாளோ? கொய் தழை கை பற்றிச்","செய்ததும் வாயாளோ? செப்பு.‘","தளிரின் துவட்சிக்கு வையைப் பெருக்குக் காரணம் என","‘புனை புனை ஏறத் தாழ்த்ததை; தளிர் இவை","நீரின் துவண்ட; சேஎய் குன்றம்; காமர்","பெருக்கு அன்றோ, வையை வரவு?‘","‘ஆம் ஆம்; அது ஒக்கும்; காதல்அம் காமம்","ஒருக்க ஒருதன்மை நிற்குமோ? ஒல்லைச்","சுருக்கமும் ஆக்கமும் --- சூள் உறல்!--- வையைப்","பெருக்கு அன்றோ? பெற்றாய் பிழை.","அருகு பதியாக அம்பியின் தாழ்ப்பிக்கும்,","குருகு இரை தேரக் கிடக்கும்பொழி காரில்,","இன் இளவேனில், இது அன்றோ வையை? நின்","வையை வயமாக வை."],"p":"","r":"The wife mocks her husband for the wilted shoots he brings, plucked for endless new guests, and asks whether the woman who wore them till his garland faded will not tell what he did with the leaf-skirt in his hand; he answers that the shoots drooped in the water and swears by the red god's hill that the Vaiyai's flood is to blame, and she agrees in scorn: does love keep one nature, now shrinking, now swelling, like the Vaiyai's flood in the rains and in early summer? Keep your Vaiyai, she says, under your own control.","n":"The husband's alibi and the wife's retort use the same river: he blames the flood for the leaves, she turns the flood's rise and fall into the very proof of his changing love. நின் வையை வயமாக வை (line 79) is a gibe: 'your' Vaiyai, the excuse he keeps to hand. The speakers alternate without being named; lines 63 and 64 and 76 are obscure in our text, and line 68 is a modern prose heading, not verse.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 61-79","lb":"PR6:61-79","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["தளிர்","தளிர்",62,"maram","ornament","tender shoots"],["தார்","தார்",65,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தழை","தழை",66,"maram","ornament","leaf-skirt"],["குருகு","குருகு",77,"pul","scene","heron"]],"en":[["hill","சேஎய் குன்றம்",70],["river","வையை",71]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:6:80-87":{"book":"paripadal","num":32,"t":["செல் யாற்றுத் தீம் புனலில் செல் மரம் போல,","வவ்வு வல்லார் புணை ஆகிய மார்பினை;","என்னும் பனியாய் இரவெல்லாம் வைகினை;","வையை உடைந்த மடை அடைத்தக்கண்ணும்","பின்னும் மலிரும் பிசிர் போல, இன்னும்","அனற்றினை துன்பு அவிய, நீ அடைந்தக்கண்ணும்,","பனித்துப் பனி வாரும் கண்ணவர் நெஞ்சம்","கனற்றுபு காத்தி, வரவு!‘"],"p":"","r":"The wife tells him that his chest, like a log drifting down the sweet flowing river, became a raft for women skilled at seizing, that he spent the night away, and that even now, like the spray that keeps welling after the breached sluice of the Vaiyai has been closed, he burns her: though he comes to put out her grief, his coming only keeps scorching the hearts of those whose eyes run with tears.","n":"The images are taken from the day's own water-play: the floats and rafts of the festival become the husband, ridden by others. The dammed sluice that still seeps answers the reconciliation he expects: his return closes the breach, but the hurt goes on leaking. Line 82 (என்னும் பனியாய் இரவெல்லாம் வைகினை) is uncertain in its first words.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 80-87","lb":"PR6:80-87","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","thalaivan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["azhugai","அசைவு"]],"k":[["புனலில்","புனல்",80,"pira","simile","river water"],["மரம்","மரம்",80,"maram","simile","drifting tree"],["மடை","மடை",83,"pira","simile","sluice"],["பிசிர்","பிசிர்",84,"pira","simile","spray"]],"en":[["river","வையை",83]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:6:88-96":{"book":"paripadal","num":33,"t":["‘நல்லாள் கரை நிற்ப, நான் குளித்த பைந் தடத்து,","நில்லாள் திரை மூழ்கி நீங்கி எழுந்து, என்மேல்","அல்லா விழுந்தாளை எய்தி, எழுந்து ஏற்று யான்","கொள்ளா அளவை, எழுந் தேற்றாள்: கோதையின்","உள் அழுத்தியாள் எவளோ? தோய்ந்தது யாது?‘ என--","தேறித் தெரிய உணர் நீ: பிறிதும் ஓர்","யாறு உண்டோ? இவ் வையை யாறு","‘இவ் வையை யாறு என்ற மாறு என்னை? கையால்","தலை தொட்டேன், தண் பரங்குன்று!‘"],"p":"","r":"The husband explains that while she stood on the bank a woman bathing in his pool, swept under by a wave, came up and fell on him unawares, and that before he could take hold of her she was up again; the wife asks who it was that pressed into his garland and what touched it, he tells her to understand that there is no other river but this Vaiyai to blame, and when she scorns the answer he swears with his hand on his head by cool Parankunram.","n":"The festival gives the husband a ready alibi, since everyone is in the water together and bodies do collide; the poem lets the excuse be plausible and the suspicion be reasonable at once. The oath is by the god's hill above the river, the same Murugan shrine the Akananuru husbands swear by. Who speaks lines 92 to 96 is not marked in the text; the split given here is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oodal","sool","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 88-96","lb":"PR6:88-96","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["தடத்து","தடம்",88,"pira","scene","pool"],["திரை","திரை",89,"pira","scene","wave"],["கோதையின்","கோதை",91,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["river","வையை",94],["hill","பரங்குன்று",96]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:6:97-107":{"book":"paripadal","num":34,"t":["‘சினவல்; நின் உண்கண் சிவப்பு அஞ்சுவாற்குத்","துனி நீங்கி, ஆடல் தொடங்கு; துனி நனி","கன்றிடின் காமம் கெடூஉம்; மகள்; இவன்","அல்லா நெஞ்சம் உறப் பூட்டக் காய்ந்தே","வல் இருள் நீயல்; அது பிழையாகும்‘ என,","இல்லவர் ஆட, இரந்து பரந்து உழந்து","வல்லவர் ஊடல் உணர்த்தர, நல்லாய்!","களிப்பர்; குளிப்பர்; காமம் கொடி விட,","அளிப்ப, துனிப்ப, ஆங்காங்கு ஆடுப;","ஆடுவார் நெஞ்சத்து அலர்ந்து அமைந்த காமம்","வாடற்க, வையை! நினக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Others urge the wife: do not be angry; he fears the reddening of your eyes, so leave the sulk and begin the play, for a sulk that festers too long spoils love, and to burn and drive off into the dark night a heart that is yours alone would be the fault; and so, while the household plays and the skilled plead and labour until the sulk is understood, the lovers revel and bathe, grant grace and sulk again here and there as love puts out its tendrils, and the poet asks the Vaiyai that the love which has bloomed in the players' hearts may never fade.","n":"The quarrel of the dialogue is not left to the two lovers: the town's skilled appeasers (வல்லவர்) do the work of reconciliation, and sulking is named as part of the play (அளிப்ப, துனிப்ப), not its interruption. The closing prayer turns the river from cause of the quarrel into keeper of the love. The counselors of lines 97 to 101 are not named; `pira` stands for them. The word கொடி for love's spreading is figurative, so it is not listed as karu.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kaamam","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 6, lines 97-107","lb":"PR6:97-107","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["உண்கண்","உண்கண்",97,"pira","scene","kohl-lined eyes"]],"en":[["river","வையை",107]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:5"},"pr:7:1-22":{"book":"paripadal","num":35,"t":["திரை இரும் பனிப் பௌவம் செவ்விதா அற முகந்து,","உர உரும் உடன்று ஆர்ப்ப, ஊர் பொறை கொள்ளாது,","கரை உடை குளமெனக் கழன்று, வான் வயிறு அழிபு,","வரைவரை தொடித்த வயங்கு வெள் அருவி--","இரவு இருள் பகலாக, இடம் அரிது செலவு என்னாது,","வலன் இரங்கு முரசின் தென்னவர் உள்ளிய","நிலன் உற நிமிர் தானை நெடு நிரை நிவப்பு அன்ன--","பெயலான் பொலிந்து, பெரும் புனல் பல நந்த,","நலன் நந்த நாடு அணி நந்தப் புலன் நந்த","வந்தன்று, வையைப் புனல்","நளி இருஞ் சோலை நரந்தம் தாஅய்,","ஒளிர் சினை வேங்கை விரிந்த இணர் உதிரலொடு,","துளியின் உழந்த தோய்வு அருஞ் சிமைதொறும்","வளி வாங்கு சினைய மா மரம் வேர் கீண்டு,","உய்ர்ந்துழி உள்ளன பயம்பிடைப் பரப்பி;","உழவர் களி தூங்க, முழவு பணை முரல,","ஆடல் அறியா அரிவை போலவும்,","ஊடல் அறியா உவகையள் போலவும்,","வேண்டு வழி நடந்து, தாங்கு தடை பொருது;","விதி ஆற்றான் ஆக்கிய மெய்க் கலவை போலப்","பொது நாற்றம் உள்உள் கரந்து, புது நாற்றம்","செய்கின்றே, செம் பூம் புனல்."],"p":"","r":"Clouds that have drawn up the cold dark sea break open like a tank whose bank has given way, with thunder roaring, and white falls wind round every hill like the risen ranks of the Pandya's army marching day and night on its chosen land; so the Vaiyai's water comes, swelling the land's good and its beauty, scattering narantham and vengai flowers, uprooting wind-bent trees and spreading their fruit over the low ground; while the ploughmen revel and the drums sound, it goes where it will like a girl who knows no dance and a joyful one who knows no sulking, fighting every dam, and like a paste mixed by rule it hides the common smells within and gives off a new fragrance, this red-flowered flood.","n":"The flood is carried by the cycle that begins at sea (line 1) and ends in the fields' good (line 9, நலன் நந்த நாடு அணி நந்த), so the praise of the river is also praise of rain. The two similes of lines 17 and 18 already give the river a woman's temper, a girl untaught and without oodal, which the water-play later in the song turns round: there the women quarrel and the river stays calm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","naadu","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 7, lines 1-22","lb":"PR7:1-22","poet":"மையோடக் கோவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பௌவம்","பௌவம்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["அருவி","அருவி",4,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["முரசின்","முரசு",6,"parai","simile","war drum"],["புனல்","புனல்",10,"pira","scene","flood water"],["நரந்தம்","நரந்தம்",11,"maram","scene","narantham grass"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",12,"maram","scene","kino tree"],["மா","மா மரம்",14,"maram","scene","great (or mango) tree"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",16,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"],["முழவு","முழவு",16,"parai","scene","drum"],["பணை","பணை",16,"parai","scene","drum"],["கலவை","கலவை",20,"pira","simile","scented paste"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவர்",6],["river","வையைப்",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:6"},"pr:7:23-50":{"book":"paripadal","num":36,"t":["‘கவிழ்ந்த புனலின் கயம் தண் கழுநீர்","அவிழ்ந்த மலர் மீதுற்றென, ஒருசார்;","மாதர் மடநல்லார், மணலின் எழுதிய","பாவை சிதைத்தது‘ என அழ, ஒருசார்;","‘அகவயல் இள நெல் அரிகால் சூடு","தொகு புனல் பரந்தெனத் துடி பட, ஒருசார்;","‘ஓதம் சுற்றியது ஊர்‘ என, ஒருசார்;","‘கார் தூம்பு அற்றது வான்‘ என, ஒருசார்;","‘பாடுவார் பாக்கம் கொண்டென,","ஆடுவார் சேரி அடைந்தென,","கழனி வந்து கால் கோத்தென,","பழன வாளை பாளை உண்டென,","வித்து இடு புலம் மேடு ஆயிற்றென,","உணர்த்த உணரா ஒள் இழை மாதரைப்","புணர்த்திய இச்சத்துப் பெருக்கத்தின் துனைந்து,","சினை வளர் வாளையின் கிளையொடு கெழீஇ,","பழன உழவர், பாய் புனல் பரத்தந்து,","இறு வரை புரையுமாறு இரு கரை ஏமத்து,","வரை புரை உருவின் நுரை பல சுமந்து,","பூ வேய்ந்து, பொழில் பரந்து;","துனைந்து ஆடுவார் ஆய் கோதையர்,","அலர் தண் தாரவர், காதில்","தளிர் செரீஇ, கண்ணி பறித்து;","கை வளை, ஆழி, தொய்யகம், புனை துகில்,","மேகலை, காஞ்சி, வாகுவலயம்,","எல்லம் கவரும் இயல்பிற்றாய்: தென்னவன்","ஒன்னார் உடை புலம் புக்கற்றால்---மாறு அட்ட","தானையான் வையை வனப்பு"],"p":"","r":"On every side the town cries out: the flood has spread over the lilies of the pond, it has wrecked the dolls the young women drew in the sand and they weep, it has reached the cut sheaves of young paddy so the drum is beaten, the tide has ringed the town, the sky has lost its sluices; it has taken the singers' hamlet and the dancers' street, run into the fields, let the carp eat the spathes, raised the seed-beds into mounds; it rushes like the swell of desire, the field-workers with the shoaling carp spread the water, the banks are guarded like hills, it bears hill-like foam, roofs itself with flowers and spreads through the groves; and as the bathers deck their ears and pluck their garlands, it carries off bangles, rings, armlets, cloth and girdles, entering like the Pandya into the land of his enemies: such is the beauty of the Vaiyai of the king whose army killed his foes.","n":"The list of cries (lines 23 to 35) is the flood seen by those it harms, and the poet keeps it in praise: losses of sand dolls and seed-beds are counted as the river's wealth. The closing simile turns the river's taking of the bathers' jewels into the Pandya's plunder of enemy country, so the river's beauty (வனப்பு, line 50) is put in the king's possession. Lines 36 and 37 (the swell of desire that joins women who will not understand when told) are hard to construe; the flood's speed seems compared to the haste of passion.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","naadu","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 7, lines 23-50","lb":"PR7:23-50","poet":"மையோடக் கோவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",23,"maram","scene","water-lily"],["மணலின்","மணல்",25,"pira","scene","sand"],["பாவை","பாவை",26,"pira","scene","sand doll"],["நெல்","நெல்",27,"maram","scene","paddy"],["துடி","துடி",28,"parai","scene","small drum"],["ஓதம்","ஓதம்",29,"pira","scene","tide"],["வாளை","வாளை",34,"pira","scene","carp"],["பாளை","பாளை",34,"maram","scene","spathe"],["உழவர்,","உழவர்",39,"seythi","scene","field-workers"],["நுரை","நுரை",41,"pira","simile","foam"],["பொழில்","பொழில்",42,"maram","scene","grove"],["தளிர்","தளிர்",45,"maram","ornament","tender shoot"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",45,"maram","ornament","head garland"],["வளை,","வளை",46,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["மேகலை,","மேகலை",47,"pira","ornament","girdle"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",48],["river","வையை",50]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:6"},"pr:7:51-76":{"book":"paripadal","num":37,"t":["புரிந்த தகையினான் யாறு ஆடுவாருள்","துரந்து புனல் தூவ, தூ மலர்க் கண்கள்","அமைந்தன; ஆங்கண், அவருள் ஒருத்தி,","கை புதைஇய வளை","ஏக்கழுத்து நாணான் கரும்பின் அணை மென் தோள்","போக்கிச் சிறைப்பிடித்தாள்; ஓர் பொன் அம் கொம்பு","பரிந்து அவளைக் கைப் பிணை நீக்குவான் பாய்வாள்;","இரும்பு ஈர் வடி ஒத்து மை விளங்கும் கண் ஒளியால்","செம்மைப் புதுப் புனல் சென்று இருளாயிற்றே;","வையைப் பெருக்கு வடிவு","விரும்பிய ஈரணி மெய் ஈரம் தீர,","சுரும்பு ஆர்க்கும் சூர் நறா ஏந்தினாள் கண் நெய்தல்;","பேர் மகிழ் செய்யும் பெரு நறாப் பேணியவே,","கூர் நறா ஆர்ந்தவள் கண்.","கண் இயல் கண்டு ஏத்தி, காரிகை நீர் நோக்கினைப்","பாண் ஆதரித்துப் பல பாட; அப் பாட்டுப்","பேணாது ஒருத்தி பேதுற; ஆயிடை,","‘என்னை வருவது எனக்கு?‘ என்று, இனையா,","நன் ஞெமர் மார்பன் நடுக்குற, நண்ணி;","சிகை கிடந்த ஊடலின் செங் கண் சேப்பு ஊர,","வகை தொடர்ந்த ஆடலுள் நல்லவர் தம்முள்","பகை தொடர்ந்து, கோதை பரியூஉ, நனி வெகுண்டு,","யாறு ஆடு மேனி அணி கண்ட தன் அன்பன்","சேறு ஆடு மேனி திருநிலத்து உய்ப்ப, சிரம் மிதித்து,","தீர்விலதாகச் செருவுற்றாள் செம் புனல்","ஊருடன் ஆடுங்கடை."],"p":"","r":"Among the bathers the water is flung till flower-like eyes redden; one woman, unashamed and proud, takes a man captive in her soft shoulders, and a gold-creeper of a girl leaps to free him from her grip, and the light of their kohl eyes, like a keen iron blade, darkens the red new flood; another, to dry her wet ornaments, lifts bee-humming toddy, and her neythal eyes turn to the strong drink; then a bard, seeing a woman's eyes, sings many songs in praise of her watery glance, and one woman, hating the song, is overcome, the broad-chested lover trembles at what will come to him, and she, eyes red with oodal, quarrels among the women, tears her garland, and in a fury brings down her man, who had looked at the ornaments of the bathing bodies, into the mud and treads on his head, fighting without end while the whole town bathes in the red flood.","n":"The song moves from the river's beauty to a lovers' quarrel without leaving the flood: the oodal of lines 67 to 76 is caused by the bard's praise of another woman's eyes and by the man's looking (யாறு ஆடு மேனி அணி கண்ட, line 73), and it is told as a public scene, the town going on with its bathing around it. The trembling lover (என்னை வருவது எனக்கு?, line 68) is comic, and the head trodden in the mud is the akam sulk acted out in full. No colophon names a speaker; the poet narrates, so there is no thurai_akam.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punalaadal","parathai","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 7, lines 51-76","lb":"PR7:51-76","poet":"மையோடக் கோவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["மலர்க்","மலர்",52,"maram","simile","flower"],["வளை","வளை",54,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["கரும்பின்","கரும்பு",55,"maram","ornament","sugarcane (painted on the shoulder)"],["கொம்பு","கொம்பு",56,"maram","simile","creeper-branch"],["இரும்பு","இரும்பு",58,"pira","simile","iron blade"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",62,"pira","scene","bee"],["நறா","நறா",62,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["நெய்தல்;","நெய்தல்",62,"maram","simile","blue water-lily"],["பாண்","பாண்",66,"seythi","scene","bard"],["கோதை","கோதை",72,"maram","ornament","garland"],["சேறு","சேறு",74,"pira","scene","mud"]],"en":[["river","வையைப்",60]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:6"},"pr:7:77-86":{"book":"paripadal","num":38,"t":["புரி நரம்பு இன் கொளைப் புகல் பாலை ஏழும்","எழூஉப் புணர் யாழும், இசையும், கூட;","குழல் அளந்து நிற்ப; முழவு எழுந்து ஆர்ப்ப;","மன் மகளிர், சென்னியர், ஆடல் தொடங்க;","பொருது இழிவார் புனல் பொற்பு, அஃது","உரும் இடி சேர்ந்த முழக்கம் புரையும்","திருமருதமுன்துறை சேர் புனற்கண் துய்ப்பார்","தாமம் தலை புனை பேஎம் நீர் வையை!","நின் பயம் பாடி விடிவுற்று ஏமாக்க ...","நின் படிந்து நீங்காமை இன்று புணர்ந்தெனவே"],"p":"","r":"The seven paalai modes on the twisted strings, the lute and the voice join, the flute keeps the measure, the drum rises and roars, and the women of the king's house and the singers begin to dance; the beauty of the water that pours down fighting its banks is like the roar where thunder strikes; and the poet hails the Vaiyai of the bewildering water, whose bathers at the ford of Tirumarutham wear garlands on their heads: singing your bounty till dawn we are safe, and pray that we may bathe in you and never leave it, now that we have joined.","n":"The music of lines 77 to 80 is the festival's, and the poem's own pan is பாலையாழ், so the song names its mode inside itself (பாலை ஏழும், line 77). Line 85 has a gap; the closing prayer asks for the bathing never to end (நீங்காமை, line 86), the same wish akam lovers make of each other, here made of the river.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","vazhipaadu","theerththam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 7, lines 77-86","lb":"PR7:77-86","poet":"மையோடக் கோவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["நரம்பு","நரம்பு",77,"yaazh","scene","lute string"],["பாலை","பாலை",77,"yaazh","scene","the paalai mode"],["யாழும்,","யாழ்",78,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["குழல்","குழல்",79,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["முழவு","முழவு",79,"parai","scene","drum"],["உரும்","உரும்",82,"pira","simile","thunder"],["தாமம்","தாமம்",84,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["river","வையை!",84]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:6"},"pr:8:1-16":{"book":"paripadal","num":39,"t":["மண்மிசை---அவிழ்துழாய் மலர்தரு செல்வத்துப்","புள்மிசைக் கொடியோனும், புங்கவம் ஊர்வோனும்,","மலர்மிசை முதல்வனும், மற்று அவனிடைத் தோன்றி","உலகு இருள் அகற்றிய பதின்மரும், இருவரும்,","மருந்து உரை இருவரும், திருந்து நூல் எண்மரும்,","ஆதிரை முதல்வனின் கிளந்த","நாதர் பன்னொருவரும், நன் திசை காப்போரும்,","யாவரும், பிறரும், அமரரும், அவுணரும்,","மேஅரு முதுமொழி விழுத் தவ முதல்வரும் ...","பற்றாகின்று, நின் காரணமாக;","பரங்குன்று இமயக் குன்றம் நிகர்க்கும்.","இமயக் குன்றினில் சிறந்து","நின் ஈன்ற நிரை இதழ்த் தாமரை","மின் ஈன்ற விளங்கு இணர் ஊழா","ஒருநிலைப் பொய்கையோடு ஒக்கும்--- நின் குன்றின்","அருவி தாழ் மாலைச் சுனை."],"p":"","r":"The poet lists the gods who have come to cling to Murugan as their refuge: Thirumal of the bird banner and blooming tulasi, the rider of the bull, the first one on the lotus, the twelve who drive darkness from the world, the two who speak of medicine, the eight of the ordered texts, the eleven lords named from the lord of Aathirai, the guardians of the quarters, gods, asuras and the great ascetics; because of him Parankundram equals the Himalaya, and the pool of his hill, where falls hang like garlands, equals the one still pool of the Himalaya where the ranked lotus that bore him blooms in flashing clusters.","n":"Lines 4 to 7 count the Vedic thirty-three by their groups (10 and 2, 2, 8, 11), so the hill is made the meeting place of the whole older pantheon. The argument of lines 11 to 16 is a transfer of sanctity: the Pandya hill equals the Himalaya because its pool equals the pool of his birth. மாலைச் சுனை (line 16) can be the garland-like pool of the falls or the pool at evening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 8, lines 1-16","lb":"PR8:1-16","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["துழாய்","துழாய்",1,"maram","ornament","tulasi"],["புள்மிசைக்","புள்",2,"pul","scene","bird of the banner"],["புங்கவம்","புங்கவம்",2,"maa","scene","bull"],["மலர்மிசை","மலர்",3,"maram","scene","lotus seat"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",13,"maram","simile","lotus"],["பொய்கையோடு","பொய்கை",15,"pira","simile","pool"],["அருவி","அருவி",16,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["சுனை","சுனை",16,"pira","simile","mountain pool"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்று",11],["hill","இமயக் குன்றம்",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:7"},"pr:8:17-28":{"book":"paripadal","num":40,"t":["முதல்வ! நின் யானை முழக்கம் கேட்ட","கதியிற்றே காரின் குரல்.","குரல் கேட்ட கோழி குன்று அதிரக் கூவ,","மத நனி வாரணம் மாறுமாறு அதிர்ப்ப,","எதிர்குதிர் ஆகின்று அதிர்ப்பு, மலை முழை.","ஏழ் புழை ஐம் புழை யாழ் இசை கேழ்த்து அன்ன, இனம்","வீழ் தும்பி வண்டொடு மிஞிறு ஆர்ப்ப, சுனை மலர,","கொன்றை கொடி இணர் ஊழ்ப்ப, கொடி மலர்","மன்றல மலர, மலர் காந்தள் வாய் நாற,","நன்று அவிழ் பல் மலர் நாற, நறை பனிப்ப,","தென்றல் அசைவரூஉம் செம்மற்றே-- அம்ம! நின்","குன்றத்தான் கூடல் வரவு."],"p":"","r":"The rain cloud's voice answers like one that has heard the trumpeting of the god's elephant; hearing it the cocks crow till the hill shakes, the musth elephants trumpet back and the caves return the echo; bees of every kind hum like the music of seven-holed and five-holed instruments, the pools bloom, the konrai lets down its clusters, the creepers flower for weddings, the kanthal opens its mouth, honey drips and the south breeze stirs, and this, the poet says, is the splendour of the coming from your hill to Koodal.","n":"The unit is a chain of answering sounds (elephant, cloud, cock, elephant, cave) that turns into a chain of opening flowers; the god's presence is heard before it is seen. கூடல் வரவு (line 28) makes the scene a procession from the hill to the Pandya city, not only a landscape.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 8, lines 17-28","lb":"PR8:17-28","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",17,"maa","simile","the god's elephant"],["காரின்","கார்",18,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["கோழி","கோழி",19,"pul","scene","cock"],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",20,"maa","scene","elephant"],["முழை","முழை",21,"pira","scene","cave"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",22,"yaazh","simile","lute"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",23,"pira","simile","bee"],["வண்டொடு","வண்டு",23,"pira","simile","bee"],["மிஞிறு","மிஞிறு",23,"pira","simile","bee"],["சுனை","சுனை",23,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["கொன்றை","கொன்றை",24,"maram","scene","konrai"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",25,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["நறை","நறை",26,"pira","scene","honey"],["தென்றல்","தென்றல்",27,"pira","scene","south wind"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",28]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:7"},"pr:8:29-46":{"book":"paripadal","num":41,"t":["குன்றம் உடைத்த ஒளிர் வேலோய்! கூடல்","மன்றல் கலந்த மணி முரசின் ஆர்ப்பு எழ,","காலொடு மயங்கிய கலிழ் கடலென,","மால் கடல் குடிக்கும் மழைக் குரலென,","ஏறு அதிர்க்கும் இந்திரன் இரும் உருமென,","மன்றல் அதிரதிர மாறுமாறு அதிர்க்கும் --நின்","குன்றம் குமுறிய உரை","‘தூது ஏய வண்டின் தொழுதி முரல்வு அவர்","காதல் மூதூர் மதில் கம்பலைத்தன்று;","வடு வகிர் வென்ற கண், மாந் தளிர் மேனி,","நெடு மென் பணைத் தோள், குறுந் தொடி, மகளிர்","ஆராக் காமம், ஆர் பொழிற் பாயல்,","வரையகத்து, இயைக்கும் வரையா நுகர்ச்சி;","முடியா நுகர்ச்சி முற்றாக் காதல்,","அடியோர் மைந்தர் அகலத்து அகலா","அலர் ஞெமல் மகன்றில் நன்னர்ப் புணர்ச்சி,","புலரா மகிழ்; மறப்பு அறியாது நல்கும்","சிறப்பிற்றே---தண் பரங்குன்று.‘"],"p":"","r":"Hailing the god whose shining spear split the mountain, the poet says that when the wedding drums of Koodal roar, the hill answers back and forth like the wind-churned sea, like the cloud drinking the ocean, like Indra's thunder, and its answer is a proclamation: the humming of the bee swarms, the lovers' messengers, rings over the walls of their old town; women with eyes finer than cut mango, bodies like mango shoots, bamboo shoulders and small bangles find on the grove beds of the hill a desire that is never sated, an enjoyment that does not end, a love never complete, and the young men's union, unparting like the makanril pair on spread flowers, is a joy that never fades; such is the fame of cool Parankundram, which gives without forgetting.","n":"The hill's fame is stated as erotic, not ascetic: the god's hill is praised as the place where love never reaches its end (ஆராக் காமம், வரையா நுகர்ச்சி, முடியா நுகர்ச்சி, முற்றாக் காதல்), a string of negated limits that matches the praise of the god's own boundlessness. The makanril, the water bird proverbial for never leaving its mate, is here part of a comparison of the young men's union, not a separate carrier, so it is not iraicchi.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 8, lines 29-46","lb":"PR8:29-46","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["முரசின்","முரசு",30,"parai","scene","drum"],["கடலென","கடல்",31,"pira","simile","sea"],["மழைக்","மழை",32,"pira","simile","cloud"],["உருமென","உரும்",33,"pira","simile","thunder"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",36,"pira","scene","bee"],["வடு","வடு",38,"maram","simile","tender mango"],["மாந்","மா",38,"maram","simile","mango"],["பணைத்","பணை",39,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["பொழிற்","பொழில்",40,"maram","scene","grove"],["மகன்றில்","மகன்றில்",44,"pul","simile","makanril bird"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",29],["hill","பரங்குன்று",46]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:7"},"pr:8:47-71":{"book":"paripadal","num":42,"t":["‘இனி, மன்னும் ஏதிலர் நாறுதி; ஆண்டுப்","பனி மலர்க் கண்ணாரோடு ஆட--நகை மலர்","மாலைக்கு மாலை வரூஉம்; வரை சூள் நில்--","காலைப் போய் மாலை வரவு.‘","‘இனி மணல் வையை இரும் பொழிலும், குன்றப்","பனி பொழி சாரலும், பார்ப்பாரும்;....","துனியல், மலருண்கண்! சொல் வேறு; நாற்றம்","கனியின் மலரின் மலிர் கால் சீப்பு இன்னது;","துனியல் நனி ‘நீ நின் சூள்.‘","‘என் பாணி நில் நில்---எலாஅ!--பாணி நீ, நின் சூள்;","சான்றாளர் ஈன்ற தகாஅத் தகாஅ மகாஅன்!--","ஈன்றாட்கு ஒரு பெண், இவள்.","\"இருள் மை ஈர் உண் கண் இலங்கு இழை ஈன்றாட்கு","அரியளோ? ஆவது அறிந்திலேன்; ஈதா;","வரு புனல் வையை மணல் தொட்டேன்; தரு மண வேள்","தண் பரங்குன்றத்து அடி தொட்டேன்\" என்பாய்;","கேளிர் மணலின் கெழுவும் இதுவோ?","ஏழ் உலகும் ஆளி திரு வரைவேல் அன்பு அளிதோ?","என்னை அருளி அருள் முருகு சூள் சூளின்,","நின்னை அருள் இல் அணங்கான் மெய் வேல் தின்னும்;","விறல் வெய்யோன் ஊர் மயில், வேல் நிழல், நோக்கி;","அறவர் அடி தொடினும், ஆங்கு அவை சூளேல்;","குறவன் மகள் ஆணை கூறு ஏலா! கூறேல்;","ஐய! சூளின், அடி தொடு குன்றொடு","வையைக்குத் தக்க மணல் சீர் சூள் கூறல்!‘"],"p":"","r":"A wife accuses her husband: he smells of strangers, has played on the hill with dewy-eyed women and comes back smiling evening after evening, gone at morning and home at night, so let him stop swearing by the hill; he answers that the scent is only the wind off the Vaigai groves and the dewy slopes, and begs her not to sulk; she cuts him off, calls him the unworthy son of worthy parents, mimics the oath he will offer (I touched the Vaigai's sand, I touched the foot of the hill of the god who grants marriage), and forbids him to swear by Murugan, by the peacock and the shadow of the spear, by the feet of holy men or by the hunter's daughter, since the true spear of that god would devour him; if he must swear, let him swear by the hill and the sand of the Vaigai.","n":"Her refusal is protective as much as scornful: she does not doubt that the god enforces an oath (அணங்கான் மெய் வேல் தின்னும், line 66), she doubts the man, and so she steers him to an oath by sand, which cannot strike him. குறவன் மகள் (line 69) is Valli, already a name to swear by. The speaker of lines 51 to 55 is not marked, and the turns between wife and husband are a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["sool","oodal","parathai","anangu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 8, lines 47-71","lb":"PR8:47-71","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மலர்க்","மலர்",48,"maram","scene","flower"],["பொழிலும்","பொழில்",51,"maram","scene","grove"],["கனியின்","கனி",54,"maram","scene","fruit"],["மலரின்","மலர்",54,"maram","scene","flower"],["மணல்","மணல்",61,"pira","scene","sand"],["மயில்","மயில்",67,"pul","scene","peacock"],["குறவன்","குறவன்",69,"seythi","scene","hill hunter"]],"en":[["river","வையை",51],["river","வையை",61],["hill","பரங்குன்றத்து",62],["river","வையைக்குத்",71]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:7"},"pr:8:72-89":{"book":"paripadal","num":43,"t":["யார் பிரிய, யார் வர, யார் வினவ, யார் செப்பு?","‘நீர் உரைசெய் நீர்மை இல் சூள் என்றி,‘ நேரிழாய்!","கய வாய் நெய்தல் அலர், கமழ்முகை மண நகை","நயவரு நறவு இதழ், மதர் உண்கண்; வாள் நுதல்;","முகை முல்லை வென்று, எழில் முத்து ஏய்க்கும் வெண் பல்","நகை சான்ற கனவு அன்று; நனவு அன்று நவின்றதை:","இடு துனி கை ஆறா என், துயர் கூரச்","சுடும், இறை; ஆற்றிசின், அடி சேர்ந்து! சாற்றுமின்","மிக ஏற்றுதும் மலர், ஊட்டுதும் அவி,","கேட்டுதும் பாணி; எழுதும் கிணைமுருகன்","தாள் தொழு தண் பரங்குன்று!","‘தெரி இழாய் செல்க!‘ என்றாய்; எல்லா! யாம் பெற்றேம்,","ஒருவர்க்கும் பொய்யா நின் வாய் இல் சூள் வௌவல்;","பருவத்துப் பல் மாண் நீ சேறவின் காண்டை","எருமை இருத் தோட்டி எள்ளீயும் காளை","செருவம் செயற்கு என்னை முன்னை, தன் சென்னி,","அருள்வயினான், தூங்கு மணி கையால் தாக்கி,","நிரைவளை ஆற்று, இருஞ் சூள்,"],"p":"","r":"The quarrel goes on (who left, who came, who asked, who told?): he says she calls his oath worthless and praises her neythal eyes, bud-like smile, honeyed lips, bright brow and teeth whiter than jasmine buds and like pearls, saying what he speaks is neither dream nor waking; she says the anger she has taken on will not cool and her grief grows, and turns to the god: let us go to his feet, offer flowers, feed the fire with oblation and beat the rhythm at cool Parankundram, where Murugan of the drum is worshipped; then she mocks the oath again, saying she has caught the oath from his mouth that never lies to anyone, and the unit ends in obscure lines about a bull, a goad and the hanging bell struck by hand.","n":"The love quarrel turns into a vow of worship in the middle of a sentence (lines 78 to 82): the god at the hill is the one place where the wife's grief can be taken. ஒருவர்க்கும் பொய்யா நின் வாய் (line 84) is sarcasm. Lines 85 to 89 (எருமை இருத் தோட்டி, காளை, தூங்கு மணி) are not secure in sense or speaker.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","sool","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 8, lines 72-89","lb":"PR8:72-89","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["ilivaral","வருத்தம்"]],"k":[["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",74,"maram","simile","water lily"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",76,"maram","simile","jasmine"],["முத்து","முத்து",76,"pira","simile","pearl"],["மலர்","மலர்",80,"maram","gift_food","flower offering"],["அவி","அவி",80,"unavu","gift_food","oblation"],["பாணி","பாணி",81,"yaazh","scene","beat"],["எருமை","எருமை",86,"maa","scene","buffalo"],["மணி","மணி",88,"pira","scene","bell"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்று",82]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:7"},"pr:8:90-111":{"book":"paripadal","num":44,"t":["வளி பொரு சேண் சிமை வரையகத்தால்","தளி பெருகும் தண் சினைய","பொழில் கொளக் குறையா மலர,","குளிர் பொய்கை அளறு நிறைய,","மருதம் நளி மணல் ஞெமர்ந்த","நனி மலர்ப் பெரு வழி,","சீறடியவர் சாறு கொள எழுந்து;","வேறுபடு சாந்தமும், வீறுபடு புகையும்,","ஆறு செல் வளியின் அவியா விளக்கமும்,","நாறு கமழ் வீயும், கூறும் இசை முழவமும்,","மணியும், கயிறும், மயிலும், குடாரியும்,","பிணிமுகம், உளப்படப் பிறவும், ஏந்தி;","அரு வரைச் சேராத் தொழுநர்,","‘கனவின் தொட்டது கை பிழையாகாது","நனவின் சேஎப்ப நின் நளி புனல் வையை","வரு புனல் அணிக எனவரம் கொள்வோரும்,","‘கரு வயிறு உறுக எனக் கடம்படுவோரும்,","‘செய் பொருள் வாய்க்கா எனச் செவி சார்த்துவோரும்,","‘ஐ அமர் அடுக என அருச்சிப்போரும்,","பாடுவார் பாணிச் சீரும், ஆடுவார் அரங்கத் தாளமும்,","மஞ்சு ஆடு மலை முழக்கும்,","துஞ்சாக் கம்பலை"],"p":"","r":"From the wind-beaten peaks the rain swells, the branches drip, the groves bloom, the pools fill with mud and the broad road is spread with marutham sand and flowers, and the small-footed women rise to join the festival; the worshippers carry sandal of many kinds, incense, lamps the wind cannot put out, fragrant flowers, drums, bells, ropes, peacocks, axes and the elephant image, and they ask their boons: some that what their hand touched in a dream should come true in waking, and that the Vaigai's flood adorn them, some vow that a child come to the womb, some whisper that their work for wealth succeed, some worship that their lord win in battle; the songs' rhythms, the dancers' beats on the stage and the thunder of the cloud-swept hill make a clamour that never sleeps.","n":"The petitions are this-worldly and specific (a lover seen in a dream, a child, wealth from work, a husband's victory), and each is matched to a ritual act: asking a boon, making a vow, speaking into the god's ear, offering worship. அரு வரைச் சேராத் தொழுநர் (line 102) may be worshippers who do not climb the hill and pray from its foot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thalam","kanavu","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 8, lines 90-111","lb":"PR8:90-111","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["வளி","வளி",90,"pira","scene","wind"],["பொழில்","பொழில்",92,"maram","scene","grove"],["பொய்கை","பொய்கை",93,"pira","scene","pool"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",94,"maram","scene","marutham tree"],["மணல்","மணல்",94,"pira","scene","sand"],["சாந்தமும்","சாந்தம்",97,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["புகையும்","புகை",97,"pira","ornament","incense"],["விளக்கமும்","விளக்கம்",98,"pira","ornament","lamp"],["வீயும்","வீ",99,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["முழவமும்","முழவம்",99,"parai","ornament","drum"],["மணியும்","மணி",100,"pira","ornament","bell"],["மயிலும்","மயில்",100,"pul","ornament","peacock"],["பிணிமுகம்","பிணிமுகம்",101,"maa","ornament","the god's elephant"],["பாணிச்","பாணி",109,"yaazh","scene","beat"],["மஞ்சு","மஞ்சு",110,"pira","scene","cloud"]],"en":[["river","வையை",104]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:7"},"pr:8:112-130":{"book":"paripadal","num":45,"t":["பைஞ் சுனைப் பாஅய் எழு பாவையர்","ஆய் இதழ் உண்கண் அலர் முகத் தாமரை,","தாட் தாமரை, தோட்தமனியக் கய மலர்,","எம் கைப் பதுமம், கொங்கைக் கய முகை,","செவ் வாய் ஆம்பல் செல் நீர்த் தாமரை,","புனற் தாமரையொடு, புலம் வேறுபாடுறாக்","கூர் ஏயிற்றார் குவிமுலைப் பூணொடு,","மாரண் ஒப்பார் மார்பு அணி கலவி;","அரிவையர் அமிர்த பானம்","உரிமை மாக்கள் உவகை அமிர்து உய்ப்ப;","மைந்தர் மார்வம் வழி வந்த,","செந் தளிர் மேனியார், செல்லல் தீர்ப்ப;","என ஆங்கு,","உடம் புணர் காதலரும் அல்லாரும் கூடி,","கடம்பு அமர் செல்வன் கடி நகர் பேண","மறு மிடற்று அண்ணற்கு மாசிலோள் தந்த","நெறி நீர் அருவி அசும்பு உறு செல்வம்,","மண் பரிய வானம் வறப்பினும், மன்னுகமா,","தண் பரங்குன்றம்! நினக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Girls rise after diving in the green pool, and their eyes, open faces, feet, golden ear ornaments, hands, budding breasts and red mouths are so like the lotus, water lily and the other flowers of the flowing water that the two cannot be told apart; the sharp-toothed women's breast ornaments meet the chests of men handsome as the god of love, the men give the women the nectar of joy as their right, and the women with bodies like red shoots ease the young men's longing; so lovers bound in marriage and those not yet together gather to honour the guarded town of the lord who dwells in the kadambu, and the poet prays that the wealth of streaming falls and springs, the gift of the god born to the dark-throated lord from the spotless lady, may last for cool Parankundram even when the sky dries up and the earth cracks.","n":"The bathing girls become the pool's flowers (புலம் வேறுபாடுறா, line 117), so the shrine's water is where love and worship join, and the closing prayer asks that this water, not the rain, be permanent. எம் கைப் பதுமம் (line 115) slips into a first-person voice. Lines 127 and 128 (மறு மிடற்று அண்ணற்கு மாசிலோள் தந்த) are read here as naming the god as the son Uma gave to Siva; they could also make the falls her gift.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thalam","vazhipaadu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 8, lines 112-130","lb":"PR8:112-130","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சுனைப்","சுனை",112,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",113,"maram","simile","lotus"],["பதுமம்","பதுமம்",115,"maram","simile","lotus"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",116,"maram","simile","white water lily"],["பானம்","பானம்",120,"unavu","gift_food","drink"],["கடம்பு","கடம்பு",126,"maram","scene","kadambu tree"],["அருவி","அருவி",128,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்றம்",130]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:7"},"pr:9:1-11":{"book":"paripadal","num":46,"t":["இரு நிலம் துளங்காமை வடவயின் நிவந்து ஓங்கி,","அரு நிலை உயர் தெய்வத்து அணங்குசால் தலை காக்கும்,","உருமுச் சூழ் சேண் சிமைஉயர்ந்தவர் உடம்பட","எரி மலர்த் தாமரை இறை வீழ்த்த பெரு வாரி","விரி சடைப் பொறை ஊழ்த்து, விழு நிகர் மலர் ஏய்ப்ப,","தணிவுறத் தாங்கிய தனி நிலைச் சலதாரி","மணி மிடற்று அண்ணற்கு, மதி ஆரல் பிறந்தோய்! நீ.","மை இரு நூற்று இமை உண்கண் மான் மறி தோள் மணந்த ஞான்று,","ஐ-இருநூற்று மெய்ந் நயனத்தவன் மகள் மலர் உண்கண்,","மணி மழை தலைஇயென, மா வேனில் கார் ஏற்று,","தணி மழை தலையின்று, தன் பரங்குன்று."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails Murugan as born of the blue-throated lord who, when the high gods of the northern mountain agreed, took on his spread matted hair the great flood that the lord of the lotus let fall and held it lightly like a flower, and says that on the day the god married the doe-eyed daughter of the thousand-eyed Indra her flower eyes rained like drops of gems, and his own Parankunram took in the dark rain in the heart of summer and the cool rain did not stop.","n":"The wedding is marked by rain out of season: the bride's eyes raining and the hill's summer rain are set side by side, so the hill is made a sacred place by an event, not by nature. Indra's thousand eyes are counted as five times two hundred (ஐ-இருநூற்று), and line 8 answers with இரு நூற்று in the bride's own epithet. Siva's holding of the Ganga is told without naming the river.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","sivam","thalam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 9, lines 1-11","lb":"PR9:1-11","poet":"குறும்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",4,"maram","scene","lotus (Brahma's seat)"],["மலர்","மலர்",5,"maram","simile","flower"],["மான்","மான்",8,"maa","scene","deer"],["மழை","மழை",10,"pira","scene","rain"],["கார்","கார்",10,"pira","scene","rain cloud"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்று",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:8"},"pr:9:12-26":{"book":"paripadal","num":47,"t":["நான்மறை விரித்து, நல் இசை விளக்கும்","வாய்மொழிப் புலவீர்! கேண்மின், சிறந்தது;","காதற் காமம், காமத்துச் சிறந்தது;","விருப்போர் ஒத்து மெய்யுறு புணர்ச்சி:","புலத்தலின் சிறந்தது, கற்பே; அது தான்","இரத்தலும் ஈதலும் இவை உள்ளீடாப்","பரத்தை உள்ளதுவே: பண்புறு கழறல்,","தோள் புதிது உண்ட பரத்தை இல் சிவப்புற","நாள் அணிந்து, உவக்கும் சுணங்கறையதுவே;","கேள் அணங்குற மனைக் கிளந்துள, சுணங்கறை;","சுணங்கறைப் பயனும் ஊடலுள் ளதுவே.","அதனால், அகறல் அறியா அணி இழை நல்லார்","இகல் தலைக்கொண்டு துனிக்கும் தவறு இலர்; இத்","தள்ளாப் பொருள் இயல்பின் தண் தமிழ் ஆய்வந்திலார்","கொள்ளார், இக் குன்று பயன்."],"p":"","r":"The poet turns to the poets of truthful speech who expound the four Vedas and tells them the order of love: what excels is desire born of love, and within desire the bodily union of two who long alike; above the quarrel stands karpu, which holds within it the courtesan with her begging and giving, the courteous reproach, and the சுணங்கறை where the wife adorns herself and rejoices to make the courtesan who newly enjoyed his shoulders flush, words spoken in the house to trouble the husband, and the fruit of that too lies in the sulk; so women who never part are not at fault when they quarrel and sulk, and those who have not studied cool Tamil with its unshakable rule of porul will not take what this hill yields.","n":"The poet addresses Vedic scholars as outsiders and tells them that what this hill teaches is the porul grammar of Tamil, so the god's household quarrel that follows (lines 27 to 69) is offered as a lesson in akam theory, not as scandal. The ranking is a small treatise: love, then equal union, then karpu, with the courtesan and the sulk placed inside karpu rather than against it. சுணங்கறை is a rare word whose sense we cannot fix, and the clause order of lines 16 to 22 is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","karpu","parathai","thamizh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 9, lines 12-26","lb":"PR9:12-26","poet":"குறும்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:8"},"pr:9:27-44":{"book":"paripadal","num":48,"t":["ஊழ் ஆரத்து ஓய் கரை நூக்கி, புனல் தந்த","காழ் ஆரத்து அம் புகை சுற்றிய தார் மார்பின்,","கேழ் ஆரம் பொற்ப வருவானைத் தொழாஅ,","‘வாழிய, மாயா! நின் தவறு இலை; எம் போலும்","கேழ் இலார் மாண் நலம் உண்கோ, திரு உடையார்","மென் தோள்மேல் அல்கி நல்கலம் இன்று?","வை எயிற்று எய்யா மகளிர் திறம் இனிப்","பெய்ய உழக்கும், மழைக் கா; மற்று ஐய!‘","கரையா வெந் நோக்கத்தான் கை சுட்டி, பெண்டின்","இகலின் இகந்தாளை, அவ் வேள் தலைக் கண்ணி","திருந்து அடி தோயத் திறை கொடுப்பானை,","‘வருந்தல்‘ என, அவற்கு மார்பு அளிப்பாளை,","‘குறுகல்‘ என்று ஒள்ளிழை கோதை கோலாக","இறுகிறுக யாத்துப் புடைப்ப;","ஒருவர் மயில் ஒருவர் ஒண் மயிலோடு ஏல,","இருவர் வான் கிளி ஏற்பில் மழலை,","செறி கொண்டைமேல் வண்டு சென்று பாய்ந்தன்றே,","வெறி கொண்டான் குன்றத்து வண்டு."],"p":"","r":"As the god comes in, his chest garland wreathed in the smoke of sandal that the river carried down breaking its banks, a bright pearl chain shining on him, one wife does not bow but says, 'Long live, Maaya, the fault is not yours: would you, who rest on the soft shoulders of the fortunate, deign to enjoy the beauty of plain women like us? The rain-grove will be trampled by what the sharp-toothed girls do'; then, pointing with her hand, crying 'don't come near', she binds tight and beats with her garland for a rod the woman who outdid her in the women's quarrel, the Vel who pays tribute with his head-garland touching her fine feet, and the woman who gives him her chest saying 'don't grieve'; and one wife's peacock closes with the other's bright peacock, the two parrots babble at the meeting, and a bee of the possessed god's hill leaps onto a thick hair-knot.","n":"'The fault is not yours' is blame in the form of a blessing. The god who bows his garland to a woman's feet pays திறை, a conquered king's tribute, so the war god is defeated in love; in the closing line he is வெறி கொண்டான், the god of the possession rite, now the cause of a domestic fray. Who bows to whose feet is a reading: the three accusatives of lines 36 to 38 can be taken as the rival, the god and the rival again, and lines 33 and 34 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","valli","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 9, lines 27-44","lb":"PR9:27-44","poet":"குறும்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["ஆரத்து","ஆரம்",27,"maram","scene","sandal tree"],["புனல்","புனல்",27,"pira","scene","flood"],["தார்","தார்",28,"maram","ornament","chest garland"],["மழைக்","மழை",34,"pira","scene","rain (grove)"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",36,"maram","ornament","head garland"],["கோதை","கோதை",39,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மயில்","மயில்",41,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கிளி","கிளி",42,"pul","scene","parrot"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",43,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:8"},"pr:9:45-69":{"book":"paripadal","num":49,"t":["தார் தார் பிணக்குவார்; கண்ணி ஓச்சித் தடுமாறுவார்;","மார்பு அணி கொங்கை வார் மத்திகையாப் புடைப்பார்;","கோதை வரிப் பந்து கொண்டு எறிவார்","பேதை மட நோக்கம் பிறிதாக, ஊத","நுடங்கு நொசி நுசுப்பார்நூழில் தலைக்கொள்ள:","கயம்படு கமழ் சென்னிக் களிற்று இயல் கைம்மாறுவார்;","வயம்படு பரிப் புரவி மார்க்கம் வருவார்","தேர் அணி அணி கயிறு தெரிபு வருவார்","வரி சிலை வளைய மார்பு உற வாங்குவார்","வாளி வாளிகள் நிலைபெற மறலுவார்","தோள் வளை ஆழி சுழற்றுவார்","மென் சீர் மயில் இயலவர்","வாள் மிகு வய மொய்ம்பின்","வரை அகலத்தவனைவானவன் மகள்","மாண் எழில் மலர் உண்கண்","மட மொழியவர் உடன் சுற்றி,","கடி சுனையுள் குளித்து ஆடுநரும்,","அறை அணிந்த அருஞ் சுனையான்","நற உண் வண்டாய் நரம்பு உளர்நரும்,","சிகை மயிலாய்த் தோகை விரித்து ஆடுநரும்;","கோகுலமாய்க் கூவுநரும்,","ஆகுலம் ஆகுநரும்","குறிஞ்சிக் குன்றவர் மறம் கெழு வள்ளி தமர்","வித்தகத் தும்பை விளைத்தலான், வென் வேலாற்கு","ஒத்தன்று, தண் பரங்குன்று."],"p":"","r":"The women of the two sides join in: they tangle garland with garland, swing their chaplets and stumble, lash with the bands of their breasts as whips, hurl balls wound with garlands, and, their innocent glances changed, their thin waists swaying, take the head of the fray, shifting into the gait of a musth elephant, coming on like horses in their course, as if sorting chariot ropes, bending striped bows to the chest, setting arrow against arrow and whirling bangles like discs, as the soft peacock-gaited women and the soft-spoken flower-eyed companions of the sky-king's daughter close round the broad-chested god; some bathe in the guarded pool, some pluck strings like honey-drinking bees, some spread their tails and dance like crested peacocks, some call like cuckoos, some raise a clamour; and because Valli's kin, the fierce hill-men of the kurinji, made of it a skilful thumbai battle, cool Parankunram became fit for the god of the victorious spear.","n":"The poem names the play-fight thumbai, the puram thinai of pitched battle, and gives it the full order of an army (elephant, horse, chariot, bow, arrow, disc) played with garlands, breastbands and balls: the war god's household quarrel is staged in war's own grammar, and the hill is said to befit him for that very reason. Lines 61 to 66 move from the fray to bathing, music and dance, perhaps the same women after the quarrel. Valli's hill kin take her side, so the co-wives' quarrel is also a quarrel of the hill against the sky.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","valli","thalam","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 9, lines 45-69","lb":"PR9:45-69","poet":"குறும்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"thumbai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["தார்","தார்",45,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",45,"maram","ornament","chaplet"],["கோதை","கோதை",47,"maram","ornament","garland"],["களிற்று","களிறு",50,"maa","scene","bull elephant"],["புரவி","புரவி",51,"maa","scene","horse"],["மயில்","மயில்",56,"pul","scene","peacock"],["சுனையுள்","சுனை",61,"pira","scene","pool"],["வண்டாய்","வண்டு",63,"pira","scene","bee"],["நரம்பு","நரம்பு",63,"yaazh","scene","lute strings"],["மயிலாய்","மயில்",64,"pul","scene","peacock"],["கோகுலமாய்","கோகுலம்",65,"pul","scene","cuckoo"],["குன்றவர்","குன்றவர்",67,"seythi","scene","hill people"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்று",69]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:8"},"pr:9:70-85":{"book":"paripadal","num":50,"t":["கடுஞ் சூர் மா முதல் தடிந்து அறுத்த வேல்","அடும் போராள! நின் குன்றின்மிசை","ஆடல் நவின்றோர் அவர் போர் செறுப்பவும்,","பாடல் பயின்றோரைப் பாணர் செறுப்பவும்,","வல்லாரை வல்லார் செறுப்பவும்,","அல்லாரை அல்லார் செறுப்பவும், ஓர் சொல்லாய்,","செம்மைப் புதுப் புனற்","தடாகம் ஏற்ற தண் சுனைப் பாங்கர்,","படாகை நின்றன்று;","மேஎ எஃகினவை;","வென்று உயர்த்த கொடி விறல் சான்றவை;","கற்பு இணை நெறியூடு அற்பு இணைக் கிழமை","நயத் தகு மரபின் வியத் தகு குமர!","வாழ்த்தினேம் பரவுதும், தாழ்த்துத் தலை, நினை யாம்","நயத்தலின் சிறந்த எம் அடியுறை,","பயத்தலின் சிறக்க, நாள்தொறும் பொலிந்தே"],"p":"","r":"Addressing the warrior whose spear cut down fierce Sur at the root of his great mango form, the poet says that on the god's hill dancers outdo dancers, panar outdo those practised in song, the skilled the skilled and the unskilled the unskilled, and, as if at one word, a banner stands beside the cool pool that has taken in the fresh red flood, with spears and with victory flags raised high that hold the worth of valour; hailing the wondrous Kumara of a lineage worth loving, lord of the bond of love along the path of the bond of karpu, he says: we have praised you and with bowed heads worship you; may our service at your feet, which excels in our love for you, excel in its fruit and flourish day by day.","n":"Line 81 sets the god's two unions in one phrase, the bond of karpu and the bond of love, which closes the argument of lines 12 to 26: the god who holds both is the model of the order the poet taught the Vedic scholars. The contest of like with like (lines 72 to 75) mirrors the co-wives' quarrel: on this hill rivalry itself keeps its proper pairs. Line 79 is a repaired reading and its sense is thin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","karpu","kalaviyal","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 9, lines 70-85","lb":"PR9:70-85","poet":"குறும்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பாணர்","பாணர்",73,"seythi","scene","bards"],["புனற்","புனல்",76,"pira","scene","flood"],["சுனைப்","சுனை",77,"pira","scene","pool"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:8"},"pr:10:1-8":{"book":"paripadal","num":51,"t":["மலைவரை மாலை அழி பெயல்காலை,","செல வரை காணாக் கடல்தலைக் கூட","நில வரை அல்லல் நிழத்த, விரிந்த","பலவுறு போர்வைப் பரு மணல் மூஉய்,","வரி அரி ஆணு முகிழ் விரி சினைய","மாந் தீம் தளிரொடு வாழையிலை மயக்கி,","ஆய்ந்து அளவா ஓசை அறையூஉப், பறை அறையப்","போந்ததுவையைப் புனல்."],"p":"","r":"When the rain has fallen on the mountain range in the evening and run on to join the sea whose bounds no one sees, easing the land's distress, the Vaigai's flood arrives, spreading its coarse sand like a many-folded coverlet, mixing tender striped mango shoots with plantain leaves, its sound beyond measure while drums are beaten to announce it.","n":"The flood is given a herald: பறை அறைய in line 7 is the drum that tells the town the water has come, so the river enters the poem like a king entering a city. மாலை in line 1 may be 'evening' or 'the range' (the hills as a garland); the reading takes the hour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","punalaadal","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 1-8","lb":"PR10:1-8","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["பெயல்காலை","பெயல்",1,"pira","scene","rain"],["கடல்தலைக்","கடல்",2,"pira","scene","sea"],["மணல்","மணல்",4,"pira","scene","sand"],["மாந்","மா",6,"maram","scene","mango"],["வாழையிலை","வாழை",6,"maram","scene","plantain"],["பறை","பறை",7,"parai","scene","drum"],["புனல்","புனல்",8,"pira","scene","flood water"]],"en":[["river","வையை",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:9-18":{"book":"paripadal","num":52,"t":["புனல் மண்டி ஆடல் புரிவான், சனம் மண்டி,","தாளித நொய்ந் நூல் சரணத்தர், மேகலை","ஏணிப்படுகால் இறுகிறுகத் தாள் இடீஇ,","நெய்த்தோர் நிற அரக்கின் நீரெக்கி யாவையும்","முத்து நீர்ச் சாந்து அடைந்த மூஉய்த் தத்தி;","புக அரும் பொங்குஉளைப் புள் இயல் மாவும்,","மிக வரினும் மீது இனிய வேழப் பிணவும்,","அகவரும் பாண்டியும், அத்திரியும், ஆய் மாச்","சகடமும், தண்டு ஆர் சிவிகையும், பண்ணி;","வகை வகை ஊழ் ஊழ் கதழ்பு மூழ்த்து ஏறி;"],"p":"","r":"To play in the flood the people crowd out, with fine threads tied at their feet and girdles of many strands pulled tight, carrying syringes of blood-red lac water and pearl-water paste, and they harness plumed horses swift as birds, she-elephants sweet to ride even when the water runs high, carts, mules, fine bullock carts and pole palanquins, and mount them in turn, hurrying in rows.","n":"The preparations are for a water-fight as much as a bath: நீரெக்கி (line 12) are squirts filled with red lac water, the festival weapon that returns in the play of lines 99 to 110.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 9-18","lb":"PR10:9-18","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["அரக்கின்","அரக்கு",12,"pira","ornament","lac dye"],["முத்து","முத்து",13,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["சாந்து","சாந்து",13,"pira","ornament","paste"],["மாவும்","மா",14,"maa","scene","horse"],["வேழப்","வேழம்",15,"maa","scene","elephant"],["அத்திரியும்","அத்திரி",16,"maa","scene","mule"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:19-40":{"book":"paripadal","num":53,"t":["முதியர், இளையர்: முகைப் பருவத்தர்,","வதி மண வம்பு அலர் வாய் அவிழ்ந்தன்னார்","இரு திரு மாந்தரும் இன்னினியோரும்","விரவு நரையோரும் வெறு நரையோரும்","பதிவத மாதர், பரத்தையர்; பாங்கர்;","அதிர் குரல் வித்தகர் ஆக்கிய தாள","விதி கூட்டிய இய மென் நடை போல,","பதி எதிர் சென்று, பரூஉக் கரை நண்ணி","நீர் அணி காண்போர்; நிரை மாடம் ஊர்குவோர்;","பேர் அணி நிற்போர்; பெரும் பூசல் தாக்குவோர்;","மா மலி ஊர்வோர்; வயப் பிடி உந்துவோர்;","வீ மலி கான் யாற்றின் துருத்தி குறுகி,","தாம் வீழ்வார் ஆகம் தழுவுவோர்; தழுவு எதிராது,","யாமக் குறை ஊடல் இன் நசைத் தேன் நுகர்வோர்;","காமக் கணிச்சியால் கையறவு வட்டித்து,","சேமத் திரை வீழ்த்து சென்று, அமளி சேர்குவோர்:","தாம் வேண்டு காதற் கணவர் எதிர்ப்பட,","பூ மேம்பாடு உற்ற புனை சுரும்பின், சேம","மட நடைப் பாட்டியர்த் தப்பி, தடை இறந்து,","தாம் வேண்டும் பட்டினம் எய்திக் கரை சேரும்","ஏழுறு நாவாய் வரவு எதிர்கொள்வார்போல்,","யாம் வேண்டும் வையைப் புனல் எதிர்கொள் கூடல்"],"p":"","r":"Old and young, girls in the bud, new brides like flowers just opened, the rich, the dear ones, the half-grey and the white-haired, chaste wives, courtesans and companions all go from the town to the wide bank in a soft pace like music set to the drummers' beat, and there some watch the water, some ride the floats, some fight, ride horses or drive she-elephants, while near the flowered island some embrace the ones they love, some refuse the embrace and taste the honey of a sulk at night's end, and some, with love as the pick that breaks resistance, drop the screen and go to bed; and Koodal receives the flood we long for as women who escape their slow old guardians to meet their husbands, as men at a port receive the ship that reaches shore.","n":"The list of bathers in line 23 sets பதிவத மாதர் and பரத்தையர் side by side, chaste wives and courtesans in one festival crowd, which the akam poems never do. The closing simile makes the whole city a lover: Koodal meets its flood as a wife meets the husband she has waited for.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","oodal","punalaadal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 19-40","lb":"PR10:19-40","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["வம்பு","வம்பு",20,"maram","simile","fresh flower"],["மா","மா",29,"maa","scene","horse"],["பிடி","பிடி",29,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["வீ","வீ",30,"maram","scene","flowers"],["தேன்","தேன்",32,"pira","simile","honey"],["சுரும்பின்","சுரும்பு",36,"pira","scene","bee"],["நாவாய்","நாவாய்",39,"pira","simile","ship"]],"en":[["river","வையை",40],["town","கூடல்",40]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:41-62":{"book":"paripadal","num":54,"t":["ஆங்க அணி நிலை மாடத்து அணி நின்ற பாங்காம்","மடப் பிடி கண்டு, வயக் கரி மால் உற்று,","நலத்த நடவாது நிற்ப; மடப் பிடி,","அன்னம் அனையாரோடு ஆயா நடை, கரிமேல்","செல் மனம் மால் உறுப்ப, சென்று; எழில் மாடத்துக்","கை புனை கிளர் வேங்கை காணிய வெருவுற்று,","மை புரை மடப் பிடி, மட நல்லார் விதிர்ப்புற,","செய் தொழில் கொள்ளாது, மதி செத்துச் சிதைதர;","கூம் கை மத மாக் கொடுந் தோட்டி கைந் நீவி","நீங்கும் பதத்தால், உருமுப் பெயர்த்தந்து","வாங்கி, முயங்கி வயப் பிடி கால்கோத்து,","சிறந்தார் நடுக்கம் சிறந்தார் களையல்","இதையும் களிறும் பிணையும் இரியச்","சிதையும் கலத்தைப் பயினான் திருத்தும்","திசை அறி நீகானும் போன்ம்","பருக் கோட்டு யாழ்ப் பக்கம் பாடலோடு ஆடல்","அருப்பம் அழிப்ப, அழிந்த மனக் கோட்டையர்,","ஒன்றோடு இரண்டா முன்தேறார், வென்றியின்,","பல் சனம் நாணிப் பதைபதைப்புமன்னவர்","தண்டம் இரண்டும் தலைஇத் தாக்கி நின்றவை","ஒன்றியும், உடம்பாடு ஒலி எழுதற்கு அஞ்சி,","நின்ற நிகழ்ச்சியும் போன்ம்."],"p":"","r":"A strong tusker sees a gentle she-elephant standing by an ornamented mansion, falls into longing and will not walk; she, going toward him with her heart on him, sees the tiger painted on the mansion wall, takes fright, ignores her driver and bolts while the women on her tremble, and the tusker shakes off his goad, trumpets like thunder, turns back, embraces her and twines his legs with hers, the beloved ending the beloved's fear like a pilot who knows the quarters mending with resin a ship whose sail and gear are giving way; then lovers whose fortified hearts the lute, song and dance have broken stand ashamed before the crowd, unable to choose, like two kings' armies that met and now stand joined, afraid to sound the cry of truce.","n":"This is not iraicchi in the brief's sense: line 52 states the sense outright (சிறந்தார் நடுக்கம் சிறந்தார் களையல்) and two explicit similes follow, so the elephants are the subject and the deletion test fails. It is the akam elephant-pair image with its meaning spelled out. The fright is caused by a painted tiger, art mistaken for a beast. Lines 53 and 56 to 62 are compressed; what இதை, களிறு and பிணை name on the ship (sail and fittings) and who the shamed ones of lines 57 to 59 are is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","anbu","acham","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 41-62","lb":"PR10:41-62","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","விலங்கு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பிடி","பிடி",42,"maa","simile","she-elephant"],["கரி","கரி",42,"maa","simile","tusker"],["அன்னம்","அன்னம்",44,"pul","simile","swan"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",46,"maa","scene","tiger (painted)"],["தோட்டி","தோட்டி",49,"seythi","scene","goad"],["பயினான்","பயின்",54,"pira","simile","resin"],["நீகானும்","நீகான்",55,"seythi","simile","ship's pilot"],["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",56,"yaazh","scene","lute"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:63-70":{"book":"paripadal","num":55,"t":["காமம் கனைந்து எழ, கண்ணின் களி எழ,","ஊர் மன்னும் அஞ்சி ஒளிப்பாரவர் நிலை","கள்ளின் களி எழக் காத்தாங்கு, அலர் அஞ்சி,","உள்ளம் உளை எழ, ஊக்கத்தான் உள் உள்","பரப்பி மதர் நடுக்கிப் பார் அலர் தூற்றக்","கரப்பார், களி மதரும் போன்ம்.","கள்ளொடு காமம் கலந்து, கரை வாங்கும்","வெள்ளம் தரும், இப் புனல்"],"p":"","r":"As love surges and joy shows in the eyes, those who hide, fearing even the town's ruler, are like drinkers who guard their drunkenness: dreading gossip, hearts in turmoil, they suppress their high spirits while the world spreads talk, and so this flood brings toddy mixed with love that tears away the banks.","n":"Love and toddy are joined twice, first as simile (the lover hides his joy as the drinker hides his drink) and then as the flood's own cargo (கள்ளொடு காமம் கலந்து). ஊர் மன்னும் (line 64) may be the town's king or simply 'the town' with a particle; the fear is in any case of being seen.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["alar","kaamam","kal","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 63-70","lb":"PR10:63-70","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","இறை"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கள்ளின்","கள்",65,"unavu","simile","toddy"],["கள்ளொடு","கள்",69,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["வெள்ளம்","வெள்ளம்",70,"pira","scene","flood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:71-88":{"book":"paripadal","num":56,"t":["புனல் பொருது மெலிந்தார் திமில் விட,","கனல் பொருத அகிலின் ஆவி கா எழ,","நகில் முகடு மெழுகிய அளறு மடை திறந்து","திகை முழுது கமழ, முகில் அகடு கழி மதியின்","உறை கழி வள்ளத்து உறு நறவு வாக்குநர்,","அரவு செறி உவவு மதியென அங்கையில் தாங்கி,","ஏறி மகர வலயம் அணி திகழ் நுதலியர்,","மதி உண் அரமகளென, ஆம்பல் வாய் மடுப்ப;","மீப்பால் வெண் துகில் போர்க்குநர்; பூப் பால்","வெண் துகில் சூழ்ப்பக் குழல் முறுக்குநர்;","செங் குங்குமச் செழுஞ் சேறு,","பங்கம் செய் அகில் பல பளிதம்,","மறுகுபட அறை புரை அறு குழவியின்","அவி அமர் அழலென அரைக்குநர்;","நத்தொடு, நள்ளி, நடை இறவு, வய வாளை,","வித்தி அலையில், ‘விளைக! பொலிக! என்பார்;","இல்லது நோக்கி, இளிவரவு கூறாமுன்,","நல்லது வெஃகி, வினை செய்வார்;"],"p":"","r":"Tired from fighting the water, bathers leave their rafts; the smoke of burnt agil rises in the grove and the paste on women's breasts runs off like an opened sluice so that every quarter smells of it; some pour toddy from a cup uncovered like the moon slipping out of a cloud, and women with makara bands on their bright foreheads hold it in their palms like the full moon caught by the serpent and drink with lily mouths like celestial women drinking the moon; some wrap white silk, some twist their hair, some grind red saffron, agil and camphor into a paste red as sacrificial fire; some release snails, crabs, prawns and vaalai fish into the waves crying 'grow and flourish', and some, looking to what they lack, do good works in hope before they would ever speak the shame of asking.","n":"Lines 85 to 88 are the rite inside the play: creatures are given back to the river with a blessing, and wishes are made by deeds instead of by asking, so that இளிவரவு, the humiliation the Kural attaches to begging, is kept out even of prayer. Several similes of lines 73 to 84 (the serpent-seized moon, the moon-drinking women, the oblation fire) are compressed and their exact tenors are a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","kal","vazhipaadu","maanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 71-88","lb":"PR10:71-88","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["திமில்","திமில்",71,"pira","scene","raft"],["அகிலின்","அகில்",72,"maram","scene","eaglewood"],["நறவு","நறவு",75,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",78,"maram","simile","water-lily"],["குங்குமச்","குங்குமம்",81,"maram","ornament","saffron"],["அகில்","அகில்",82,"maram","ornament","eaglewood paste"],["நத்தொடு","நத்து",85,"pira","scene","snail"],["நள்ளி","நள்ளி",85,"pira","scene","crab"],["இறவு","இறவு",85,"pira","scene","prawn"],["வாளை","வாளை",85,"pira","scene","vaalai fish"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:89-98":{"book":"paripadal","num":57,"t":["மண் ஆர் மணியின் வணர் குரல் வண்டு ஆர்ப்ப,","தண் அம் துவர் பல ஊட்டிச் சலம் குடைவார்;","எண்ணெய் கழல இழை துகள் பிசைவார்;","மாலையும் சாந்தும் மதமும் இழைகளும்,","கோலம் கொள, நீர்க்குக் கூட்டுவார்; அப் புனல்","உண்ணா நறவினை ஊட்டுவார்; ஒண் தொடியார்","வண்ணம் தெளிர, முகமும் வளர் முலைக்","ஆட்டு அயர்ந்துஅரி படும் ஐ விரை மாண் பகழி","அரம் தின் வாய் போன்ம் போன்ம் போன்ம்","பின்னும், மலர்க் கண் புனல்"],"p":"","r":"While bees hum with a curving voice like a cast bell, women steep cool red dyes and plunge into the water, knead ornament-dust to wash off oil, add garlands, sandal, musk and jewels to the water so that it is adorned, and feed the flood a toddy it cannot drink; the bright-bangled women's colour gleams, and after the bathing their eyes, streaked red, look like the file-sharpened tips of fine arrows, and so does the flower-eyed flood.","n":"The bathers treat the river as a guest or a lover to be dressed and given drink: the water is garlanded, scented and offered toddy (உண்ணா நறவினை ஊட்டுவார்). Line 97 repeats போன்ம் three times and the sense of lines 95 to 98 is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","azhagu","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 89-98","lb":"PR10:89-98","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",89,"pira","scene","bee"],["துவர்","துவர்",90,"pira","ornament","red dye"],["மாலையும்","மாலை",92,"maram","ornament","garland"],["சாந்தும்","சாந்து",92,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["மதமும்","மதம்",92,"pira","ornament","musk"],["நறவினை","நறவு",94,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:99-110":{"book":"paripadal","num":58,"t":["தண்டித் தண்டின் தாய்ச் செல்வாரும்,","கண்டல் தண் தாது திரை நுரை தூவாரும்,","வெய்ய திமிலின் விரை புனலோடு ஓய்வாரும்,","மெய்யது உழவின் எதிர் புனல் மாறு ஆடிப்","பைய விளையாடுவாரும், மென் பாவையர்","செய்த பூஞ் சிற்றடிசில் இட்டு உண்ண ஏற்பார்,","இடுவார் மறுப்பார் சிறுகு இடையார்","பந்தும் கழங்கும் பல களவு கொண்டு ஓடி,","அம் தண் கரை நின்று பாய்வாராய், மைந்தர்","ஒளிறு இலங்கு எஃகொடு வாள் மாறு உழக்கி,","களிறு போர் உற்ற களம்போல, நாளும்","தெளிவு இன்று, தீம் நீர்ப் புனல்."],"p":"","r":"Some push across with poles, some scatter the cool kandal pollen and the foam of the waves, some rest with rafts in the swift water, some swim slowly against the current, some take and eat the little flower-rice the soft girls cooked as the givers offer and refuse, slender girls snatch balls and kazhangu and run to leap from the bank, and young men clash with gleaming spears and swords, so that, like a field where elephants have fought, the sweet water of the flood is never clear, day after day.","n":"The simile turns the festival into a battle: the clear river is muddied as a war-field is trampled by elephants, and the young men's mock fight with weapons belongs to the same picture. The kandal is a seashore plant, out of place in the river scene.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 99-110","lb":"PR10:99-110","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"],["nakai","இளமை"]],"k":[["கண்டல்","கண்டல்",100,"maram","scene","screw pine"],["திமிலின்","திமில்",101,"pira","scene","raft"],["சிற்றடிசில்","அடிசில்",104,"unavu","gift_food","toy rice"],["களிறு","களிறு",109,"maa","simile","tusker"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:111-124":{"book":"paripadal","num":59,"t":["மதி மாலை மால் இருள் கால் சீப்ப, கூடல்","வதி மாலை, மாறும் தொழிலான், புது மாலை","நாள் அணி நீக்கி, நகை மாலைப் பூ வேய்ந்து,","தோள் அணி, தோடு, சுடர் இழை, நித்திலம்;","பாடுவார் பாடல், பரவல், பழிச்சுதல்,","ஆடுவார் ஆடல், அமர்ந்த சீர்ப் பாணி,","நல்ல கமழ் தேன் அளி வழக்கம், எல்லாமும்,","பண் தொடர் வண்டு பரிய எதிர் வந்து ஊத,","கொண்டிய வண்டு கதுப்பின் குரல் ஊத,","தென் திசை நோக்கித் திரிதர்வாய்; மண்டு கால் சார்வா,","நளிர் மலைப் பூங்கொடித் தங்குபு உகக்கும்","பனி வளர் ஆவியும் போன்ம், மணி மாடத்து","உள் நின்று தூய பனிநீருடன் கலந்து,","கால் திரிய ஆர்க்கும் புகை"],"p":"","r":"When the evening wind under the moon sweeps the dark away, the people change their dress at the settling evening in Koodal, put off the day's ornaments and put on fresh garlands of opened flowers, armlets, earrings, bright jewels and pearls; there are singers' songs, praise and hymns, dancers' dances to the measured beat, and the fragrant honey and bees, all of it, as bees that follow the melody hum against it and bees settled in the hair hum in tune; and as you turn toward the south, the smoke from inside the gemmed mansions, mixed with pure scented water, rises with the turning wind like the mist that rises over the flowering creepers of a cold hill.","n":"The poet turns to address the river (திரிதர்வாய், line 120) as it flows on south, so the town's evening is seen as what the river leaves behind. The closing simile brings a hill's mist into the city.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","punalaadal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 111-124","lb":"PR10:111-124","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["பூ","பூ",113,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["நித்திலம்","நித்திலம்",114,"pira","ornament","pearls"],["தேன்","தேன்",117,"pira","scene","honey"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",118,"pira","scene","bee"],["பூங்கொடித்","பூங்கொடி",121,"maram","simile","flowering creeper"],["பனிநீருடன்","பனிநீர்",123,"pira","scene","scented water"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",111]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:10:125-130":{"book":"paripadal","num":60,"t":["இலம்படு புலவர் ஏற்ற கை ஞெமரப்","பொலம் சொரி வழுதியின், புனல் இறை பரப்பி,","செய்யில் பொலம் பரப்பும் செய் வினை ஓயற்க","வருந்தாது வரும் புனல் விருந்து அயர் கூடல்,","அருங் கறை அறை இசை வயிரியர், உரிமை","ஒருங்கு அமர் ஆயமொடு, ஏத்தினர் தொழவே"],"p":"","r":"Like the Vazhuthi who pours gold until the outstretched hands of poets in want are full, the flood spreads its water and pours gold on the fields, and may that work never cease, says Koodal, which without strain feasts the flood that comes as a guest, while musicians and the household companions together praise it and bow.","n":"The usual direction of Tamil praise is reversed: kings are likened to rain and river, but here the river is likened to the king, so the Pandya's giving is the fixed measure and nature is measured against it. The city treats the flood as a guest (விருந்து அயர் கூடல்). அருங் கறை in line 129 is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["eegai","virundhu","vendhan","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 10, lines 125-130","lb":"PR10:125-130","poet":"கரும்பிள்ளைப் பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"],["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["புனல்","புனல்",126,"pira","scene","flood water"],["வயிரியர்","வயிரியர்",129,"seythi","scene","musicians"]],"en":[["king","வழுதி",126],["town","கூடல்",128]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:9"},"pr:11:1-15":{"book":"paripadal","num":61,"t":["‘விரி கதிர் மதியமொடு, வியல் விசும்பு, புணர்ப்ப,","எரி, சடை, எழில் வேழம், தலையெனக் கீழ் இருந்து,","தெரு இடைப்படுத்த மூன்று ஒன்பதிற்று இருக்கையுள்","உருகெழு வெள்ளி வந்து ஏற்றியல் சேர,","வருடையைப் படிமகன் வாய்ப்ப, பொருள் தெரி","புந்தி மிதுனம் பொருந்த, புலர் விடியல்","அங்கி உயர் நிற்ப, அந்தணன் பங்குவின்","இல்லத் துணைக்கு உப்பால் எய்த, இறை யமன்","வில்லின் கடை மகரம் மேவ, பாம்பு ஒல்லை","மதியம் மறைய, வரு நாளில்வாய்ந்த","பொதியில் முனிவன் புரை வரைக் கீறி","மிதுனம் அடைய, விரி கதிர் வேனில்","எதிர் வரவு மாரி இயைக என இவ் ஆற்றால்","புரை கெழு சையம் பொழி மழை தாழ,","நெரிதரூஉம் வையைப் புனல்"],"p":"","r":"The poet fixes the hour of the flood by the sky: with the moon among the twenty-seven stars that the fire-star, the matted one and the elephant head, Venus come to the Bull, the earth's son to the Ram, Mercury joined to the Twins, the fire high at dawn, the other planets at their stations by the Bow and Capricorn, the serpent swiftly hiding the moon, and the star of the sage of Pothiyil reaching the Twins, so that rain might come to meet the blazing summer; and by this course the rain falls low on the lofty Sahya and the Vaigai's flood comes crowding down.","n":"The song opens not with a god but with a dated sky: the river's coming is placed inside the order of stars and planets, as if the flood were a festival with its own horoscope. Which planet each name in lines 7 to 9 stands for (அந்தணன், பங்கு, இறை யமன்) is a reading, not a certainty, and the eclipse of line 9 (பாம்பு ... மதியம் மறைய) may be a fixed event or a figure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 1-15","lb":"PR11:1-15","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மாரி","மாரி",13,"pira","scene","rain"],["மழை","மழை",14,"pira","scene","rain"],["புனல்","புனல்",15,"pira","scene","flood"]],"en":[["hill","பொதியில்",11],["hill","சையம்",14],["river","வையைப்",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:16-30":{"book":"paripadal","num":62,"t":["‘வரையன புன்னாகமும்,","கரையன சுரபுன்னையும்,","வண்டு அறைஇய சண்பக நிரை, தண் பதம்","மனைமாமரம், வாள்வீரம்,","சினை வளர் வேங்கை, கணவிரி காந்தள்,","தாய தோன்றி தீயென மலரா,","ஊதை அவிழ்த்த உடை இதழ் ஒள் நீலம்,","வேய் பயில் சோலை அருவி தூர்த்தரப்","பாய் திரை உந்தித் தருதலான்ஆய் கோல்","வயவர் அரி மலர்த் துறை என்கோ?","அரி மலர் மீப் போர்வை, ஆரம் தாழ் மார்பின்,","திரை நுரை மென் பொகுட்டுத் தேம் மணச் சாந்தின்","அரிவையது தானை என்கோ? கள் உண்ணூஉப்","பருகு படி மிடறு என்கோ?பெரிய","திருமருத நீர்ப் பூந் துறை.‘"],"p":"","r":"The flood brings down the punnai of the hills and the surapunnai of the banks, rows of champak hummed over by bees, the house mango, the vaal veeram, the branching vengai, the wide-opened kaanthal, thondri blooming like fire and bright blue lilies opened by the wind, choking the falls of the bamboo groves and driving them on its leaping waves, so that the poet asks whether to call the great Thirumarudam ford a ford of flowers cut by the reapers, a woman's garment with flowers for its upper cloth, foam for its pearl-hung breast and fragrant paste for its sandal, or the throat of a drinker gulping toddy.","n":"The three 'shall I call it' questions (என்கோ, lines 25 to 29) give the same ford three bodies: worked land, a dressed woman and a drunk man, so the flood is at once harvest, ornament and intoxication. ஆய் கோல் வயவர் (lines 24, 25) may be reapers or warriors; the sense of the first figure is not secure. The flowers are mostly of the hill, carried down into the town's ford.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 16-30","lb":"PR11:16-30","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["புன்னாகமும்","புன்னாகம்",16,"maram","scene","punnai"],["சுரபுன்னையும்","சுரபுன்னை",17,"maram","scene","surapunnai"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",18,"pira","scene","bee"],["சண்பக","சண்பகம்",18,"maram","scene","champak"],["மனைமாமரம்","மனை மாமரம்",19,"maram","scene","mango"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",20,"maram","scene","vengai"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",20,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",21,"maram","simile","glory lily"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",22,"maram","scene","blue lily"],["வேய்","வேய்",23,"maram","scene","bamboo"],["அருவி","அருவி",23,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:31-45":{"book":"paripadal","num":63,"t":["‘ஆம் நாள் நிறை மதி அலர்தரு பக்கம் போல்,","நாளின், நாளின், நளி வரைச் சிலம்பு தொட்டு,","நிலவுப் பரந்தாங்கு, நீர் நிலம் பரப்பி,","உலகு பயம் பகர; ஓம்பு பெரும் பக்கம்","வழியது பக்கத்து அமரர் உண்டி","மதி நிறைவு அழிவதின், வரவு சுருங்க;","எண் மதி நிறை, உவா இருள் மதி போல","நாள் குறைபடுதல் காணுநர் யாரே?","சேண் இகந்து கல் ஊர்ந்த மாண் இழை வையை!","வயத் தணிந்து ஏகு, நின் யாணர் இறு நாள் பெற!","மா மயில் அன்னார், மறையில் புணர் மைந்தர்,","காமம் கள விட்டு, கைகொள் கற்பு உற்றென,","மல்லல் புனல் வையை! மா மலை விட்டு, இருத்தல்","இல்லத்து நீ தனிச் சேறல் இளிவரல்:‘","என ஆங்கு"],"p":"","r":"Like the waxing fortnight of the full moon the flood grows day by day, touches the slopes of the thick hills and spreads over the land as moonlight spreads, so that the world yields its fruit; then, as the moon's fullness wastes in the fortnight when the gods take their food from it, the flow shrinks, and who notices the days run down like the moon of the dark new-moon after its fullness? So the poet asks the jewelled Vaigai, who came from afar riding over stones, to go gently that her plenty may last its full term, and tells her that, as peacock-like women who joined young men in secret leave stealth and take up chastity, for her to leave the great mountain and go alone to a house would be a shame.","n":"The river is spoken to as a bride: the move from களவு to கற்பு (line 42) is the passage she is making from the hill to the plain, and the poet's counsel is that she not go alone, that is, that her flood go with its full train. The word இளிவரல் (line 44) is a judgment of shame, not the meyppadu of that name, whose causes are age and illness. The run of lines 41 to 44 is compressed and its exact construction is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","karpu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 31-45","lb":"PR11:31-45","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மயில்","மயில்",41,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[["river","வையை",39],["river","வையை",43]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:46-61":{"book":"paripadal","num":64,"t":["கடை அழிய நீண்டு அகன்ற கண்ணாளைக் காளை","படையொடும் கொண்டு பெயர்வானைச் சுற்றம்","இடை நெறித் தாக்குற்றது ஏய்ப்ப, அடல் மதுரை","ஆடற்கு நீர் அமைந்தது, யாறு","ஆற்று அணி, வெள் வாள் விதிர்ப்போர், மிளிர் குந்தம் ஏந்துவோர்,","கொள்வார் கோல் கொள்ளக் கொடித் திண் தேர் ஏறுவோர்,","புள் ஏர் புரவி பொலம் படைக் கைம்மாவை","வெள்ள நீர் நீத்தத்துள் ஊர்பு ஊர்பு உழக்குநரும்,","கண் ஆரும் சாயற் கழித் துரப்போரை","வண்ண நீர் கரந்த வட்டு விட்டு எறிவோரும்,","மணம் வரு மாலையின் வட்டிப்போரைத்","துணி பிணர் மருப்பின் நீர் எக்குவோரும்,","தெரி கோதை நல்லார் தம் கேளிர்த் திளைக்கும்","உருகெமு தோற்றம் உரைக்குங்கால், நாளும்","பொரு களம் போலும் தகைத்தேபரி கவரும்","பாய் தேரான் வையை அகம்."],"p":"","r":"As when kinsmen fall on a youth in mid-road as he carries off with his band a girl whose long wide eyes outrun their corners, the flood strikes warlike Madurai and its water is fit for play: the river's array is men brandishing white swords and lifting gleaming lances, men mounting strong bannered chariots as the drivers take the goad, men who ride bird-swift horses and gold-trapped elephants again and again through the flood, men who throw balls hiding coloured water at those who chase with poles, and men who squirt water from rough horns at those who whirl fragrant garlands, so that, when one tells the fearsome sight of the well-garlanded women sporting with their lovers, the Vaigai's waters are every day like a field of battle.","n":"The water-sport is drawn entirely in the terms of war (swords, lances, chariots, horses, elephants, a battlefield), and the opening simile is itself an elopement broken by the girl's kin: the flood that breaks on the town is the pursuing family, and the town is the couple. Lines 54 to 57 (கழித் துரப்போரை, வட்டு, மருப்பின் நீர் எக்குவோரும்) name games whose exact form is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["inbam","udanpokku","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 46-61","lb":"PR11:46-61","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["புள்","புள்",52,"pul","simile","bird"],["புரவி","புரவி",52,"maa","scene","horse"],["கைம்மாவை","கைம்மா",52,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மாலையின்","மாலை",56,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கோதை","கோதை",58,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["town","மதுரை",48],["river","வையை",61]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:62-73":{"book":"paripadal","num":65,"t":["நீர் அணி வெறி செறி மலர் உறு கமழ் தண்","தார் வரை அகலத்து, அவ் ஏர் அணி நேர் இழை","ஒளி திகழ் தகை வகை செறி பொறி","புனை வினைப் பொலங் கோதையவரொடு,","பாகர் இறை வழை மது நுகர்பு, களி பரந்து,","நாகரின் நல் வள வினை வயவு ஏற நளி புணர்மார்,","காரிகை மது ஒருவரின் ஒருவர் கண்ணின் கவர்புற,","சீர் அமை பாடற் பயத்தால் கிளர் செவி தெவி,","உம்பர் உறையும் ஒளி கிளர் வான் ஊர்பு ஆடும்","அம்பி கரவா வழக்கிற்றே, ஆங்கு அதை","கார் ஒவ்வா வேனில் கலங்கித் தெளிவரல்,","நீர் ஒவ்வா வையை! நினக்கு."],"p":"","r":"Men with chests broad as hills, wearing cool garlands thick with fragrant water-flowers, go with women of fine jewels and worked gold garlands; they drink the honeyed wine, the elation spreads, and the townsmen press close to embrace; each draws the other's beauty in through the eyes, ears stirred by well-made song, on boats that ride like the bright cars of those who dwell above, a custom with nothing hidden; and so, O Vaigai, your turbid summer water and its clearing are not like the rains.","n":"The unit is one long run of attributes (lines 62 to 65 heap seven qualifiers on the women's garlands) closed by a plain address to the river. கரவா வழக்கிற்று (line 71), 'a custom that does not hide', makes the lovers' play public, the opposite of the secrecy of களவு in the earlier unit. Lines 66, 67 and 72, 73 are obscure in construction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","inbam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 62-73","lb":"PR11:62-73","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",62,"maram","ornament","flower"],["தார்","தார்",63,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கோதையவரொடு","கோதை",65,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மது","மது",66,"unavu","gift_food","wine"]],"en":[["river","வையை",73]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:74-92":{"book":"paripadal","num":66,"t":["கனைக்கும் அதிகுரல் கார் வானம் நீங்க,","பனிப் படு பைதல் விதலைப் பருவத்து,","ஞாயிறு காயா நளி மாரிப் பின் குளத்து,","மா இருந் திங்கள் மறு நிறை ஆதிரை","விரிநூல் அந்தணர் விழவு தொடங்க,","புரி நூல் அந்தணர் பொலம் கலம் ஏற்ப,","‘வெம்பாதாக, வியல் நில வரைப்பு!‘ என","அம்பா ஆடலின் ஆய் தொடிக் கன்னியர்,","முனித் துறை முதல்வியர் முறைமை காட்ட,","பனிப் புலர்பு ஆடி, பரு மணல் அருவியின்","ஊதை ஊர்தர, உறை சிறை வேதியர்","நெறி நிமிர் நுடங்கு அழல் பேணிய சிறப்பின்,","தையல் மகளிர் ஈர் அணி புலர்த்தர,","வையை! நினக்கு மடை வாய்த்தன்று.","மையாடல் ஆடல் மழ புலவர் மாறு எழுந்து,","பொய் ஆடல் ஆடும் புணர்ப்பின் அவர், அவர்","தீ எரிப் பாலும் செறி தவம் முன் பற்றியோ,","தாய் அருகா நின்று தவத் தைந் நீராடுதல்?","நீ உரைத்தி, வையை நதி!"],"p":"","r":"When the thundering rain clouds have gone and the cold dewy shivering season comes, after the heavy rains when the sun does not scorch, on the Aathirai of the full moon the learned brahmins begin the festival and thread-wearing brahmins receive the golden vessels; praying that the wide earth not be scorched, the maidens of fine bangles in the ambaa bath, shown the order of the rite by the elder women of the ford, bathe at the cold dawn, and as the wind blows over the sandy stream they dry their wet clothes at the leaning flame the vedic priests tend; so, O Vaigai, your offering was fitting. Then the poet asks the river: is it through hard penance done before, beside the fire, that these girls bathe in the Thai water standing near their mothers, while the young poets rise against one another in their contests?","n":"The bath is for the land, not (as later tradition has it) for a husband: the girls' stated prayer is வெம்பாதாக, வியல் நில வரைப்பு (line 80). Brahmin priests, elder women and mothers frame the maidens' rite, so the unmarried girls' bathing is a public act under guardians. Lines 88 and 89 (மையாடல் ஆடல் மழ புலவர் மாறு எழுந்து, பொய் ஆடல்) are obscure; the contest of the young poets is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theerththam","vazhipaadu","mazhai","thavam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 74-92","lb":"PR11:74-92","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",78,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["மணல்","மணல்",83,"pira","scene","sand"],["ஊதை","ஊதை",84,"pira","scene","cold wind"],["வேதியர்","வேதியர்",84,"seythi","scene","vedic priests"],["அழல்","அழல்",85,"pira","scene","fire"]],"en":[["river","வையை",87],["river","வையை",92]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:93-114":{"book":"paripadal","num":67,"t":["ஆயிடை, மா இதழ் கொண்டு, ஓர் மட மாதர் நோக்கினாள்,","வேய் எழில் வென்று வெறுத்த தோள்; நோக்கி,","சாய் குழை பிண்டித் தளிர் காதில், தையினாள்;","பாய் குழை நீலம் பகலாகத் தையினாள்;","‘குவளைக் குழைக்காதின் கோலச் செவியின்","இவள் செரீஇ, நான்கு விழி படைத்தாள்‘ என்று","நெற்றி விழியா நிறை திலகம் இட்டாளே,","கொற்றவை கோலம் கொண்டு, ஒர் பெண்","பவள வளை செறித்தாட் கண்டு, அணிந்தாள், பச்சைக்","குவளைப் பசுந் தண்டு கொண்டு.","கல்லகாரப் பூவால் கண்ணி தொடுத்தாளை,","‘நில்லிகா!‘ என்பாள்போல், நெய்தல் தொடுத்தாளே","மல்லிகா மாலை வளாய்","தண்டு தழுவா, தாவு நீர் வையையுள்,","கண்ட பொழுதில், கடும் புனல் கை வாங்க,","நெஞ்சம் அவள் வாங்க, நீடு புணை வாங்க,","நேரிழை நின்றுழிக் கண் நிற்ப, நீர் அவன்","தாழ்வுழி உய்யாது தான் வேண்டும் ஆறு உய்ப்ப;","ஆயத்துடன் நில்லாள் ஆங்கு அவன் பின் தொடரூஉ,","தாய் அத் திறம் அறியாள், தாங்கி, ‘தனிச் சேறல்;","ஆயத்தில் கூடு‘ எந்று அரற்றெடுப்பத் தாக்கிற்றே","சேய் உற்ற கார் நீர் வரவு."],"p":"","r":"On the bank the girls adorn one another in rivalry: one, her shoulders surpassing the bamboo, sets tender asoka shoots and a blue lily at her ears, so that it is said she has made herself four eyes; another answers with a tilaka like a forehead eye, taking the look of Kotravai; one, seeing a girl in coral bangles, puts on green lily stalks; and against one who strings a kallakaaram wreath another, as if saying 'stop!', winds neythal round a jasmine garland; then in the leaping Vaigai a youth loses hold of his float the moment he sees one of them, the strong current takes his hand, she takes his heart and the long raft is swept off, his eyes stay where she stands while the water carries him where it will, she leaves her companions to follow him, and her mother, not knowing why, holds her back crying 'don't go alone, keep with your group', as the new flood from far away comes striking down.","n":"Three pulls in one line (கை வாங்க, நெஞ்சம் அவள் வாங்க, புணை வாங்க, lines 107, 108): the river, the girl and the float each take something from him, so the flood that carries the body is the outward form of the look that carries the heart. The mother's தனிச் சேறல் (line 112) repeats the poet's word to the river in line 44: the girl and the river are warned in the same phrase. நில்லிகா and மல்லிகா (lines 104, 105) are a pun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalaviyal","kaamam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 93-114","lb":"PR11:93-114","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kaikkilai","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["வேய்","வேய்",94,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["பிண்டித்","பிண்டி",95,"maram","ornament","asoka"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",96,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["குவளைக்","குவளை",97,"maram","ornament","blue lily"],["பவள","பவளம்",101,"pira","ornament","coral"],["குவளைப்","குவளை",102,"maram","ornament","lily stalk"],["கல்லகாரப்","கல்லகாரம்",103,"maram","ornament","kallakaaram flower"],["நெய்தல்","நெய்தல்",104,"maram","ornament","white lily"],["மல்லிகா","மல்லிகை",105,"maram","ornament","jasmine"],["புனல்","புனல்",107,"pira","scene","flood"]],"en":[["river","வையையுள்",106]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:115-132":{"book":"paripadal","num":68,"t":["‘நீ தக்காய், தைந் நீர்! நிறம் தெளிந்தாய்‘ என்மாரும்,","‘ \"கழுத்து அமை கை வாங்காக் காதலர்ப் புல்ல,","விழுத் தகை பெறுக!\" என வேண்டுதும்‘ என்மாரும்,","‘பூ வீழ் அரியின் புலம்பப் போகாது,","யாம் வீழ்வார், ஏமம் எய்துக!‘ என்மாரும்,","‘ \"கிழவர் கிழவியர்\" என்னாது, ஏழ்காறும்,","மழ ஈன்று மல்லற் கேள் மன்னுக!\" என்மாரும்","‘கண்டார்க்குத் தாக்கு அணங்கு, இக் காரிகை; காண்மின்:","பண்டாரம், காமன் படை, உவள் கண்; காண்மின்:","நீல் நெய் தாழ் கோதையவர் விலக்க நில்லாது,","பூ ஊது வண்டினம் யாழ் கொண்ட கொளை கேண்மின்:","கொளைப் பொருள் தெரிதரக் கொளுத்தாமல், குரல் கொண்ட","கிளைக்கு உற்ற உழைச் சுரும்பின் கேழ் கெழு பாலை இசை ஒர்மின்:","பண் கண்டு திறன் எய்தாப் பண் தாளம் பெறப் பாடி,","கொண்ட இன் இசைத் தாளம் கொளை சீர்க்கும் விரித்து ஆடும்","தண் தும்பியினம் காண்மின்: தான் வீழ் பூ நெரித்தாளை","முனை கெழு சின நெஞ்சின் முன் எறிந்து, பின்னும்,","கனை வரல் ஒரு தும்பி காய் சினத்து இயல் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Some say 'you are worthy, Thai water, your colour has cleared'; some 'we pray that we may win the honour of embracing lovers whose arms never leave our necks'; some 'may those we love not leave us lonely as the bee leaves a fallen flower, but be our safe keeping'; some 'never saying old man and old woman, for seven births may our rich kin last, bearing children'; and the onlookers call out: this beauty is a goddess that strikes whoever sees her, her eyes are Kaaman's treasury and army; hear the swarm of bees that will not leave though the women with oiled lily garlands drive them off, holding a tune as on a lute; hear the bright paalai melody of the bees attending their kin; see the cool thumbis dancing to the beat; see the one thumbi in burning anger that dives first at the girl who crushed the flower it loved.","n":"The four prayers rise in scope: a clear river, a lover's embrace, a lover who will not leave, and a household that lasts seven births without growing old. The angry thumbi of lines 130 to 132 guards the flower it loves against the girl who crushed it; it could be read as a figure for the lover's jealousy, but no situation in the unit is anchored to it, so it is left as scene. The music terms of lines 126 to 129 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","anangu","piravi","makkal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 115-132","lb":"PR11:115-132","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["accham","அணங்கு"],["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["அரியின்","அரி",118,"pira","simile","bee"],["கோதையவர்","கோதை",124,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",125,"pira","scene","bees"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",125,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["சுரும்பின்","சுரும்பு",127,"pira","scene","bee"],["பாலை","பாலை",127,"yaazh","scene","paalai mode"],["தும்பியினம்","தும்பி",130,"pira","scene","dragonflies"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",132,"pira","scene","dragonfly"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:11:133-140":{"book":"paripadal","num":69,"t":["என ஆங்கு","இன்ன பண்பின் நின் தைந் நீராடல்","மின் இழை நறு நுதல் மகள் மேம்பட்ட","கன்னிமை கனியாக் கைக்கிளைக் காம","இன் இயல் மாண் தேர்ச்சி இசை பரிபாடல்","முன் முறை செய் தவத்தின் இம் முறை இயைந்தேம்;","மறு முறை அமையத்தும் இயைக!","நறு நீர் வையை நயத் தகு நிறையே!"],"p":"","r":"So the poet closes: through penance done before, we have come together this time in your Thai bathing of such a kind, this sweet, finely wrought paripadal song of the kaikkilai love of a maiden of shining jewels and fragrant brow whose maidenhood has not ripened; may we come together again at the next time too, O fragrant-watered Vaigai, fulness worth desiring.","n":"The song names its own genre and theme in lines 136 and 137: கைக்கிளைக் காம(ம்) of a maiden whose maidenhood has not ripened, which fits the broken-off look of lines 93 to 114. இம் முறை ... மறு முறை (lines 138, 139) can be this festival and the next, or this birth and the next; the merit of earlier penance (முன் முறை செய் தவம்) favours the larger reading but does not fix it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam","theerththam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 11, lines 133-140","lb":"PR11:133-140","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[["river","வையை",140]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:10"},"pr:12:1-8":{"book":"paripadal","num":70,"t":["வளி பொரு மின்னொடு வான் இருள் பரப்பி,","விளிவு இன்று, கிளையொடு மெல் மலை முற்றி,","தளி பொழி சாரல் ததர் மலர் தாஅய்;","ஒளி திகழ் உத்தி உருகெழு நாகம்,","அகரு, வழை, ஞெமை, ஆரம், இனைய","தகரமும், ஞாழ்லும், தாரமும், தாங்கி,","நளி கடல் முன்னியது போலும், தீம் நீர்","வளி வரல் வையை வரவு."],"p":"","r":"Clouds lit by wind-struck lightning spread darkness over the sky and close round the soft hills without pause, the rain-washed slopes shed their flowers, and the Vaiyai comes down carrying the bright-hooded cobra, eaglewood, vazhai, gnemai, sandal and the fragrant thagaram, gnaazhal and deodar, its sweet water and wind seeming to make for the full sea.","n":"The song opens not at Madurai but at the source: the river's load is a list of hill things (kurinji trees and a hill snake), so the flood that reaches the town in line 9 arrives as the hills' gift. The simile of line 7 gives the river a will (முன்னியது, 'aimed at'): it seems bound for the sea, though the town will claim it first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 12, lines 1-8","lb":"PR12:1-8","poet":"நல்வழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",3,"maram","scene","flower"],["நாகம்","நாகம்",4,"pira","scene","cobra"],["அகரு","அகரு",5,"maram","scene","eaglewood"],["வழை","வழை",5,"maram","scene",""],["ஞெமை","ஞெமை",5,"maram","scene",""],["ஆரம்","ஆரம்",5,"maram","scene","sandal tree"],["தகரமும்","தகரம்",6,"maram","scene",""],["ஞாழ்லும்","ஞாழல்",6,"maram","scene",""],["தாரமும்","தாரம்",6,"maram","scene","deodar"]],"en":[["river","வையை",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:11"},"pr:12:9-35":{"book":"paripadal","num":71,"t":["‘வந்து மதுரை மதில் பொரூஉம், வான் மலர் தாஅய்,","அம் தண் புனல் வையை யாறு‘ எனக் கேட்டு,","மின் அவிர் ஒளி இழை வேயு மோரும்,","பொன் அடர்ப் பூம் புனை திருத்துவோரும்,","அகில்கெழு சாந்தம் மாற்றி ஆற்றப்","புகைகெழு சாந்தம் பூசுவோரும்,","கார் கொள் கூந்தல் கதுப்பு அமைப்போரும்,","வேர் பிணி பல் மலர் வேயுமோரும்,","புட்டகம் பொருந்துவ புனைகுவோரும்,","கட்டிய கயில் அணி காழ் கொள்வோரும்;","வாச நறு நெய் ஆடி, வான் துகள்","மாசு அறக் கண்ணாடி வயக்கி, வண்ணமும்","தேசும் ஒளியும் திகழ நோக்கி,","வாச மணத் துவர் வாய்க் கொள்வோரும்;","இடு புணர் வளையொடு தொடு தோள்வளையார்,","கட்டு வடக் கழலினர், மட்டு மாலையர்,","ஓசனை கமழும் வாச மேனியர்,","மட மா மிசையோர்,","பிடிமேல் அன்னப் பெரும் படை அனையோர்","கடு மா கடவுவோரும், களிறுமேல் கொள்வோரும்,","வடி மணி நெடுந் தேர் மா முள் பாய்க்குநரும்,","விரைபு விரைபு மிகை மிகை ஈண்டி,","ஆடல் தலைத்தலை சிறப்ப, கூடல்","உரைதர வந்தன்று, வையை நீர்; வையைக்","கரை தர வந்தன்று, காண்பவர் ஈட்டம்;","நிவந்தது, நீத்தம் கரைமேலா: நீத்தம்","கவர்ந்தது போலும், காண்பவர் காதல்"],"p":"","r":"Hearing that the cool lovely Vaiyai has come strewing flowers and beating against Madurai's wall, the town makes ready: some put on gleaming jewels, some set gold-leaf flower ornaments, some change their eaglewood paste for smoked sandal, some dress their dark hair or bind in flowers, some put on fitting cloth and clasped bead strings, some bathe in scented oil, polish their mirrors and study their colour and glow, and chew fragrant betel; men and women in bangles, armlets, anklets and honeyed garlands, fragrant from a league off, ride gentle horses, sit on she-elephants like a host of swans, spur swift horses and tuskers and belled chariots, crowding in ever greater haste with dancing everywhere, so that the water came to be Koodal's talk and the crowd came to fill its banks; the flood rose over the banks, and the watchers' longing rose as if it had seized the flood.","n":"Lines 11 to 22 are a catalogue built on the repeated -ஓரும் ('some ... some'), the whole town as a list of acts of dressing; the river's arrival is first a rumour (எனக் கேட்டு, line 10) and the festival is the town's answer to news. Lines 32 to 35 balance the water and the crowd in matched clauses (வந்தன்று ... வந்தன்று), and the last simile turns it round: not the flood taking the people, but their longing taking the flood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","azhagu","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 12, lines 9-35","lb":"PR12:9-35","poet":"நல்வழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["அகில்கெழு","அகில்",13,"maram","ornament","eaglewood paste"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",13,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["மலர்","மலர்",16,"maram","ornament","flower"],["நெய்","நெய்",19,"pira","ornament","scented oil"],["துவர்","துவர்",22,"unavu","gift_food","betel"],["மாலையர்","மாலை",24,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மா","மா",26,"maa","scene","horse"],["பிடிமேல்","பிடி",27,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["அன்னப்","அன்னம்",27,"pul","simile","swan"],["களிறுமேல்","களிறு",28,"maa","scene","tusker"]],"en":[["town","மதுரை",9],["river","வையை",10],["town","கூடல்",31]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:11"},"pr:12:36-44":{"book":"paripadal","num":72,"t":["முன் துறை நிறை அணி நின்றவர் மொழி மொழி","ஒன்று அல, பலபல உடன் எழுந்தன்று; அவை","எல்லாம் தெரியக் கேட்குநர் யார்? அவை","கில்லா; கேள்வி கேட்டன சிலசில:","ஒத்த குழலின் ஒலி எழ முழவு இமிழ்","மத்தரி தடாரி தண்ணுமை மகுளி","ஒத்து அளந்து; சீர் தூக்கி; ஒருவர் பிற்படார்;","நித்தம் திகழும் நேர் இறை முன்கையால்","அத் தக அரிவையார் அளத்தல் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"At the front ford the voices of those standing in their finery rise together, not one but many, and no one can hear them all; of what can be heard, the flute sounds in tune, the muzhavu roars with the maththari, thadaari, thannumai and maguli drums, all measured together on the beat, none falling behind, and the young women with their bright even-wristed forearms keep the time: look at them.","n":"The poet stages the limits of report: the crowd's words cannot all be heard (lines 37 to 39), so he picks out some (கேள்வி கேட்டன சிலசில), and the episodes that follow are offered as samples overheard at the ford. The rhythm of the drums is kept by women's hands, the festival's order made visible in the same crowd that is speaking at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 12, lines 36-44","lb":"PR12:36-44","poet":"நல்வழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["குழலின்","குழல்",40,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["முழவு","முழவு",40,"parai","scene","drum"],["மத்தரி","மத்தரி",41,"parai","scene",""],["தடாரி","தடாரி",41,"parai","scene",""],["தண்ணுமை","தண்ணுமை",41,"parai","scene",""],["மகுளி","மகுளி",41,"parai","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:11"},"pr:12:45-74":{"book":"paripadal","num":73,"t":["‘நாணாள்கொல்தோழி! \"நயன் இல் பரத்தையின்","தோள் நலம் உண்டு, துறந்தான்\" என, ஒருத்தி","யாணர் மலி புனல் நீத்தத்து இரும் பிடி","சேண வெரிநின் சிறந்தானோடு ஏறினாள்,","நாணுக் குறைவு இலள்; நங்கை மற்று?‘ என்மரும்,","‘கோட்டியுள் கொம்பர் குவி முலை நோக்குவோன்","ஓட்டை மனவன்; உரம் இலி‘ என்மரும்,","‘சொறிந்ததூஉம் சொற்றதூஉம் பற்றாள்; நிறம் திரிந்தாள்;","நெஞ்சத்தை நீத்தாள், நெறி செல்வான் பின்: நிறை","அஞ்சிக் கழியாமோ, அன்பு உற்றால்?‘ என்மரும்,","‘பூண் ஆரம் நோக்கிப் புணர் முலை பார்த்தான், உவன்;","அமிர்து அன நோக்கத்து அணங்கு ஒருத்தி பார்ப்ப,","கமழ் கோதை கோலாப் புடைத்து, தன் மார்பில்","இழையினைக் கை யாத்து, இறுகிறுக்கி வாங்கி,","‘பிழையினை என்ன, பிழை ஒன்றும் காணான்,","தொழுது பிழை கேட்கும் தூயவனைக் காண்மின்.","‘பார்த்தாள், ஒருத்தி நினை என, ‘பார்த்தவளைப்","பொய்ச் சூளாள் என்பது அறியேன், யான்‘ என்று இரந்து,","மெய்ச் சூள் உறுவானை, மெலியல், ‘ பொய்ச் சூள்‘ என்று,","ஒல்லுவ சொல்லாது, உரை வழுவச் சொல்ல;","உறைத்தும் செறுத்தும் உணர்த்துவானைப்","புல்லாது ஊடிப் புலந்து நின்றவள்","பூ எழில் வண்ண நீர் பூரித்த வட்டு எறிய,","வேல் எழில் உண்கண் எறி நோக்கம் பட்ட புண்","பாய் குருதி சோர, பகை இன்று உளம் சோர,","நில்லாது நீங்கி நிலம் சோர; அல்லாந்து","மல் ஆர் அகலம் வடு அஞ்சி, மம்மர் கூர்ந்து,","எல்லாத் துனியும் இறப்ப, தன் காதலன்","நல் ஏர் எழில் ஆகம் சேர்வித்தல், எஞ்ஞான்றும்","வல்லதால், வையைப் புனல்"],"p":"","r":"Voices in the crowd: one says, is she not ashamed, friend, the woman who said he had enjoyed the beauty of a graceless courtesan's shoulders and left her, and now rides the she-elephant in the flood with that man; another, that the man who eyes the breasts of a slender girl in the company is a leaky-minded weakling; another, that a woman who heeds neither the scratches nor the words, whose colour has changed and who has followed the man down the road, shows that restraint takes fright and leaves once love has come; another bids them look at the 'pure' one who, having eyed a woman's necklace and breasts while a goddess-like woman with a nectar look watched, is beaten by his wife with her garland, his hands tied tight with her ornament, and, seeing no fault in himself, bows and asks to be told it; when she says a woman looked at him, he pleads that he never knew the woman was one of false oaths, and to his true oaths she says 'false' and twists his words; sulking and refusing him, she flings a ball of flower-coloured water, and as if from the wound of her spear-like eyes his blood runs, his heart fails and he sinks to the ground; then, troubled and afraid of a scar on his strong chest, all her quarrel passes, and the Vaiyai's flood, as always, brings her back to her lover's lovely breast.","n":"Every episode is reported speech of onlookers (என்மரும், 'some say'), so the oodal is seen from outside, as gossip and spectacle, not voiced by the lovers. தூயவனைக் காண்மின் (line 60) is irony: the onlooker has just said the man eyed another woman's breasts. The coloured water of the festival game becomes, by metaphor, the blood of the wound her glance made, and the reconciliation is credited at the end not to either lover but to the river (வல்லதால், வையைப் புனல்): the sulk scene is told as praise of the Vaiyai. Who swears and who answers in lines 61 to 64 is hard to fix.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai","sool","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 12, lines 45-74","lb":"PR12:45-74","poet":"நல்வழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","kandor",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["பிடி","பிடி",47,"maa","scene","she-elephant"],["புனல்","புனல்",47,"pira","scene","flood water"],["கொம்பர்","கொம்பர்",50,"maram","scene","branch (said of a slender girl)"],["அமிர்து","அமிர்து",56,"unavu","simile","nectar"],["கோதை","கோதை",57,"maram","ornament","garland"],["நீர்","நீர்",67,"pira","scene","coloured water"]],"en":[["river","வையை",74]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:11"},"pr:12:75-85":{"book":"paripadal","num":74,"t":["என ஆங்கு","மல்லிகை, மௌவல், மணம் கமழ் சண்பகம்,","அல்லி, கழுநீர், அரவிந்தம், ஆம்பல்,","குல்லை, வகுளம், குருக்கத்தி, பாதிரி,","நல் இணர் நாகம், நறவம், சுரபுன்னை,","எல்லாம் கமழும் இரு சார் கரை கலிழ;","தேறித் தெளிந்து, செறி இருள் மால் மலை;","பாறைப் பரப்பில் பரந்த சிறை நின்று;","துறக்கத்து எழிலைத் தன் நீர் நிழல் காட்டும்:","கார் அடு காலை, கலிழ் செங் குருதித்தே","போர் அடு தானையான் யாறு"],"p":"","r":"So it goes; with jasmine, mauval, fragrant champak, alli, kazhuneer, lotus, aambal, kullai, vakulam, kurukkaththi, paathiri, the clustered naagam, naravam and surapunnai all smelling sweet, the two banks run muddy; then, settling and clearing, the river from the great dark hill, held by dams spread over the rock beds, shows heaven's beauty in the reflection of its water; and in the morning of the rains it is red with turbid blood, the river of the king whose army kills in war.","n":"Three states of the one river follow each other: muddy with flowers, clear enough to mirror heaven, and red as blood in the monsoon. The last is turned to the king's praise: the flood's red silt is read as the blood of his wars, so the river is named by its owner's killing (போர் அடு தானையான் யாறு). The flower list mixes plants of every landscape (jasmine of mullai, lotus and aambal of the fields, punnai of the shore), the river gathering them all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhagu","vendhan","mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 12, lines 75-85","lb":"PR12:75-85","poet":"நல்வழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மல்லிகை","மல்லிகை",76,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["மௌவல்","மௌவல்",76,"maram","scene",""],["சண்பகம்","சண்பகம்",76,"maram","scene","champak"],["அல்லி","அல்லி",77,"maram","scene","water lily"],["கழுநீர்","கழுநீர்",77,"maram","scene","blue water lily"],["அரவிந்தம்","அரவிந்தம்",77,"maram","scene","lotus"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",77,"maram","scene","white water lily"],["குல்லை","குல்லை",78,"maram","scene",""],["வகுளம்","வகுளம்",78,"maram","scene",""],["குருக்கத்தி","குருக்கத்தி",78,"maram","scene",""],["பாதிரி","பாதிரி",78,"maram","scene",""],["நாகம்","நாகம்",79,"maram","scene","naagam tree"],["நறவம்","நறவம்",79,"maram","scene",""],["சுரபுன்னை","சுரபுன்னை",79,"maram","scene",""]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:11"},"pr:12:86-91":{"book":"paripadal","num":75,"t":["சுடு நீர் வினைக் குழையின் ஞாலச் சிவந்த","கடி மலர்ப் பிண்டி தன் காதில் செரீஇ,","விடு மலர்ப் பூங் கொடி போல நுடங்கி,","அடிமேல் அடிமேல் ஒதுங்கி, தொடி முன்கைக்","காரிகை ஆகத் தன் கண்ணி திருத்தினாள்,","நேர் இறை முன்கை நல்லவள்; கேள் காண்மின்."],"p":"","r":"Having set in her ear a fragrant asoka flower that hangs red like an ear-ring of fire-worked gold, swaying like a flowering creeper, stepping foot after foot, the good woman with even-wristed forearms straightened her garland on the breast of the bangled lovely one: look, she is with her own.","n":"A single festival figure singled out for the audience (காண்மின், 'look'). Whose breast (ஆகம்) the garland is set on, and whether கேள் in line 91 is her lover or her companion, cannot be fixed from these lines; if it is her lover, this is a small scene of union after the quarrel of lines 45 to 74.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["azhagu","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 12, lines 86-91","lb":"PR12:86-91","poet":"நல்வழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["பிண்டி","பிண்டி",87,"maram","ornament","asoka flower"],["கொடி","பூங்கொடி",88,"maram","simile","flowering creeper"],["கண்ணி","கண்ணி",90,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:11"},"pr:12:92-101":{"book":"paripadal","num":76,"t":["துகில் சேர் மலர் போல், மணி நீர் நிறைந்தன்று ;","‘புனல்‘ என, மூதூர் மலிந்தன்று, அவர் உரை ;","உரையின் உயர்ந்தன்று, கவின்","போர் ஏற்றன்று, நவின்று ; தகரம்","மார்பு அழி சாந்தின் மணல் அளறு பட்டன்று ;","துகில் பொசி புனலின், கரை கார் ஏற்றன்று","விசும்பு கடி விட்டன்று, விழவுப் புனல் ஆங்க.","இன்பமும், கவினும், அழுங்கல் மூதூர்,","நன்பல நன்பல நன்பல--வையை!--","நின் புகழ் கொள்ளாது, இம் மலர் தலை உலகே"],"p":"","r":"The gem-clear water is full like a cloth sewn with flowers; 'the flood!' says the old town, its talk swelling, and the beauty rises above the talk; the crowd clashes and jostles again and again; the thagaram and sandal washed from chests turn the sand to mire; with the water that soaks the clothes the banks take on darkness; the sky lets loose, and so is the festival flood: old town of pleasure, beauty and clamour, many good things, many good things, Vaiyai, this flower-topped world cannot hold your fame.","n":"The close is a run of short clauses each ending in -அன்று, as if listing the day's events at speed, and it ends on a hyperbole of measure: the world, though crowned with flowers, is too small for the river's fame. Several clauses (போர் ஏற்றன்று, நவின்று; கரை கார் ஏற்றன்று; விசும்பு கடி விட்டன்று) are terse and their sense is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","punalaadal","inbam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 12, lines 92-101","lb":"PR12:92-101","poet":"நல்வழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",92,"maram","simile","flower"],["தகரம்","தகரம்",95,"maram","ornament","fragrant paste"],["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",96,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["புனலின்","புனல்",97,"pira","scene","flood water"]],"en":[["river","வையை",100]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:11"},"pr:13:1-13":{"book":"paripadal","num":77,"t":["மணி வரை ஊர்ந்த மங்குல் ஞாயிற்று","அணி வனப்பு அமைந்த பூந் துகில், புனை முடி,","இறு வரை இழிதரும் பொன் மணி அருவியின்","நிறனொடு மாறும் தார், புள்ளுப் பொறி புனை கொடி,","விண் அளி கொண்ட வியன் மதி அணி கொளத்","தண் அளி கொண்ட அணங்குடை நேமி மால்!","பருவம் வாய்த்தலின் இரு விசும்பு அணிந்த","இரு வேறு மண்டிலத்து இலக்கம் போல,","நேமியும் வளையும் ஏந்திய கையாந்--","கருவி மின் அவிர் இலங்கும் பொலம் பூண்,","அருவி உருவின் ஆரமொடு, அணிந்த நின்","திரு வரை அகலம் --தொழுவோர்க்கு","உரிது அமர் துறக்கமும் உரிமை நன்கு உடைத்து."],"p":"","r":"The poet hails Maal, whose flowered cloth has the beauty of the sun riding over a cloud-capped gem hill, whose garland vies in colour with a waterfall of gold and gems, whose banner bears the bird and whose discus has the cool grace of the wide moon; the discus and conch in his hands are like the two different discs that adorn the sky in their season, and his holy chest, with gold ornaments flashing like lightning and a garland shaped like a waterfall, holds by right for those who worship it the heaven that is theirs.","n":"The whole unit is a single figure of the god as a mountain: the cloth is the sun on the peak, the garland the waterfall, the ornaments the lightning, the discus and conch the sun and moon. The last clause turns the description to its use: the chest is not only beautiful but is where worshippers' heaven lies (உரிது அமர் துறக்கமும் உரிமை நன்கு உடைத்து).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 13, lines 1-13","lb":"PR13:1-13","poet":"நல்லெழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["அருவியின்","அருவி",3,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["புள்ளுப்","புள்",4,"pul","scene","bird (on the banner)"],["வளையும்","வளை",9,"pira","ornament","conch"],["அருவி","அருவி",11,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:12"},"pr:13:14-21":{"book":"paripadal","num":78,"t":["சுவைமை, இசைமை, தோற்றம், நாற்றம், ஊறு,","அவையும் நீயே, அடு போர் அண்ணால்!","அவைஅவை கொள்ளும் கருவியும் நீயே;","முந்து யாம் கூறிய ஐந்தனுள்ளும்,","ஒன்றனில் போற்றிய விசும்பும் நீயே;","இரண்டின் உணரும் வளியும் நீயே;","மூன்றின் உணரும் தீயும் நீயே;","நான்கின் உணரும் நீரும் நீயே;"],"p":"","r":"Taste, sound, sight, smell and touch are you, lord of killing war, and so are the faculties that take each of them; of the five just named, the sky praised as known by one is you, the wind known by two is you, the fire known by three is you, and the water known by four is you.","n":"The count is cumulative: each element is known through one more of the five than the one before (sky by sound, wind by sound and touch, and so on), so the list is a small theory of the elements, not a catalogue. The earth, which would be known by all five, is not named; the unit stops at water and line 22 turns with அதனால் to the worlds, so either the earth is left for the hearer to supply or the poet means the whole of what follows to stand for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thaththuvam","viyaabagam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 13, lines 14-21","lb":"PR13:14-21","poet":"நல்லெழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:12"},"pr:13:22-36":{"book":"paripadal","num":79,"t":["அதனால், நின் மருங்கின்று--மூ-ஏழ் உலகமும்,","மூலமும், அறனும், முதன்மையின் இகந்த","காலமும், விசும்பும், காற்றொடு கனலும்","தன் உரு உறழும் பாற்கடல் நாப்பண்,","மின் அவிர் சுடர் மணி ஆயிரம் விரித்த","கவை நா அருந் தலைக் காண்பின் சேக்கைத்","துளவம் சூடிய அறிதுயிலோனும்--","மறம் மிகு மலி ஒலி மாறு அடி தானையால்,","திறன் இகந்து வரூஉம் அவர் உயிர் அகற்றும்","விறல் மிகு வலி ஒலி பொலிபு அகழ் புழுதியின்,","நிறன் உழும் வளை வாய் நாஞ்சிலோனும்--","நானிலம் துளக்கு அற முழு முதல் நாற்றிய","பொலம் புனை இதழ் அணி மணி மடற் பேர் அணி","இலங்கு ஒளி மருப்பின் களிறும் -- ஆகி,","மூஉரு ஆகிய தலைபிரி ஒருவனை!"],"p":"","r":"Therefore the thrice-seven worlds, the root, aram, time that passed beyond all firstness, the sky, and the wind with the fire are of your side; and you are the one who, divided, became three forms: the one wearing tulasi who lies in waking sleep on the bed of the serpent with forked tongues and a thousand spread hoods flashing with gems, in the midst of the milk sea; the one of the curved plough who ploughs into the chests of those who come against him beyond measure with a roaring army, raising dust as he digs; and the tusker with the bright shining tusk that planted the whole root so that the four lands should not shake.","n":"The three forms are the reclining Maal, the plough-bearer and the boar who raised the earth; the poem makes the plough-bearer, elsewhere Maal's elder brother, a form of Maal himself (மூஉரு ஆகிய தலைபிரி ஒருவனை). களிறு usually means an elephant, but the tusk that props the earth points to the boar. Whose form the milk sea rivals in line 25 (தன் உரு உறழும்) is unclear: the white serpent's seems likelier than the dark god's. The syntax of lines 22 to 24 is loose, and the reading of நின் மருங்கின்று as 'at your side, from you' is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","viyaabagam","andam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 13, lines 22-36","lb":"PR13:22-36","poet":"நல்லெழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["பாற்கடல்","பால் கடல்",25,"pira","scene","milk sea"],["துளவம்","துளவம்",28,"maram","ornament","tulasi"],["களிறும்","களிறு",35,"maa","scene","boar"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:12"},"pr:13:37-48":{"book":"paripadal","num":80,"t":["படர் சிறைப் பல் நிறப் பாப்புப் பகையைக்","கொடியெனக் கொண்ட கோடாச் செல்வனை;","ஏவல் இன் முது மொழி கூறும்,","சேவல் ஓங்கு உயர் கொடிச் செல்வ! நல் புகழவை;","கார், மலர்ப் பூவை, கடலை, இருள், மணி,","அவை ஐந்தும் உறழும் அணி கிளர் மேனியை;","வலம்புரி, வாய்பொழி, அதிர்பு வான், முழக்குச் செல்,","அவை நான்கும் உறழும்--அருள், செறல், வயின் மொழி;","முடிந்ததும், முடிவதும், முகிழ்ப்பதும், அவை மூன்றும்","கடந்து, அவை அமைந்த கழலின் நிழலவை;","இருமை வினையும் இல, ஏத்துமவை;","ஒருமை வினை மேவும் உள்ளத்தினை;"],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the unswerving rich one who took as banner the many-coloured, wide-winged enemy of snakes, lord of the high bird banner whom good praises name in the sweet ancient word of command; his beautiful body rivals five things, the rain cloud, the kaaya flower, the sea, darkness and the sapphire, and four things, the right-whorled conch, the pouring cloud, the rumbling sky and the roaring thunderbolt, rival his grace, his wrath and his words; the shade of his anklet lies past what is finished, what is passing and what is still budding; those who praise it are without the two deeds, and his heart holds to a single deed.","n":"The poet counts his comparisons aloud (அவை ஐந்தும், அவை நான்கும், அவை மூன்றும்), so the unit is built as a falling series five, four, three, two, one: five dark things for the body, four sounds for grace, wrath and speech, three times surpassed by the feet, two deeds absent, one deed in his heart. Line 47 can be read either way: the praisers have no twofold karma, or the god's feet that they praise have none. How the four sounds are shared out among arul, seral and vayin mozhi is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","vinai","kaalam","thiruvadi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 13, lines 37-48","lb":"PR13:37-48","poet":"நல்லெழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["சேவல்","சேவல்",40,"pul","scene","male bird (Garuda)"],["கார்","கார்",41,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["பூவை","பூவை",41,"maram","simile","kaaya flower"],["கடலை","கடல்",41,"pira","simile","sea"],["மணி","மணி",41,"pira","simile","sapphire"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",43,"pira","simile","right-whorled conch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:12"},"pr:13:49-63":{"book":"paripadal","num":81,"t":["அடை இறந்து அவிழ்ந்த வள் இதழ்த் தாமரை","அடியும், கையும், கண்ணும், வாயும்;","தொடியும், உந்தியும், தோள் அணி வலயமும்,","தாளும், தோளும், எருத்தொடு, பெரியை;","மார்பும், அல்குலும், மனத்தொடு, பரியை;","கேள்வியும், அறிவும், அறத்தொடு, நுண்ணியை;","வேள்வியும், மறனும், விருப்பொடு வெய்யை;","அறாஅ மைந்தின், செறாஅச் செங்கண்,","செரு மிகு திகிரிச் செல்வ! வெல் போர்","எரி நகை இடை இடுபு இழைத்த நறுந் தார்ப்","புரி மலர்த் துழா அய் மேவல் மார்பினோய்!","அன்னை எனநினைஇ, நின் அடி தொழுதனெம்;","பல் மாண் அடுக்க இறைஞ்சினெம் வாழ்த்தினெம்--","முன்னும் முன்னும் யாம் செய் தவப் பயத்தால்;","இன்னும் இன்னும் எம் காமம் இதுவே!"],"p":"","r":"Feet, hands, eyes and mouth are lotuses opened past their sheath; you are great in feet, shoulders and neck, wide in chest, hips and mind, subtle in learning, knowledge and aram, ardent in sacrifice, valour and desire, of unfailing strength and red eyes that do not rage, rich lord of the war-winning discus, whose chest loves the fragrant twisted tulasi garland set with fire-bright flowers; thinking of you as our mother we have worshipped your feet, bowed many times and praised you, by the fruit of the penance we did before and before; still more and still more, this is our desire.","n":"The last line uses காமம், the word for erotic longing, for the wish to worship: what the devotees desire is not a gift but more of this same worship, which they treat as the fruit of past penance. The god is thought of as mother (அன்னை எனநினைஇ), not father or king. The catalogue groups the body by threes and fours with a quality for each group (பெரியை, பரியை, நுண்ணியை, வெய்யை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vazhipaadu","thavam","kaamam","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 13, lines 49-63","lb":"PR13:49-63","poet":"நல்லெழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["தாமரை","தாமரை",49,"maram","scene","lotus"],["துழா","துழாய்",59,"maram","ornament","tulasi"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:12"},"pr:14:1-18":{"book":"paripadal","num":82,"t":["கார் மலி கதழ் பெயல் தலைஇ, ஏற்ற","நீர் மலி நிறை சுனை பூ மலர்ந்தனவே;","தண் நறுங் கடம்பின் கமழ் தாது ஊதும்","வண்ண வண்டு இமிர் குரல் பண்ணை போன்றனவே;","அடியுறைமகளிர் ஆடும் தோளே,","நெடு வரை அடுக்கத்து வேய், போன்றனவே;","வாகை ஒண் பூப் புரையும் முச்சிய","தோகை ஆர் குரல் மணந்து தணந்தோரை,","‘நீடன்மின் வாரும்‘ என்பவர் சொல் போன்றனவே;","நாள் மலர்க் கொன்றையும் பொலந் தார் போன்றன;","வெல் இணர் வேங்கை வியல் அறைத் தாயின,","அழுகை மகளிர்க்கு உழுவை செப்ப;","நீர் அயல் கலித்த நெரி முகைக் காந்தள்","வார் குலை அவிழ்ந்த வள் இதழ் நிரைதொறும்,","விடு கொடிப் பிறந்த மென் தகைத் தோன்றிப்","பவழத்து அன்ன வெம் பூத் தாஅய்,","கார் மலிந்தன்று, நின் குன்று போர் மலிந்து,","சூர் மருங்கு அறுத்த சுடர்ப் படையோயே!"],"p":"","r":"The heavy rain has poured, the brimming pools are in flower; the hum of the bright bees sipping the pollen of the cool kadamba is like a band of musicians; the shoulders of the women who dwell at your feet as they dance are like the bamboo on the high slopes; the full cry of the peacock with its crest like the bright vaakai flower is like the words of those who tell lovers who joined and left, 'do not delay, come'; the fresh konrai is like a golden garland; the vengai's blossoms have spread over the broad rock so that it shows the women in tears a tiger; and along the kaanthal by the water and the soft thonri on its creepers, flowers hot as coral have spread: your hill is full of the rains, you of the shining weapon who cut down Sur in battle.","n":"The peacock simile carries the akam convention of the rains as the season the absent lover promised to return by: on the god's hill the peacock's cry is heard as the very summons 'do not delay, come', so a love situation sits inside the praise without a speaker of its own. Line 12 is hard: it may mean that the flower-strewn rock looks like a tiger to the weeping women, or that it reports a tiger to them; the sense of அழுகை மகளிர் is not fixed.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["paruvam","mazhai","azhagu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 14, lines 1-18","lb":"PR14:1-18","poet":"கேசவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["சுனை","சுனை",2,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",3,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",4,"pira","simile","bee"],["வேய்","வேய்",6,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["வாகை","வாகை",7,"maram","simile","vaakai flower"],["தோகை","தோகை",8,"pul","simile","peacock"],["கொன்றையும்","கொன்றை",10,"maram","simile","konrai"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",11,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["உழுவை","உழுவை",12,"maa","scene","tiger"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",13,"maram","scene","kaanthal"],["தோன்றிப்","தோன்றி",15,"maram","simile","thonri"],["பவழத்து","பவழம்",16,"pira","simile","coral"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:12"},"pr:14:19-32":{"book":"paripadal","num":83,"t":["கறை இல் கார் மழை பொங்கி அன்ன","நறையின் நறும் புகை நனி அமர்ந்தோயே!","அறு முகத்து ஆறு-இரு தோளால் வென்றி","நறு மலர் வள்ளிப் பூ நயந்தோயே!","கெழீஇக் கேளிர் சுற்ற, நின்னை","எழீஇப் பாடும் பாட்டு அமர்ந்தோயே!","பிறந்த ஞான்றே, நின்னை உட்கிச்","சிறந்தோர் அஞ்சிய சீர் உடையோயே!","இரு பிறப்பு, இரு பெயர், ஈர நெஞ்சத்து,","ஒரு பெயர், அந்தணர் அறன் அமர்ந்தோயே!--","அன்னை ஆகலின், அமர்ந்து யாம் நின்னை,","துன்னித் துன்னி, வழிபடுவதன் பயம்","இன்னும் இன்னும் அவை ஆகுக--","தொன் முதிர் மரபின் நின் புகழினும் பலவே!"],"p":"","r":"The poet hails the god who delights in the fragrant smoke of naRai rising like a spotless dark rain cloud, who with six faces and twelve shoulders won and loved the fragrant-flowered Valli, who delights in the song his kin raise as they gather round him, whose glory on the day he was born made the great ones fear him, and who delights in the aram of the brahmins, twice-born, of two names, of moist heart and one name; since you are our mother, the fruit of our worshipping you again and again, close by you, let it be these same things more and more, more even than the many of your fame of ancient ripened tradition.","n":"The four delights the litany names are offerings and worship (the smoke, the song, the brahmins' aram) with the love of Valli set among them, so the god's love-match is counted as one of the things worship gives him. The prayer asks that the fruit of worship be அவை, 'those', most naturally the same acts again, as Paripadal 13 asks. The brahmins' string in lines 27 and 28 (இரு பிறப்பு, இரு பெயர், ... ஒரு பெயர்) is a riddle of counts whose exact referents (the two names, the one name) are not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","valli","andhanar","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 14, lines 19-32","lb":"PR14:19-32","poet":"கேசவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["நறையின்","நறை",20,"maram","gift_food","fragrant naRai (incense)"],["வள்ளிப்","வள்ளி",22,"maram","scene","valli flower (Valli)"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:12"},"pr:15:1-18":{"book":"paripadal","num":84,"t":["புல வரை அறியாப் புகழொடு பொலிந்து,","நில வரைத் தாங்கிய நிலைமையின் பெயராத்","தொலையா நேமி முதல், தொல் இசை அமையும்","புலவர் ஆய்பு உரைத்த புனை நெடுங் குன்றம்","பல; எனின், ஆங்கு-அவை பலவே: பலவினும்,","நிலவரை ஆற்றி, நிறை பயன் ஒருங்கு உடன்","நின்று பெற நிகழும் குன்று--அவை சிலவே:","சிலவினும் சிறந்தன, தெய்வம் பெட்புறும்","மலர் அகல் மார்பின் மை படி குடுமிய","குல வரை சிலவே: குல வரை சிலவினும்","சிறந்தது--கல் அறை கடாம் கானலும் போலவும்,","புல்லிய சொல்லும் பொருளும் போலவும்,","எல்லாம் வேறு வேறு உருவின் ஒரு தொழில் இருவர்த்","தாங்கும் நீள் நிலை ஓங்கு இருங்குன்றம்.","நாறு இணர்த் துழாயோன் நல்கின் அல்லதை","ஏறுதல் எளிதோ, வீறு பெறு துறக்கம்?","அரிதின் பெறு துறக்கம் மாலிருங்குன்றம்","எளிதின் பெறல் உரிமை ஏத்துகம், சிலம்ப."],"p":"","r":"Of the hills that poets have praised, starting from the world-rim mountain that holds the earth, many exist, few among them bring the world its full fruit, fewer still are the clan mountains with clouded peaks, and best of those is this great high hill that bears the two who differ in form but share one deed, joined like the rock cleft and its falls, like word and meaning; heaven is not easy to climb unless the one of the fragrant tulasi grants it, but at Maalirunkunram it is easily won, and that right is what we praise.","n":"The opening narrows like a sieve (பல, then சில, then குல வரை சில, then சிறந்தது) until only one hill is left. Heaven is named twice in two lines: hard to win elsewhere (அரிதின் பெறு துறக்கம்), easy here (எளிதின் பெறல் உரிமை), so the shrine is praised as a shorter road to the same end, and even that road depends on the god's grant (நல்கின் அல்லதை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","thiruvarul","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 15, lines 1-18","lb":"PR15:1-18","poet":"இளம்பெருவழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["துழாயோன்","துழாய்",15,"maram","ornament","tulasi (worn by the god)"]],"en":[["hill","மாலிருங்குன்றம்",17]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:15:19-37":{"book":"paripadal","num":85,"t":["அரா அணர் கயந் தலைத் தம்முன் மார்பின்","மரா மலர்த்தாரின் மாண் வரத் தோன்றி,","அலங்கும் அருவி ஆர்த்து இமிழ்பு இழிய,","சிலம்பாறு அணிந்த, சீர் கெழு திருவின்","சோலையொடு தொடர் மொழி மாலிருங்குன்றம்--","தாம் வீழ் காமம் வித்துபு விளைக்கும்--","நாமத் தன்மை நன்கனம் படி எழ,","யாமத் தன்மை இவ் ஐ இருங்குன்றத்து,","மன் புனல் இள வெயில் வளாவ இருள் வளர்வென,","பொன் புனை உடுக்கையோன் புணஎந்து அமர் நிலையே","நினைமின், மாந்தீர்! கேண்மின், கமழ் சீர்!","சுனையெலாம் நீலம் மலர, சுனை சூழ்","சினையெலாம் செயலை மலர, காய் கனி","உறழ, நனை வேங்கை ஒள் இணர் மலர,","மாயோன் ஒத்த இன் நிலைத்தே.","சென்று தொழுகல்லீர்! கண்டு பணிமின்மே--","இருங்குன்று என்னும் பெயர் பரந்ததுவே","பெருங் கலி ஞாலத்துத் தொன்று இயல் புகழது","கண்டு, மயர் அறுக்கும் காமக் கடவுள்."],"p":"","r":"The Silambaaru comes down the hill, its swaying falls roaring, as fine as the white kadamba garland on the chest of the god's elder brother whose head is hooded by the snake, and so adorns the hill whose name runs on with Solai, Maalirunkunram, a hill that sows and ripens whatever desire people long for; think, people, how the gold-clad one dwells there joined with his brother, as darkness deepens where the young sunlight mingles with the water, and hear his fragrant fame; with blue lilies open in every spring, asoka in flower on every branch around them, ripe fruit beside unripe and the vengai's bright clusters blooming, the hill has the sweet state of Maayon himself; you who cannot go to worship, look at it and bow: the name Irunkunram has spread, its fame is ancient in the clamouring world, and the god who grants desire takes away confusion from those who see it.","n":"The hill is made to resemble the god twice: its falls are the elder brother's white garland, and its flowering takes on Maayon's own state (மாயோன் ஒத்த). Line 34 lets those who cannot make the journey worship by sight alone (கண்டு பணிமின்), so the hill visible from afar is itself an object of worship. Lines 24 to 28 are compressed and the image of light mingling with dark water may be read of the two brothers or of the hill at dusk.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thalam","azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 15, lines 19-37","lb":"PR15:19-37","poet":"இளம்பெருவழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["மரா","மரா",20,"maram","simile","white kadamba"],["அருவி","அருவி",21,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["சோலையொடு","சோலை",23,"maram","scene","grove"],["நீலம்","நீலம்",30,"maram","scene","blue water-lily"],["செயலை","செயலை",31,"maram","scene","asoka"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",32,"maram","scene","vengai tree"]],"en":[["river","சிலம்பாறு",22],["hill","மாலிருங்குன்றம்",23],["hill","இருங்குன்று",35]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:15:38-53":{"book":"paripadal","num":86,"t":["மக முயங்கு மந்தி வரைவரை பாய,","முகிழ் மயங்கு முல்லை முறை நிகழ்வு காட்ட,","மணி மருள் நல் நீர்ச் சினை மடமயில் அகவ,","குருகு இலை உதிர, குயிலினம் கூவ,","பகர் குழல் பாண்டில் இயம்ப அகவுநர்","நா நவில் பாடல் முழவு எதிர்ந்தன்ன,","சிலம்பின் சிலம்பு இசை ஓவாது--ஒன்னார்க்","கடந்து அட்டான் கேழ் இருங்குன்று","தையலவரொடும், தந்தாரவரொடும்,","கைம் மகவோடும், காதலவரொடும்,","தெய்வம் பேணித் திசை தொழுதனிர் செல்மின்--","புவ்வத் தாமரை புரையும் கண்ணன்,","வௌவல் கார் இருள் மயங்கு மணி மேனியன்,","எவ்வயின் உலகத்தும் தோன்றி, அவ் வயின்","மன்பது மறுக்கத் துன்பம் களைவோன்","அன்பு-அது மேஎய் இருங்குன்றத்தான்."],"p":"","r":"The monkey with her young clasped to her leaps from rock to rock, the budding mullai shows the seasons turning in order, the young peahen calls from branches over water clear as gems, leaves fall and the koels call, flute and cymbals play and the singers' practised songs answer the drum, so that the hill's echo never stops: to this dark hill of him who overcame and slew his foes go, with your wives, with your parents, with the infants in your arms and with those you love, honouring the god and bowing toward his quarter; he is the one with eyes like the lotus, his gem-like body mingled with the dark of the rainy night, who appears in whatever world it may be and takes away the suffering when its people are troubled, and who dwells in love on Irunkunram.","n":"The call to pilgrimage names the whole household one by one (wives, parents, infants, loved ones), so the worship is a family journey, not a lone ascetic's. The god's coming is stated in general (எவ்வயின் உலகத்தும் தோன்றி) and his dwelling in particular (இருங்குன்றத்தான்), and the hinge between them is love (அன்பு-அது மேஎய்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","sutram","idumbai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 15, lines 38-53","lb":"PR15:38-53","poet":"இளம்பெருவழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["மந்தி","மந்தி",38,"maa","scene","female monkey"],["முல்லை","முல்லை",39,"maram","scene","jasmine"],["மடமயில்","மயில்",40,"pul","scene","peahen"],["குயிலினம்","குயில்",41,"pul","scene","koel"],["குழல்","குழல்",42,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["பாண்டில்","பாண்டில்",42,"yaazh","scene","cymbals"],["முழவு","முழவு",43,"parai","simile","drum"],["தாமரை","தாமரை",49,"maram","simile","lotus"]],"en":[["hill","இருங்குன்று",45]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:15:54-66":{"book":"paripadal","num":87,"t":["கள் அணி பசுந் துளவினவை, கருங் குன்றனையவை;","ஒள் ஒளியவை, ஒரு குழையவை;","புள் அணி பொலங் கொடியவை;","வள் அணி வளை நாஞ்சிலவை,","சலம் புரி தண்டு ஏந்தினவை;","வலம்புரி வய நேமியவை;","வரி சிலை வய அம்பினவை;","புகர் இணர் சூழ் வட்டத்தவை; புகர் வாளவை;","என ஆங்கு-","நலம் புரீஇ அம் சீர் நாம வாய்மொழி","இது என உரைத்தலின், எம் உள் அமர்ந்து இசைத்து, இறை,","‘இருங்குன்றத்து அடி உறை இயைக!‘ என,","பெரும் பெயர் இருவரைப் பரவுதும், தொழுதே"],"p":"","r":"He is named by what he bears: the honeyed fresh tulasi and a form like a dark hill, the bright light and the single earring, the bird on the golden banner, the plough with its strong ring, the club held in anger, the right-whorled conch and the strong discus, the striped bow and its arrows, the disc ringed with bright clusters and the bright sword; since the true sacred word says this is so, and the lord dwells within us and sounds there, we bow and praise the two of great name, asking that we may come to dwell at the feet on Irunkunram.","n":"The litany runs in plurals (-அவை) and mixes the marks of both brothers, the elder's single earring and plough with the younger's tulasi, conch and discus, so the weapons are counted as one possession; only the last line says the praise goes to two (பெரும் பெயர் இருவரை). The god is placed at once on the hill and inside the worshipper (எம் உள் அமர்ந்து இசைத்து), and the prayer asks for the hill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thirunaamam","iraivan","thiruvadi","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 15, lines 54-66","lb":"PR15:54-66","poet":"இளம்பெருவழுதியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["துளவினவை","துளவம்",54,"maram","ornament","tulasi"],["புள்","புள்",56,"pul","scene","bird on the banner"],["வலம்புரி","வலம்புரி",59,"pira","ornament","right-whorled conch"]],"en":[["hill","இருங்குன்றத்து",65]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:16:1-19":{"book":"paripadal","num":88,"t":["கரையே--கை வண் தோன்றல் ஈகை போன்ம் என,","மை படு சிலம்பின் கறியொடும், சாந்தொடும்,","நெய் குடை தயிரின் நுரையொடும், பிறவொடும்,","எவ் வயினானும்--மீதுமீது அழியும்.","துறையே--முத்து நேர்பு புணர் காழ், மத்தக நித்திலம்,","பொலம் புனை அவிர் இழை, கலங்கல் அம் புனல் மணி","வலம் சுழி உந்திய, திணை பிரி புதல்வர்","கயந் தலை முச்சிய முஞ்சமொடு தழீஇ,","தம்தம் துணையோடு ஒருங்கு உடன் ஆடும்","தத்து அரிக் கண்ணார் தலைத்தலை வருமே","செறுவே--விடு மலர் சுமந்து, பூ நீர் நிறைதலின்,","படு கண் இமிழ் கொளை பயின்றனர் ஆடும்,","களி நாள் அரங்கின் அணி நலம் புரையும்.","காவே--சுரும்பு இமிர் தாதொடு தலைத்தலை மிகூஉம்","நரந்த நறு மலர் நன்கு அளிக்கும்மே","கரைபு ஒழுகு தீம் புனற்கு எதிர் விருந்து அயர்வ போல்.","கான்-அலம் காவும், கயமுன், துருத்தியும், தேன்","தேன் உண்டு பாடத் திசைதிசைப் பூ நலம்","பூத்தன்று--வையை வரவு."],"p":"","r":"The banks, as if copying the giving of a generous-handed lord, wear away everywhere under the pepper and sandal of the dark hill, the foam of curd and much else; at the fords women with darting eyes come crowd after crowd to bathe with their partners, holding their little tufted sons with the munjam on their heads, while pearl strings, pearls from the elephant's brow, gold jewels and gems whirl to the right in the turbid water; the fields, carrying shed flowers and brimming with flower-water, are as lovely as a stage on festival day where dancers trained to the drum's beat perform; the groves give their fragrant narantham, thick with the pollen the bees hum over, as if feasting the sweet flowing water as a guest; in forest groves, ponds and islets the bees drink and sing, and flower-beauty bloomed in every quarter: such is the Vaiyai's coming.","n":"The unit is built on four places, each with its own simile: banks (கரையே), fords (துறையே), fields (செறுவே), groves (காவே). The banks' loss to the flood is praised as giving, and the groves' flowers as a host's welcome, so the river is both the guest that is fed and the cause of a generosity that costs the land.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","azhagu","virundhu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 16, lines 1-19","lb":"PR16:1-19","poet":"நல்வழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["கறியொடும்","கறி",2,"maram","scene","pepper"],["சாந்தொடும்","சாந்து",2,"maram","scene","sandalwood"],["தயிரின்","தயிர்",3,"unavu","scene","curd"],["முத்து","முத்து",5,"pira","scene","pearl"],["நித்திலம்","நித்திலம்",5,"pira","scene","pearl"],["மலர்","மலர்",11,"maram","scene","flowers"],["சுரும்பு","சுரும்பு",14,"pira","scene","bee"],["நரந்த","நரந்தம்",15,"maram","simile","narantham flower"],["தேன்","தேன்",17,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[["river","வையை",19]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:16:20-31":{"book":"paripadal","num":89,"t":["கருங்கையின் ஆயத்தார் சுற்றும் எறிந்து,","குரும்பையின் முலைப் பட்ட பூ நீர் துடையாள்,","பெருந் தகை மீளி வருவானைக் கண்டே,","இருந் துகில் தானையின் ஒற்றி, ‘பொருந்தலை;","பூத்தனள்; நீங்கு‘ எனப் பொய் ஆற்றால், தோழியர்--","தோற்றம் ஓர் ஒத்த மலர் கமழ் தண் சாந்தின்","நாற்றத்தின் போற்றி, நகையொடும் போத்தந்து,","இருங் கடற்கு ஊங்கு இவரும் யாறு எனத் தங்கான்,","மகிழ, களிப் பட்ட தேன் தேறல் மாற்றி,","குருதி துடையாக் குறுகி, மரு(வ), இனியர்,","‘பூத்தனள் நங்கை; பொலிக!‘ எனநாணுதல்","வாய்த்தன்றால்--வையை வரவு."],"p":"","r":"Her companions splash water all round, and a girl who has not wiped the flower-water from her young breasts sees a noble young man coming and presses her thick cloth's hem to herself, while her friends tell him by a lie, 'don't come near, she is in her flowering, go'; they guard her with the scent of cool sandal that looks just like blood and send him off laughing; but he, like a river rushing on to the great sea, does not hold back, leaves the heady honeyed wine, comes close without wiping away the red, and embraces her sweetly, so that their cry 'the lady has flowered, hail!' turned to shame: such is the Vaiyai's coming.","n":"The friends use the bar of menstruation (பூத்தனள்) to keep a lover off, with red sandal standing in for blood; he ignores the bar, and the same word comes back as a joke (பூத்தனள் நங்கை; பொலிக). Whose shame line 30 means, the girl's or the lying friends', is not fixed by the text. The refrain வையை வரவு makes the scene part of the flood's arrival.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["theettu","punarchi","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 16, lines 20-31","lb":"PR16:20-31","poet":"நல்வழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["pira","kandor",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["குரும்பையின்","குரும்பை",21,"maram","simile","young coconut"],["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",25,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கடற்கு","கடல்",27,"pira","simile","sea"],["யாறு","யாறு",27,"pira","simile","river"],["தேறல்","தேறல்",28,"unavu","gift_food","honeyed wine"]],"en":[["river","வையை",31]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:16:32-38":{"book":"paripadal","num":90,"t":["மலையின் இழி அருவி மல்கு இணர்ச் சார்ச் சார்க்","கரை மரம் சேர்ந்து கவினி; மடவார்","நனை சேர் கதுப்பினுள் தண் போது, மைந்தர்","மலர் மார்பின் சோர்ந்த மலர் இதழ், தா அய்;","மீன் ஆரம் பூத்த வியன் கங்கை நந்திய","வானம் பெயர்ந்த மருங்கு ஒத்தல், எஞ்ஞான்றும்,","தேன் இமிர் வையைக்கு இயல்பு."],"p":"","r":"The falls coming down from the hill join the bank trees beside the groves full of clusters and grow lovely, and the cool buds from the women's hair and the petals slipped from the men's flowered chests spread over the water, so that the Vaiyai, humming with bees, always looks like the sky where the wide Ganga of the heavens, flowered with stars, has passed.","n":"The stars are said to flower (மீன் ஆரம் பூத்த), so the simile runs both ways: flowers on the river are stars, and the stars of the heavenly river are flowers. The flowers are the lovers' castoffs, so the river's sky is made of the night's adornments.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 16, lines 32-38","lb":"PR16:32-38","poet":"நல்வழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",32,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மரம்","மரம்",33,"maram","scene","bank trees"],["போது","போது",34,"maram","ornament","flower buds"],["இதழ்","இதழ்",35,"maram","ornament","petals"]],"en":[["river","வையைக்கு",38]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:16:39-47":{"book":"paripadal","num":91,"t":["கள்ளே புனலே புலவி இம் மூன்றினும்,","ஒள் ஒளி சேய்தா ஒளி கிளர் உண் கண் கெண்டை,","பல் வரி வண்டினம் வாய் சூழ் கவினொடும்,","வெல் நீர் வீவயின் தேன் சோர, பல் நீர்","அடுத்துஅடுத்து ஆடுவார்ப் புல்ல, குழைந்து","வடுப் படு மான்மதச் சாந்து ஆர் அகலத்தான்,","எடுத்த வேய் எக்கி நூக்கு உயர்பு தாக்கத்","தொடுத்த தேன் சோரும் வரை போலும், தோற்றம்--","கொடித் தேரான் வையைக்கு இயல்பு."],"p":"","r":"In toddy, in water and in sulking, these three, the women's bright kohl-lined eyes, red-streaked like kendai fish, with bee swarms circling their mouths and honey dripping where the flowers fall, the bathers who plunge again and again in the deep water are embraced, and the man whose chest is smeared with musk paste and marked with scars yields to them; the sight is like a hill where a hanging honeycomb drips when bent bamboo springs up and strikes it: such is the nature of the Vaiyai of the king of the bannered chariot.","n":"The three in which the eyes redden, toddy, water and the sulk, set drink, bathing and quarrel side by side as one play. The closing simile makes the embrace a sudden release, as the bamboo springs back and the honey falls. The syntax of lines 40 to 44 is loose, and who embraces whom can be read more than one way.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kal","oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 16, lines 39-47","lb":"PR16:39-47","poet":"நல்வழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[["pira","kandor",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["கெண்டை","கெண்டை",40,"pira","simile","carp fish"],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",41,"pira","scene","bees"],["தேன்","தேன்",42,"pira","scene","honey"],["மான்மதச்","மான்மதம்",44,"pira","ornament","musk"],["வேய்","வேய்",45,"maram","simile","bamboo"],["தேன்","தேன்",46,"pira","simile","honeycomb"]],"en":[["river","வையைக்கு",47]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:16:48-55":{"book":"paripadal","num":92,"t":["வரை ஆர்க்கும் புயல்; கரை","திரை ஆர்க்கும், இத் தீம் புனல்;","கண்ணியர் தாரர், கமழ் நறுங் கோதையர்,","பண்ணிய ஈகைப் பயன் கொள்வான், ஆடலால்","நாள் நாள், உறையும், நறுஞ் சாந்தும், கோதையும்,","பூத்த புகையும், அவியும் புலராமை","மறாஅற்க, வானம்; மலிதந்து நீத்தம்","வறாஅற்க, வைகை! நினக்கு"],"p":"","r":"Clouds roar on the hills and this sweet water roars in waves on the banks; so that those who wear chaplets, chest garlands and fragrant hair garlands may take by bathing, day after day, the fruit of the gifts they have made, may the sky never refuse to keep the rain, the fragrant sandal, the garlands, the flowering smoke and the offerings from drying, and may your flood swell and never run dry, Vaigai.","n":"The prayer ties rite to rain: the sky is asked not to let the offerings dry (புலராமை), and bathing in the river is when the giver collects the fruit of past giving (பண்ணிய ஈகைப் பயன்). The river is addressed directly only in the last word, நினக்கு.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","vazhipaadu","eegai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 16, lines 48-55","lb":"PR16:48-55","poet":"நல்வழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["புயல்","புயல்",48,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["சாந்தும்","சாந்து",52,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கோதையும்","கோதை",52,"maram","ornament","garland"],["அவியும்","அவி",53,"unavu","gift_food","oblation"]],"en":[["river","வைகை",55]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:13"},"pr:17:1-21":{"book":"paripadal","num":93,"t":["தேம் படு மலர், குழை, பூந் துகில், வடி மணி,","ஏந்து இலை சுமந்து; சாந்தம் விரைஇ,","விடை அரை அசைத்த, வேலன், கடிமரம்","பரவினர் உரையொடு பண்ணிய இசையினர்,","விரிமலர் மதுவின் மரன் நனை குன்றத்து","கோல் எரி, கொளை, நறை, புகை, கொடி, ஒருங்கு எழ--","மாலை மாலை, அடி உறை, இயைநர்,","மேலோர் உறையுளும் வேண்டுநர் யாஅர்?","ஒருதிறம், பாணர் யாழின் தீங் குரல் எழ,","ஒருதிறம், யாணர் வண்டின் இமிர் இசை எழ,","ஒருதிறம், கண் ஆர் குழலின் கரைபு எழ,","ஒருதிறம், பண் ஆர் தும்பி பரந்து இசை ஊத,","ஒருதிறம், மண் ஆர் முழவின் இசை எழ,","ஒருதிறம், அண்ணல் நெடு வரை அருவி நீர் ததும்ப,","ஒருதிறம், பாடல் நல் விறலியர் ஒல்குபு நுடங்க,","ஒருதிறம், வாடை உளர்வயின் பூங் கொடி நுடங்க,","ஒருதிறம், பாடினி முரலும் பாலை அம் குரலின்","நீடுகிளர் கிழமை நிறை குறை தோன்ற,","ஒருதிறம், ஆடு சீர் மஞ்ஞை அரி குரல் தோன்ற,","மாறுமாறு உற்றன் போல் மாறு எதிர் கோடல்","மாறு அட்டான் குன்றம்--உடைத்து."],"p":"","r":"Carrying honeyed flowers, tender leaves, flowered cloth, cast bells and the raised leaf-spear, mixing sandal, the velan ties a ram at the guarded tree and the worshippers praise with words and with tuned song; on this hill whose trees are wet with the honey of open flowers, torch, song, fragrance, smoke and banners rise together, and those who join the god's feet evening after evening want no dwelling of the gods above; on one side the bards' lute sounds and on another the bees hum, here the flute and there the tumbi drones its tune, here the drum and there the waterfall brims, here the singing women sway and there the flower creepers sway in the north wind, here the paadini's paalai voice shows the rising and falling notes and there the peacock calls, so that, as if in rivalry, each answers the other: such is the hill of him who slew the foe.","n":"The passage is built as ten alternating sides (ஒருதிறம்), and each human performance is matched by a thing of the hill doing the same act: lute and bee, flute and tumbi, drum and falls, swaying singers and swaying creepers, the paadini's voice and the peacock's. The closing line names the god by the same word as the contest, மாறு: the slayer of the foe (மாறு அட்டான்) owns a hill where every sound meets its rival (மாறு எதிர் கோடல்). Line 8 sets the worship above heaven itself: who would want the gods' abode?","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","velan","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 17, lines 1-21","lb":"PR17:1-21","poet":"நல்லழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",1,"maram","ornament","flowers carried as offering"],["குழை","குழை",1,"maram","ornament","tender leaves"],["விடை","விடை",3,"maa","gift_food","ram (offered)"],["வேலன்","வேலன்",3,"seythi","scene","diviner-priest"],["கடிமரம்","கடிமரம்",3,"maram","scene","guarded tree"],["மரன்","மரம்",5,"maram","scene","trees"],["பாணர்","பாணர்",9,"seythi","simile","bards"],["யாழின்","யாழ்",9,"yaazh","simile","lute"],["வண்டின்","வண்டு",10,"pira","simile","bee"],["குழலின்","குழல்",11,"yaazh","simile","flute"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",12,"pira","simile","humming bee"],["முழவின்","முழவு",13,"parai","simile","drum"],["அருவி","அருவி",14,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["விறலியர்","விறலி",15,"seythi","simile","singing dancers"],["கொடி","பூங் கொடி",16,"maram","simile","flowering creeper"],["பாடினி","பாடினி",17,"seythi","simile","woman singer"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",19,"pul","simile","peacock"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:17:22-39":{"book":"paripadal","num":94,"t":["பாடல் சான்று பல் புகழ் முற்றிய","கூடலொடு பரங்குன்றின் இடை,","கமழ் நறுஞ் சாந்தின் அவர்அவர் திளைப்ப,","நணிநணித்து ஆயினும், சேஎய்ச் சேய்த்து;","மகிழ் மிகு தேஎம் கோதையர் கூந்தல் குஞ்சியின்","சோர்ந்து அவிழ் இதழின் இயங்கும் ஆறு இன்று.","வசை நீங்கிய வாய்மையால், வேள்வியால்,","திசை நாறிய குன்று அமர்ந்து, ஆண்டுஆண்டு","ஆவி உண்ணும் அகில் கெழு கமழ் புகை","வாய்வாய் மீ போய், உம்பர் இமைபு இறப்ப;","தேயா மண்டிலம் காணுமாறு இன்று.","வளை முன் கை வணங்கு இறையார்,","அணை மென் தோள் அசைபு ஒத்தார்","தார் மார்பின் தகை இயலார்,","ஈர மாலை இயல் அணியார்,","மனம் மகிழ் தூங்குநர் பாய்பு உடன் ஆட,","சுனை மலர்த் தாது ஊதும் வண்டு ஊதல் எய்தா;","அனைய, பரங்குன்றின் அணி."],"p":"","r":"On the road between Koodal, rich in song and fame, and Parankunram, people revel smeared with fragrant sandal, so crowded that what is near and near again is far and far, and petals loosened from the women's garlands and the men's tufts leave no room to walk; on the hill that sends its fragrance to every quarter by blameless truth and sacrifice, the smoke of agil that consumes the offerings rises here and there till the gods above blink and the unwaning sun cannot be seen; and women with bangled forearms and soft shoulders, men with garlanded chests, all leap together on the swings, so that the bees that suck the pollen of the pool flowers cannot get to them: such is the beauty of Parankunram.","n":"Each image measures the crowd by what it blocks: the near road becomes far, the fallen petals close the path, the smoke shuts out the sun and makes even the unblinking gods blink (இமைபு), and the swingers keep the bees from the pool flowers. The festival is praised by its excess, not by any rite described in detail.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","vizhavu","aaguthi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 17, lines 22-39","lb":"PR17:22-39","poet":"நல்லழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["சாந்தின்","சாந்து",24,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["இதழின்","இதழ்",27,"maram","scene","petals"],["அகில்","அகில்",30,"maram","scene","agil incense"],["வளை","வளை",33,"pira","ornament","bangles"],["தார்","தார்",35,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மாலை","மாலை",36,"maram","ornament","garland"],["சுனை","சுனை",38,"pira","scene","hill pool"],["மலர்த்","மலர்",38,"maram","scene","flowers"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",38,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[["town","கூடலொடு",23],["hill","பரங்குன்றின்",23]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:17:40-53":{"book":"paripadal","num":95,"t":["கீழோர் வயல் பரக்கும், வார் வெள் அருவி பரந்து ஆனாது ஆரோ;","மேலோர் இயங்குதலால், வீழ் மணி நீலம் செறு உழக்கும் அரோ;","தெய்வ விழவும், திருந்து விருந்து அயர்வும்,","அவ் வெள் அருவி அணி பரங் குன்றிற்கும்,","தொய்யா விழுச் சீர் வளம் கெழு வையைக்கும்,","கொய் உளை மான் தேர்க் கொடித்தேரான் கூடற்கும்,","கை ஊழ் தடுமாற்றம் நன்று.","என ஆங்கு,","மணி நிற மஞ்ஞை ஓங்கிய புட் கொடி,","பிணிமுகம் ஊர்ந்த வெல் போர், இறைவ!","பணி ஒரீஇ, நின் புகழ் ஏத்தி,","அணி நெடிங் குன்றம் பாடுதும்; தொழுதும்;","அவை யாமும் எம் சுற்றமும் பரவுதும்--","ஏம வைகல் பெறுக, யாம் எனவே"],"p":"","r":"The long white falls spread over the fields of those below without ceasing, and as those above move about, the gems they drop churn the blue paddy plots; the god's festival and the fine feasting of guests pass, hand to hand in turn, between this hill of white falls, the Vaiyai of unfailing wealth and the Koodal of the king of the maned-horse banner chariot, and that alternation is good; so the poet turns to the god, lord who rode Pinimukam, with the peacock raised on his bird banner, and says: leaving other service and praising your fame, we sing your tall hill and bow, we and our kin, that we may have days of safety.","n":"The hill, the river and the city are joined by one exchange: the falls water the fields below, and the god's festival and the feasting of guests move between the three places. பிணிமுகம் is the name of the god's mount; whether it is the elephant or the peacock is not settled from this line, which also names the peacock on the banner.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","virundhu","sutram","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 17, lines 40-53","lb":"PR17:40-53","poet":"நல்லழிசியார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",40,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["மணி","மணி",41,"pira","scene","gem"],["மான்","மான்",45,"maa","scene","horse"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",48,"pul","scene","peacock (on the banner)"]],"en":[["hill","பரங் குன்றிற்கும்",43],["river","வையைக்கும்",44],["town","கூடற்கும்",45],["king","கொடித்தேரான்",45]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:18:1-6":{"book":"paripadal","num":96,"t":["போர் எதிர்ந்து ஏற்றார் மதுகை மதம் தப,","கார் எதிர்ந்து ஏற்ற கமஞ் சூல் எழிலிபோல்,","நீர் நிரந்து ஏற்ற நிலம் தாங்கு அழுவத்து,","சூர், நிரந்து சுற்றிய, மா தபுத்த வேலோய்! நின்","சீர் நிரந்து ஏந்திய குன்றொடு நேர் நிரந்து,","ஏறுமாறு ஏற்கும் இக் குன்று."],"p":"","r":"O spear-bearer who, like the full-bellied cloud that meets the rainy season, met those who came against you in battle and broke the pride of their strength, and who in the water-spread expanse that holds up the earth destroyed the mango of Sur and his ring of attendants: this hill takes up the challenge of standing level with the hill that bears your glory.","n":"The hill is not said to equal the god's other hill outright: ஏறுமாறு ஏற்கும் makes it a contender that accepts the contest, the same language of rivalry the companion song 17 uses for the hill's sounds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","maram","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 18, lines 1-6","lb":"PR18:1-6","poet":"குன்றம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","தறுகண்"]],"k":[["எழிலிபோல்","எழிலி",2,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["மா","மா மரம்",4,"maram","scene","mango (Sur's form)"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:18:7-15":{"book":"paripadal","num":97,"t":["தலைமகன் ஊடல் உணர்ப்பிக்கும் திறம்","ஒள் ஒளி மணிப் பொறி ஆல் மஞ்ஞை நோக்கித் தன்","உள்ளத்து நினைப்பானைக் கண்டனள், திரு நுதலும்:","‘உள்ளியது உணர்ந்தேன்; அஃது உரை இனி, நீ எம்மை","எள்ளுதல் மறைத்தல் ஓம்பு‘ என்பாளைப் பெயர்த்து, அவன்,","‘காதலாய்! நின் இயல் களவு எண்ணிக் களி மகிழ்","பேதுற்ற இதலைக் கண்டு, யான் நோக்க, நீ எம்மை","ஏதிலா நோக்குதி‘ என்று, ஆங்கு உணர்ப்பித்தல்","ஆய் தேரான் குன்ற இயல்பு."],"p":"","r":"Seeing her man gaze at a bright peacock with gem-like eyespots and brood within, the woman of the lovely brow says she knows what he was thinking and bids him tell it and not mock her or hide it; he answers: my love, I was thinking how the peacock had stolen your bearing, and when I looked at a quail dazed in its revelry you looked at me as at a stranger; so he talks her out of the sulk, and that, says the poet, is the way of the god's hill.","n":"The quarrel turns on a look: she reads his silent gaze as thought of another woman, he turns the same gaze into praise of her, because what he was looking at had taken her nature (நின் இயல் களவு). The scene is offered as the hill's character (குன்ற இயல்பு), so the god's hill is praised by the quick mending of lovers' quarrels on it. Why the quail enters his answer is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oodal","ninaivu","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 18, lines 7-15","lb":"PR18:7-15","poet":"குன்றம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[["thalaivan","thalaivi",null,null,null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",8,"pul","scene","peacock"],["இதலைக்","இதல்",13,"pul","scene","quail"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:18:16-22":{"book":"paripadal","num":98,"t":["ஐ வளம் பூத்த அணி திகழ் குன்றின்மேல்,","மை வளம் பூத்த மலர் ஏர் மழைக் கண்ணார்,","கை வளம் பூத்த வடு வொடு, காணாய் நீ?","மொய் வளம் பூத்த முயக்கம், யாம் கைப்படுத்தேம்:","மெய் வளம் பூத்த விழை தகு பொன் அணி--","நை வளம் பூத்த நரம்பு இயை சீர்ப் பொய் வளம்","பூத்தன--பாணா! நின் பாட்டு."],"p":"","r":"On the lovely hill where every kind of richness blooms, she says to the bard: do you not see the marks that the hands of women with rain-cool eyes like collyrium-dark flowers have made bloom on him? we have caught the embrace that bloomed thick, and the desirable gold ornaments that bloomed on his body; bard, your song on tuned strings has bloomed only a richness of lies.","n":"Every line turns on the same phrase, வளம் பூத்த, so the hill's blooming richness, the marks on the man, the embrace and the ornaments all bloom alike, and the bard's song blooms too, but with lies. The speaker is not named; the wife is the likeliest reading of யாம் கைப்படுத்தேம், though the friend speaking for her is possible.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 18, lines 16-22","lb":"PR18:16-22","poet":"குன்றம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","paanan",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",17,"maram","simile","flower"],["பொன்","பொன் அணி",20,"pira","ornament","gold ornaments"],["நரம்பு","நரம்பு",21,"yaazh","scene","lute strings"],["பாணா","பாணன்",22,"seythi","scene","bard"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:18:23-30":{"book":"paripadal","num":99,"t":["தண் தளிர் தருப் படுத்து, எடுத்து உரைஇ,","மங்குல் மழை முழங்கிய விறல் வரையால்,","கண் பொருபு சுடர்ந்து, அடர்ந்து, இடந்து,","இருள் போழும் கொடி மின்னால்","வெண் சுடர் வேல் வேள்! விரை மயில் வேல் ஞாயிறு!--நின்","ஒண் சுடர் ஓடைக் களிறு ஏய்க்கும் நின் குன்றத்து,","எழுது எழில் அம்பலம் காமவேள் அம்பின்","தொழில் வீற்றிருந்த நகர்."],"p":"","r":"O Vel of the white-flaming spear, sun of the swift peacock and the spear: on your hill, which with its victorious peaks where the rain clouds thundered over gathered tender shoots, and with its creeper lightning that dazzles the eyes, flares, presses on and splits the dark, looks like your elephant with its bright forehead plate, stands the city where the lovely painted hall is the seat of the work of Kama's arrows.","n":"The hill is first likened to the god's own war elephant, the lightning playing the part of its gleaming forehead plate, and then holds a painted hall given over to the god of love's arrows: the war god's hill houses the love god's work.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thalam","iraivan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 18, lines 23-30","lb":"PR18:23-30","poet":"குன்றம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["தளிர்","தளிர்",23,"maram","scene","tender shoots"],["மழை","மழை",24,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["மின்னால்","மின்",26,"pira","scene","lightning"],["மயில்","மயில்",27,"pul","scene","peacock (the god's mount)"],["களிறு","களிறு",28,"maa","simile","war elephant"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:18:31-42":{"book":"paripadal","num":100,"t":["ஆர் ததும்பும் அயில் அம்பு நிறை நாழி","சூர் ததும்பும் வரைய காவால்,","கார் ததும்பு நீர் ததும்புவன சுனை,","ஏர் ததும்புவன பூ அணி செறிவு,","போர் தோற்றுக் கட்டுண்டார் கை போல்வ--கார் தோற்றும்","காந்தள், செறிந்த கவின்,","கவின் முகை, கட்டு அவிழ்ப்ப, தும்பி; கட்டு யாழின்","புரி நெகிழ்ப்பார் போன்றன கை.","அச்சிரக்கால் ஆர்த்து--அணி மழை--கோலின்றே,","வச்சிரத்தான் வானவில்லு.","வில்லுச் சொரி பகழியின், மென் மலர் தாயின","வல்லுப் போர் வல்லாய்! மலைமேல் மரம்."],"p":"","r":"With groves on the dread-haunted slopes thick as a quiver full of sharp arrows, the pools brim with rain water and the clustered flowers brim with beauty; the close-packed kanthal that heralds the rains is like the hands of men defeated in war and bound, and the tumbi bees loosening the bound buds are like hands loosening the twisted strings of a lute; the wind of the dewy season has drawn on the lovely clouds the rainbow of the thunderbolt-bearer, and on the mountain, you who are skilled at the game of dice, the trees have spread soft flowers like arrows rained from a bow.","n":"Two linked images use one bud: the closed kanthal is a bound captive's hands, and the bee that opens it is a player's hand loosening lute strings, so the flower passes from an image of war to one of music. The address வல்லுப் போர் வல்லாய், to one skilled in dice, begins the dice imagery of the next lines; who is addressed is not clear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thalam","mazhai","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 18, lines 31-42","lb":"PR18:31-42","poet":"குன்றம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["காவால்","கா",32,"maram","scene","groves"],["சுனை","சுனை",33,"pira","scene","hill pool"],["பூ","பூ",34,"maram","scene","flowers"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",36,"maram","simile","kanthal (glory lily)"],["முகை","முகை",37,"maram","simile","bud"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",37,"pira","simile","humming bee"],["யாழின்","யாழ்",37,"yaazh","simile","lute"],["மலர்","மலர்",41,"maram","simile","flowers"],["மரம்","மரம்",42,"maram","simile","trees"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:18:43-57":{"book":"paripadal","num":101,"t":["வட்டு உருட்டு வல்லாய்! மலைய--நெட்டுருட்டுச்","சீர் ததும்பும் அரவமுடன் சிறந்து,","போர் ததும்பும் அரவம் போல்,","கருவி ஆர்ப்ப, கருவி நின்றன--குன்றம்","அருவி ஆர்ப்ப, முத்து அணிந்தன, வரை;","குருவி ஆர்ப்ப, குரல் குவிந்தன, தினை;","எருவை கோப்ப, எழில் அணி திருவில்","வானில் அணித்த, வரி ஊதும் பல் மலரால்,","கூனி வளைத்த--சுனை,","புரி உறு நரம்பும் இயலும் புணர்ந்து,","சுருதியும் பூவும் சுடரும் கூடி,","எரி உருகு அகிலோடு ஆரமும் கமழும்,","செரு வேற் தானைச் செல்வ! நின் அடி உறை,","உரிதினின் உறை பதிச் சேர்ந்தாங்கு,","பிரியாது இருக்க--எம் சுற்றமோடு உடனே!"],"p":"","r":"You skilled at rolling dice: on the mountain, with the swelling clatter of the long throw, like the din of battle, the clouds roar and stand; as the falls roar the peaks wear pearls, as the sparrows chirp the millet bends its ears, the kites gather, and the pools, with many flowers where striped bees hum, curve like the lovely rainbow set in the sky; and where strings and song join, where the sruti, flowers and lamps come together and burning agil and sandal give their fragrance, lord of the army with the war spear, may we stay at your feet without parting, with our kin, as one comes to dwell in the town that is rightly one's own.","n":"The closing prayer asks not for gifts but for dwelling: to stay at the god's feet as in one's own rightful town, never parting (பிரியாது), the same word akam uses for lovers. The run of lines 46 to 51 pairs each sound of the hill with what it brings about (falls and pearls, sparrows and millet); எருவை may be the kite or a kind of reed, and lines 43 to 46 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thiruvadi","vazhipaadu","sutram","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 18, lines 43-57","lb":"PR18:43-57","poet":"குன்றம்பூதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அருவி","அருவி",47,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["முத்து","முத்து",47,"pira","scene","pearls (spray)"],["குருவி","குருவி",48,"pul","scene","sparrow"],["தினை","தினை",48,"unavu","scene","millet"],["எருவை","எருவை",49,"pul","scene","kite (or reed)"],["மலரால்","மலர்",50,"maram","simile","flowers"],["சுனை","சுனை",51,"pira","simile","hill pool"],["நரம்பும்","நரம்பு",52,"yaazh","scene","lute strings"],["பூவும்","பூ",53,"maram","scene","flowers"],["அகிலோடு","அகில்",54,"maram","scene","agil incense"],["ஆரமும்","ஆரம்",54,"maram","scene","sandalwood"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:14"},"pr:19:1-18":{"book":"paripadal","num":102,"t":["நில வரை அழுவத்தான் வான் உறை புகல் தந்து,","புல வரை அறியாத புகழ் பூத்த கடம்பு அமர்ந்து,","‘அரு முனி மரபின் ஆன்றவர் நுகர்ச்சி மன்","இரு நிலத்தோரும் இயைக!‘ என, ஈத்த நின்","தண் பரங்குன்றத்து, இயல் அணி, நின் மருங்கு","சாறு கொள் துறக்கத்தவளொடு","மாறு கொள்வது போலும், மயிற்கொடி வதுவை.","புலத்தினும் போரினும் போர் தோலாக் கூடல்,","கலப்போடு இயைந்த இரவுத் தீர் எல்லை,","அறம் பெரிது ஆற்றி, அதன் பயன் கொண்மார்,","சிறந்தோர் உலகம் படருநர் போல,","உரி மாண் புனை கலம் ஒண் துகில் தாங்கி,","புரி மாண் புரவியர், போக்கு அமை தேரர்,","தெரி மலர்த் தாரர், தெரு இருள் சீப்ப, நின்","குன்றொடு கூடல் இடையெல்லாம் ஒன்றுபு","நேர் பூ நிறை பெய்து இரு நிலம் பூட்டிய","தார் போலும், மாலைத் தலை நிறையால்--தண் மணல்","ஆர் வேலை யாத்திரை செல் யாறு."],"p":"","r":"The poet says that at cool Parankunram, which the god seated by the famed kadambu gave so that the people of the wide earth too might share what the worthy of the sages' line enjoy, the adorned wedding with the peacock-like bride beside him seems to vie with that of the bride from heaven; and that at the end of night the crowds of Koodal, never beaten in learning or in war, go out in fine ornaments and bright cloth, on horses and in chariots, wearing chosen garlands and driving the dark from the streets, like doers of great aram setting out for the world of the excellent to take its fruit, so that the whole way from the hill to Koodal, packed with garlanded heads, is like a garland laid on the earth: the cool-sanded road the pilgrims take.","n":"The hill is framed as a gift: heaven's enjoyment brought within reach of ordinary people (lines 1 to 5). The peacock-creeper bride (மயிற்கொடி) is most likely Valli and the one of heaven (துறக்கத்தவள்) the daughter of Indra, so the earthly wedding is said to rival the heavenly one; the construal of lines 5 to 7 is not secure. The festival crowd is pictured through the reward of aram, riders in finery going to heaven, which turns a pilgrimage into a foretaste of the world to come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","valli","vazhipaadu","aram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 19, lines 1-18","lb":"PR19:1-18","poet":"நப்பண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["கடம்பு","கடம்பு",2,"maram","scene","kadamba tree"],["மயிற்கொடி","மயில் கொடி",7,"pul","simile","peacock (the bride)"],["புரவியர்","புரவி",13,"maa","scene","horse"],["மலர்த்","மலர்",14,"maram","ornament","flower garland"],["பூ","பூ",16,"maram","simile","flowers"],["மணல்","மணல்",17,"pira","scene","sand"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்றத்து",5],["town","கூடல்",8]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:15"},"pr:19:19-37":{"book":"paripadal","num":103,"t":["சுடரொடு சூழ்வரு தாரகை மேருப்","புடை வரு சூழல்--புலம் மாண் வழுதி","மட மயில் ஓரும் மனையவரோடும்,","கடன் அறி காரியக் கண்ணவரோடும்--நின்","சூர் உறை குன்றின் தட வரை ஏறி மேல்","பாடு வலம் திரி பண்பின்-- பழ மதிச்","சூடி அசையும் சுவல்மிசைத் தானையின்,","பாடிய நாவின், பரந்த உவகையின்,","நாடும் நகரும் அடைய அடைந்தனைத்தே,","படு மணி யானை நெடியாய்! நீ மேய","கடி நகர் சூழ் நுவலுங்கால்.","தும்பி தொடர் கதுப்ப தும்பி தொடர் ஆட்டி,","வம்பு அணி பூங் கயிறு வாங்கி, மரன் அசைப்பார்--","வண் தார்ப் புரவி வழி நீங்க வாங்குவார்;","திண் தேர் வழியின் செல நிறுப்பார்--கண்டக்","கரும்பு கவழம் மடுப்பார்; நிரந்து","பரி நிமிர் தானையான் பாசறை நீர்த்தே,","குருகு எறி வேலோய்! நின் குன்றக் கீழ் நின்ற","இடை நிலம்: யாம் ஏத்தும் ஆறு!"],"p":"","r":"Like the stars that circle Meru with the sun, the Pandya Vazhuthi of the excellent land, with the women of his household gentle as young peahens and his officers who know their duty, climbs the broad slope of the god's hill where the fearful powers dwell and goes round it to the right, and the whole land and city arrive with cloths swaying on shoulders, singing tongues and spreading joy; and when one tells of the guarded town around the tall lord of the belled elephant, the ground below the hill, where people tie elephants to trees with flowered ropes, lead garlanded horses off the road, halt strong chariots along the way and feed mouthfuls of sugarcane, is like the camp of a king whose army has rearing horses: that is how we praise it.","n":"The king does not go before the god as a ruler receiving homage: he joins the circling crowd, and the poet likens his round to the stars that go round Meru with the sun, so the shrine becomes the centre and the Pandya one of its orbiting lights. The pilgrims' camp below the hill is in turn likened to a war camp, the king's world borrowed to describe a festival.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","vazhipaadu","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 19, lines 19-37","lb":"PR19:19-37","poet":"நப்பண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["மயில்","மயில்",21,"pul","scene","peahen"],["யானை","யானை",28,"maa","scene","elephant"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",30,"pira","scene","bee"],["மரன்","மரம்",31,"maram","scene","tree"],["புரவி","புரவி",32,"maa","scene","horse"],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",34,"maram","gift_food","sugarcane"],["கவழம்","கவழம்",34,"unavu","gift_food","fodder mouthful"],["குருகு","குருகு",36,"pul","scene","crane (in the hill's name)"]],"en":[["hill","மேருப்",19],["king","வழுதி",20],["hill","சூர் உறை குன்றின்",23],["hill","குருகு",36]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:15"},"pr:19:38-57":{"book":"paripadal","num":104,"t":["குரங்கு அருந்து பண்ணியம் கொடுப்போரும்,","கரும்பு கருமுகக் கணக்கு அளிப்போரும்,","தெய்வப் பிரமம் செய்குவோரும்,","கை வைத்து இமிர்பு குழல் காண்குவோரும்,","யாழின் இளி குரல் சமம் கொள்வோரும்,","வேள்லியின் அழகு இயல் விளம்புவோரும்;","கூர நாண் குரல் கொம்மென ஒலிப்ப,","ஊழ் உறமுரசின் ஒலி செய்வோரும்;","என்றூழ் உற வரும் இரு சுடர் நேமி","ஒன்றிய சுடர்நிலை உள்படுவோரும்;","‘இரதி காமன், இவள் இவன்‘ எனாஅ,","விரகியர் வினவ, வினா இறுப்போரும்;","‘இந்திரன், பூசை; இவள் அகலிகை; இவன்","சென்ற கவுதமன்; சினன் உறக் கல் உரு","ஒன்றிய படி இது‘ என்று உரை செய்வோரும்:","இன்ன பலபல எழுத்து நிலை மண்டபம்,","துன்னுநர் சுட்டவும், சுட்டு அறிவுறுத்தவும்,","நேர் வரை விரி அறை வியல் இடத்து இழைக்கச்","சோபன நிலையது--துணி பரங்குன்றத்து","மாஅல் முருகன் மாட மருங்கு."],"p":"","r":"Beside the temple of Maal Murugan on clear Parankunram stands a hall of paintings in its beauty: around it some give food to the monkeys and sugarcane to the black-faced langurs, some play the divine pirama, some set fingers to the flute, some tune the ili and kural strings of the lute, some speak of the beauty of the sacrifice, some beat the drum in order while the taut string hums, and some lose themselves in the picture of the wheel of the two lights that summer brings; some answer the curious who ask who is who, saying 'this is Rati and this Kama', and some explain 'this is Indra as a cat, she is Ahalya, he is Gautama who had gone out, and this is how she took the form of stone in his anger': many such paintings, drawn across the wide rooms of the facing rock, so that visitors point and are taught by pointing.","n":"This is one of the earliest Tamil records of a painted shrine hall and of how it was used: not only looked at but explained aloud, story by story, to visitors who asked (வினவ, வினா இறுப்போரும்; சுட்டவும், சுட்டு அறிவுறுத்தவும்). The paintings are of northern stories (Rati and Kama, Indra, Ahalya and Gautama) beside a Tamil god's temple. தெய்வப் பிரமம் (line 40) is read as a stringed instrument, and what the sun-and-moon picture shows (lines 46, 47) is not certain.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thalam","isai","azhagu","vazhipaadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 19, lines 38-57","lb":"PR19:38-57","poet":"நப்பண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["குரங்கு","குரங்கு",38,"maa","scene","monkey"],["பண்ணியம்","பண்ணியம்",38,"unavu","gift_food","sweet food"],["கரும்பு","கரும்பு",39,"maram","gift_food","sugarcane"],["கருமுகக்","கருமுகம்",39,"maa","scene","black-faced langur"],["குழல்","குழல்",41,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["யாழின்","யாழ்",42,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["முரசின்","முரசு",45,"parai","scene","drum"],["பூசை","பூசை",50,"maa","scene","cat (Indra's disguise)"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்றத்து",56]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:15"},"pr:19:58-66":{"book":"paripadal","num":105,"t":["பிறந்த தமரின் பெயர்ந்து, ஒரு பேதை,","பிறங்கல் இடைஇடைப் புக்குப் பிறழ்ந்து, ‘யான்","வந்த நெறியும் மறந்தேன்; சிறந்தவர்","ஏஎ, ஓஒ!‘ என விளி ஏற்பிக்க,","‘ஏஎ, ஓஒ‘ என்று ஏலா அவ் விளி","அவ் இசை முழை ஏற்று அழைப்ப, அழைத்துழிச்","செல்குவள் ஆங்குத் தமர்க் காணாமை","மீட்சியும், கூஉக் கூஉ மேவும் மடமைத்தே","வாழ்த்து உவப்பான் குன்றின் வகை."],"p":"","r":"A simple girl strays from the kin she came with, wanders in among the rocks and, lost, cries that she has forgotten even the path she came by, calling 'ee! oo!' to her people; the call that they do not take up is taken up by the echoing cave, which calls back, and she goes where it called, finds no kin there, turns back and calls 'koo, koo' once more: such, full of innocence, is the hill of the god who delights in praise.","n":"The innocence (மடமைத்தே) is given to the hill itself: the cave answers a call it does not understand, as artless as the girl who follows its answer, so the joke falls on the hill as much as on her. ஏலா அவ் விளி (line 62) is the call her kin do not answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 19, lines 58-66","lb":"PR19:58-66","poet":"நப்பண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["பிறங்கல்","பிறங்கல்",59,"pira","scene","rock heaps"],["முழை","முழை",63,"pira","scene","cave"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:15"},"pr:19:67-84":{"book":"paripadal","num":106,"t":["நனி நுனி நயவரு சாய்ப்பின் நாறு இணர்ச்","சினை போழ் பல்லவன் தீம் சுனை உதிர்ப்ப,","உதிர்த்த சுனையின் எடுத்த தலைய","அலர் முகிழ் உற, அவை கிடப்ப,","‘தெரி மலர், நனை, உறுவ,","ஐந் தலை அவிர் பொறி அரவம்; மூத்த","மைந்தன்; அருகு ஒன்று மற்று-இளம் பார்ப்பு‘ என","ஆங்கு இள மகளிர் மருள-- பாங்கர்,","பசும்பிடி இள முகிழ், நெகிழ்ந்த வாய் ஆம்பல்,","கைபோல் பூத்த கமழ் குலைக் காந்தள்,","எருவை நறுந் தோடு, எரி இணர் வேங்கை,","உருவம் மிகு தோன்றி, ஊழ் இணர் நறவம்,","பருவம் இல் கோங்கம், பகை மலர் இலவம்;","நிணந்தவை, கோத்தவை, நெய்தவை, தூக்க","மணந்தவை, போல, வரை மலை எல்லாம்","நிறைந்தும், உறழ்ந்தும், நிமிர்ந்தும், தொடர்ந்தும்;","விடியல் வியல் வானம் போலப் பொலியும்--","நெடியாய்! நின் குன்றின்மிசை."],"p":"","r":"On the god's hill a branch bent at its tip with fragrant clusters sheds its shoots into a sweet pool, and the buds lifting their heads from the pool where they fell open as they lie, so that the young girls there are thrown into confusion and say 'chosen flowers and buds; no, a five-headed serpent with shining spots, the elder son, and beside it a young hatchling'; nearby, young pasumpidi buds, loose-mouthed water lilies, kaanthal clusters flowering like hands, fragrant eruvai blades, flame-clustered vengai, splendid thondri, narava in clusters, kongu that flowers out of season and the ilavam with its hostile red fill the slopes, crossing, rising and running on like flowers bound, strung, woven, hung and mixed, and the hill shines like the wide sky at dawn.","n":"The girls' mistake is turned into a small family: a five-headed snake, its elder son and a young hatchling, read off a cluster of floating buds. The list of flowers is the hill's own garland, made by no hand yet described in the five ways garland makers work them.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 19, lines 67-84","lb":"PR19:67-84","poet":"நப்பண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["இணர்ச்","இணர்",67,"maram","scene","flower cluster"],["சுனை","சுனை",68,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["அரவம்","அரவம்",72,"pira","simile","serpent"],["பசும்பிடி","பசும்பிடி",75,"maram","scene","pasumpidi plant"],["ஆம்பல்","ஆம்பல்",75,"maram","scene","water lily"],["காந்தள்","காந்தள்",76,"maram","simile","kaanthal"],["எருவை","எருவை",77,"maram","scene","eruvai reed"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",77,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["தோன்றி","தோன்றி",78,"maram","scene","glory lily"],["நறவம்","நறவம்",78,"maram","scene","narava creeper"],["கோங்கம்","கோங்கம்",79,"maram","scene","kongu tree"],["இலவம்","இலவம்",79,"maram","scene","silk-cotton tree"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:15"},"pr:19:85-105":{"book":"paripadal","num":107,"t":["நின யானைச் சென்னி நிறம் குங்குமத் தால்","புனையா, பூ நீர் ஊட்டி, புனை கவரி சார்த்தா.","பொற் பவழப் பூங் காம்பின் பொற்குடை ஏற்றி,","மலிவுடை உள்ளத்தான் வந்து செய் வேள்வியுள்,","பல் மணம் மன்னு பின் இருங் கூந்தலர்,","கன்னிமை கனிந்த காலத்தார், நின்","கொடி ஏற்று வாரணம் கொள் கவழ மிச்சில்--","மறு அற்ற மைந்தர் தோள் எய்தார்; மணந்தார்","முறுவல் தலையளி எய்தார்--நின் குன்றம்","குறுகிச் சிறப்பு உணாக்கால்.","குறப் பிணாக் கொடியைக் கூடியோய்! வாழ்த்துச்","சிறப்பு உணாக் கேட்டி செவி.","உடையும் ஒலியலும் செய்யை; மற்று ஆங்கே","படையும் பவழக் கொடி நிறம் கொள்ளும்;","உருவும் உருவத் தீ ஒத்தி; முகனும்","விரி கதிர் முற்றா விரி சுடர் ஒத்தி;","எவ்வத்து ஒவ்வா மா முதல் தடிந்து,","தெவ்வுக் குன்றத்துத் திருந்து வேல் அழுத்தி,","அவ் வரை உடைத்தோய்! நீ இவ் வரை மருங்கில்","கடம்பு அமர் அணி நிலை பகர்ந்தேம்;","உடங்கு அமர் ஆயமொடு ஏத்தினம், தொழுதே!"],"p":"","r":"When a man of full heart comes to make a rite, painting the head of your elephant with kunkumam, giving it flower-water, setting chowries on it and raising a golden umbrella on a staff of gold and coral, the maidens with long dark tresses of many scents, whose maidenhood has ripened, will not reach the arms of spotless young men, and the wives will not win the smiling grace of those who married them, unless they come near your hill and eat as a sacred thing the leftover of the mouthful taken by the elephant that bears your banner; you who joined the creeper of a kurava girl, take in with your ear the praise as your sacred food: your dress and cloak are red, your weapon takes the colour of the coral creeper, your form is like fire and your face like the spreading sun before its rays are full; you who cut down the great mango-root of the matchless foe, drove your spear into the hostile hill and broke it, we have told your beautiful stance by the kadambu on this hill and have praised you, worshipping, together with our company.","n":"Two kinds of sacred food are set side by side: the girls must eat the elephant's leftover as சிறப்பு உணா to win husbands, and the god is asked to take the singers' praise as his சிறப்பு உணா through the ear (lines 94 to 96). வாரணம் (line 91) could also mean the cock of his banner, but கவழம், a mouthful, fits the elephant whose decoration opens the passage. The red god of lines 97 to 100 is the image at the shrine, addressed by a company of worshippers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","varaivu","valli","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 19, lines 85-105","lb":"PR19:85-105","poet":"நப்பண்ணனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["யானைச்","யானை",85,"maa","scene","elephant"],["குங்குமத்","குங்குமம்",85,"pira","ornament","kunkumam"],["பூ","பூ",86,"maram","gift_food","flower-water"],["கவரி","கவரி",86,"pira","ornament","chowrie"],["பவழப்","பவழம்",87,"pira","ornament","coral"],["வாரணம்","வாரணம்",91,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கவழ","கவழம்",91,"unavu","gift_food","fodder mouthful"],["கொடியைக்","கொடி",95,"maram","simile","creeper (Valli)"],["பவழக்","பவழம்",98,"pira","simile","coral"],["மா","மா",101,"maram","scene","mango (the foe's form)"],["கடம்பு","கடம்பு",104,"maram","scene","kadamba tree"]],"en":[["hill","தெவ்வுக் குன்றத்துத்",102]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:15"},"pr:20:1-13":{"book":"paripadal","num":108,"t":["கடல் குறைபடுத்த நீர் கல் குறைபட எறிந்து,","உடல் ஏறு உருமினம் ஆர்ப்ப, மலை மாலை","முற்றுபு முற்றுபு, பெய்து--சூல் முதிர் முகில்--","பொருது இகல் புலி போழ்ந்த பூ நுதல் எழில் யானைக்","குருதிக் கோட்டு அழி கறை தெளி பெறக் கழீஇயின்று","காலைக் கடல் படிந்து, காய் கதிரோன் போய வழி","மாலை மலை மணந்து, மண் துயின்ற கங்குலான்","வான் ஆற்றும் மழை தலைஇ; மரன் ஆற்றும் மலர் நாற்றம்,","தேன் ஆற்றும் மலர் நாற்றம், செறு வெயில் உறு கால","கான் ஆற்றும் கார் நாற்றம், கொம்பு உதிர்த்த கனி நாற்றம்;","தான், நாற்றம் கலந்து உடன் தழீஇ வந்து, தரூஉம், வையை.","தன் நாற்றம் மீது, தடம் பொழில் தான், யாற்று","வெந் நாற்று வேசனை நாற்றம் குதுகுதுப்ப,"],"p":"","r":"Clouds that drank the sea until it shrank break on the hills with roaring thunder, rain on them again and again at evening, wash clean the blood on the tusks of an elephant that fought a tiger, and through the sleeping night pour down, so that the Vaiyai arrives bearing, all mixed together, the scent of tree blossom, of honey flowers, of the rain-soaked forest after scorching summer, and of fruit shaken from the boughs, its own fragrance rising above the river's rank flood smell.","n":"The opening is a chain of scents (நாற்றம், five times in lines 8 to 13) rather than of sights: the river is known by what it carries from the hills before it is seen. The elephant's tusk washed of a tiger's blood brings hill violence down into the river that will carry the town's play. வேசனை (line 13) is an unusual word in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 20, lines 1-13","lb":"PR20:1-13","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["கடல்","கடல்",1,"pira","scene","sea"],["முகில்","முகில்",3,"pira","scene","cloud"],["புலி","புலி",4,"maa","scene","tiger"],["யானைக்","யானை",4,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மரன்","மரம்",8,"maram","scene","trees"],["தேன்","தேன்",9,"pira","scene","honey bees"],["கனி","கனி",10,"maram","scene","fruit"],["பொழில்","பொழில்",12,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[["river","வையை",11]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:16"},"pr:20:14-26":{"book":"paripadal","num":109,"t":["ஊர்ஊர் பறை ஒலி கொண்டன்று; உயர் மதிலில்","நீர் ஊர் அரவத்தால் துயில் உணர்பு எழீஇ,","திண் தேர்ப் புரவி வங்கம் பூட்டவும்,","வங்கப் பாண்டியில் திண் தேர் ஊரவும்,","வயமாப் பண்ணுந மதமாப் பண்ணவும்,","கயமாப் பேணிக் கலவாது ஊரவும்,","மகளிர் கோதை மைந்தர் புனையவும்,","மைந்தர் தண் தார் மகளிர் பெய்யவும்,","முந்துறல் விருப்பொடு முறை மறந்து அணிந்தவர்,","ஆடுவார் பொய்தல் அணி வண்டு இமிர் மணல்","கோடு ஏறு எருத்தத்து இரும் புனலில் குறுகி,","மாட மறுகின் மருவி மறுகுற,","கூடல் விழையும் தகைத்து--தகை வையை."],"p":"","r":"Drums sound in town after town; woken by the roar of the water climbing the high wall, people in their haste yoke chariot horses to boats and drive boat-carts as chariots, harness rutting elephants with gear meant for war horses, men put on women's garlands and women men's, all order forgotten in the wish to be first, and they crowd the mansion streets down to the bee-loud sand of the great flood: such is the Vaiyai that Koodal longs for.","n":"Lines 16 to 21 are a set of crossed pairs (horse and boat, horse-gear and elephant, women's garland and men's), each half reversing the other; the comedy of confusion is also love's mixing, which the closing lines of the poem will name as the river's nature. கூடல் in line 26 is both the town's name and 'union'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 20, lines 14-26","lb":"PR20:14-26","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","பேதைமை"]],"k":[["பறை","பறை",14,"parai","scene","drum"],["புரவி","புரவி",16,"maa","scene","horse"],["வங்கம்","வங்கம்",16,"pira","scene","boat"],["வயமாப்","வயமா",18,"maa","scene","horse"],["மதமாப்","மதமா",18,"maa","scene","rutting elephant"],["கயமாப்","கயமா",19,"maa","scene","elephant"],["கோதை","கோதை",20,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தார்","தார்",21,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",23,"pira","scene","bee"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",26],["river","வையை",26]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:16"},"pr:20:27-47":{"book":"paripadal","num":110,"t":["புகை வகை தைஇயினார் பூங் கோதை நல்லார்,","தகை வகை தைஇயினார் தார்;","வகைவகை தைஇயினார் மாலை, மிகமிகச்","சூட்டும் கன்ணியும் மோட்டு வலையமும்","இயல் அணி, அணி நிற்ப ஏறி; அமர் பரப்பின்","அயல் அயல் அணி நோக்கி--ஆங்கு ஆங்கு வருபவர்","இடு வளை ஆரமோடு ஈத்தான் உடனாக,","கெடு வளை பூண்டவள் மேனியில் கண்டு,","நொந்து, ‘அவள் மாற்றாள் இவள்‘ என நோக்க,","தந்த கள்வன் சமழ்ப்பு முகம் காண்மின்;","செருச் செய்த வாளி சீற்றத்தவை அன்ன","நேர் இதழ் உண்கணார் நிரை காடாக,","ஓடி ஒளித்து, ஒய்யப் போவாள் நிலை காண்மின்.","என--ஆங்கு,","ஒய்யப் போவாளை, ‘உறழ்த்தோள் இவ் வாணுதல்‘","வையை மடுத்தால் கடல் எனத் தெய்ய","நெறி மணல் நேடினர் செல்ல, சொல் ஏற்று,","‘செறி நிரைப் பெண்‘ --வல் உறழ்பு--‘யாது தொடர்பு?‘ என்ன","மறலினாள், மாற்றாள் மகள்","வாய் வாளா நின்றாள்,","செறிநகை சித்தம் திகைத்து."],"p":"","r":"Women decked in garlands of every kind climb up and look at one another's finery across the crowd, and there a wife, her husband beside her, sees on another woman's body the bangle and necklace he gave away, and, hurt, marks her as the rival; the poet bids us look at the giver, the thief, and his shamed face, and at the other woman running to hide among women whose eyes are like the arrows of battle, slipping away; the wife's women hunt her along the sand, demand what tie she has with him, and she, defiant at first, stands dumb, her wits gone.","n":"The proof is an object: the bangle the wife lost (கெடு வளை) appears on the rival, so the quarrel that follows is about property as much as love. The poet calls the husband கள்வன், the thief, before anyone in the poem does. Lines 41 to 45 are compressed and the speakers are not named; whether 'மாற்றாள் மகள்' (line 45) is the rival herself or her daughter is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["parathai","oodal","kalavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 20, lines 27-47","lb":"PR20:27-47","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["pira","kandor",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["கோதை","கோதை",27,"maram","ornament","garland"],["தார்","தார்",28,"maram","ornament","garland"],["மாலை","மாலை",29,"maram","ornament","garland"],["வளை","வளை",33,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["ஆரமோடு","ஆரம்",33,"pira","ornament","necklace"],["மணல்","மணல்",43,"pira","scene","sand"]],"en":[["river","வையை",42]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:16"},"pr:20:48-67":{"book":"paripadal","num":111,"t":["ஆயத்து ஒருத்தி, அவளை, ‘அமர் காமம்","மாயப் பொய் கூட்டி மயக்கும் விலைக் கணிகை!","பெண்மைப் பொதுமைப் பிணையிலி! ஐம் புலத்தைத்","துற்றவ துற்றும் துணை இதழ் வாய்த் தொட்டி!","முற்றா நறு நறா மொய் புனல் அட்டி,","காரிகை நீர் ஏர் வயல், காமக் களி நாஞ்சில்,","மூரி தவிர முடுக்கு முது சாடி!","மட மதர் உண்கண் கயிறாக வைத்துத்","தட மென் தோள் தொட்டு, தகைத்து, மட விரலால்","இட்டார்க்கு யாழ் ஆர்த்தும் பாணியில், எம் இழையைத்","தொட்டு, ஆர்த்தும் இன்பத் துறைப் பொதுவி! கெட்டதைப்","பொய்தல் மகளிர் கண் காண இகுத்தந்து, இவ்","வையைத் தொழுவத்துத் தந்து, வடித்து, இடித்து,","மத்திகை மாலையா மோதி, அவையத்துத்","தொடர்ந்தேம்--எருது தொழில் செய்யாது ஓட","விடும் கடன் வேளாளர்க்கு இன்று-படர்ந்து, யாம்,","தன் மார்பம் தண்டம் தரும் ஆரத்தாள் மார்பும்,","நின் மார்பும், ஓர் ஒத்த நீர்மைய கொல்?‘ என்னாமுன்","தேடினாள் ஏச, சில மகளிர் மற்று அதற்கு","ஊடினார், வையையகத்து,"],"p":"","r":"One of the wife's company turns on the other woman: a bought courtesan who mixes love with tricks and lies, whose womanhood is common to all, an old vat that pours raw toddy and drives the plough of love's frenzy through the field of beauty, a herdswoman of the pleasure-ford who strings our jewel like a lute for whoever pays; we brought the stray before the bathing women into this Vaiyai pen, broke him, beat him with garlands for a whip and followed him to the assembly, for farmers do not let an ox run off unworked; is your chest the same as hers whose chest wears the necklace he paid as forfeit?; and while she rails, some women take offence at it there in the Vaiyai.","n":"The abuse is built as a farming allegory: the husband is the household's ox, the courtesan a herdswoman (பொதுவி, also 'woman held in common') who drives him off, and the wife's party the farmers who own the work. The whipping with garlands (மத்திகை மாலையா) turns the river festival's flower-play into a mock punishment. Lines 51, 54 and 64 are obscure in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 20, lines 48-67","lb":"PR20:48-67","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thozhi","parathai",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["நறா","நறா",52,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["நாஞ்சில்","நாஞ்சில்",53,"seythi","scene","plough"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",57,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["மாலையா","மாலை",61,"maram","scene","garland"],["எருது","எருது",62,"maa","scene","ox"],["வேளாளர்க்கு","வேளாளர்",63,"seythi","scene","farmers"]],"en":[["river","வையை",60]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:16"},"pr:20:68-83":{"book":"paripadal","num":112,"t":["‘சிந்திக்க தீரும் பிணியாட் செறேற்க;","மைந்து உற்றாய், வெஞ் சொல்; மட மயிற் சாயலை","வந்திக்க வார்‘ என --- ‘மனத் தகக நோய் இது;","வேற்றாரை வேற்றார் தொழுதல் இளிவரவு;","போற்றாய் காண், அன்னை! புரையோய்! புரை இன்று,","மாற்றாளை மாற்றாள் வரவு.‘","‘அ... சொல் நல்லவை நாணாமல்","தந்து முழவின் வருவாய்! நீ வாய்வாளா;","எந்தை எனக்கு ஈத்த இடு வளை, ஆரப் பூண்","வந்த வழி நின்பால் மாயக் களவு அன்றேல்,","தந்தானைத் தந்தே, தருக்கு.‘","மாலை அணிய விலை தந்தான்; மாதர் நின்","கால சிலம்பும் கழற்றுவான்; சால,","அதிரல் அம் கண்ணி! நீ அன்பன் எற்கு அன்பன்;","கதுவாய்; அவன் கள்வன்; கள்வி நான் அல்லேன்.‘","என ஆங்கு--"],"p":"","r":"Peacemakers ask the wife's side to stop harsh words and go and salute the soft, peacock-graced woman; the answer is that this is a sickness of the heart, that it is shameful for strangers to bow to strangers and unfitting for a rival to go to a rival; the wife tells the silent courtesan that if the bangle and necklace given to her came to her by no trick of theft, let her give back the man who gave them and then be proud; and the courtesan answers that he paid her price for garlands, that he would strip even the anklets from the wife's feet, that the wife's beloved is her beloved too: he is the thief, and she is no thief.","n":"The courtesan's defence turns the word the poet used at line 36 back on the husband: கள்வன் is his, கள்வி she refuses (line 82). Her claim that he paid (விலை தந்தான்) makes the gift a fair purchase and the wife's ornaments his to give. எந்தை (line 76) may be the wife's father, which would make the bangle her own dowry, or her lord; the reading changes how sharp her charge is. Line 74 has a gap, and line 82 கதுவாய் is obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["parathai","oodal","kalavu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 20, lines 68-83","lb":"PR20:68-83","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["thalaivi","parathai",null,"karpu",null],["parathai","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["nakai","எள்ளல்"]],"k":[["மயிற்","மயில்",69,"pul","scene","peacock"],["முழவின்","முழவு",75,"parai","scene","drum"],["வளை","வளை",76,"pira","ornament","bangle"],["மாலை","மாலை",79,"maram","ornament","garland"],["சிலம்பும்","சிலம்பு",80,"pira","ornament","anklet"],["அதிரல்","அதிரல்",81,"maram","ornament","athiral flower"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:16"},"pr:20:84-98":{"book":"paripadal","num":113,"t":["வச்சிய மானே! மறலினை மாற்று; உமக்கு","நச்சினார் ஈபவை நாடு அறிய நும்மவே","சேக்கை இனியார்பால் செல்வான் மனையாளால்","காக்கை கடிந்து ஒழுகல் கூடுமோ? கூடா;","தகவுடை மங்கையர் சான்றாண்மை சான்றார்","இகழினும், கேள்வரை ஏத்தி இறைஞ்சுவார்;","நிகழ்வது அறியாது--நில்லு நீ, நல்லாய்!","‘மகளிரை மைந்துற்று அமர்பு உற்ற மைந்தர்","அகலம் கடிகுவேம்‘ என்பவை யார்க்கானும்","முடி பொருள் அன்று--முனியல்!","கட வரை நிற்குமோ காமம்? கொடி இயலாய்!‘","வையை நீர் மலர்களுடன் வந்து கூடல் மதிலின்","என--ஆங்கு","இன்ன துணியும் புலவியும் ஏற்பிக்கும்,","தென்னவன் வையைச் சிறப்பு,"],"p":"","r":"The onlookers tell the courtesan, doe of Vachchi, to stop her defiance, since what her lovers give is hers in the sight of the land, and tell the wife that a woman whose husband goes to the sweet beds of others cannot spend her life driving crows away: women of worth, even when their husbands slight their virtue, praise and bow to them, so let her stop, for no one has ever managed to bar the chests of men who chase women, and love does not stay within bounds; such resolves and sulks the Pandya's Vaiyai brings on.","n":"The counsel is double: it gives the courtesan her gifts by public right (நாடு அறிய நும்மவே) and asks the wife to accept what cannot be stopped, so the ideal of the wife who bows to her husband (lines 88, 89) is offered as resignation, not as a source of power. வச்சிய (line 84) is unclear: a place or a people. Line 95 is a modern prose heading, not verse; lines 87 to 89 are not in sangamtamil.com.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["karpu","kaamam","oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 20, lines 84-98","lb":"PR20:84-98","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["kandor","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["மானே","மான்",84,"maa","scene","doe"],["காக்கை","காக்கை",87,"pul","scene","crow"],["கொடி","கொடி",94,"maram","scene","creeper"]],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",98],["river","வையை",98]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:16"},"pr:20:99-112":{"book":"paripadal","num":114,"t":["கொடி இயலார் கைபோல் குவிந்த முகை,","அரவு உடன்றவைபோல் விரிந்த குலை,","குடை விரிந்தவை போலக் கோலும் மலர்,","சுனை கழிந்து தூங்குவன நீரின் மலர்,","சினை விரிந்து உதிர்ந்த வீ, புதல் விரி போதொடும்,","அருவி சொரிந்த திரையின் துரந்து;","நெடு மால் கருங்கை நடு வழிப் போந்து--","கடு மா களிறு அணைத்துக் கைவிடு நீர் போலும்","நெடு நீர் மலி புனல், நீள் மாடக் கூடல்","கடி மதில் பெய்யும் பொழுது.","நாம் அமர் உடலும் நட்பும், தணப்பும்,","காமமும் கள்ளும் கலந்து உடன் பாராட்ட,","தாம் அமர் காதலரொடு ஆடப் புணர்வித்தல்","பூ மலி வையைக்கு இயல்பு"],"p":"","r":"Buds folded like the hands of slender women, clusters spread like angry serpents' hoods, flowers opened like parasols, water flowers swept out of the pools, blossoms fallen from the boughs and buds from the bushes, all driven on by the waves the waterfalls poured, come down the middle way, and the full flood falls on the guarded wall of tall-mansioned Koodal like water that a fierce bull elephant holds in its trunk and lets go; and it is the nature of the flower-filled Vaiyai to bring lovers together to play, so that quarrel, friendship, parting, love and toddy are all mixed and praised as one.","n":"The close makes the quarrel of the middle of the poem part of the river's gift: the list in lines 109 and 110 puts the fight and the parting beside friendship and love, so the bitter exchange of wife and courtesan is counted among the festival's pleasures. The hill flowers come down to the town's wall, so kurinji things end in a marutham scene. நெடு மால் கருங்கை (line 105) is obscure in our text.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","oodal","kal","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 20, lines 99-112","lb":"PR20:99-112","poet":"நல்லந்துவனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["முகை","முகை",99,"maram","simile","buds"],["அரவு","அரவு",100,"pira","simile","snake"],["குலை","குலை",100,"maram","simile","flower clusters"],["மலர்","மலர்",101,"maram","simile","flowers"],["சுனை","சுனை",102,"pira","scene","hill pool"],["அருவி","அருவி",104,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["களிறு","களிறு",106,"maa","simile","bull elephant"],["கள்ளும்","கள்",110,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",107],["river","வையை",112]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:16"},"pr:21:1-17":{"book":"paripadal","num":115,"t":["ஊர்ந்ததை--எரி புரை ஓடை இடை இமைக்கும் சென்னி,","பொரு சமம் கடந்த புகழ் சால், வேழம்.","தொட்டதை--தைப்பு அமை சருமத்தின், தாள் இயை தாமரை","துப்பு அமை துவர் நீர்த் துறை மறை அழுத்திய,","வெரிநத் தோலொடு, முழு மயிர் மிடைந்த,","வரி மலி அர உரி வள்பு கண்டன்ன,","புரி மென் பீலிப் போழ் புனை அடையல்.","கையதை--கொள்ளாத் தெவ்வர் கொள் மாமுதல் தடிந்து,","புள்ளொடு பெயரிய பொருப்புப் புடை திறந்த வேல்;","பூண்டதை--சுருளுடை வள்ளி இடை இடுபு இழைத்த","உருள் இணர்க் கடம்பின் ஒன்ணுபடு கமழ் தார்.","அமர்ந்ததை--புரையோர் நாவில் புகழ் நலம் முற்றி,","நிரை ஏழ் அடுக்கிய நீள் இலைப் பாலை","அரை வரை மேகலை, அளி நீர்ச் சூழி,","தரை விசும்பு உகந்த தண் பரங்குன்றம்","‘குன்றத்து அடி உறை இயைக!‘ எனப் பரவுதும்--","வென்றிக் கொடி அணி வெல்வ! நிற் தொழுது."],"p":"","r":"The poet names the god's possessions one by one, what he rides (a war elephant famed for battles won, a fire-bright frontlet flashing on its head), what he has put on (a garment of stitched hide and strips of soft peacock feather, worked like a striped snakeskin strap), what is in his hand (the spear that cut down the mango trunk of the unyielding foe and split open the hill named after a bird), what he wears (a fragrant garland of round kadamba clusters woven through with curling valli creeper) and what he has settled on (cool Parankunram, praised on the tongues of the worthy, girdled at its waist by seven rows of long-leaved paalai trees, with pools of sweet water, a hill that earth and sky delight in), and prays, worshipping the victor with the banner, that dwelling at the foot of that hill be granted.","n":"The unit is a catechism: five neuter nouns (ஊர்ந்ததை, தொட்டதை, கையதை, பூண்டதை, அமர்ந்ததை, 'what he rode, touched, held, wore, settled on') each answered in turn, and the last answer is a place, so the hill is counted among the god's belongings like his spear and garland. The prayer asks for nothing but residence: குன்றத்து அடி, the foot of the hill, also reads as the god's feet. Lines 3 to 7, the garment, are compressed and their syntax is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","thalam","vazhipaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 21, lines 1-17","lb":"PR21:1-17","poet":"நல்லச்சுதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["வேழம்","வேழம்",2,"maa","scene","elephant"],["அர","அரவு",6,"pira","simile","snake"],["பீலிப்","பீலி",7,"pul","ornament","peacock feather"],["வள்ளி","வள்ளி",10,"maram","ornament","valli creeper"],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",11,"maram","ornament","kadamba"],["தார்","தார்",11,"maram","ornament","garland"],["பாலை","பாலை",13,"maram","scene","paalai tree"],["சூழி","சூழி",14,"pira","scene","pool"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்றம்",15]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:21:18-29":{"book":"paripadal","num":116,"t":["சுடு பொன் ஞெகிழத்து முத்து அரி சென்று ஆர்ப்ப,","துடியின் அடி பெயர்த்து, தோள் அசைத்துத் தூக்கி,","அடு நறா மகிழ் தட்ப ஆடுவாள் தகைமையின்,","நுனை இலங்கு எஃகெனச் சிவந்த நோக்கமொடு","துணை அணை கேள்வணைத் துனிப்பவள் நிலையும்;","நிழல் காண் மண்டிலம் நோக்கி,","அழல் புனை அவிர் இழை திருத்துவாள் குறிப்பும்;","பொதிர்த்த முலையிடைப் பூசிக் சந்தனம்","உதிர்த்து, பின் உற ஊட்டுவாள் விருப்பும்;","பல் ஊழ் இவை இவை நினைப்பின், வல்லோன்","ஓவத்து எழுது எழில் போலும்--மா தடிந்-","திட்டோய்! நின் குன்றின்மிசை."],"p":"","r":"On the god's hill one woman, dancing with anklets of gold whose pearl pellets ring as she steps to the drum and swings her arms, held back by the joy of brewed toddy, turns eyes red as a bright spear point on the husband who would embrace her and sulks; another looks into a mirror to see her reflection and sets right her fire-bright jewels; another smears sandal between her swelling breasts, rubs it off, and wants it applied again; thought over and over, says the poet to the god who cut down the mango, these scenes are like the beauty a master paints in a picture.","n":"The three women are given as states (நிலையும், குறிப்பும், விருப்பும்: her stance, her intent, her desire), so the poet paints moods, not deeds, and calls the whole a painting; the sulk, the mirror and the sandal are the household's oodal and toilet carried up to the shrine hill. Line 26 (பின் உற ஊட்டுவாள்) is compressed, and who is to apply the sandal again is our reading; line 21 is a repaired line.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["oodal","azhagu","kal","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 21, lines 18-29","lb":"PR21:18-29","poet":"நல்லச்சுதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":1,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["முத்து","முத்து",18,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["துடியின்","துடி",19,"parai","scene","hand drum"],["நறா","நறா",20,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["சந்தனம்","சந்தனம்",25,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:21:30-38":{"book":"paripadal","num":117,"t":["மிசை படு சாந்தாற்றி போல, எழிலி","இசை படு பக்கம், இரு பாலும் கோலி,","விடு பொறி மஞ்ஞை பெயர்பு உடன் ஆட;","விரல் செறி தூம்பின் விடு துளைக்கு ஏற்ப,","முரல் குரற் தும்பி அவிழ் மலர் ஊத;","யாணர் வண்டினம் யாழ் இசை பிறக்க;","பாணி முழவு இசை அருவி நீர் ததும்ப;","ஒருங்கு பரந்தவை எல்லாம் ஒலிக்கும்-","இரங்கு முரசினான் குன்று."],"p":"","r":"On the hill of the god with the resounding drum, everything sounds at once: the spotted peacock, spreading its tail on both sides like a fan raised overhead at the thunder of the cloud, dances; the humming thumbi blows into the opened flowers as a flute is blown through the holes the fingers close; the new-come swarms of bees bring forth the music of the lute; and the waterfall brims with the sound of the beating drum.","n":"No human player is named: the hill itself is the orchestra, peacock for dancer, thumbi for flute, bees for lute, falls for drum, and the only drum that is a drum is the god's own (இரங்கு முரசினான்), so the god is named by the one instrument the creatures answer. A line before line 30 may have been dropped as a heading (flag).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thalam","azhagu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 21, lines 30-38","lb":"PR21:30-38","poet":"நல்லச்சுதனார்","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",30,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["மஞ்ஞை","மஞ்ஞை",32,"pul","simile","peacock"],["தூம்பின்","தூம்பு",33,"yaazh","simile","flute"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",34,"pira","simile","humming bee"],["மலர்","மலர்",34,"maram","scene","flower"],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",35,"pira","simile","bee swarm"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",35,"yaazh","simile","lute"],["முழவு","முழவு",36,"parai","simile","drum"],["அருவி","அருவி",36,"pira","simile","waterfall"],["முரசினான்","முரசு",38,"parai","scene","war drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:21:39-53":{"book":"paripadal","num":118,"t":["தாழ் நீர் இமிழ் சுனை நாப்பண் குளித்து, அவண்","மீ நீர் நிவந்த விறலிழை, ‘ கேள்வனை","வேய் நீர் அழுந்து தன் கையின் விடுக என,","பூ நீர் பெய் வட்டம் எறிய, புணை பெறாது","அரு நிலை நீரின் அவள் துயர் கண்டு,","கொழுநன் மகிழ் தூங்கி, கொய் பூம் புனல் வீழ்ந்து,","தழுவும் தகை வகைத்து--தண் பரங்குன்று","வண்டு ஆர் பிறங்கல் மைந்தர் நீவிய","தண் கமழ் சாந்தம் தைஇய வளியும்,","கயல் புரை கண்ணியர் கமழ் துகள் உதிர்த்த","புயல் புரை கதுப்பகம் உளரிய வளியும்,","உருள்இணர்க் கடம்பின் நெடுவேட்கு எடுத்த","முருகு கமழ் புகை நுழைந்த வளியும்,","அசும்பும் அருவி அரு விடர்ப் பரந்த-","பசும் பூண் சேஎய்!-நின் குன்றம் நன்கு உடைத்து."],"p":"","r":"A woman in fine jewels who has bathed in the middle of a deep, echoing pool rises above the water; in the play, as a bowl of flower-water is flung, she has no float and is in distress in the deep water, and her husband, seeing it, giddy with joy, drops into the flower-strewn water and embraces her: such is cool Parankunram; and the god's hill, its falls spreading over steep clefts, has these winds in plenty, the wind mixed with the cool fragrant sandal that young men rubbed on, the wind that stirred the cloud-dark hair of fish-eyed women and shook out its scented dust, and the wind that passed through the fragrant smoke raised for the tall Vel of the kadamba.","n":"The three winds (வளியும் three times) carry the hill's three kinds of scent, men's sandal, women's hair, the god's incense, so lovers and worship are one air on the hill. The rescue in the pool turns a moment of danger into the embrace, the husband's joy (மகிழ் தூங்கி) coming before he plunges. Lines 40 to 42 are hard: who is told to let go of whom (கேள்வனை ... விடுக) and who throws the bowl is our reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","thalam","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 21, lines 39-53","lb":"PR21:39-53","poet":"நல்லச்சுதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"kurinji","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["சுனை","சுனை",39,"pira","scene","mountain pool"],["பூ","பூ",42,"maram","scene","flower"],["புனல்","புனல்",44,"pira","scene","water"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",46,"pira","scene","bee"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",47,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["கயல்","கயல்",48,"pira","simile","carp"],["புயல்","புயல்",49,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["கடம்பின்","கடம்பு",50,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["நெடுவேட்கு","நெடுவேள்",50,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"],["அருவி","அருவி",52,"pira","scene","waterfall"]],"en":[["hill","பரங்குன்று",45]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:21:54-70":{"book":"paripadal","num":119,"t":["கண் ஒளிர் திகழ் அடர், இடுசுடர் படர் கொடி மின்னுப் போல்,","ஒண் நகை தகை வகை நெறிபெற இடைஇடை இழைத்து யாத்த","செண்ணிகைக் கோதை கதுப்போடு இயல,","மணி மருள் தேன் மகிழ் தட்ப, ஒல்கிப்","பிணி நெகிழப் பைந் துகில், நோக்கம் சிவப்பு ஊர,","பூங் கொடி போல நுடங்குவாள், ஆங்குத் தன்","சீர்தகு கேள்வன் உருட்டும் துடிச் சீரான்,","கோடு அணிந்த முத்து ஆரம் ஒல்க ஓசிபவள் ஏர்-","ஆடை அசைய, அணி அசைய, தான் அசையும்","வாடை உளர் கொம்பர் போன்ம்.","வாளி புரள்பவை போலும், துடிச் சீர்க்குத்","தோள் ஊழ் பெயர்ப்பவள் கண்","மாறு அமர் அட்டவை மற வேல் பெயர்ப்பவை;","ஆறு இரு தோளவை; அறு முகம் விரித்தவை;","நன்று அமர் ஆயமோடு ஒருங்கு, ‘நின் அடி உறை","இன்று போல் இயைக!‘ எனப் பரவுதும்-","ஒன்றார்த் தேய்த்த செல்வ! நிற் தொழுதே"],"p":"","r":"A woman whose chaplet, set at intervals with bright gold plates flashing like a creeper of lightning, falls along her hair, held back by honey liquor like a gem, sways, her green cloth loosening and her eyes reddening, bending like a flowering creeper to the beat of the drum her worthy husband rolls, her pearl chain swaying on her breasts, her cloth swaying, her ornaments swaying and she swaying like a branch ruffled by the north wind; her eyes as she shifts her shoulders to the drum are like rolling arrows; and the poet, hailing the god who wielded the fierce spear that won the battle, of twelve shoulders and six spread faces, prays with his glad company that their dwelling at the god's feet be granted as it is today, worshipping the lord who wore down his foes.","n":"The husband plays the drum and the wife dances, so a married pair performs the hill's rite together, and the prayer at the close asks that 'today' recur (இன்று போல் இயைக), the same refrain as line 16 with the hill's foot now the god's feet. Lines 66 and 67 may continue the woman's eyes (that drive the spear, as his do) or turn to the god in epithets; we read them as epithets.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vazhipaadu","azhagu","iraivan","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 21, lines 54-70","lb":"PR21:54-70","poet":"நல்லச்சுதனார்","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"],["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மின்னுப்","மின்னு",54,"pira","simile","lightning"],["கோதை","கோதை",56,"maram","ornament","chaplet"],["தேன்","தேன்",57,"unavu","gift_food","honey liquor"],["கொடி","கொடி",59,"maram","simile","creeper"],["துடிச்","துடி",60,"parai","scene","hand drum"],["முத்து","முத்து",61,"pira","ornament","pearl"],["வாடை","வாடை",63,"pira","simile","north wind"],["கொம்பர்","கொம்பர்",63,"maram","simile","branch"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:22:1-16":{"book":"paripadal","num":120,"t":["ஒளிறு வாட் பொருப்பன் உடல் சமத்து இறுத்த","களிறு நிரைத் தவைபோல் கொண்மூ நெரிதர,","அரசு படக் கடந்த ஆனாச் சீற்றத்தவன்","முரசு அதிர்பவைபோல் முழங்கு இடி பயிற்றி,","ஒடுங்கார் உடன்றவன் தானை வில் விசை","விடும் கணை ஒப்பின் கதழ் உறை சிதறூஉ,","கண் ஒளிர் எஃகின் கடிய மின்னி, அவன்","வண்மைபோல் வானம் பொழிந்த நீர்-மண்மிசை","ஆனாது வந்து தொகுபு ஈண்டி, மற்று-அவன்","தானையின் ஊழி....தாவூக் கத்தின்,","போன நிலம் எல்லாம் போர் ஆர் வயல் புகுத-",".... ......... நீக்கிப் பு.. ..... .....","கான மலைத்தரை கொன்று மணல பினறி","வான மலைத்த ......வ ........ ....... ........",".... லைத்தவ மண முரசு எறிதா,","தானைத் தலைத்தலை வந்து மைந்து உற்று,"],"p":"","r":"Clouds press on like the rows of elephants that the shining-sworded lord of hills halted in the battle where bodies fell; thunder rolls like the drum of the one of unceasing wrath who overcame kings; the rain falls scattering swift drops like the arrows loosed by the bows of his army against the unyielding; lightning flashes fierce as his gleaming spear; and the water the sky poured like his bounty gathers on the earth without stopping and, like his army, enters all the land where it went, the fields full of sheaves; the rest of the passage, broken by gaps, speaks of hills and sand, a drum beaten, and the flood coming on head to head in strength.","n":"Every part of the storm is copied from the king: his elephants, drum, arrows, spear and, last, his giving, so the monsoon that makes the Vaiyai is told as the king's war and the king's bounty at once, and the flood that enters the fields is his army taking ground. Lines 10 and 12 to 15 have gaps, and line 13 is garbled.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","vendhan","eegai","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 22, lines 1-16","lb":"PR22:1-16","poet":"தெரியவில்லை","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["களிறு","களிறு",2,"maa","simile","elephant"],["கொண்மூ","கொண்மூ",2,"pira","scene","rain cloud"],["முரசு","முரசு",4,"parai","simile","war drum"],["இடி","இடி",4,"pira","scene","thunder"],["உறை","உறை",6,"pira","scene","raindrops"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:22:17-25":{"book":"paripadal","num":121,"t":["பொறிவி யாற்றுறி--துவர், புகை, சாந்தம்","எறிவன எக்குவ ஈரணிக்கு ஏற்ற,","நறவு அணி பூந் துகில் நன் பல ஏந்தி,","பிற தொழின ....ம் பின்பின் தொடர;","செறி வினைப் பொலிந்த செம் பூங் கண்ணியர்,","ஈர் அமை வெட்சி இதழ் புனை கோதையர்,","தார் ஆர் முடியர், தகை கெழு மார்பினர்;","மாவும், களிறும், மணி அணி வேசரி,","காவு நிறையக் கரை நெரிபு ஈண்டி;"],"p":"","r":"People come carrying red dye, incense and sandal paste, things fit for the wet-dress of the bathing, and many fine flowered cloths, with others following behind; women with red flower chaplets of close work and garlands of petals of fresh vetchi, men with garlands and crowns and handsome chests; and horses, elephants and gem-adorned mules crowd the bank and fill the groves.","n":"ஈரணி, the dress for wet play, names a festival kit: things are brought to be worn in the water. Line 17 is garbled after a dropped heading and line 20 has a gap, so the opening of the list is our reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["azhagu","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 22, lines 17-25","lb":"PR22:17-25","poet":"தெரியவில்லை","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["துவர்","துவர்",17,"pira","ornament","red dye"],["சாந்தம்","சாந்தம்",17,"maram","ornament","sandal paste"],["வெட்சி","வெட்சி",22,"maram","ornament","vetchi flower"],["மாவும்","மா",24,"maa","scene","horse"],["களிறும்","களிறு",24,"maa","scene","elephant"],["வேசரி","வேசரி",24,"maa","scene","mule"],["காவு","கா",25,"maram","scene","grove"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:22:26-35":{"book":"paripadal","num":122,"t":["வேல் ஆற்றும் பொய்ம்பனின், விரை மலர் அம்பினோன்","போல், ஆற்று முன்பின் புனை கழல் மைந்தரொடு,","தார் அணி மைந்தர் தவப் பயன் ஆம் எனக்","கார் அணி கூந்தல், கயற் கண், கவிர் இதழ்,","வார் அணி கொம்மை, வகை அமை மேகலை,","ஏர் அணி இலங்கு எயிற்று, இலங்கு நகையவர்-","‘சீர் அணி வையைக்கு அணிகொல்லோ? வையைதன்","நீர் அணி நீத்தம் இவர்க்கு அணிகொல்? எனத்","தேருநர் தேருங்கால், தேர்தற்கு அரிது காண்-","தீரமும் வையையும் சேர்கின்ற கண் கவின்"],"p":"","r":"With young men in warrior anklets, strong as the god who wields the spear and lovely as the god of the fragrant flower arrows, come women counted the fruit of the garlanded men's penance, with hair dark as the rain cloud, carp eyes, lips like the kavir flower, long-banded breasts, girdles of fine work and bright teeth and smiles; those who try to judge ask whether these are an ornament to the famed Vaiyai or the Vaiyai's ornamented flood an ornament to them, and cannot decide: such is the beauty where the bank and the Vaiyai meet.","n":"The praise is a doubt that will not resolve (தேர்தற்கு அரிது): the river and the women each seem the other's ornament, so the festival beauty belongs to neither alone but to their meeting (தீரமும் வையையும் சேர்கின்ற). The men are given two gods for models at once, Murugan for strength and Kama for beauty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","punarchi","ilamai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 22, lines 26-35","lb":"PR22:26-35","poet":"தெரியவில்லை","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"],["marutkai","புதுமை"]],"k":[["மலர்","மலர்",26,"maram","simile","flower"],["கார்","கார்",29,"pira","simile","rain cloud"],["கயற்","கயல்",29,"pira","simile","carp"],["கவிர்","கவிர்",29,"maram","simile","coral-tree flower"]],"en":[["river","வையை",32]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:22:36-50":{"book":"paripadal","num":123,"t":["மண் கணை முழவின் இன் கண் இமிழ்விற்கு","எதிர்வ பொருவி ..... மேறு மாறு இமிழ்ப்ப,","கவர் தொடை நல் யாழ் இமிழ, காவில்","புகர் வரி வண்டினம் பூஞ் சினை இமிர,","ஊது சீர்த்தீம் குழல் இயம்ப, மலர்மிசைத்","தாது ஊது தும்பி தவிர்பு அல இயம்ப,",".... துடிச் சீர் நடத்த வளி நடன்","மெல் இணர்ப் பூங்கொடி மேவர நுடங்க,","ஆங்கு அவை தத்தம் தொழில் மாறு கொள்ளும்-","தீம் புனல் வையைத் திருமருத முந்துறையால்","கோடுளர் குரல் பொலி ஒலி துயல் இருங் கூந்தல்,","... ..... .......புரை தீர் நெடு மென்","தோள் தாழ்பு தழை மலர் துவளா வல்லியின்,","நீள் தாழ்பு தோக்கை, நித்தில வரிச் சிலம்பு,","...... ........... ......... .........."],"p":"","r":"To the sweet beat of the earth-clay drum something answers sound for sound (the line is broken), the lute of forked strings sounds, in the grove the spotted swarms of bees hum on the flowering branches, the sweet flute plays its measure, the thumbi sipping pollen on the flowers sounds without pause, and, as the hand drum keeps time, the wind, a dancer, makes the soft-clustered flowering creeper sway: so at the Thirumarutham ford of the sweet-watered Vaiyai all these take up each other's work; then, in broken lines, a woman is described, dark heavy hair swinging, long soft shoulders drooping like a creeper whose leaves and flowers do not wilt, a long trailing cloth and anklets striped with pearls.","n":"தத்தம் தொழில் மாறு கொள்ளும், 'they exchange their work', is the point: at the ford the town's instruments and the grove's creatures answer each other, the wind takes the dancer's part and the bees the singer's. Lines 37, 42, 47 and 50 are broken, and the woman of lines 46 to 50 cannot be fully read.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["azhagu","thalam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal 22, lines 36-50","lb":"PR22:36-50","poet":"தெரியவில்லை","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புலன்"]],"k":[["முழவின்","முழவு",36,"parai","scene","drum"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",38,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["காவில்","கா",38,"maram","scene","grove"],["வண்டினம்","வண்டு",39,"pira","scene","bee swarm"],["சினை","சினை",39,"maram","scene","branch"],["குழல்","குழல்",40,"yaazh","scene","flute"],["தும்பி","தும்பி",41,"pira","scene","humming bee"],["துடிச்","துடி",42,"parai","scene","hand drum"],["வளி","வளி",42,"pira","simile","wind"],["பூங்கொடி","பூங்கொடி",43,"maram","simile","flowering creeper"],["வல்லியின்","வல்லி",48,"maram","simile","creeper"],["நித்தில","நித்தில",49,"pira","ornament","pearl"]],"en":[["river","வையை",45]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:17"},"pr:thirattu:1:1-17":{"book":"paripadal","num":124,"t":["வான் ஆர் எழிலி மழை வளம் நந்த,","தேன் ஆர் சிமைய மலையின் இழிதந்து,","நான் மாடக் கூடல் எதிர்கொள்ள, ஆனா","மருந்து ஆகும் தீம் நீர் மலி துறை மேய","இருந்தையூர் அமர்ந்த செல்வ! நின்","திருந்துஅடி தலை உறப் பரவுதும், தொழுது.","ஒருசார்-அணி மலர் வேங்கை, மராஅ, மகிழம்,","பிணி நெகிழ் பிண்டி, நிவந்து சேர்பு ஓங்கி,","மணி நிறம் கொண்ட மலை.","ஒருசார்-தண் நறுந் தாமரைப் பூவின் இடைஇடை","வண்ண வரி இதழ்ப் போதின்வாய் வண்டு ஆர்ப்ப,","விண் வீற்றிருக்கும் கய மீன் விரி தகையின்","கண் வீற்றிருக்கும் கயம்.","ஒருசார்-சாறுகொள் ஓதத்து இசையொடு மாறுஉற்று","உழவின் ஓதை பயின்று, அறிவு இழந்து","திரிநரும், ஆர்த்து நடுநரும், ஈண்டித்,","திரு நயத்தக்க வயல்."],"p":"","r":"Where the river, swollen by the rain of the sky-filling clouds, comes down from the honeyed peaks and is met by four-towered Koodal, at the ghat brimming with sweet water that is a medicine, the singers bow their heads to the well-formed feet of the lord seated at Irunthaiyur and praise him; around him lie, on one side, a hill gone gem-dark with vengai, maraa, makizham and opening asoka trees, on another a pond whose lotus buds hum with bees and whose flowers sit in it like the stars spread in the sky, and on another fields where ploughing noise rivals the festival music of the flood, revellers wander dazed and planters shout, fields fit for the goddess of fortune to desire.","n":"The shrine is placed by water before it is placed by anything else: the river is not named, only traced from rain to hill to the city's welcome, and the ghat's water is called a medicine (மருந்து ஆகும் தீம் நீர், line 4), so the holiness of the site begins as the healing of its ford. The ஒருசார் (on one side) refrain that begins at line 7 lays out the god's surroundings as a ring of landscapes, hill, pond and field, each closed by a one-word line (மலை, கயம், வயல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vazhipaadu","thiruvadi","theerththam","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-17","lb":"PRthirattu:1-17","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","செல்வம்"]],"k":[["எழிலி","எழிலி",1,"pira","scene","cloud"],["மழை","மழை",1,"pira","scene","rain"],["தேன்","தேன்",2,"pira","scene","honey"],["நீர்","நீர்",4,"pira","scene","water"],["வேங்கை","வேங்கை",7,"maram","scene","vengai tree"],["மராஅ","மரா",7,"maram","scene","kadamba"],["மகிழம்","மகிழ்",7,"maram","scene","makizham tree"],["பிண்டி","பிண்டி",8,"maram","scene","asoka"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",10,"maram","scene","lotus"],["வண்டு","வண்டு",11,"pira","scene","bee"],["உழவின்","உழவு",15,"seythi","scene","ploughing"],["நடுநரும்","நடுநர்",16,"seythi","scene","planters"]],"en":[["town","கூடல்",3],["town","இருந்தையூர்",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:18"},"pr:thirattu:1:18-29":{"book":"paripadal","num":125,"t":["ஒருசார்-அறத்தொடு வேதம் புணர் தவம் முற்றி,","விறல் புகழ் நிற்ப, விளங்கிய கேள்வித்","திறத்தின் திரிவு இல்லா அந்தணர் ஈண்டி,","அறத்தின் திரியா, பதி.","ஆங்கு ஒருசார்-உண்ணுவ, பூசுவ, பூண்ப, உடுப்பவை","மண்ணுவ, மணி பொன் மலைய, கடல்,","பண்ணியம், மாசு அறு பயம் தரு காருகப்","புண்ணிய வணிகர் புனை மறுகு ஒருசார்","விளைவதை வினை எவன் மென் புல வன் புலக்","களமர் உழவர் கடி மறுகு பிறசார்","ஆங்க அனையவை நல்ல நனி கூடும் இன்பம்","இயல் கொள நண்ணியவை"],"p":"","r":"On one side is the settlement that does not swerve from aram, where brahmins gather who have completed the penance joined to aram and the Veda, whose fame of mastery stands and who never stray in the way of their learning by hearing; on another the decked street of meritorious merchants and weavers, selling what is eaten, smeared, worn, put on and washed with, gems and gold, the goods of hill and sea and wares that yield clean profit; on another the guarded street of the field hands and ploughmen of soft and hard lands; and on every side such good things meet, so that delight comes in its proper measure.","n":"The catalogue gives each people its virtue in the word that names it: the brahmins' settlement does not swerve from aram (அறத்தின் திரியா, line 21), the merchants are புண்ணிய வணிகர் (line 25), meritorious traders, and the farmers' street is guarded (கடி மறுகு). Merit is assigned to trade as well as to rite, so the shrine town is pictured as a whole social order, not a priestly enclave. Line 26 (விளைவதை வினை எவன்) does not construe as it stands.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["andhanar","aram","vedham","uzhavu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 18-29","lb":"PRthirattu:18-29","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",20,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["மணி","மணி",23,"pira","scene","gem"],["கடல்","கடல்",23,"pira","scene","sea"],["காருகப்","காருகம்",24,"seythi","scene","weaving"],["வணிகர்","வணிகர்",25,"seythi","scene","merchants"],["களமர்","களமர்",27,"seythi","scene","field hands"],["உழவர்","உழவர்",27,"seythi","scene","ploughmen"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:18"},"pr:thirattu:1:30-48":{"book":"paripadal","num":126,"t":["வண்டு பொரேரென எழ,","வண்டு பொரேரென எழும்;","கடிப் புகு வேரிக் கதவமிற் றோட்டி-","கடிப்பு இகு காதில் கனம் குழை தொடர-","மிளிர் மின் வாய்ந்த விளங்கு ஒளி நுதலார்","ஊர் களிற்றன்ன செம்மலோரும்","வாய் இருள் பனிச்சை வரி சிலைப் புருவத்து","ஒளி இழை ஒதுங்கிய ஒண் நுதலோரும்,","புலத்தோடு அளவிய புகழ் அணிந்தோரும்,","நலத்தோடு அளவிய நாண் அணிந்தோரும்,","விடையோடு இகலிய விறல் நடையோரும்","நடை மடம் மேவிய நாண் அணிந்தோரும்,","கடல் நிரை திரையின் கரு நரையோரும்-","மடையர், குடையர், புகையர், பூ ஏந்தி","இடை ஒழிவு இன்றி, அடியுறையார் ஈண்டி,","விளைந்தார் வினையின் விழுப் பயன் துய்க்கும்","துளங்கா விழுச் சீர்த் துறக்கம் புரையும்-","இரு கேழ் உத்தி அணிந்த எருத்தின்","வரை கெழு செல்வன் நகர்."],"p":"","r":"As the bees rise with a rush, there crowd without pause at the shrine of the lord of the hill, whose neck bears the two-coloured mark, the devotees: women with heavy ear-rings and brows bright as lightning, men noble as those who ride elephants, women with dark plaits and bow-curved brows, men adorned with a fame that matches their learning, women adorned with a modesty that matches their beauty, men whose victorious gait rivals the bull, women of gentle gait, old men grey as the ranked waves of the sea, bearing food offerings, umbrellas, incense and flowers, so that the town is like the unshaken heaven where those whose deeds have ripened enjoy their great fruit.","n":"The crowd is drawn in matched pairs, each man's line answered by a woman's (fame matched to learning against modesty matched to beauty, lines 38 and 39; the bull's gait against the gait of innocence, lines 40 and 41), so the worshippers are a society of couples before they are a congregation. The heaven the town resembles is one of enjoyment earned by ripened deeds (விளைந்தார் வினையின் விழுப் பயன் துய்க்கும், line 45), not of release. Who the lord of the hill is (lines 47, 48) is not certain; the two-coloured mark on the neck (உத்தி) may be the spots of a serpent's hood, which would tie this shrine to the serpent lord of lines 83 and 84. Lines 30 to 32 are obscure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["adiyaar","vazhipaadu","vinai","naanam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 30-48","lb":"PRthirattu:30-48","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["வண்டு","வண்டு",30,"pira","scene","bee"],["களிற்றன்ன","களிறு",35,"maa","simile","elephant"],["விடையோடு","விடை",40,"maa","simile","bull"],["திரையின்","திரை",42,"pira","simile","wave"],["பூ","பூ",43,"maram","gift_food","flowers offered"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:18"},"pr:thirattu:1:49-67":{"book":"paripadal","num":127,"t":["வண்டொடு தும்பியும் வண் தொடை யாழ் ஆர்ப்ப,","விண்ட கட கரி மேகமொடு அதிரத்","தண்டா அருவியொடு இரு முழவு ஆர்ப்ப,","அரி உண்ட கண்ணாரொடு ஆடவர் கூடிப்","புரிவுண்ட பாடலொடு ஆடலும் தோன்ற,","சூடு நறவொடு தாமம் முகிழ் விரியச்","சூடா நறவொடு காமம் விரும்ப,","இனைய பிறவும், இவை போல்வனவும்,","அனையவை எல்லாம் இயையும்-புனை இழைப்","பூ முடி நாகர் நகர்.","மணி மருள் தகை வகை நெறி செறி ஒலி பொலி அவிர் நிமிர் புகழ் கூந்தல்","பிணி நெகிழ் துளையினை தெளி ஒளி திகழ் ஞெகிழ் தெரி அரி","மது மகிழ்பு அரி மலர் மகிழ் உண்கண், வாணுதலோர்-","மணி மயில் தொழில் எழில் இகல் மலி திகழ் பிறிது","இகழ் கடுங் கடாக் களிற்று அண்ணலவரோடு,","அணி மிக வந்து இறைஞ்ச, அல் இகப்ப, பிணி நீங்க,","நல்லவை எல்லாம் இயைதரும்-தொல் சீர்","வரை வாய் தழுவிய கல் சேர் கிடக்கைக்","குளவாய் அமர்ந்தான் நகர்."],"p":"","r":"With bees and humming beetles and the well-strung lute resounding, the musth elephant trumpeting with the thunder of the clouds, the twin drums sounding with the unceasing falls, men joined with women whose eyes are streaked red, dance appearing with well-wrought song, garland buds opening with the honey that is worn and desire wanting more with the toddy that is not, all these and others like them meet in the town of the Nagas with flowered crowns; and in the town of the one seated at Kulavaay, on the stony bed that hugs the hill's mouth, women with gem-like rippling hair, anklets and flower-like eyes come richly adorned with lords like musth elephants and bow, the darkness passes, affliction leaves and all good things come together.","n":"Lines 54 and 55 play on one word: the honey that is worn (சூடு நறவு) opens the garland buds, the honey that is not worn, the drunk toddy, makes desire grow; the shrine festival is openly a festival of drink and love, and the poem lists it among the things that befit the god's town without apology. Both shrines end on a நகர் line (58, 67), continuing the shrine-by-shrine walk of lines 47 and 48. Lines 59 to 63 are long strings of attributes that are hard to divide.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["vazhipaadu","inbam","kaamam","kal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 49-67","lb":"PRthirattu:49-67","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["வண்டொடு","வண்டு",49,"pira","scene","bee"],["தும்பியும்","தும்பி",49,"pira","scene","beetle"],["யாழ்","யாழ்",49,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["கரி","கரி",50,"maa","scene","elephant"],["மேகமொடு","மேகம்",50,"pira","scene","cloud"],["அருவியொடு","அருவி",51,"pira","scene","waterfall"],["முழவு","முழவு",51,"parai","scene","drum"],["தாமம்","தாமம்",54,"maram","ornament","garland"],["நறவொடு","நறவு",55,"unavu","gift_food","toddy"],["மயில்","மயில்",62,"pul","scene","peacock"],["களிற்று","களிறு",63,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["town","குளவாய்",67]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:18"},"pr:thirattu:1:68-86":{"book":"paripadal","num":128,"t":["திகழ் ஒளி முந்நீர் கடைந்த அக் கால், வெற்புத்","திகழ்பு எழ வாங்கித் தம் சீர்ச் சிரத்து ஏற்றி,","மகர மறி கடல் வைத்து நிறுத்துப்","புகழ்சால் சிறப்பின் இரு திறத்தோர்க்கும்","அமுது கடைய, இரு வயின் நாண் ஆகி,","மிகாஅ இரு வடம் ஆழியான் வாங்க,","உகாஅ வலியின் ஒரு தோழம் காலம்","அறாஅது அணிந்தாரும் தாம்;","மிகாஅ மறலிய மே வலி எல்லாம்","புகாஅ, எதிர் பூண்டாரும் தாம்;","மணி புரை மாமலை ஞாறிய ஞாலம்","அணிபோல் பொறுத்தாரும் தாஅம்; பணிபு இல் சீர்ச்","செல் விடைப் பாகன் திரிபுரம் செற்றுழிக்","கல் உயர் வென்னி இமய வில் நாண் ஆகித்","தொல் புகழ் தந்தாரும் தாம்","அணங்குடை அருந் தலை ஆயிரம் விரித்த","கணங்கொள் சுற்றத்து அண்ணலை வணங்கி,","நல் அடி ஏத்தி நிற் பரவுதும்-","எல்லேம் பிரியற்க எம் சுற்றமொடு ஒருங்கே"],"p":"","r":"When the bright ocean was churned, it was they who drew up the mountain, bore it on their own heads and set it in the sea of leaping makaras, who for the two famed hosts became the rope at both ends while the discus-bearer pulled the twin cords, and wore it unbroken through ages with strength that did not give; it was they who met head-on all the overwhelming might that came against them; they who bore like an ornament the earth on which the great gem-like mountains stand; and they who, when the rider of the bull destroyed the three cities, became the string of the Himalaya bow and gave it its ancient fame: bowing to that lord with his host who spreads a thousand awful heads and extolling his good feet, we praise you, and ask that none of us be parted, all together with our kin.","n":"Each deed closes on தாம் (they themselves, lines 75, 77, 79, 82), so the churning, the bearing of the earth and the bowstring of the three-city war, which other poems give to Mal or to Shiva, are counted here as the serpent's own fame (தொல் புகழ் தந்தாரும் தாம்). The serpent lord is bowed to first and the god praised through him (நல் அடி ஏத்தி நிற் பரவுதும், line 85), a shrine-order of attendant before master. The closing wish is not for release but that the worshippers and their kin stay together. Line 74 (ஒரு தோழம் காலம்) and lines 76 and 77 are hard to construe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iraivan","amudham","vazhipaadu","sutram"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 68-86","lb":"PRthirattu:68-86","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["முந்நீர்","முந்நீர்",68,"pira","scene","sea"],["வெற்புத்","வெற்பு",68,"pira","scene","mountain"],["மகர","மகரம்",70,"pira","scene","makara fish"],["கடல்","கடல்",70,"pira","scene","sea"],["விடைப்","விடை",80,"maa","scene","bull"]],"en":[["battle","திரிபுரம்",80],["hill","இமய",81]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:18"},"pr:thirattu:2:1-10":{"book":"paripadal","num":129,"t":["மா நிலம் தோன்றாமை மலி பெயல் தலைஇ,","ஏம நீர் எழில் வானம் இகுத்தரும் பொழுதினான்,","நாக நீள் மணி வரை நறு மலர் பல விரைஇ,","காமரு வையை சுடுகின்றே, கூடல்.","'நீர் அணி கொண்டன்று வையை' என விரும்பி,","தார் அணி கொண்ட உவகை தலைக்கூடி,","ஊர் அணி கோலம் ஒருவர் ஒருவரின்","சேர் அணி கொண்டு, நிறம் ஒன்று வெவ்வேறு","நீர் அணி கொண்டநிறை அணி அங்காடி,","ஏர் அணி கொண்டார், இயல்"],"p":"","r":"When heavy rain has poured from the lovely sky so that the wide land vanishes, the Vaiyai comes down gathering many fragrant flowers from the tall gem-bright hill of Naagam to Koodal; the town, glad that the river has put on its ornament of water, puts on garlands and every finery, each person outdoing the next, and the market is filled with wares of every colour.","n":"The word அணி (ornament) rings through lines 5 to 10: the river is adorned with water, the people with garlands, the town with its dress, the market with goods, so river and city dress for each other before anyone bathes. Line 4 reads சுடுகின்றே, which gives no sense; சூடுகின்றே (Koodal wears the Vaiyai like a garland) fits the line of ornament imagery but is a correction.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","punalaadal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-10","lb":"PRthirattu:1-10","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["பெயல்","பெயல்",1,"pira","scene","rain"],["மலர்","மலர்",3,"maram","scene","flowers"],["தார்","தார்",6,"maram","ornament","garland"]],"en":[["hill","நாக நீள் மணி வரை",3],["river","வையை",4],["town","கூடல்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:19"},"pr:thirattu:2:11-27":{"book":"paripadal","num":130,"t":["கை புனை தாரினர், கண்னியர்,","ஐ எனும் ஆவியர், ஆடையர்,","நெய் அணி கூந்தலர், பித்தையர்,","மெய் அணி யானை மிசையராய், ஒய்யெனத்","தங்காச் சிறப்பின் தளிர் இயலார் செல்ல;","பொங்கு புரவிப்புடைப் போவோரும், பொங்கு சீர்","வையமும் தேரும் அமைப்போரும்; எவ் வாயும்","பொய்யாம் போய் என்னாப் புடை கூட்டிப் போவநர்","மெய்யாப்பு வெய் ஆர மூடுவார்; வையத்துக்கு","கூடுவார்; ஊடல் ஒழிப்பார்; உணர்குவார்:","ஆடுவார், பாடுவார்; ஆர்ப்பார், நகுவார்; நக்கு","ஓடுவார்; ஓடித் தளர்வார்; போய், உற்றவரைத்","தேடுவார்: ஊர்க்குத் திரிவார் இலராகி-","கற்றாரும், கல்லாதவரும், கயவரும்,","பெற்றாரும், பெற்றான் பிழையாத பெண்டிரும்,","பொற்றேரான் தானும், பொலம் புரிசைக் கூடலும்,","முற்றின்று-வையைத் துறை."],"p":"","r":"Women soft as tender shoots ride out on decked elephants, with garlands in their hands, wreaths, fine scent, dress, oiled hair and tied locks; others go beside prancing horses or harness carts and chariots, wrap themselves against the heat, make up their quarrels, dance, sing, shout, laugh, run and tire and search for their own people, until no one is left wandering in the town: the learned and the unlearned, the base, parents, wives who never fail their husbands, the king of the golden chariot and gold-walled Koodal itself crowd the Vaiyai's ford.","n":"The list in lines 24 to 27 deliberately levels the city: learning and its lack, the low and the chaste, the king and the walls themselves, all end at one ford, and line 23 says that nobody stays behind. Lines 18 and 19 are hard to construe in our text; the reading of them as people wrapping against the heat is a guess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","inbam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 11-27","lb":"PRthirattu:11-27","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["தாரினர்","தார்",11,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கண்னியர்","கண்ணி",11,"maram","ornament","head wreath"],["யானை","யானை",14,"maa","scene","elephant"],["புரவிப்புடைப்","புரவி",16,"maa","scene","horse"]],"en":[["king","பொற்றேரான்",26],["town","கூடலும்",26],["river","வையை",27]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:19"},"pr:thirattu:2:28-45":{"book":"paripadal","num":131,"t":["துறை ஆடும் காதலர் தோள் புணையாக,","மறை ஆடுவாரை அறியார் மயங்கிப்","பிறை ஏர் நுதலியர் எல்லாரும் தம் முன்","நிகழும் நிகழ்ச்சி எம்பால் என்று, ஆங்கே","இகல் பல செல்வம் விளைத்தவட் கண்டு, இப்பால்,","அகல் அல்கும் வையைத் துறை,","காதலான் மார்பின் கமழ் தார், புனல் வாங்கி,","ஏதிலாள் கூந்தலிடைக் கண்டு, 'மற்று அது","தா தா' என்றாளுக்குத்,' தானே புறன் தந்து","வேய்தந்தது'. 'என்னை? விளைந்தமை மற்று அது","நோதலே செய்யேன், நுணங்கு இழையாய்! இச் செவ்வி","போதல் உண்டாம்கொல்? அறிந்து புனல் புணர்த்தது!","ஓஓ! பெரிதும் வியப்பு","கயத் தகக பூப் பெய்த காமக் கிழமை","நயத் தகு நல்லாளைக் கூடுமா கூடும்","முயக்குக்கு, செவ்வி முலையும் முயக்கத்து","நீரும் அவட்குத் துணை; கண்ணின் நீர் விட்டோய்!","நீயும் அவட்குத் துணை"],"p":"","r":"At the ford where lovers bathe with each other's shoulders for rafts, the crescent-browed women, not seeing who bathes in secret, each take whatever happens before them to be about themselves; one, seeing the flood carry the fragrant garland from her lover's chest into a stranger's hair, cries 'give it, give it', and the other answers that the flood itself turned and set it there: why should she grieve at what fell out, would such a chance come twice, the river joined it knowingly, what a wonder; and the weeping woman is told that the rival's young breasts and the water serve the other's embrace, and that her own tears serve it too.","n":"The rival's defence makes the flood an accomplice that 'knew' what it was doing (அறிந்து புனல் புணர்த்தது), so the festival river replaces the go-between of the akam poems. The last lines turn on the wife: her tears, like the water, only help the other woman, a hard comfort that tells her weeping is a loss in the quarrel. The speakers are not named and line 41 (கயத் தகக) is corrupt, so the division of voices is a reading.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 28-45","lb":"PRthirattu:28-45","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[["parathai","thalaivi",null,"karpu",null]],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","புதுமை"],["azhugai","இழவு"]],"k":[["பிறை","பிறை",30,"pira","simile","crescent moon"],["தார்","தார்",34,"maram","ornament","garland"],["புனல்","புனல்",34,"pira","scene","flood water"],["பூப்","பூ",41,"maram","ornament","flowers"]],"en":[["river","வையை",33]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:19"},"pr:thirattu:2:46-57":{"book":"paripadal","num":132,"t":["பணிவு இல் உயர் சிறப்பின் பஞ்சவன் கூடல்,","மணி எழில், மா மேனி, முத்த முறுவல்,","அணி பவளச் செவ் வாய், அறம் காவற் பெண்டிர்","மணி அணிந்த தம் உரிமை மைந்தரோடு ஆடித்","தணிவின்று, வையைப் புனல்","'புனலூடு போவது ஓர் பூ மாலை கொண்டை,","எனலூழ் வகை எய்திற்று' என்று ஏற்றுக்கொண்ட","புனலூடு நாடு அறியப் பூ மாலை அப்பி,","நினைவாரை நெஞ்சு இடுக்கண் செய்யும் கனல்புடன்,","கூடாமுன், ஊடல் கொடிய திறம் கூடினால்","ஊடாளோ? ஊர்த்து அலர் வந்து ஊர்ந்து,","என ஆங்கு-"],"p":"","r":"In the Pandya's unbowed Koodal the wives who guard virtue, gem-fair, dark-bodied, pearl-smiling and coral-lipped, play without pause in the Vaiyai's flood with their own jewelled husbands; and one woman, saying that a garland drifting down the flood came to her hair-knot by the turn of fate, takes it and wears it plainly for the land to see, with a heat that wounds the hearts of those who love; before the lovers meet the sulk grows cruel, and once they meet will she not sulk? The gossip of the town comes and spreads.","n":"The unit sets two kinds of women in one flood: the wives who guard aram and bathe with their own husbands, and the woman who wears a floating garland as fate's gift so that everyone may see it. The open wearing is the point: what the river delivers by chance becomes a public sign, and the town's அலர் follows. Lines 51 to 56 are knotted in our text and the reading of who sulks is not secure.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["oodal","alar","karpu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 46-57","lb":"PRthirattu:46-57","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["முத்த","முத்து",47,"pira","scene","pearl"],["பவளச்","பவளம்",48,"pira","scene","coral"],["மணி","மணி",49,"pira","ornament","gems"],["மாலை","பூ மாலை",51,"maram","ornament","flower garland"]],"en":[["king","பஞ்சவன்",46],["town","கூடல்",46],["river","வையை",50]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:19"},"pr:thirattu:2:58-71":{"book":"paripadal","num":133,"t":["'ஈப் பாய் அடு நறாக் கொண்டது, இவ் யாறு' எனப்","பார்ப்பார் ஒழிந்தார், படிவு.","'மைந்தர் மகளிர் மண விரை தூவிற்று' என்று,","அந்தணர் தோயலர், ஆறு,","'வையை தேம் மேவ வழுவழுப்பு உற்றென'","ஐயர், வாய்பூசுறார், ஆறு.","விரைபு இரை விரை துறை கரை அழிபு இழிபு ஊர","ஊர்தரும் புனல்,","கரையொடு கடலிடை வரையொடு கடலிடை நிரைநிரை","நீர் தரு நுரை,","நுரையுடன் மதகுதொறு இழிதரு புனல் கரை புரளிய","செலும்மறி கடல்,","புகும் அளவுஅளவு இயல் இசை சிறை தணிவின்று,","வெள்ள மிகை."],"p":"","r":"Saying that the river has taken in brewed toddy that bees swarm over, the brahmins give up their dip; saying that it carries the wedding scents young men and women have scattered, the anthanar will not bathe; saying that the Vaiyai is slimy with honey, the aiyar will not even rinse their mouths; and the flood, rushing, roaring, breaking the banks and pouring down, piles foam in rows between bank, hill and sea, spills over every sluice and rolls on to the sea, overflowing every dam.","n":"Three names for the priestly class (பார்ப்பார், அந்தணர், ஐயர்) each refuse for a different stain, toddy, lovers' scent and honey, and each stain is a sign of the festival the rest of the town is enjoying: what bars the priest is what makes the river a feast. Lines 64 to 71 stack verbs and nouns without connectives so that the sound runs like the water; the short lines 65, 67, 69 and 71 are probably the half-lines the source leaves unnumbered.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["andhanar","thoomai","punalaadal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 58-71","lb":"PRthirattu:58-71","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["ஈப்","ஈ",58,"pira","scene","bees"],["நறாக்","நறா",58,"unavu","scene","toddy"],["பார்ப்பார்","பார்ப்பான்",59,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["விரை","விரை",60,"pira","scene","perfume"],["அந்தணர்","அந்தணர்",61,"seythi","scene","brahmins"],["தேம்","தேம்",62,"unavu","scene","honey"],["நுரை","நுரை",67,"pira","scene","foam"],["கடல்","கடல்",69,"pira","scene","sea"]],"en":[["river","வையை",62]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:19"},"pr:thirattu:2:72-87":{"book":"paripadal","num":134,"t":["வரை பல புரை உயர் கயிறு அணி பயில் தொழில்","மணி அணி யானை மிசை, மைந்தரும் மடவாரும்,","நிரைநிரை குழீஇயினர் உடன் சென்று,","குரு மணி யானை இயல் தேர்ப் பொருநன்","திருமருத முன்துறை முற்றம் குறுகித்,","தெரி மருதம் பாடுப, பிணி கொள் யாழ்ப் பாணர்,","பாடிப் பாடி, பாய் புனல்","ஆடி ஆடி, அருளியவர்","ஊடி ஊடி, உணர்த்தப் புகன்று","கூடிக் கூடி, மகிழ்பு மகிழ்பு,","தேடித் தேடி, சிதைபு சிதைபு,","சூடிச் சூடி, தொழுது தொழுது,","மழுபொடு நின்ற மலி புனல் வையை","விழு தகை நல்லாரும் மைந்தரும் ஆடி,","இமிழ்வது போன்றது, இந் நீர்--குணக்குச் சான்றீர்!","முழுவதும் மிச்சிலா உண்டு."],"p":"","r":"On jewelled elephants tall as many hills, young men and women gather in rows and go together to the front ford of Tirumarutham of the king of the gem-hung elephants and chariots, where bards with tight-strung lutes sing the chosen marutham mode; singing and singing, bathing and bathing in the leaping flood, sulking and sulking, longing to be won back, uniting again and again, glad and glad, searching and searching, worn and worn, wearing flowers and bowing again and again, the good women and men bathe in the full Vaiyai until it seems to roar, and the water, the poet tells the worthies of the east, has been wholly used, left like leavings.","n":"Lines 78 to 83 double every verb (பாடிப் பாடி, ஆடி ஆடி, ஊடி ஊடி, கூடிக் கூடி) so the cycle of sulking and reunion becomes the rhythm of the bathing itself, sung to the marutham mode that belongs to that quarrel. Lines 84 to 87 are obscure: மழுபொடு is not a word we can fix, and the address குணக்குச் சான்றீர் and the close on leavings (மிச்சிலா) are read tentatively.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punalaadal","oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 72-87","lb":"PRthirattu:72-87","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"marutham","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"oodal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["யானை","யானை",73,"maa","simile","elephant"],["மணி","மணி",73,"pira","ornament","bells"],["மருதம்","மருதம்",77,"yaazh","scene","marutham mode"],["யாழ்ப்","யாழ்",77,"yaazh","scene","lute"],["பாணர்","பாணன்",77,"seythi","scene","bards"],["புனல்","புனல்",78,"pira","scene","flood water"]],"en":[["king","பொருநன்",75],["river","வையை",84]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:19"},"pr:thirattu:2:88-101":{"book":"paripadal","num":135,"t":["சாந்தும், கமழ் தாரும், கோதையும், சுண்ணமும்,","கூந்தலும் பித்தையும் சோர்ந்தன பூவினும், அல்லால்,","சிறிதானும் நீர் நிறம்","தான் தோன்றாது--இவ் வையை ஆறு,","மழை நீர் அறு குளத்து வாய்பூசி ஆடும்","கழு நீர மஞ்சனக் குங்குமக் கலங்கல்","வழி நீர்; விழு நீர அன்று--வையை","வெரு வரு கொல் யானை வீங்கு தோள் மாறன்,","உரு கெழு கூடலவரொடு, வையை","வரு புனல் ஆடிய தன்மை பொருவுங்கால்-","இரு முந்நீர் வையம் பிடித்து என்னை? யான் ஊர்க்கு","ஒரு நிலையும் ஆற்ற இயையா! அரு மரபின்,","அந்தர வான் யாற்று, ஆயிரம் கன்ணினான்","இந்திரன் ஆடும் தகைத்து"],"p":"","r":"Apart from sandal paste, fragrant garlands, wreaths, scented powder and the flowers slipped from women's hair and men's locks, the water of this Vaiyai shows no colour of its own: it is not clean water but the run-off, cloudy with saffron and bath paste, of those who rinse their mouths at the rain-filled tank and bathe; and when one weighs how Maran of the swelling shoulders and the dreaded killing elephants bathed in the coming flood with the fierce people of Koodal, what is holding the sea-girt world beside it? it is like Indra of the thousand eyes bathing in the river of the high sky.","n":"The closing praise turns the impurity the priests shunned (lines 58 to 63) into glory: the river has no colour but the town's ornaments, and the king bathing in that very water is likened to Indra in the heavenly river. Lines 98 and 99 (இரு முந்நீர் வையம் பிடித்து என்னை? யான் ஊர்க்கு ஒரு நிலையும் ஆற்ற இயையா) are uncertain; they may mean that ruling the whole world is nothing beside this bathing, or that the speaker cannot match the sight with any state of the town.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["vendhan","punalaadal","azhagu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 88-101","lb":"PRthirattu:88-101","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["சாந்தும்","சாந்து",88,"pira","ornament","sandal paste"],["தாரும்","தார்",88,"maram","ornament","garland"],["கோதையும்","கோதை",88,"maram","ornament","garland"],["சுண்ணமும்","சுண்ணம்",88,"pira","ornament","scented powder"],["பூவினும்","பூ",89,"maram","ornament","flowers"],["மழை","மழை",92,"pira","scene","rain"],["குங்குமக்","குங்குமம்",93,"maram","ornament","saffron"],["யானை","யானை",95,"maa","scene","elephant"]],"en":[["king","மாறன்",95],["river","வையை",96]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:19"},"pr:thirattu:3":{"book":"paripadal","num":136,"t":["அறவோர் உள்ளார் அரு மறை காப்ப,","..... ...... ....... .......","செறுநர் விழையாச் செறிந்த நங் கேண்மை","மறுமுறை யானும் இயைக! நெறி மாண்ட","தண் வரல் வையை எமக்கு."],"p":"","r":"With the righteous there to guard the rare scripture, the speaker prays that the close bond between us, which no enemy could wish to break, be joined to us again in the next round as well, and that the cool-flowing Vaigai of excellent course be ours.","n":"மறுமுறை may mean the next birth or simply the next turn of the festival, when the bathers hope to come to the river again; the lost line 2 kept whatever joined the guardians of the scripture to the prayer, so the bond (நங் கேண்மை) may be the lovers' or the worshippers' with the river.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["marumai","natpu","vedham"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-5","lb":"PRthirattu:1-5","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[],"en":[["river","வையை",5]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:4":{"book":"paripadal","num":137,"t":["தெரிமாண் தமிழ் மும்மைத் தென்னம் பொருப்பன்","பரிமா நிரையின் பரந்தன்று வையை."],"p":"","r":"The Vaigai spread out like the ranks of horses of the Pandya, lord of the southern mountain, who is versed in the threefold Tamil.","n":"The king is named not by conquest but by learning (தெரிமாண் தமிழ் மும்மை, the threefold Tamil of verse, song and drama), and the flood borrows its force from his horses, so river, king and Tamil are praised in one breath.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","vendhan","padai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-2","lb":"PRthirattu:1-2","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["பரிமா","பரிமா",2,"maa","simile","horse"]],"en":[["king","தென்னம் பொருப்பன்",1],["river","வையை",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:5":{"book":"paripadal","num":138,"t":["மண் ஆர்ந்து இசைக்கும் முழவொடு கொண்ட தோள்","கண்ணாது உடன் வீழுங் காரிகை! கண்டோர்க்குத்","தம்மொடு நிற்குமோ நெஞ்சு?"],"p":"","r":"Addressing a lovely woman whose shoulders keep time with the clay-smeared drum and fall in unison with it without her seeming to heed, the speaker asks whether the hearts of those who watch her can stay with them.","n":"The syntax of lines 1 and 2 is open: கொண்ட தோள் may be her shoulders moving with the drum in a dance, or a man's drum-like shoulders into whose embrace she falls without a thought; on either reading the closing question turns her beauty into the onlookers' loss of heart, the love-at-sight that the fragment leaves without a named lover.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kaamam","nenju"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-3","lb":"PRthirattu:1-3","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[],"k":[["முழவொடு","முழவு",1,"parai","scene","drum"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:6":{"book":"paripadal","num":139,"t":["முன்பு உற்று அறியா முதல் புணர்ச்சி மொய் குழலை","இன்பு உற்று அணிந்த இயல் அணியும் வன் பணியும்","நாண் எனும் தொல்லை அணி என்ன நல்நுதலை ... ... ...னந்து"],"p":"","r":"At a first union she had never known before, the girl with thick tresses wore with joy her natural beauty and her strong jewels, and with them modesty, called her ancient ornament; the rest of the line is lost.","n":"Three ornaments are ranked: the beauty she was born with (இயல் அணி), the jewels she put on (வன் பணி), and modesty, which is called older than both (தொல்லை அணி), so the shame that might seem to hold her back at the first union is counted as her oldest adornment.","x":0,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","naanam","azhagu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-3","lb":"PRthirattu:1-3","pat":"","ap":"akam","th":"kurinji","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","புணர்வு"]],"k":[["பணியும்","பணி",2,"pira","ornament","jewel"]],"en":[],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:7":{"book":"paripadal","num":140,"t":["உலகம் ஒரு நிறையாத் தான் ஓர் நிறையாப்","புலவர் புலக் கோலால் தூக்க, உலகு அனைத்தும்","தான் வாட, வாடாத தன்மைத்தே- தென்னவன்","நான்மாடக் கூடல் நகர்."],"p":"","r":"When poets set the world in one pan and the city in the other and weighed them with the balance of their learning, the whole world drooped while the Pandya's four-towered Koodal stayed unfaded.","n":"வாட does double work: in a balance the pan that goes down is the heavier, but வாடு is also to wither, so the world 'sinks' and 'fades' at once while Koodal, the one that does not fade (வாடாத), is praised for a permanence the scale cannot measure; the judges are poets, not kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","arivu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-4","lb":"PRthirattu:1-4","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[],"en":[["king","தென்னவன்",3],["town","நான்மாடக் கூடல்",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:8":{"book":"paripadal","num":141,"t":["மாயோன் கொப்பூழ் மலர்ந்த தாமரைப்","பூவொடு புரையும், சீர் ஊர்; பூவின்","இதழகத்து அனைய தெருவம்; இதழகத்து","அரும் பொகுட்கு அனைத்தே, அண்ணல் கோயில்;","தாதின் அனையர், தண் தமிழ்க் குடிகள்;","தாது உண் பறவை அனையர், பரிசில் வாழ்நர்;","பூவினுள் பிறந்தோன் நாவினுள் பிறந்த","நான்மறைக் கேள்வி நவில் குரல் எடுப்ப","ஏம இன் துயில் எழிதல் அல்லதை,","வாழிய வஞ்சியும் கோழியும் போலக்","கோழியின் எழாது, எம் பேர் ஊர் துயிலே."],"p":"","r":"Our great town is like the lotus that blossomed from Mayon's navel: its streets are the petals, the lord's palace the pericarp, its Tamil households the pollen and those who live on gifts the bees that eat it; it wakes from its safe sweet sleep only to the voices reciting the four Vedas born from the tongue of Brahma born in that flower, and not, like Vanji and Kozhi, to the cock's crow.","n":"The flower is a map of a society: palace at the centre, households as the pollen that is the flower's wealth, and gift-seekers as the bees that feed on the households, not on the palace. The last lines pun on கோழி, the Chola city Uraiyur and the cock (line 11): the rival capitals wake to a bird, Madurai to the Veda, so the praise ranks the three capitals by the sound each wakes to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thamizh","parisil","vedham","kudi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-11","lb":"PRthirattu:1-11","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["marutkai","பெருமை"]],"k":[["மாயோன்","மாயோன்",1,"deyvam","scene","Vishnu"],["தாமரைப்","தாமரை",1,"maram","simile","lotus"],["தாதின்","தாது",5,"pira","simile","pollen"],["பறவை","பறவை",6,"pira","simile","bee"],["கோழியின்","கோழி",11,"pul","scene","cock"]],"en":[["town","வஞ்சி",10],["town","கோழி",10]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:9":{"book":"paripadal","num":142,"t":["தண் தமிழ் வேலித் தமிழ்நாட்டகம் எல்லாம்","நின்று நிலைஇப் புகழ் பூத்தல் அல்லது,","குன்றுதல் உண்டோ மதுரை-கொடித் தேரான்","குன்றம் உண்டாகும் அளவு?"],"p":"","r":"Can Madurai ever decline, rather than stand firm and blossom in fame throughout the Tamil land fenced by cool Tamil, as long as the hill of the king with the bannered chariot endures?","n":"The land is fenced not by mountains or sea but by its language (தண் தமிழ் வேலி). This is the first of three fragments built on one frame (thirattu 9, 10, 11): each ends 'as long as the king's ... exists', and the measure moves from the hill to the river to his word. Whose hill is meant (the Pandya's, or the god's Parankundram) the fragment does not settle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","thamizh","naadu"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-4","lb":"PRthirattu:1-4","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[],"en":[["town","மதுரை",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:10":{"book":"paripadal","num":143,"t":["செய்யாட்கு இழைத்த திலகம்போல், சீர்க்கு ஒப்ப,","வையம் விளங்கிப் புகழ் பூத்தல் அல்லது,","பொய்யாதல் உண்டோ மதுரை--புனை தேரான்","வையை உண்டாகும் அளவு?"],"p":"","r":"Like the tilaka drawn on the brow of the lady of fortune, matching her beauty, Madurai lights the world and blossoms in fame; can it ever prove false as long as the Vaigai of the king of the adorned chariot flows?","n":"The world is the goddess's face and Madurai the mark at its centre: the simile makes the city the one point that gives the whole its auspiciousness, and பொய்யாதல் ('turning false') treats fame as a promise the city keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","azhagu","selvam"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-4","lb":"PRthirattu:1-4","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[["செய்யாட்கு","செய்யாள்",1,"deyvam","simile","Lakshmi"]],"en":[["town","மதுரை",3],["river","வையை",4]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:11":{"book":"paripadal","num":144,"t":["கார்த்திகை காதில் கன மகர குண்டலம்போல்,","சீர்த்து விளங்கித் திருப் பூத்தல் அல்லது,","கோத்தை உண்டாமோ மதுரை-கொடித் தேரான்","வார்த்தை உண்டாகும் அளவு?"],"p":"","r":"Like the heavy makara earring on the ear of Karthigai, Madurai shines in excellence and blossoms in prosperity; can any loss come to it as long as the word of the king of the bannered chariot stands?","n":"கோத்தை is a rare word; its place in the frame, where the sister fragments have குன்றுதல் (decline) and பொய்யாதல் (proving false), fixes it as loss or defect. Karthigai is the asterism pictured as a woman, so the city is a jewel in the sky's ear. The third measure is வார்த்தை, the king's word or renown, so the city's endurance ends tied to speech rather than to hill or river.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","selvam","sol"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-4","lb":"PRthirattu:1-4","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","புகழ்மை"]],"k":[],"en":[["town","மதுரை",3]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:12":{"book":"paripadal","num":145,"t":["ஈவாரைக் கொண்டாடி, ஏற்பாரைப் பார்த்து உவக்கும்--","சேய் மாடக் கூடலும், செவ்வேள் பரங்குன்றம்,","வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார் எனப்படுவார்; மற்றையார்","போவார் ஆர், புத்தேள் உலகு?"],"p":"","r":"High-towered Koodal and the red Vel's Parankundram, which honour those who give and rejoice to see those who receive: those who live there are the ones rightly called living, and who among the rest would go to the world of the gods?","n":"The city is praised for rejoicing at the sight of receivers (ஏற்பாரைப் பார்த்து உவக்கும்), not only at givers: asking is part of the honoured life, not its shame. Line 4 is compressed; it may ask who would go to the gods' world, or who among those not living there is going to it, but either way heaven is ranked below life in Koodal.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","marumai"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-4","lb":"PRthirattu:1-4","pat":"","ap":"puram","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["perumitham","கொடை"]],"k":[["செவ்வேள்","செவ்வேள்",2,"deyvam","scene","Murugan"]],"en":[["town","கூடலும்",2],["hill","பரங்குன்றம்",2]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"},"pr:thirattu:13":{"book":"paripadal","num":146,"t":["'வையை வருபுனல் ஆடல் இனிதுகொல்?","செவ் வேற் கோ குன்றம் நுகர்தல் இனிதுகொல்?","வை வேல் நுதி அன்ன கண்ணார் துணையாக","எவ்வாறு செயவாம்கொல், யாம்?\" என, நாளும்,","வழி மயக்குற்று மருடல் நெடியான்","நெடு மாடக் கூடற்கு இயல்பு."],"p":"","r":"Is it sweeter to bathe in the Vaigai's rising flood, or to enjoy the hill of the king of the red spear, and how shall we spend it with women whose eyes are like the sharp spear's point as our companions? To be lost every day in such doubt about which way to go is the nature of tall-towered Koodal of Nediyon.","n":"The praise is put as a complaint: the city has so many pleasures (river, sacred hill, women) that its people lose their way among them daily (வழி மயக்குற்று மருடல்). The love matter is the city's, not a lover's: no one is parted, and the choice of pleasures is itself the proof of Koodal's plenty. நெடியான் may be Thirumal or the Pandya.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punalaadal","inbam","punarchi"],"pos":[],"nm":"Paripadal thirattu, lines 1-6","lb":"PRthirattu:1-6","pat":"","ap":"mixed","th":"paadaan","cth":"","ctu":"","thd":0,"uri":"punarthal","mx":0,"sp":[],"pt":[],"mp":[["uvagai","விளையாட்டு"]],"k":[["வருபுனல்","புனல்",1,"pira","scene","flood"]],"en":[["river","வையை",1],["hill","குன்றம்",2],["town","கூடற்கு",6]],"dv":[],"ch":"pr:20"}},"links":[{"a":"pr:1:1-13","b":"pn:56","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 56.3-6 sets out the conch-white plough-bearer with the palmyra banner and the gem-dark one with the bird banner as two of four separate gods; Paripadal 1.3-11 gives the same emblems (வால் வளை மேனி, நாஞ்சில், சேவல் அம் கொடி) to one god addressed in the singular.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the pair shows that the emblem sets of Balarama and Thirumal were fixed common iconography, but that the Paripadal's Vaishnava singer had already fused the two brothers into one object of worship that the puram poet still counted as two gods.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:1:29-39","b":"t:13","type":"tension","note":"Paripadal 1.32-33 says Thirumal's coming is hard to know even for sages free of confusion; Tirumanthiram 13 puts Vishnu who measured the earth among the gods who, measuring by thought, still cannot conceive the Lord. On the plain reading the two give the unknowable highest place to different gods, and one puts the other's god among the baffled seekers.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same argument from unknowability served two rival theologies, and the Saiva poet reuses it by moving Thirumal from the place of the unknown into the place of the seeker, so ineffability became a way to rank gods."},{"a":"pr:1:40-51","b":"pn:59","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 59 tells the Pandya he is like the sun to his foes and like the moon to us (ஞாயிறு அனையை ... திங்கள் அனையை); Paripadal 1.41-45 tells the god he is grace to those who love him and terror to foes, the moon and the burning sun, with no marker of likeness.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The devotional poet took the king-praise formula of grace to friends and fire to foes and dropped its marker of comparison, so what the court poet offers as a simile becomes in the hymn a statement of identity."},{"a":"pr:1:52-68","b":"pn:56","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 56 makes the Pandya equal (ஒத்தீயே) to each of four gods in one quality; Paripadal 1.53-57 says no one can be found of whom to say the god is like them, so he is like only himself (நின்னோர் அனையை); both describe the bird-bannered god's body with the same formula, மண்ணுறு மணி (Pura 56.5, Pari 1.62).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The pairing shows a limit to the பூவைநிலை way of praising a king by the gods: the likeness runs one way only, since a king can be measured by a god but the hymn finds nothing by which to measure the god."},{"a":"pr:1:52-68","b":"k:7","type":"develops","note":"Kural 7 says only those who reach the feet of the one without comparison (தனக்குவமை இல்லாதான்) are freed from the mind's care; Paripadal 1.53-68 finds no likeness for the god (நின்னோர் அனையை) and ends with hearts made secure (ஏமுறு நெஞ்சத்தேம்) worshipping the shade of his feet.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet's two terms, the incomparable one and refuge at his feet, appear in the hymn as a sequence: the singer first tries and fails to find a simile, and only then turns to the feet, so the Kural's maxim reads as the summary of a hymn's movement."},{"a":"pr:2:1-19","b":"p:21","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 2.5-12 tells the ages of the world in the order sky (as sound), wind, fire, water, earth; Thiruppugazh 21 names Mayon as the one who became sky, wind, fire, water and earth in the same order, and the seven worlds, yet touches none of them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The five-element order credited to Thirumal survives a thousand years into Arunagirinathar, but what the Sangam singer told as successive ages of the world's making the later poet states as the god's present pervasion of all things."},{"a":"pr:2:20-35","b":"t:7","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 7 declares the Lord elder to the three (மூவர்க்கும் மூத்தவன்) and father to the one who calls him father; Paripadal 2.20-23 makes Thirumal younger to those who call him younger than the conch-white brother and elder to those who call him elder.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poets let the god become the kin-name the worshipper uses, but only the Paripadal applies the rule to seniority itself, so where Tirumular fixes his Lord as eldest the earlier hymn shows rank among gods as a matter of the believer's tradition."},{"a":"pr:2:20-35","b":"pr:1:1-13","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 1.1-5 addresses Thirumal with Balarama's emblems as one god (ஒரு குழை ஒருவனை); Paripadal 2.20-23 names the conch-white palmyra-banner brother as a second figure and leaves open whether Thirumal is his younger or his elder.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two hymns show that the fusion of the brothers in the first song was a devotional claim made against a known two-brother story, which the second song keeps in view by refusing to settle their order."},{"a":"pr:2:52-60","b":"k:1","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1 takes the letter அ, first of all letters, as the figure of the First Lord who is first of the world; Paripadal 2.58-59 uses the pointing letters அ, உ and எ (அவ்வும், உவ்வும், எவ்வயினோயும்) to say the god is that, this and whatever place.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Two early poets built theology from the Tamil alphabet in different ways: the Kural uses the order of the letters to make God the origin, while the hymn uses the pointing letters to make God every possible referent, origin set beside pervasion.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:2:61-76","b":"k:259","type":"tension","note":"Paripadal 2.62-65 counts the seizing of the goat in the sacrifice and the fire oblation as the god's own food; Kural 259 says not to take one creature's life and eat it is better than pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices. On the plain reading the hymn honours the animal sacrifice that the couplet ranks below abstention.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The hymn's aside that others will not accept the brahmins' rite (பிறர் உடம்படுவாரா) and the Kural's ranking of not killing over sacrifice show the same quarrel from both sides, so the dispute over animal sacrifice was live within the Tamil tradition itself."},{"a":"pr:3:15-30","b":"pr:2:1-19","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 2.16-17 names one age of creation after the boar form (கேழல் திகழ்வரக் கோலமொடு பெயரிய ஊழி); Paripadal 3.21-24 tells the deed itself, the god as a boar ploughing up the great earth with his tusk when fire, death and the sun gathered in the sea at the age's end.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two hymns show the boar descent known in Sangam Vaishnava worship both as a label for a cosmic age and as a narrated rescue of the earth from the flood, so the avatar was already a deed that also served to count time.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:3:31-50","b":"pr:1:29-39","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 1.32-39 says even sages free of delusion find the god hard to know and asks him to accept inadequate praise; Paripadal 3.45-47 asks whether anyone but the god himself could know what is like him (நின்னைப் புரை நினைப்பின் நீ அலது உணர்தியோ).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The first hymn stops at the limit of the praiser's knowledge, while the third turns that limit into a claim about the god, that he is the only knower of himself, so the pair shows the apology of the Tamil hymn hardening into a doctrine of self-knowledge."},{"a":"pr:3:51-62","b":"k:4","type":"develops","note":"Kural 4 names God as the one without liking or aversion (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்); Paripadal 3.53-58 makes Maal first of the immortals and first also of the asuras who fled from him into the sea, and asks whether there is any 'these are enemies, these friends' for those who know his nature.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the god's freedom from liking and aversion as a bare attribute, while the hymn tests it against the war of gods and demons, where the god plainly took a side, and saves it by making him lord of both, so the pair shows the attribute had to be defended against a mythology of partisan war."},{"a":"pr:3:63-80","b":"pn:35","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 35.14-21 tells Killivalavan that his sky-touching umbrella is not to keep off the sun but to shelter suffering subjects, under a sceptre like aram itself; Paripadal 3.74-76 says the god, with grace as umbrella and aram as sceptre, made the twenty-one worlds a single shade with no second shade.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poet has to argue the king into reading his umbrella as protection, a meaning the king may fail, whereas the hymn makes protection the umbrella's very substance in the god and takes over the royal boast of one shade with no rival, so the pair shows divine kingship and the counsel to kings using one ideal to measure each other.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:3:63-80","b":"t:2639","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 3.63 says 'you are the fragrance in the flower' (பூவினுள் நாற்றம் நீ) in a run of identities; Tirumanthiram 2639 says the Lord is like the fragrance in the flower, the ghee in milk and the juice in fruit (பூவினுள் நாற்றமும் போலுளன்), mingled everywhere.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same image of fragrance in the flower is an identity in the Sangam hymn, where the god is the essence of each thing, and a simile in Tirumular, where the Lord is like that essence and stands mingled in things, so the pair shows the later Saiva poet keeping the image while holding the god at one remove from the world he pervades."},{"a":"pr:4:25-36","b":"t:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Tirumanthiram 10 says he himself is the earth, the sky, the fire, the sun, the moon, the rain and the sea (தானே opening each line); Paripadal 4.25-34 says only that two of the god's qualities are in each of the sun, moon, rain, earth, flower, water, sky and wind, and that these came about by parting from him (நிற் பிரிந்து).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two lists cover nearly the same things, but the Sangam hymn keeps the god outside them, each element holding a pair of his qualities as a share that left him, while Tirumular erases the gap and makes him the things themselves, so the pair shows identity with the elements was a later and stronger claim than participation."},{"a":"pr:4:25-36","b":"pr:1:40-51","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 1.40-51 says you are the moon, the sun, the sky and the earth; Paripadal 4.25-32 says your coolness is in the moon, your heat in the sun, your form and sound in the sky.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Inside one collection the god is first said to be the moon and the sun and then said to have only lent them his qualities, so the anthology holds both the identity form and the participation form of praise without choosing between them."},{"a":"pr:4:37-48","b":"pn:56","type":"tension","note":"Purananuru 56.1-8 gives four gods four banners: the bull, the palm (பனைக்கொடி) of the conch-white one with the plough, the bird of the sapphire-dark one, the peacock; Paripadal 4.37-41 gives the dark god the bird, the palm, the plough and the elephant, and says there is no banner that is not his. On the plain reading the palm banner belongs to a different god in one poem and to Mal in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The puram poet needs four separate gods so that the king can match each in one excellence, while the devotional poet folds the elder brother's palm and plough into Mal's own emblems, so the pair shows how a hymn's claim of sole lordship rearranged the very iconography the court poets used as a fixed set.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:4:11-24","b":"p:870","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 4.11-21 tells of பிருங்கலாதன் tormented by his father, the god embracing the son and tearing the father's chest with his claws from the broken pillar (ஒடி தூண்); Thiruppugazh 870 tells of a child chanting நம நாராயணாய, the father asking where his god is, and the lion bursting from the pillar to tear Hiranya's chest.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the Sangam hymn the story turns on the god's two acts on two chests, the embrace and the tearing, while Arunagirinathar's version turns on the child's recitation of the name and the father's challenge, so the pair shows the Prahlada story shifting its centre from the god's protecting hand to the power of the uttered name."},{"a":"pr:4:49-56","b":"k:4","type":"develops","note":"Kural 4 names God as the one without liking or aversion (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்); Paripadal 4.49-55 says the god has harm and grace, cruelty and rightness wherever these are found and none where they are not, and treats the lives of those who praise him and those who do not without exchange, having neither foes nor kin.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural defines the god by what he lacks, while the hymn shows what that lack allows: he can hold cruelty and grace alike because they answer what is in things rather than his own preference, so freedom from liking and aversion is seen to include, not exclude, the god's harshness."},{"a":"pr:4:57-73","b":"t:3021","type":"develops","note":"Tirumanthiram 3021 asks where he is and answers that the Lord stands everywhere like rain; Paripadal 4.65-70 names the banyan, the kadamba, the river islet and the trackless hill, where he dwells under a different name in each, and concludes எவ் வயினோயும் நீயே, you are the one in every place.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Tirumular reaches omnipresence through the image of rain falling on all places alike, while the Sangam poet reaches it by gathering particular shrines with their own local names, so the pair shows the claim 'he is everywhere' first serving to unite many local cults under one god before it became a claim about space."},{"a":"pr:4:57-73","b":"pr:2:20-35","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 2.20-23 says the god is younger to those who call him younger and elder to those who call him elder; Paripadal 4.69-73 says he bears a different name in each place, sits in the folded hands of his devotees and is the servant (ஏவலாளன்) of each of them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rule that the god takes the place each believer gives him begins in Paripadal 2 as a way out of a dispute over rank, and in Paripadal 4 it is extended to names, places and finally to service, so the pair shows accommodation growing from a theological truce into the god's subordinating himself to every worshipper."},{"a":"pr:5:1-21","b":"pp:1:38-66","type":"same_image","note":"Paripadal 5.1-4 (பனிக் கடல் ... புக்கு ... சூர் மா முதல் தடிந்து) and Thirumurugatruppadai 45-46 and 60 (பனிக்கடல் கலங்கவுள் புக்குச் சூர்முதல் தடிந்த ... மாமுதல் தடிந்த) tell the Sur deed in nearly the same words.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The near-identical phrasing in two different collections shows the Sur killing already had a fixed verbal formula, a shared stock of Murugan praise that both poets drew on rather than one poem borrowing a picture from the other.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:5:1-21","b":"a:242","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 242 calls the velan who holds the veri rite ignorant (அறியா வேலன்): he blames the god for what is love; Paripadal 5.15-17 says the velan's praise and veri are neither true nor false, since the world lies within the god's bound.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the pair shows that the akam mockery of the velan was not a rejection of Murugan: the devotional poet keeps the rite valid at the level of the god while conceding that its particular verdicts may miss, which is exactly the room the akam friend exploits.","gr":4},{"a":"pr:5:1-21","b":"p:38","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 5.9 names the split hill only by its crane name (குருகொடு பெயர் பெற்ற மால் வரை); Thiruppugazh 38 names it க்ரவுஞ்சம் and has it split together with the drying of the sea.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Sangam poet renders the Sanskrit name Krauncha by its Tamil meaning, a hill named with the crane, while Arunagirinathar uses the Sanskrit name outright, so the pair shows the same deed passing from a translated to a borrowed name across a thousand years.","gr":5},{"a":"pr:5:22-47","b":"pn:55","type":"same_image","note":"Purananuru 55.1-2 (மலைப் பெருவில் பாம்புஞாண் ... ஒரு கணை ... மூவெயில்) and Paripadal 5.24-25 (நாகம் நாணா, மலை வில்லாக, மூவகை ஆர் எயில் ஓர் அழல்-அம்பின்) give the three-forts deed in the same parts: mountain bow, serpent string, one arrow; both also turn on Siva's forehead eye.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same tripura formula serves a puram poet as the vehicle of counsel to a Pandya king and a devotional poet as the prelude to Murugan's birth, showing that Siva's deed was common stock usable for statecraft and worship alike in the Sangam period.","gr":6},{"a":"pr:5:22-47","b":"pp:1:148-159","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 151-154 shows Siva only as one of three lords who come to see Murugan, with Uma, three unwinking eyes and the three forts destroyed; Paripadal 5.22-47 tells how that same god's union with Uma led to Murugan's birth.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guide poem gives the kinship only as epithets (son of the god of the banyan, line 256) and shows Siva as a visiting lord, while the Paripadal narrates the birth in full, so the pair shows the story was known to both but told only where a poem chose to narrate rather than to direct a pilgrim.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:5:48-70","b":"pp:1:250-265","type":"same_image","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 253-255 (நெடும்பெருஞ் சிமையத்து நீலப் பைஞ்சுனை ... அறுவர் பயந்த) and Paripadal 5.48-49 (இமயத்து நீலப் பைஞ் சுனைப் பயந்தோர்) set the birth in the same blue pool and give him six mothers.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both poems use நீலப் பைஞ்சுனை and the six who bore him, but only the Paripadal says who the six were and why their chastity stayed whole, so the guide poem's single epithet is seen to presuppose a full birth story its hearers already knew.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:5:48-70","b":"p:372","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 5 has six of the seven sages' wives bear him; Thiruppugazh 372 names him the six-faced one who drank at the breasts of the six (அறுவர் முலையுணு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Paripadal the six are mothers by conception through the fire, while in the Thiruppugazh they have become nurses he drank from, so the pair shows the six mothers' role shifting from bearing to nursing as the Krittika story settled.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:5:71-82","b":"pp:1:277-295","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai ends with the god giving the suppliant a rare gift (பெறலரும் பரிசில்) that makes him stand alone in the world; Paripadal 5.79-81 says we ask not wealth, gold or pleasure but grace, love and aram.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guide poem frames devotion in the bard's economy of gifts, while the Paripadal explicitly refuses material gifts, so the pair shows two Sangam answers to what one should ask of Murugan, the patron's reward and the ethical state.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:5:71-82","b":"k:241","type":"develops","note":"Kural 241 ranks the wealth of அருள் above the wealth of goods, which even the base possess; Paripadal 5.80-81 refuses பொருளும் பொன்னும் and asks அருளும் அன்பும் அறனும்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural makes the ranking a general maxim about kinds of wealth, and the Paripadal turns that same ranking into the content of a prayer, showing the ethical scale used as the measure of what to ask a god for.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:5:71-82","b":"p:986","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 5 asks Murugan for love (அன்பும்) instead of wealth; Thiruppugazh 986 asks for love growing ever greater, with tears, melting and faltering speech, and only then for the stainless meaning.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Sangam prayer names love as one of three ethical gifts, while the Thiruppugazh makes love itself the whole request and describes its bodily signs, so the pair traces anbu changing from a virtue asked for into the felt experience of bhakti.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:6:1-13","b":"k:18","type":"develops","note":"Kural 18 says that if the sky dries, festival and worship cease even for the gods; Paripadal 6.1-13 shows the rain that has come ending in incense, flowers, oblation and fire (புகை, பூ, அவி ஆராதனை, அழல்) at the river's arrival.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural argues from absence that worship hangs on rain, and the Paripadal shows the positive side as one continuous scene from sea to cloud to river to offering, adding what the couplet leaves out: the same flood that feeds the rite also brings lovers together, so festival here means love as much as worship."},{"a":"pr:6:14-25","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1251 has desire's axe break the door of restraint (நிறை); Paripadal 6.22-23 has the flood break its dam as lovers of one desire break the restraint that fills the heart (உளம் நிறை உடைத்தென).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's woman speaks of restraint broken as her private defeat, while the Paripadal makes the same breaking of நிறை the measure of a flood the whole town celebrates with drums, so the pair shows the Kural's image of a lost guard was also, in the festival, a public and welcome release."},{"a":"pr:6:26-43","b":"a:296","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 296 makes the husband's bathing in the great Vaigai with a dark girl the gossip of Koodal; Paripadal 6.26-43 shows the whole town, young and old, rich and strong, going down to play in the same river.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read against the Paripadal's crowd at the fords, the Akananuru friend's charge cannot be that he bathed in the Vaigai, since everyone did; the scandal lies in whom he bathed with, so the akam reproach depends on a public festival that the akam poem itself never describes.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:6:61-79","b":"n:370","type":"qualifies","note":"Naladiyar 370 says fresh floodwater and a courtesan's friendship are alike, both ending when their source ends; Paripadal 6.72-75 has the wife say love's shrinking and swelling is the Vaiyai's flood.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Naladiyar uses the flood's ebb to condemn bought love and spare the household, but the Paripadal puts the same flood image in the wife's mouth against her own husband, so the didactic contrast of faithful wife and fickle courtesan breaks down once the river festival shows the husband's love rising and falling like the stream."},{"a":"pr:6:80-87","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"Kural 1134 says love's fierce flood carries off the raft (புணை) of shame and manliness; Paripadal 6.80-81 says the husband's chest, like a log in the river, became a raft (புணை) for women skilled at seizing.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the Kural the raft is the lover's own virtue swept away by his desire, in the Paripadal the man himself is the raft that others ride, so the same river figure moves from an inner confession to a wife's accusation, and the festival's real floats show where the Kural's metaphor came from."},{"a":"pr:6:80-87","b":"n:222","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 222 says those who live by water do not rage when the flood breaks the dam but dam it again, as one bears a valued friend's repeated wrongs; Paripadal 6.83-87 says that even when the breached sluice is closed the spray still wells, as her burning goes on after he returns.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The two poems use the same breached dam for opposite claims: the Naladiyar makes re-damming the picture of calm forbearance that works, while the Paripadal wife says closing the breach does not stop the leak, so the pair shows that the moralist's patience is exactly what the wronged wife declares impossible."},{"a":"pr:6:88-96","b":"a:266","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 266 the wife says the husband's water-play with young women in the evening flood is gossip but that what hurts is his oath by Murugan of Alaivay; in Paripadal 6.88-96 the husband, accused after the water-play, swears by cool Parankunram.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both husbands answer a water-play charge with an oath to a Murugan shrine, but the Akananuru wife treats the oath as the worse betrayal while the Paripadal lets the oath pass into a reconciliation brokered by others, so the pair shows the festival hymn softening into play what the akam anthology records as a wound."},{"a":"pr:6:88-96","b":"k:1313","type":"develops","note":"In Kural 1313 the man complains that whatever garland he wears she says he wore it for another woman; in Paripadal 6.91-92 the wife asks who pressed into his garland and what touched it, and he blames the river.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the suspicious garland as a pure case of love's jealousy, with no cause given, while the Paripadal gives the garland a real crushing and a real crowd in the water, so the pair shows the couplet's general complaint distilled from a festival in which the evidence truly could point either way."},{"a":"pr:6:97-107","b":"k:1302","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1302, spoken by the friend, says sulking is like salt and a little too much spoils it; Paripadal 6.98-99 has those around the wife say that if the sulk festers much love is spoiled (துனி நனி கன்றிடின் காமம் கெடூஉம்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same counsel to a sulking wife appears in both, but the Paripadal shows it said aloud at the festival and then acted on by skilled pleaders until the sulk is understood, so the Kural's measure of salt turns out to belong to a social practice of reconciliation, not only to one friend's private advice."},{"a":"pr:7:1-22","b":"k:17","type":"develops","note":"Kural 17 says even the wide sea would lose its nature if the cloud that drew from it gave nothing back; Paripadal 7.1-10 begins with clouds that have drawn the dark sea fully (பௌவம் செவ்விதா அற முகந்து) and ends the sentence with the land's good and beauty swelling as the Vaiyai comes.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet states the exchange between sea and cloud as a warning about withholding, while the song follows the same water through rain, falls and river into the Pandya fields, so together they show that the Kural's abstract cycle was the felt shape of the flood festival, praised in the hymn as the return the couplet only demands."},{"a":"pr:7:23-50","b":"ai:69","type":"same_image","note":"Ainkurunuru 69 has the wife say she has seen the husband's woman crying, eyes reddened, when the cool flood with its flowers swept her sand-play away at the crowded ford; Paripadal 7.25-26 lists among the town's cries the young women weeping that the flood broke the dolls they drew in the sand.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the hymn the weeping over sand dolls is one anonymous cry among many on flood day, while the akam wife picks out one such girl to mock as her husband's new woman, so the pair shows the akam poet taking a stock festival scene and turning it into a mark of the rival's childishness.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:7:51-76","b":"a:266","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 266 has the wife tell her husband that his bathing yesterday in the new flood with young women, gazing again and again at one woman's rain-cool eyes, is now loud gossip; Paripadal 7.65-76 shows the scene itself: a bard praises a bather's eyes, the lover who looked at the bathers is pulled into the mud and his head trodden on by the woman in her sulk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru gives only the aftermath, the wife's reproach once talk has spread, whereas the Paripadal shows the gazing at the eyes and the quarrel breaking out at once in the river, so together they show the river bathing as the very occasion of the marutham sulk, with its offence and its punishment both public."},{"a":"pr:7:51-76","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1311 has the woman refuse her lover's chest because all women feast on him with their eyes; in Paripadal 7.69-75 the woman's fury falls on her lover because he looked at the ornaments of the bathing women (யாறு ஆடு மேனி அணி கண்ட).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The couplet makes the man's being looked at enough for oodal, the hymn makes his looking the offence, and the song's crowded bathing shows why both could be true at once: in the festival everyone sees and is seen, so the sulk needs no act of straying, only the eyes."},{"a":"pr:7:77-86","b":"n:370","type":"tension","note":"Naladiyar 370 makes the fresh flood (புதுப்புனல்) a type of what does not last: it ends when the rain ends; Paripadal 7.84-86 prays to the Vaiyai's flood that the bathing in it may never be left (நின் படிந்து நீங்காமை).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same new flood that the ethical quatrain uses to prove that pleasures and loves dry up is, in the festival hymn, the object of a prayer for permanence, so the pair shows the didactic poet reversing the devotional value of the river by fixing on its ending rather than its yearly return.","gr":4},{"a":"pr:8:1-16","b":"pr:5:48-70","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 5.48-49 narrates the birth on a lotus bed in the green pool of the Himalaya; Paripadal 8.11-16 says Parankundram equals the Himalaya because its pool equals that pool of the lotus that bore him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Read together, the birth story of song 5 becomes the title deed of song 8: the poet claims the Himalaya's sanctity for a Pandya hill by matching its pool to the birth pool, so the myth is used to found a local shrine."},{"a":"pr:8:1-16","b":"pp:1:160-176","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 167 gives the attending gods as a sum (நால்வேறு இயற்கைப் பதினொரு மூவரொடு); Paripadal 8.4-7 names the same thirty-three group by group: twelve, two who speak of medicine, eight, and eleven from the lord of Aathirai.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The guide poem's 'eleven times three of four kinds' is spelled out in the Paripadal as four counted groups, showing that the Vedic thirty-three were known in Tamil by their divisions and that both poets made Murugan the one they attend.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:8:17-28","b":"pp:1:67-77","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 71-77 places the hill west of Koodal, with bees humming over pool flowers that open like eyes; Paripadal 8.17-28 makes the same bees, pools and flowers the god's procession, the coming from your hill to Koodal (குன்றத்தான் கூடல் வரவு).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guide poem fixes Parankundram by its position beside the city, while the Paripadal turns the road between them into a seasonal procession of sound and bloom, so together they show the shrine defined by its tie to Madurai rather than as a lone mountain retreat."},{"a":"pr:8:47-71","b":"nt:386","type":"qualifies","note":"In Natrinai 386 the girl refuses the hero's offer of a dread oath by the god (அணங்குடை அருஞ் சூள்) because men of his kin do not need it, and he comes with marriage; in Paripadal 8.65-71 the wife refuses her husband's oath by Murugan because the god's spear would devour a false swearer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both women stop a man from swearing by the god, but for opposite reasons, trust in his kin in the courtship poem and certainty of his guilt in the marriage quarrel, which shows that refusing the god-oath was a recognised move whose meaning depended on the stage of love."},{"a":"pr:8:47-71","b":"a:166","type":"develops","note":"In Akananuru 166 the husband soothes his wife by calling on the Velur god to afflict him if he went to the other woman, and the other woman exposes the lie; in Paripadal 8.65-66 the wife forbids such an oath by Murugan, since the god's true spear would eat him.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Akananuru shows the god-oath used lightly as a husband's comfort, the Paripadal shows a wife who takes the same self-curse seriously enough to block it, so together they reveal that the oath was believed to bind the swearer bodily and that a knowing wife could protect a lying husband from it."},{"a":"pr:8:47-71","b":"ku:53","type":"tension","note":"Kuruntokai 53 says the oath the man swore at the sandy ford with the hill goddesses as witness is what afflicts us, the women (எம்மணங் கினவே); Paripadal 8.66 says the god's spear will devour the man himself if he swears falsely.","grade":"forced","better":"develops","te":"On the plain reading of each, the two poems disagree on whom a broken god-witnessed oath strikes, the deceived women in the Kuruntokai and the perjurer in the Paripadal, so the pair shows two Sangam answers to where the god's punishment falls."},{"a":"pr:8:90-111","b":"pr:5:71-82","type":"tension","note":"Paripadal 5.79-81 says we ask not wealth, gold or pleasure but grace, love and aram; at Parankundram in Paripadal 8.103-108 the festival crowd asks for a dream lover made real, a child in the womb, success in making wealth (செய் பொருள் வாய்க்க) and victory in war.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same collection holds a poet's refusal of worldly boons and a festival crowd's petitions for exactly those boons, so the Paripadal's devotion is not one ethic: the singer's ideal prayer and the people's practical vows stand side by side in it."},{"a":"pr:8:90-111","b":"p:8","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 8.96-108 shows the crowd at Parankundram carrying flowers, incense and lamps and asking for children, wealth and victory; Thiruppugazh 8, sung at Parangiri, confesses that the poet never worshipped with flowers or walked round the hill and asks only that Murugan come at the hour of death.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rites the Sangam festival performs as means to this-world ends become, at the same hill a thousand years later, the list of duties the devotee confesses he failed, and the request moves from children and wealth to rescue at death."},{"a":"pr:8:112-130","b":"k:17","type":"tension","note":"Kural 17 says even the great sea loses its nature if the cloud withholds rain; Paripadal 8.128-130 prays that the hill's streaming falls and springs last even when the sky dries and the earth cracks (மண் பரிய வானம் வறப்பினும், மன்னுகமா).","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies","te":"The Kural makes every water, the sea included, depend on rain, and the Paripadal asks that the god's waterfall be exempt from that law, which is what marks the shrine's water as sacred rather than natural."},{"a":"pr:9:1-11","b":"pp:1:1-11","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 6 names the god only as husband of a bright-browed woman of spotless chastity (மறுவில் கற்பின் வாணுதற் கணவன்); Paripadal 9.8-11 names her as the daughter of the thousand-eyed one and sets the wedding at Parankunram, marked by rain in summer.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Held together, the guide poem's opening epithet turns out to rest on a wedding story that the Paripadal ties to one hill, which explains why Parankunram, the hill of that marriage, is the first abode the guide poem then visits."},{"a":"pr:9:12-26","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1330 says sulking is love's pleasure and its pleasure is the embrace that follows; Paripadal 9.12-22 ranks love, equal union and karpu, and places the courtesan, the reproach and the சுணங்கறை inside karpu, with their fruit in the sulk (ஊடல்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural gives the sulk's worth as a man's two-line saying, while the Paripadal builds it into a ranked theory addressed to Vedic scholars and then uses it to excuse a god's wives, so the pair shows oodal already held as doctrine of Tamil porul, citable to outsiders and to the gods."},{"a":"pr:9:27-44","b":"k:1311","type":"qualifies","note":"In both a wife refuses the husband who has been with other women: Kural 1311 says 'I will not come near your chest, philanderer' (நண்ணேன் பரத்தநின் மார்பு); in Paripadal 9.30-40 the wife blesses him ironically and cries 'don't come near' (குறுகல்), while the rival gives him her chest (மார்பு அளிப்பாளை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's refusal is private and the rival only imagined, while the Paripadal stages the same refusal with the rival present and offering the very chest the wife denies, so the pair shows the one gesture of refusing the chest working as a quarrel between two women, not only between wife and husband."},{"a":"pr:9:27-44","b":"p:12","type":"qualifies","note":"Thiruppugazh 12, a Parankunram song, has Indra's daughter and the hunter girl both embrace Murugan's twelve shoulders; Paripadal 9 sets the same two wives at the same hill in a quarrel with blows of garlands.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The later hymn keeps the two consorts side by side in a single embrace, while the Sangam song lets them fight over him at the same shrine, which shows the co-wives' rivalry was once part of the hill's sacred lore and was smoothed away as the pair became fixed icons of devotion."},{"a":"pr:9:45-69","b":"p:18","type":"develops","note":"Thiruppugazh 18 names Murugan of south Parankunram as the one who took the girl the hill people gave (குன்றவர்கள் தந்தபெண்); Paripadal 9.67-69 has Valli's kin, the kurinji hill-men (குறிஞ்சிக் குன்றவர் ... வள்ளி தமர்), turn the wives' quarrel into a thumbai battle on that hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In the hymn the hill people appear only as givers of the bride, while in the Paripadal they stay her partisans after the marriage and fight for her against the sky-born wife, so the pair shows Valli's marriage remembered as an alliance with her kin that carried on into the god's household."},{"a":"pr:9:70-85","b":"pp:1:78-102","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 101-102 gives one of the six faces to smiling with Valli, the hill-dwellers' daughter, after its opening epithet for the chaste wife; Paripadal 9.81 names the god as lord of the bond of love along the path of the bond of karpu (கற்பு இணை நெறியூடு அற்பு இணைக் கிழமை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guide poem lists the two marriages separately, one as an epithet and one as a face's work, while the Paripadal joins them in one phrase after teaching the order of love, so the pair shows the two consorts being read as the two branches of akam, karpu and the love of free choice, united in one god."},{"a":"pr:10:19-40","b":"a:296","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 296's friend turns the hero away because he bathed in the great Vaigai with the dark girl (பெருநீர் வையை அவளொடு ஆடி) and the talk is like Koodal's clamour; Paripadal 10.19-23 lists chaste wives and courtesans (பதிவத மாதர், பரத்தையர்) going together to the Vaigai at Koodal.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Since the festival put wives and courtesans in the same flood openly, the friend's charge in Akananuru 296 cannot be the bathing itself, a licensed public pleasure, but the companion he chose, so the akam poem turns a shared civic rite into private evidence."},{"a":"pr:10:41-62","b":"ku:37","type":"develops","note":"Kuruntokai 37's tusker strips yaa branches to ease his mate's hunger, left for the heroine to read as the hero's love; Paripadal 10.42-52 has a tusker turn back to embrace his terrified mate and says outright சிறந்தார் நடுக்கம் சிறந்தார் களையல்.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Paripadal poet spells out in one line the sense that Kuruntokai 37 leaves implied, which shows that the caring elephant pair was read by the poets themselves as an image of the lover who removes the beloved's distress."},{"a":"pr:10:63-70","b":"k:1145","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1145 says love grows sweeter each time it is exposed, as drinking is craved each time one is drunk; Paripadal 10.63-68 likens lovers who hide their love from gossip to drinkers who hide their drunkenness.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both join love and toddy, but the Kural's lover welcomes exposure while the Paripadal's festival lovers hide from it, so the same comparison serves to praise and to fear gossip, and the couplet's bravado reads as one stance among those the poets knew."},{"a":"pr:10:63-70","b":"a:266","type":"qualifies","note":"Akananuru 266's wife says her husband played in the evening flood with young women and lost his shame (நாண்இழந்து), and the talk is loud as a feast; Paripadal 10.63-68 shows lovers in the flood hiding their joy for fear of gossip (அலர் அஞ்சி).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Paripadal shows that the flood-play crowd expected lovers to hide, so the wife's charge that he 'lost his shame' accuses him of breaking a restraint the festival itself kept, not of joining the play.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:10:71-88","b":"k:1066","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1066 says nothing degrades the tongue more than asking, even water for a cow; Paripadal 10.87-88 has bathers who, seeing what they lack, do good works in hope before they speak the shame of asking (இளிவரவு கூறாமுன்).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The festival bathers apply the Kural's horror of asking even to what they want from the river, choosing an offering over a spoken request, so the ethic against begging shaped how people framed a petition in worship."},{"a":"pr:10:125-130","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 211 measures giving without return by the rain, to which the world gives nothing back; Paripadal 10.125-127 measures the Vaigai's flood over the fields by the Vazhuthi who pours gold into poets' hands.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Kural makes nature the model for the giver, while the Paripadal makes the king the model for nature, so the pair shows the comparison between rain and royal giving could run in both directions, with the Pandya's gift treated as the surer standard."},{"a":"pr:10:125-130","b":"pn:3","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 3 counsels the Pandya Vazhuthi that suppliants have crossed deadly roads and his strength is to read their need and end their poverty; Paripadal 10.125-126 names the Vazhuthi who pours gold into the hands of poets in want (இலம்படு புலவர்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the puram poet urges on a Vazhuthi as a duty to be kept, the devotional poet takes as so settled a fact of the Pandya house that it can serve as the vehicle for praising the river itself."},{"a":"pr:11:31-45","b":"k:334","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 11.37-38 asks who notices the days run down (நாள் குறைபடுதல் காணுநர் யாரே) as the flood wanes like the moon; Kural 334 says the day is a saw cutting life, for those who can perceive it (உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்).","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"Both turn on the same observation, that daily loss goes unseen, but the Paripadal draws from it a request to the river to slow down and keep its plenty, while the Kural draws a warning to the perceptive; the image of unnoticed waning could serve plenty as well as renunciation."},{"a":"pr:11:62-73","b":"a:296","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 11.62-71 praises the Vaigai water-sport of men with gold-garlanded women as an open custom (கரவா வழக்கிற்று); in Akananuru 296 the friend refuses a husband who bathed in the great Vaigai with another woman and says it is already common talk.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same Madurai river festival that the devotional song celebrates as a public glory with nothing hidden is, seen from the wife's door, the proof and occasion of a husband's straying, so the openness the Paripadal praises is exactly what feeds the alar of the marutham quarrel."},{"a":"pr:11:74-92","b":"nt:80","type":"develops","note":"Natrinai 80 names in one line a girl who bathes in the cold pond of the month of Thai with her jewelled companions (தைஇத் திங்கள் தண் கயம் படியும்); Paripadal 11.74-92 sets out the rite itself: Aathirai, the brahmins, the elder women of the ford, the dawn bath and the fire.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poem's passing mention of a Thai bath points to a guarded public rite with priests, elder women and mothers, so the girl a lover sees there is seen at a sanctioned gathering of maidens, which is why such a meeting could happen and why shyness must restrain her."},{"a":"pr:11:74-92","b":"k:55","type":"tension","note":"In Paripadal 11.80 unmarried girls bathe under the brahmins' fire rite praying 'may the wide land not be scorched'; Kural 55 says it rains at the word of the wife who does not worship a god but worships her husband. On the plain reading these name incompatible sources of the rain women can bring: public worship by maidens, or a wife's devotion that sets worship of a god aside.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Held together, the pair shows two rival accounts of women's power over the rain, the festival rite of the unmarried at the river and the wife's domestic devotion, and the Kural's தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் reads as a choice of the second over the first."},{"a":"pr:11:93-114","b":"kl:98","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 11.106-110 tells literally of a youth whom the Vaigai's current carries off while a girl takes his heart (கடும் புனல் கை வாங்க, நெஞ்சம் அவள் வாங்க); in Kalittokai 98 a husband swears he was delayed bathing in the Vaigai's new flood, and his wife answers that people saw the new flood drag him off, a flood that was a woman.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The wife's figure of the woman as a flood that carried him off is the Paripadal's festival event turned into an accusation, so the river festival was both the occasion of a man being swept away by a look and the stock excuse for it, and the Kalittokai's irony depends on the audience knowing the Paripadal scene."},{"a":"pr:11:115-132","b":"ku:49","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 11.120-121 has the bathing women ask that for seven births, without growing old, their rich kin last and bear children; in Kuruntokai 49 a wife asks her husband that even when this birth gives way to the next he be her husband and she the one close to his heart.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"What the Kuruntokai wife says privately to one man, the Paripadal's women ask together of the river at a public festival, so the wish to keep the same spouse beyond this birth was a recognised petition of the Thai rite, not only one woman's vow of love."},{"a":"pr:11:115-132","b":"k:1082","type":"same_image","note":"Paripadal 11.122-123 has onlookers call a girl a striking goddess (தாக்கு அணங்கு) whose eyes are Kaaman's army (காமன் படை); Kural 1082 has the man say that her returning his look is a striking goddess come with an army (தாக்கணங்கு தானைக்கொண் டன்ன).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The rare compound and the army come together in both, so the Kural's lover is speaking a festival idiom: what the Paripadal's crowd shouts as a warning to all who see the girl, the Kural turns into one man's private experience of a single returned glance."},{"a":"pr:12:9-35","b":"kl:67","type":"same_image","note":"Kalittokai 67.3-5 says the Vaiyai beating against the wall (மதில் பொரூஉம்) is the only enemy the walled town has known; Paripadal 12.9 has the town hear that the Vaiyai has come beating against Madurai's wall (மதுரை மதில் பொரூஉம்) and rush out in finery to meet it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The akam poem turns the river's assault on the wall into a boast that no army ever reached Madurai, and the hymn shows what that only siege was like inside the walls, a summons that sends the whole town to dress and ride out, so together they show the flood as the city's one welcomed attack, the festival standing where war stands for other towns."},{"a":"pr:12:45-74","b":"kl:67","type":"qualifies","note":"In Kalittokai 67.6-9 the wife says she will sulk when her husband comes showing the wounds and scars (புண், வடு) that other women's garlands (கோதையால் அலைத்த) left on him, and her heart gives way when she sees him; in Paripadal 12.57-73 the wife herself beats her husband with her garland (கமழ் கோதை கோலாப் புடைத்து), then fears a scar on his chest (வடு அஞ்சி) and her quarrel passes.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Kalittokai the garland-wound is evidence against the husband, written on him by rival women, while in the hymn the wife wields the garland herself and her fear of the scar is what ends her sulk, so the pair shows the garland-blow as a festival game of the Vaiyai town whose meaning, accusation or claim, depends on whose hand struck it.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:12:45-74","b":"k:1321","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1321 has the woman say that though he has no fault, his grace has the power to bring on a sulk; in Paripadal 12.55-60 the man has eyed another woman's breasts, and when beaten he 'sees no fault' (பிழை ஒன்றும் காணான்) and bows asking to be told it, and the onlookers call him the pure one (தூயவனைக் காண்மின்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet takes the faultless lover of the oodal at the woman's word, while the hymn shows a crowd that has watched him look and mocks his innocence as he begs to hear his fault, so together they show that the faultless husband of the sulk was a convention its audience knew to be a pose, and that the Kural's version is the inside view of a scene the festival saw from outside."},{"a":"pr:12:45-74","b":"pr:7:51-76","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 7.65-76 has a woman, angry that her lover looked at the bathers, punish him in the river; Paripadal 12.55-74 has the same offence of looking at another woman at the flood and the same sulk, carried through the blow and his fall to her return to his breast, which the poet credits to the Vaiyai's flood (வல்லதால், வையைப் புனல்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Song 7 leaves the river quarrel at the lover's punishment, while song 12 carries the same quarrel to its making-up and gives the river the credit, so the two Vaiyai songs show that the oodal episode was told in the hymns in order to praise the flood as the reconciler, the go-between that akam poems give to a friend or a bard."},{"a":"pr:12:75-85","b":"kv:30","type":"same_image","note":"Kalavazhi Narpathu 30 likens the pooled blood of the battlefield, dragging killed elephants, to a flood that rolls boulders down the hill (மலையுருட்டு நீர்போல்); Paripadal 12.84-85 sees the Vaiyai in the morning of the rains red with turbid blood (கலிழ் செங் குருதித்தே) and names it the river of the king whose army kills in war.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The war poem uses the hill torrent as the image for battle blood and the hymn uses battle blood as the image for the monsoon torrent, so held together they show the comparison running both ways for Tamil poets: the king's killing is a flood and his river's flood is his killing, the river's redness counting as his fame."},{"a":"pr:12:92-101","b":"k:1064","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1064 says the dignity that will not beg even when nothing is left is too great for all the world to hold (இடமெல்லாம் கொள்ளா); Paripadal 12.101 ends by telling the Vaiyai that this flower-topped world cannot hold its fame (நின் புகழ் கொள்ளாது, இம் மலர் தலை உலகே).","grade":"forced","better":"same_image","te":"The same measure, a greatness the world is too small to hold, is given by the hymn to a river's yearly plenty and by the Kural to a destitute man's refusal to ask, so the pair shows the ethical book taking the praise formula of abundance and fixing it on the one thing that has no abundance at all."},{"a":"pr:13:14-21","b":"k:27","type":"qualifies","note":"Both name the same five, taste, light or sight, touch, sound and smell; the Kural, in its chapter on renunciants, puts the world in the grasp of the one who discerns them, while Paripadal 13 says the five and the faculties that take them are the god.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain","te":"The Kural's five, which the renunciant must master, are in the Paripadal the god himself, so the same old list of five could ground either a discipline of withdrawal from the senses or a worship through them."},{"a":"pr:13:14-21","b":"t:385","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 13 orders the elements sky, wind, fire, water by how many of the five each is known through; Tirumanthiram 385 tells the same chain as emergence, space, air, then a doubtful கான், water, the five together.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Because the Paripadal's count puts fire between wind and water, it supports reading the Tirumanthiram's obscure கான் as fire, and shows the creation chain the later Saiva text tells already present in an early Tamil hymn as a theory of how each element is known."},{"a":"pr:13:22-36","b":"pr:2:20-35","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 2 says Maal is younger to those who call him the white one's younger brother and elder to those who call him elder; Paripadal 13 makes the plough-bearer one of Maal's own three forms.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Held together, the two hymns show Paripadal poets resolving the kinship of Maal and the plough-bearer in two ways: one makes the order of birth depend on who is speaking, the other dissolves the kinship entirely by making the brother a form of the one god."},{"a":"pr:13:37-48","b":"k:5","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 5 says the two deeds born of darkness do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise; Paripadal 13 line 47 says the two deeds are absent where his feet are praised, and gives the god a heart set on a single deed.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Paripadal sets the Kural's claim inside a theology: the praisers are free of both deeds because the god himself is beyond the three times and holds to one deed, so the Kural's freedom from karma through praise is grounded in what the praised one is.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:13:49-63","b":"pr:14:19-32","type":"same_image","note":"Two hymns by different poets to different gods, Nallezhuthiyar's to Maal and Kesavanar's to Sevvel, close the same way: the god thought of as mother, worship repeated, and இன்னும் இன்னும் asking for more of it.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Since the same close serves two poets and both Maal and Murugan, the mother-address and the prayer for more worship belong to a shared Paripadal liturgy, not to one poet's devotion or one god's cult."},{"a":"pr:13:49-63","b":"pr:5:71-82","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 5 refuses wealth, gold and pleasure and asks the god for grace, love and aram; Paripadal 13 asks for nothing but more of the worship, which it calls the fruit of past penance and names its kaamam.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read in sequence the two closes show a step in the hymns' idea of what to ask: from the god's virtues instead of wealth to worship as its own reward, so that by Paripadal 13 the act of praising is both the fruit of tapas and the whole of desire."},{"a":"pr:14:1-18","b":"a:194","type":"same_image","note":"In Akananuru 194 the heroine hears the peacock cry in the very rains her lover named for his return and he has not come; in Paripadal 14 the peacock's cry on the god's rainy hill is like the words calling the departed lover 'do not delay, come'.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Paripadal's simile only works for a hearer who knows the mullai convention the Akananuru poem dramatises, the rains as the deadline of return, so the devotional poet borrows the heroine's grief as an ornament of the god's hill and makes the peacock say the summons she can only feel."},{"a":"pr:14:19-32","b":"pp:1:177-189","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai describes the twice-born at Eragam in full, never failing in the six duties, keeping the three fires and chanting at the proper hour; Paripadal 14 compresses them to a riddle of counts, இரு பிறப்பு, இரு பெயர், ஒரு பெயர், and says the god delights in their aram.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The idyll's full portrait supplies what the Paripadal's terse count assumes, so its இரு பிறப்பு is the same இருபிறப்பாளர் the idyll names, and the brahmins' ritual life is shown to be, for both poems, one of the things Murugan is praised for enjoying."},{"a":"pr:15:1-18","b":"pr:13:1-13","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 13.10-13 says the god's chest holds by right (உரிமை) the heaven of those who worship it; Paripadal 15.15-18 says heaven is hard to climb unless the tulasi-wearer grants it, but is gained easily, by right (உரிமை), at Maalirunkunram.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The same claim of a worshipper's right to heaven is lodged first in the god's body and then in a hill one can walk to, so the pair shows Thirumal's grace being given a place on the map near Madurai.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:15:1-18","b":"pr:thirattu:12","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal thirattu 12 says those who live in Koodal and Parankunram are the ones truly living, and asks who would go to the world of the gods; Paripadal 15.15-18 keeps heaven as the goal and praises Maalirunkunram as the easy way to it.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"One Madurai poet makes the sacred hill a substitute that makes heaven unwanted and another makes it a means to heaven, so the shrine hills could be praised either as better than the world above or as its gate.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:15:54-66","b":"pr:1:1-13","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 1.1-13 describes two forms in turn, the white one with the hooded heads, the curved plough and a single earring, then the dark one with the bird banner; Paripadal 15.54-61 lists the single earring, the ringed plough, the tulasi, conch, discus and bird banner in one run of plurals and worships them as the two of great name.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"Song 1 keeps the brothers' marks apart in two portraits, while song 15 pours them into one litany and addresses the pair, so together they show the Balarama and Krishna emblems hardening into a shared catalogue of one double deity.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:15:54-66","b":"pr:13:22-36","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 13.22-36 makes the plough-bearer one of three forms of a single god (மூஉரு ஆகிய தலைபிரி ஒருவனை); Paripadal 15 keeps them as two, different in form but one in deed (வேறு வேறு உருவின் ஒரு தொழில் இருவர்), and bows to the two.","grade":"weak","better":"tension","te":"Against song 13's solution, which absorbs the brother as a form of the one god, song 15 keeps two persons and finds their unity in a single deed, like word and meaning, so the Paripadal holds two theologies of the pair.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:15:38-53","b":"k:4","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 4 says those who reach the feet of the one without liking or aversion have no suffering anywhere at any time; Paripadal 15.49-53 says the god appears in whatever world and removes suffering when people are troubled, and sends worshippers with their families to his hill.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural puts the movement on the devotee who reaches the feet, while the hymn puts it on a god who comes into each world and on the pilgrim's journey to one hill, so release from suffering is framed as both an inward arrival and a visit.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:16:48-55","b":"k:18","type":"develops","note":"Kural 18 warns that if the sky dries up, festival and worship will cease here, even for the gods; Paripadal 16.48-55 prays that the sky never refuse, so that the sandal, garlands, smoke and offerings of the river festival never dry and the Vaigai never runs dry.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The Kural states the dependence of worship on rain as a general warning, and the hymn turns the same dependence into a prayer said at the festival it describes, so the couplet's rule is shown at work in the Vaigai rites.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:16:32-38","b":"pr:12:75-85","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 12.81-83 has the settled Vaiyai show heaven's beauty as a reflection in its water; Paripadal 16.32-38 makes the flood itself look like the sky through which the star-flowered heavenly Ganga runs, with the lovers' fallen flowers as its stars.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"One poet finds the sky in the clear river after the flood and another in the flood's floating flowers, so the pair shows the Vaiyai as heaven on earth being made both from stillness and from festival litter.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:16:1-19","b":"k:215","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 215 likens the wealth of a wise man who helps the world to the village tank filled with water; Paripadal 16.1-4 likens the banks, worn away as the flood carries off pepper, sandal and curd foam, to the giving of a generous lord.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Kural uses full water as the image of a giver's wealth, and the Paripadal uses a giver as the image of water taking from its banks, so the pair shows the river-and-giving figure running both ways, with the Paripadal stressing that giving is a wearing away.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:17:1-21","b":"pr:8:17-28","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 8.17-28 has the hill answer back and forth (மாறுமாறு அதிர்ப்ப) with the cloud, the cock, the elephants and the caves, and the bees hum like the lute; Paripadal 17.9-21 sets the bards' lute, flute, drum, dancers and singer side by side with bees, tumbi, falls, creepers and peacock, each answering the other (மாறு எதிர் கோடல்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In song 8 the echo contest is among the hill's own things and the lute only a likeness for the bees, while song 17 puts the festival's real musicians into the contest against the hill, so together they show the Parankunram echo becoming a set piece in which worshippers and mountain compete as equals."},{"a":"pr:17:1-21","b":"pr:9:70-85","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 9.70-85 says that on the god's hill dancers outdo dancers and bards outdo those practised in song, the skilled the skilled; Paripadal 17.9-21 matches each human performer not with another performer but with a thing of the hill doing the same act.","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Song 9 makes the festival a rivalry of like with like among people, and song 17 widens the rival to the bee, the falls and the peacock, so the pair shows that for these poets the god's hill was praised by contest itself, and the strongest praise was to have nature enter the contest.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:17:22-39","b":"pp:1:67-77","type":"qualifies","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 67-77 marks the god's dwelling on the hill west of Koodal by the bees that sleep in the lotus and hum round the pool flowers opening like eyes; Paripadal 17.37-38 says that when the swingers leap together on festival day the bees that suck the pool flowers' pollen cannot reach them.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The guide poem gives the hill west of Koodal at rest, its pool bees the sign of the god's ordinary dwelling, and the hymn uses the same pool bees to measure the festival, when the crowd drives them off, so the pair shows the same place on its common day and on its feast day."},{"a":"pr:17:40-53","b":"k:43","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 43 makes the chief duty of the householder the care of ancestors, gods, guests, kin and self; Paripadal 17.42-46 says the god's festival and the fine feasting of guests pass in turn between the hill, the Vaiyai and Koodal, and the song closes with a prayer made with kin (யாமும் எம் சுற்றமும்).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural lays the duties to god, guest and kin on one household, while the hymn shows the same duties carried by a whole region in turn, hill, river and city, so the pair shows the couplet's private order also lived as the civic round of Madurai's festivals."},{"a":"pr:18:1-6","b":"pr:8:1-16","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 8.10-11 says that because of the god Parankunram equals the Himalaya (பரங்குன்று இமயக் குன்றம் நிகர்க்கும்); Paripadal 18.5-6 says this hill takes up the contest to stand level with the hill that bears the god's glory (ஏறுமாறு ஏற்கும் இக் குன்று).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Song 8 states the equality as a settled fact granted by the god's presence, and song 18 turns it into a challenge the hill still accepts, so read together they show the Paripadal poets making Parankunram's rank against the northern mountain a standing claim each hymn renews."},{"a":"pr:18:7-15","b":"k:1320","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1320 has the man say that even when he looks at her thinking, she flares up: whom were you thinking of as you looked? In Paripadal 18.8-14 the woman sees him gaze at a peacock and think, says she knows his thought and demands it, and he answers that he was thinking how the peacock had stolen her bearing.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The couplet ends on the woman's suspicion with no reply, while the hymn supplies the reply that disarms it, that the thought was of her through the bird that took her grace, so the pair shows the silent gaze as a fixed cause of sulking and the lover's answer as part of the same convention."},{"a":"pr:18:7-15","b":"kl:95","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 95 has the husband say he only watched a quail fight (குறும்பூழ் போர்), and the wife turns each kind of quail fight into a figure for his affairs; in Paripadal 18.12-14 the man says he looked at the peacock and at a quail dazed with revelry, and the woman looked at him as a stranger.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"In the Kalittokai the man's watching of birds is an alibi that the wife exposes, while in the Paripadal his gaze at birds is a lover's thought of her that he can explain away, so the pair shows the same excuse of bird-watching used once for a lie caught and once for a sulk mended on the god's hill."},{"a":"pr:18:16-22","b":"ai:47","type":"develops","note":"Ainkurunuru 47 has the woman tell the man that her companions know he tells many lies, just like his bard; Paripadal 18.16-22 shows her making the bard see the nail marks, the embrace and the ornaments on the man and calling his song a bloom of lies (பொய் வளம் பூத்தன).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Ainkurunuru gives the charge that the bard lies as his master does, and the hymn gives the proof shown to the bard's face, the marks the hill's women left, so together they show the god's festival hill as one of the places where marutham's straying was caught, with the bard's song refuted by the body."},{"a":"pr:18:31-42","b":"ku:1","type":"same_image","note":"Kuruntokai 1 says the hill of the Red One, who killed the demons till the field ran red, is thick with kanthal clusters the colour of blood (குருதிப் பூவின் குலைக்காந் தட்டே); Paripadal 18.35-36 says the close-packed kanthal of the god's hill are like the hands of men defeated in war and bound.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Both poets make the kanthal of Murugan's hill a sign of his war, but the Kuruntokai reads it by colour, the blood of the slain, and the Paripadal by shape, the clasped hands of the captured, so the pair shows the flower fixed as the god's war emblem while each poet chose a different basis for it."},{"a":"pr:19:1-18","b":"pr:9:1-11","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 9.8-11 says that when the god married the fawn-like girl, the eyes of the daughter of the thousand-eyed one rained like rain in summer at Parankunram; Paripadal 19.5-7 says the wedding with the peacock-like bride at the same hill seems to vie with that of the bride from heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In song 9 the second marriage is a wound to the first wife, while song 19 turns the same two weddings into a contest of splendour in which the earthly bride matches the heavenly one, so the hill's lore of two marriages could be sung either as a domestic grief or as the claim that Parankunram brings heaven's festival down to earth."},{"a":"pr:19:1-18","b":"pp:1:67-77","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 71-77 places the god's hill west of Koodal of crowded mansion streets, among wide muddy fields of lotus and bees; Paripadal 19.8-18 fills the whole stretch between the hill and Koodal (குன்றொடு கூடல் இடையெல்லாம்) with a pre-dawn procession of riders, chariots and garlanded heads.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The guide poem gives the pilgrim the hill's place relative to the city and its quiet fields, and the hymn shows the same stretch on festival day as one continuous human garland, so together they show Parankunram as Madurai's own shrine, whose road from the city was itself part of the festival."},{"a":"pr:19:1-18","b":"k:37","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 37 tells us to see the way of aram in the difference between the one who bears the palanquin and the one who rides in it; Paripadal 19.10-13 likens the festival riders on horses and in chariots, in fine ornament and cloth, to those who have done much aram and set out for the world of the excellent to take its fruit.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Both picture the fruit of aram as riding in finery, but the Paripadal puts that ride in the passage to the next world and uses it only as a simile for festival-goers, while the Kural reads it in this world's ranks, so the pair shows the Kural's hard claim taking a shared image of heavenly reward and pressing it into a judgement on living people."},{"a":"pr:19:38-57","b":"k:25","type":"develops","note":"Kural 25 names Indra, lord of the wide heaven, as witness enough to the strength of one who has quenched the five senses; Paripadal 19.50-52 records visitors at the Parankunram painted hall explaining a picture: Indra as the cat, Ahalya, Gautama who had gone out, and her form turned to stone in his anger.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"On the reading that Indra is witness through his own fall, the Kural's single name assumes a story that the Paripadal shows painted on a Madurai temple wall and told aloud to anyone who asked, so the couplet's allusion was not learned lore but a tale ordinary pilgrims were taught from pictures."},{"a":"pr:19:58-66","b":"pr:8:17-28","type":"same_image","note":"Paripadal 8.19-21 has the hill's caves (மலை முழை) answer back and forth the cock's crow and the musth elephants' trumpeting that answer the god's elephant and the thunder; Paripadal 19.62-65 has the echoing cave (இசை முழை) take up a lost girl's call and lead her astray.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"In song 8 the cave echo multiplies the god's elephant, his cock and the storm into a sign that the whole hill answers him, while in song 19 the same caves fool a lost girl and the poet calls this the hill's innocent nature, so the pair shows the Parankunram echo used both as a mark of divine presence and as the hill's playful character."},{"a":"pr:19:85-105","b":"a:98","type":"qualifies","note":"In Akananuru 98 the girl fears that if Murugan, the tall Vel smelling of veri, should actually relieve her at the mother's rite, her lover will hear that her pain was something else; Paripadal 19.89-94 says maidens will not reach young men's arms, nor wives their husbands' grace, unless they eat the leftover of the god's elephant at his hill.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The akam girl's dread makes sense only if Murugan really does rule a girl's union, and the Paripadal states that power openly as a festival rite that maidens and wives perform, so together they show that the akam irony about the veri rite rests on a public belief in the god as the giver of husbands, not on disbelief in him."},{"a":"pr:19:85-105","b":"pp:1:206-217","type":"develops","note":"Thirumurugatruppadai 206-217 describes the god as red, in red clothing (செய்யன் சிவந்த ஆடையன்), dancing on every hill with the women's soft shoulders in his arms; Paripadal 19.97-100 tells him his dress and cloak are red, his weapon coral-coloured, his form like fire and his face like the young sun.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The guide poem's red god is roaming from hill to hill in the kurinji dance, while the hymn addresses the same red figure as the image standing by the kadambu at one shrine, praised by a gathered company, so the pair shows one colour iconography carried from the wandering god of the hill people to the fixed god of a temple."},{"a":"pr:20:68-83","b":"a:186","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 186 has the courtesan say, for the wife's women to overhear, that the wife's real enemy is the husband who leaves women pale and lives in her own house; Paripadal 20.79-82 has the courtesan tell the wife to her face that he is the thief and she is not (அவன் கள்வன்; கள்வி நான் அல்லேன்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Together the two show that shifting the blame onto the husband was the courtesan's settled defence in marutham, and that what the Akananuru gives as an overheard boast the festival poem stages as a face-to-face exchange, with the husband's gift on her body as the evidence and a crowd to hear both sides."},{"a":"pr:20:68-83","b":"k:913","type":"tension","note":"Kural 913 makes the embrace of women who love for wealth a falsehood, like holding a stranger's corpse in the dark; in Paripadal 20.79-82 the paid woman herself argues that the payment was fair (விலை தந்தான்) and that the falsehood is the man's.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural judges the bought embrace from the client's side, so the deceit is hers; the Paripadal lets the woman answer from inside the bargain, and on that reading the two cannot both stand, since one places the lie in her love and the other in his gift of what was not his to give."},{"a":"pr:20:48-67","b":"n:385","type":"develops","note":"Naladiyar 385 has the wife wonder how women who embrace many chests for gain can do so daily; Paripadal 20.48-51 has the wife's companion call the courtesan a bought woman whose womanhood is common to all (பெண்மைப் பொதுமைப் பிணையிலி).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Naladiyar's contrast of chaste wife and common woman is a private wondering, while in the Paripadal the same contrast becomes public abuse at the river, worked out as a farmer's claim on a straying ox, which shows the wife's chastity defended as a household's ownership of the husband and not only as her own shame."},{"a":"pr:20:84-98","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 55 says the wife who rises worshipping her husband commands the rain; Paripadal 20.88-89 has onlookers tell a wronged wife that worthy women praise and bow to their husbands even when the husbands slight them (கேள்வரை ஏத்தி இறைஞ்சுவார்).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural offers the wife's worship of her husband as a source of power over nature, but the Paripadal shows the same ideal used in a quarrel to silence a wife whose husband has given her bangle away, so the couplet's praise of devotion could serve in practice as counsel to put up with a man's straying.","gr":3},{"a":"pr:20:84-98","b":"k:1251","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1251 has the woman say desire's axe breaks the door of restraint; Paripadal 20.91-94 has the onlookers say no one can bar men's chests from women and ask whether love stays within its bounds (கட வரை நிற்குமோ காமம்?).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the Kural the claim that desire breaks every bound is a woman's confession about her own longing, while in the Paripadal the same claim is turned into an excuse for the husband's straying and urged on the wife, which shows how one maxim about love's force served opposite ends depending on whose desire it named."},{"a":"pr:20:99-112","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1330 says sulking is love's pleasure and its pleasure is the embrace that follows; Paripadal 20.109-112 says it is the Vaiyai's nature to join lovers so that quarrel, friendship, parting, love and toddy are praised together.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural's rule that the sulk sweetens union is a statement about two lovers, and the Paripadal extends it to a whole town's festival, closing a poem whose centre was a bitter public quarrel by counting that quarrel among the river's pleasures, so the couplet's private economy of oodal is shown working at civic scale."},{"a":"pr:21:1-17","b":"k:10","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 10 says only those who reach the Lord's feet (இறைவன் அடிசேரா தார்) cross the sea of births; Paripadal 21.16 asks only that dwelling at the foot of the god's hill (குன்றத்து அடி உறை) be granted, and line 68 repeats it as dwelling at the god's feet (நின் அடி உறை).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The Kural's feet are a way out of rebirth, while the Paripadal makes the same feet a place on the ground, the foot of one hill where worshippers ask to go on living, so the pair shows the god's feet as a refuge of release in the ethical book and as a home on earth in the older festival hymn."},{"a":"pr:21:18-29","b":"k:1090","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1090 says brewed toddy (அடுநறா) delights only those who drink it, unlike love, which delights those who see it; Paripadal 21.20 has a dancer held by the joy of brewed toddy (அடு நறா மகிழ்) sulking at her husband, and the poet says such scenes, when recalled, are a painting's beauty.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The Kural separates the drinker's private pleasure from the beholder's pleasure in love, while the Paripadal mixes them in one woman, drunk and sulking at her husband, and makes her a picture for onlookers on the hill, so held together they show that at the shrine festival even the drinker's mood became something seen and enjoyed by others."},{"a":"pr:21:30-38","b":"a:82","type":"same_image","note":"Akananuru 82.1-10 makes the hill country a stage: wind in bamboo the flute, the waterfall the drum, bees the lute, monkeys the audience and the peacock a dancer; Paripadal 21.30-38 has the peacock dance, the thumbi as flute, bees as lute and the waterfall as drum on the god's hill.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"In the Akananuru the hill's orchestra is a love poem's setting, praising the hill man's country and ending in the woman's lonely night, while the Paripadal keeps the same instruments, drops the monkey audience and the lovers, and gives the scene to the god with the drum, so the pair shows a figure from akam landscape reused as worship of the hill's own god."},{"a":"pr:21:39-53","b":"pr:6:88-96","type":"qualifies","note":"In Paripadal 6.88-92 a husband says a woman bathing in his pool, swept under by a wave, fell on him unbidden, and swears by cool Parankunram; in Paripadal 21.39-45 a wife at a pool on Parankunram loses her float and her husband plunges in and embraces her.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The Vaiyai song's husband uses a fall in the water as an alibi for touching another woman and swears by Parankunram, while the Parankunram song shows the water embrace on that hill as a husband's rescue of his own wife, so the pair shows one festival accident read either as an excuse for straying or as wedded love."},{"a":"pr:21:54-70","b":"pr:5:71-82","type":"qualifies","note":"Paripadal 5.79-81 says what we ask of the god is not wealth, gold or pleasure (பொருளும் பொன்னும் போகமும் அல்ல) but grace, love and aram; Paripadal 21.68-70, after the drinking and dancing of the hill, asks that dwelling at his feet be granted as today (இன்று போல்), with their company.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The two Murugan songs end in prayers to the same god that pull apart: one refuses pleasure and asks for inner gifts, the other asks that a festival day of liquor, dance and couples simply recur, so the anthology holds both a moral and a festive idea of what worship should obtain.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:22:1-16","b":"pn:107","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 107 answers the poets who praise Pari alone: there is also the rain (மாரியும் உண்டு) that sustains the world; Paripadal 22.8 says the sky poured water like the king's bounty (அவன் வண்மைபோல் வானம் பொழிந்த நீர்), after likening clouds, thunder, drops and lightning to his elephants, drum, arrows and spear.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Kapilar measures the patron against the rain and lets the rain win, while the Paripadal measures the rain against the king and makes the whole storm a copy of his war and gifts, so held together they show the rain-and-giving comparison run in both directions, with the river hymn making the king the original and nature the likeness."},{"a":"pr:22:26-35","b":"pr:8:112-130","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 8.112-117 says the eyes, faces, feet, hands, breasts and lips of girls rising from the hill pool cannot be told apart from the lotus and lilies of the water; Paripadal 22.32-34 says onlookers cannot decide whether the women adorn the Vaiyai or the Vaiyai's flood adorns them.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The hill song confuses women and flowers part by part, while the river song lifts the doubt to the whole, women against river, and refuses to settle which is the ornament, so the pair shows the Paripadal using the undecided likeness as its stock praise of festival bathing and widening it from a catalogue to a single question."},{"a":"pr:22:36-50","b":"pr:21:30-38","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 21.30-38 makes the peacock, thumbi, bees and waterfall into dancer, flute, lute and drum on the god's hill; Paripadal 22.36-44 has real drum, lute, flute and hand drum sounding with bees and thumbi at the Vaiyai ford, the wind as dancer (வளி நடன்), and says they exchange their work (தொழில் மாறு கொள்ளும்).","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"On the hill the orchestra is made of creatures only, while at the river the town's instruments and the grove's creatures play together and trade parts, so the two songs, set side by side in one chapter, show the hill as nature performing for its god and the ford as the town and nature performing with each other."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:1:68-86","b":"pn:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Purananuru 55.1-4 gives the mountain bow strung with a snake (பாம்புஞாண்) and the three forts burned with one arrow as the blue-throated god's feat, an emblem to measure the Pandya by; Paripadal Thirattu 1.80-82 says the serpent became the string of the Himalaya bow when the bull-rider destroyed the three cities and that it was they who gave him that ancient fame.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The same snake-strung bow is Shiva's glory in a court poem and the serpent's own merit in a shrine hymn, so the pair shows that a fixed myth could be re-credited to whichever power a cult served, the bowstring becoming the hero once it had a temple."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:1:68-86","b":"pr:1:1-13","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 1.1-2 opens with the god's form whose thousand awful hooded heads (ஆயிரம் விரித்த அணங்குடை அருந் தலை) rise over his crown, white as a conch and carrying the plough; Paripadal Thirattu 1.83-85 uses the same words for a separate lord with his host, bowed to before the god is praised.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"One formula line serves in the first song as a form of Mal himself and in this song as an attendant lord with his own retinue and his own deeds, so the anthology shows the serpent both absorbed into the god's body and kept as a distinct object of worship at his shrine."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:1:68-86","b":"p:11","type":"qualifies","note":"Thiruppugazh 11 names Murugan's uncle as the one who sleeps on the serpent and who churned the sea of old (பனகம் துயில்கின்ற ... கடல் முன்பு கடைந்த); Paripadal Thirattu 1.68-75 gives the churning to the serpent, who lifted the mountain and was the rope while the discus-bearer (ஆழியான்) only pulled.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"By Arunagirinathar's time the churning is simply Mal's act and the serpent his bed, while the Sangam hymn praises the rope for not breaking, so the pair shows the serpent losing its share of the myth as it became the god's couch."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:1:30-48","b":"k:1103","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1103 asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one is sweeter than sleep in the soft arms of the one you love; Paripadal Thirattu 1.33-46 lists the shrine crowd as matched men and women and says the god's town is like the heaven where ripened deeds are enjoyed.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The couplet sets lovers' joy against Mal's heaven as rivals, but the hymn builds its likeness of heaven out of the paired lovers in the god's own crowd, so the opposition the Kural plays on was not one the temple festival felt."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:1:30-48","b":"pt:31","type":"qualifies","note":"Patitruppattu 31.1-10 shows crowds from the four quarters who go unfed (உண்ணாப் பைஞ்ஞிலம்), bathe at the cold ghat and praise the red feet of the tulasi-garlanded lord before going home glad; Paripadal Thirattu 1.43-46 shows the crowd at the god's town bearing food offerings, umbrellas, incense and flowers, in a town like a heaven of enjoyment.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read together the two give two modes of early Mal worship, a fasting and bathing crowd in the Chera poem and a feasting, paired and pleasure-seeking crowd in the Koodal hymn, so devotion to the same god was already practised as abstinence in one place and as festival in another."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:2:28-45","b":"kl:98","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 98 has the husband excuse his absence by the Vaiyai's new flood (வையை வரு புனல் ஆடத் தவிர்ந்தேன்); Paripadal thirattu 2.36-39 has the rival excuse the lover's garland in her hair by the same flood (தானே புறன் தந்து வேய்தந்தது ... அறிந்து புனல் புணர்த்தது).","grade":"weak","better":"develops","te":"Held together, the two show that the festival flood was the stock excuse of the whole triangle: the man pleads it to his wife, and the other woman pleads it to the wife too, so the wife's scorn in the Kalittokai answers an alibi that every party in the Vaiyai festival could use."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:2:58-71","b":"pr:6:44-60","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 6.44-51 has the learned brahmins dismayed that the flood is changed by flowers, roots, the food of common people and spilled toddy; thirattu 2.58-63 splits the refusal among பார்ப்பார், அந்தணர் and ஐயர, each for its own stain: toddy, lovers' wedding scents, honey.","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"The two songs together show that the priests' refusal to bathe was a set piece of the Vaiyai festival song, and the fragment's three causes make plain what song 6 only lists: every stain that bars the priest is a mark of the festival the town is enjoying, so the scene sets ritual purity against the river's festive use."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:2:46-57","b":"a:296","type":"develops","note":"Akananuru 296 says the man's bathing in the great Vaiyai with the dark girl has become gossip in Koodal (அலர்ஆ கின்று, அது பலர்வாய்ப் பட்டே); thirattu 2.51-56 shows a woman wearing a garland from the flood openly, for the land to know (நாடு அறியப் பூ மாலை அப்பி), and the town's gossip following (ஊர்த்து அலர்).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The fragment shows how the gossip the Akananuru friend throws at the man was made: a love token worn in plain sight at the river festival, chosen by the woman as display, so the akam reproach of public shame rests on a festival custom in which the other woman wanted to be seen.","gr":4},{"a":"pr:thirattu:2:88-101","b":"pr:10:125-130","type":"develops","note":"Paripadal 10.125-127 likens the flood pouring gold on the fields to the Vazhuthi pouring gold into poets' hands; thirattu 2.95-101 likens Maran bathing in the same flood with the people of Koodal to Indra bathing in the river of the sky.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Read together, the two Vaiyai songs place the Pandya on both sides of the flood: song 10 makes the river like the king in giving, the fragment makes the king like a god in bathing, so the king stands between the river's bounty below and Indra's heaven above, and the festival praises him through both."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:7","b":"k:118","type":"develops","note":"Kural 118 likens the worthy man's impartiality to a balance that levels itself before it weighs; thirattu 7 has poets weigh the world against Koodal with the balance of their learning (புலக் கோலால் தூக்க).","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Read beside the Kural's just balance, the fragment's praise borrows the authority of an impartial weighing, so the poets present their verdict on Koodal as fairness, the ornament of the worthy, rather than as a court singer's flattery."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:8","b":"kl:35","type":"same_image","note":"Kalittokai 35 line 2 likens humming bees in the boughs to the enjoyment of those who live on a diligent man's wealth; thirattu 8 likens those who live on gifts (பரிசில் வாழ்நர்) to bees eating pollen.","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"The bee and the dependent were interchangeable figures that ran in both directions, a season described through patronage in Kalittokai and a society through a flower in the Paripadal, and only the Paripadal says whom the dependents feed on: the households, not the palace."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:9","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 233 says nothing in the world stands undying except towering fame; thirattu 9 says Madurai's fame will stand and blossom as long as the king's hill exists (குன்றம் உண்டாகும் அளவு).","grade":"weak","better":"agrees","te":"The Kural makes fame the one thing that does not perish, while the Paripadal blessing gives even the city's fame a term measured by a hill, so the poets' praise of undying renown was bounded by the oldest lasting things of the land."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:9","b":"pr:thirattu:11","type":"develops","note":"Thirattu 9, 10 and 11 share one frame ('can Madurai decline ... as long as the king's ... exists'); 9 measures it by his hill, 10 by his Vaigai, 11 by his word (வார்த்தை).","grade":"weak","better":null,"te":"Taken as a set, the fragments move the city's guarantee from a hill and a river to the king's word, so the last stanza rests Madurai's permanence on speech and renown, the poet's own stock, rather than on the land."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:12","b":"pn:38","type":"develops","note":"Purananuru 38 says bards think of Killivalavan's land even in the gods' golden grove, because there no one with means gives and no one in want asks (உடையோர் ஈதலும் இல்லோர் இரத்தலும்); thirattu 12 says Koodal honours givers and rejoices at receivers, so who would go to the gods' world (புத்தேள் உலகு)?","grade":"sound","better":null,"te":"Purananuru 38 supplies the reason the fragment leaves unsaid, that heaven is poorer for lacking the exchange of giving and asking, so the Paripadal's preference for Koodal over heaven rests on an older argument first made for a king's land and here transferred to a city and its god's hill.","gr":2},{"a":"pr:thirattu:12","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"Kural 222 says taking is bad even if called the good path to heaven, and giving good even if there were no higher world; thirattu 12 praises a city that rejoices to see receivers (ஏற்பாரைப் பார்த்து உவக்கும்) and ranks its life above heaven. On the reading that ஏற்பார் are those who take gifts, the fragment honours the very taking the Kural calls bad.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"Both cut the value of giving loose from heaven, but the Kural does it by condemning the receiver while the Paripadal makes the receiver's presence a joy of the city, so the two hold opposite views of whether asking belongs to the good life."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:6","b":"k:1014","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1014 asks whether shame (நாணுடைமை) is not an ornament of the worthy; thirattu 6 calls modesty the girl's ancient ornament (நாண் எனும் தொல்லை அணி) at her first union.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image","te":"The same figure of shame as an ornament serves the Kural for the moral restraint of the worthy and the Paripadal for a bride's reserve at first union, which shows that naanam named both and that the poets ranked the bride's reserve above her jewels as the Kural ranks restraint above pride."},{"a":"pr:thirattu:13","b":"kl:35","type":"qualifies","note":"Kalittokai 35 counts Koodal's seasonal pleasures (bees on the Vaigai's sandbank, the festival, the townsmen savouring poets' new words) as the times the absent man promised to return; thirattu 13 lists the Vaigai flood, the god's hill and spear-eyed women as pleasures among which Koodal's people lose their way daily.","grade":"forced","better":null,"te":"The same catalogue of Koodal's pleasures marks the hour of a promised return in the Kalittokai and causes daily indecision in the Paripadal, so the city's festive plenty was a stock that akam used to sharpen absence and city praise used to show abundance."}]});
